<graph edgedefault="undirected"><node id="Yellow Mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Yellow Mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Yellow mealworms, the larval stage of *Tenebrio molitor* Linnaeus (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae), are widely recognized both as a mass-produced feed source for birds, reptiles, amphibians, fish, and as a sustainable protein option for human and animal consumption, and as an emerging biological model in environmental research. They have gained particular attention for their unique capacity to ingest, fragment, and partially metabolize a broad spectrum of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low- and high-density polyethylene (LDPE, HDPE), polyurethane (PU) foams, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA), Styrofoam, and even polypropylene-based mask materials. This ability positions yellow mealworms as a promising natural system for addressing the global challenge of plastic waste management.  

Experimental studies have demonstrated that yellow mealworms can survive on diets composed solely of plastics, although their growth, weight gain, and pupation rates are significantly reduced compared to those reared on nutrient-rich natural substrates such as wheat bran. In some cases, such as with HDPE film, larvae have shown modest weight gain, indicating that certain polymers may provide limited nutritional or energetic value. However, overall developmental performance remains inferior under plastic-only diets, highlighting the restricted nutritional contribution of synthetic polymers.  

A central aspect of their biodegradation ability lies in the role of the gut microbiome. Feeding trials reveal that ingestion of plastics induces substantial shifts in both bacterial and fungal communities within the larval gut. Commonly identified microbial taxa include members of Enterobacteriaceae, *Spiroplasma*, *Lactobacillus*, and *Enterococcus*. These microbial communities are believed to facilitate the depolymerization of plastics, enabling assimilation of some breakdown products and excretion of degraded residues. Isolated bacterial strains from yellow mealworms have been shown to degrade polystyrene independently, reinforcing the hypothesis that gut microbes are key drivers of plastic biodegradation.  

Yellow mealworms have also been studied in comparative contexts alongside other insect larvae, such as superworms and lesser waxworms, to evaluate differences in plastic ingestion, survival, and microbial dependence. Findings suggest that while yellow mealworms exhibit notable plastic-degrading capacity, the efficiency and mechanisms may vary across insect species, with some evidence pointing to gut microbe-dependence in polyethylene degradation.  

Beyond their ecological significance, yellow mealworms are valued for their nutritional properties. They are rich in protein and are increasingly considered a sustainable food and feed alternative, aligning with global efforts to develop environmentally friendly protein sources.  

In summary, yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) serve a dual role as both a practical protein resource and a model organism for studying insect-mediated plastic biodegradation. Their ability to ingest and metabolize diverse synthetic polymers, coupled with the adaptive functions of their gut microbiota, underscores their potential contribution to innovative, biologically based strategies for mitigating plastic pollution while also supporting sustainable food systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Yellow mealworms, larvae of Tenebrio molitor, are studied for their ability to digest and biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
