<node id="Tenebrio molitor">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">**Comprehensive Summary of Tenebrio molitor**  

Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the yellow mealworm, mealworm beetle, or darkling beetle, is a beetle species within the family Tenebrionidae and order Coleoptera. It undergoes complete metamorphosis with four distinct stages: egg, larva (mealworm), pupa, and adult beetle. The larval stage, widely recognized as “mealworms,” is the most studied due to its ecological, nutritional, and scientific importance.  

A defining characteristic of Tenebrio molitor is its ability to ingest and partially biodegrade a wide range of synthetic polymers and organic substrates. Its larvae have been shown to consume plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyurethane foams, polylactic acid (PLA), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyester, and even graphene oxide. They are also capable of degrading cellulose and plastic mixtures, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE). This biodegradation process is facilitated by the insect’s gut microbiota, which includes bacterial strains such as *Klebsiella oxytoca*, *Bacillus* species, and *Enterobacter hormaechei*. These microbes play a central role in polymer breakdown and influence gut microbial diversity depending on diet. While Tenebrio molitor larvae can survive on plastic-based diets, such feeding regimes may affect their growth, reproduction, and microbial balance. Additionally, their frass (insect waste) has been investigated for applications such as biochar production from agricultural residues, highlighting their potential in waste valorization and circular economy strategies.  

Beyond environmental applications, Tenebrio molitor is increasingly recognized as a sustainable food and feed source. Its larvae are rich in protein, fats, chitosan, and essential micronutrients, making them a promising alternative to conventional livestock-based proteins. They have been incorporated into experimental diets for poultry, pigs, and aquaculture species, and processed into products such as mealworm powders. Byproducts such as frass and exuviae (shed exoskeletons) also hold potential for agricultural and industrial use. The species has been evaluated as a novel food ingredient in Europe and is part of global initiatives promoting edible insects as a sustainable protein source to enhance food security.  

In scientific research, Tenebrio molitor serves as an important model organism in entomology, physiology, and molecular biology. It has been studied for its immune defenses, including the regulation of antimicrobial genes such as *Coleopterin* during bacterial challenges, providing insights into insect immunity and host–microbe interactions. The sequencing of its reference genome (Tenebrio molitor_v3) has enabled advanced genetic and molecular studies, including investigations into gut microbial dynamics under different diets. Research has also examined its adaptability to environmental conditions, developmental biology, and unique pupal structures such as gin-traps, contributing to a broader understanding of insect physiology and ecological interactions.  

In summary, Tenebrio molitor is a species of global significance due to its multifaceted roles. Its ability to biodegrade plastics positions it as a potential contributor to sustainable waste management and environmental remediation. Its nutritional composition supports its use as an alternative protein source in food and feed systems, while its role as a model organism advances scientific knowledge of insect biology, immunity, and host–microbe relationships. Collectively, these attributes underscore Tenebrio molitor’s importance in addressing contemporary challenges in sustainability, food security, and biological research.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworm, is a species of darkling beetle whose larvae can consume and degrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;A beetle species (Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae) studied for developmental plasticity under different diets.</data>
