<node id="Polyurethane (PU)">
  <data key="d0">Polyurethane (PU)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyurethane (PU) is a versatile petroleum-based plastic polymer characterized by carbamate (urethane) bonds. It is one of the most widely used synthetic materials, appearing in both thermoplastic and thermoset forms, and is integral to numerous industrial and consumer applications. PU is commonly employed in coatings, adhesives, sealants, elastomers, insulation materials, furniture, footwear, automotive components such as seats, textiles, and foam products. Its adaptability, durability, and performance properties have made it a ubiquitous material in daily life.  

Despite its widespread utility, polyurethane poses significant environmental challenges. It is highly resistant to natural degradation, exhibits low biodegradability, and is difficult to recycle. As a result, large volumes of PU waste are disposed of through incineration or landfilling, contributing to plastic pollution and long-term environmental persistence.  

In recent years, research has explored potential biological pathways for polyurethane degradation. Studies have shown that certain fungi and insect larvae, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), are capable of ingesting and partially breaking down polyurethane materials. These findings suggest possible avenues for biotechnological approaches to PU waste management, although such methods remain experimental and are not yet scalable solutions to the global problem of polyurethane pollution.  

Overall, polyurethane (PU) is a widely used and highly functional synthetic polymer that plays a critical role in modern industries and consumer products. However, its environmental impact, stemming from poor recyclability and resistance to degradation, has prompted growing scientific interest in alternative disposal and biodegradation strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384995</data>
</node>
<node id="Polyether-PU">
  <data key="d0">Polyether-PU</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane primarily used for thermal insulation. It is less biodegradable than polyester-PU and was the focus of the biodegradation study with yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane polymer that has been studied for biodegradation by fungi such as Cladosporium species.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
