  <data key="d5">1756370590</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Microbiome">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microbiome</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The **gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) larvae** is a diverse and dynamic microbial community residing in the insect’s digestive tract, composed predominantly of bacteria and, to a lesser extent, fungi. This microbiome plays a central role in maintaining host health, supporting metabolism, and enabling unique ecological functions, most notably the biodegradation of synthetic polymers. Similar microbial communities have also been studied in related insect larvae, such as *Zophobas atratus*, to better understand their role in plastic degradation.  

Research has shown that the gut microbiome of *T. molitor* is highly adaptable, with its composition and diversity shifting in response to dietary inputs. When larvae are fed conventional cereal-based diets, the microbial community structure differs significantly from that observed under plastic-based diets, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyether-based polyurethane (polyether-PU), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polypropylene (PP). These dietary shifts selectively enrich microbial taxa or consortia capable of polymer depolymerization and metabolism, demonstrating the microbiome’s responsiveness to both nutritional and chemical environments.  

Experimental evidence confirms that the gut microbiome directly contributes to plastic biodegradation. Microorganisms within the gut secrete extracellular enzymes and metabolites that depolymerize and metabolize plastic-derived compounds. In some cases, microbial extracts or supernatants derived from the gut have been shown to degrade plastics outside the host, although complete and efficient degradation often requires the synergistic interaction between the insect host and its microbiome. This highlights the importance of host–microbe interactions in enabling effective biodegradation processes.  

Beyond polymer breakdown, the gut microbiome is essential for host physiology, contributing to nutrient cycling, including carbon and nitrogen turnover during plastic degradation. It is also sensitive to external factors such as diet composition, polymer type, and antibiotic exposure. Studies analyzing bacterial richness and diversity under different feeding conditions have demonstrated that shifts in microbial community structure directly influence both host health and the efficiency of plastic biodegradation. For example, variations in microbial abundance and diversity have been observed when larvae ingest polystyrene of different molecular weights, indicating that the microbiome dynamically adjusts to the chemical complexity of ingested polymers.  

Comparative studies with other insect larvae, such as *Zophobas atratus*, have further emphasized the role of microbial enzymatic activity in facilitating the breakdown of recalcitrant plastics like polystyrene and polyethylene. These investigations underscore the broader ecological and biotechnological significance of insect gut microbiomes as natural systems for polymer degradation.  

In summary, the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae represents a specialized, diet-responsive microbial ecosystem with profound ecological and applied relevance. Its demonstrated ability to adapt to synthetic polymer diets and contribute to the degradation of chemically diverse plastics positions it as a promising natural model for developing sustainable strategies to mitigate plastic waste through insect–microbe interactions and microbial metabolic processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a