<node id="Plastic Pollution">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Pollution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic pollution is a major global environmental issue characterized by the accumulation, persistence, and mismanagement of non-biodegradable plastics in natural ecosystems. It primarily results from the widespread production, use, and improper disposal of petroleum-based plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polyurethane (PU). Due to their resistance to natural degradation, these materials accumulate in landfills, rivers, lakes, soils, and oceans, where they fragment into microplastics and nanoplastics. These smaller particles are particularly concerning because they infiltrate food chains, water supplies, and even the air, posing risks to both ecological systems and human health.  

In marine and terrestrial environments, plastic pollution disrupts habitats, threatens biodiversity, and endangers wildlife through ingestion, suffocation, and entanglement. The introduction of toxic substances from plastics into ecosystems further amplifies environmental harm. For humans, the presence of microplastics in food, drinking water, and the atmosphere raises concerns about long-term health effects, though the full extent of these impacts is still under investigation. The persistence of plastics in the environment makes this form of pollution one of the most pressing ecological challenges of the modern era.  

The problem is exacerbated by poor recycling rates, uncontrolled dumping, and the prevalence of single-use plastics, which contribute significantly to waste accumulation. While some research highlights potential biological solutions, such as the ability of certain organisms like mealworms to degrade polystyrene, these approaches remain limited in scale. Addressing plastic pollution effectively requires systemic and coordinated strategies, including the reduction of single-use plastics, the adoption of sustainable waste management practices, and the development and promotion of biodegradable alternatives.  

Overall, plastic pollution represents a critical and persistent environmental threat driven by the durability and ubiquity of synthetic polymers. Its global scale, contribution to microplastic contamination, and harmful effects on ecosystems and human health underscore the urgent need for innovative, sustainable, and large-scale interventions to mitigate and ultimately eliminate its impact.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
