<node id="Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR)">
  <data key="d0">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique widely applied to investigate the chemical composition, structural changes, and degradation pathways of polymers and plastics. The method functions by detecting the vibrational modes of molecular bonds, which allows for the identification and monitoring of functional groups within complex materials. This capability makes FTIR particularly effective for assessing chemical modifications such as depolymerization, oxidation, and bond scission, processes that are central to understanding both abiotic and biotic polymer breakdown.  

In the context of polymer degradation, FTIR has been extensively used to study structural transformations in synthetic materials. For example, it has revealed the scission of ether and urethane bonds in polyether-based polyurethane (PU), as well as chemical changes in PU foams and virgin PU during experimental treatments. Similarly, FTIR has been employed to characterize degradation in polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and waste composite (WC) films, providing evidence of functional group alterations that signify chemical transformation.  

FTIR also plays a critical role in biodegradation research, where it confirms microbial and biological activity on plastics. Studies have demonstrated its effectiveness in detecting functional group changes in plastics degraded by microorganisms such as *Priestia megaterium* S1. By comparing spectra before and after microbial treatment, FTIR provides direct evidence of chemical modifications that occur during biological digestion of polymers.  

A particularly notable application of FTIR is in insect-mediated plastic degradation studies. It has been used to analyze frass (excreta) from larvae such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) fed with plastics including PS, PE, LDPE, PP, and even mask materials. By comparing feedstocks with frass samples, FTIR reveals characteristic changes in functional groups, thereby confirming digestion and chemical transformation within the insect gut. This application highlights the technique’s ability to trace degradation pathways and quantify the extent of chemical alteration in biologically processed plastics.  

Overall, Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is a versatile and indispensable tool for monitoring chemical and structural changes in polymers. Its sensitivity to functional group alterations enables researchers to evaluate degradation mechanisms, confirm biodegradation processes, and characterize the chemical fate of plastics across environmental, microbial, and biological contexts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386487</data>
</node>
<node id="X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS)">
  <data key="d0">X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">XPS is an analytical technique used to analyze changes in polyether-PU polymer fragments recovered from frass.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS is a method used to verify changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;X-ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is a technique used to verify changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;XPS was used to analyze chemical changes in degraded PS films.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
