</node>
<node id="Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)">
  <data key="d0">Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is an analytical method widely employed to investigate the thermal stability and degradation behavior of materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation studies. The technique measures changes in a sample’s weight as it is subjected to controlled heating, thereby providing insights into thermal degradation processes and stability profiles. TGA has been applied to a variety of materials, including plastics, polyurethane (PU) foams, polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE-1), and mask materials, to assess how their thermal properties evolve during or after biodegradation.  

In biodegradation research, TGA is used to characterize the extent of material breakdown by comparing thermal stability before and after exposure to biological agents. For example, it has been employed to determine the reduced thermal stability of polyether-based polyurethane foam following biodegradation, highlighting the material’s susceptibility to microbial or enzymatic attack. Similarly, TGA has been used to evaluate the thermal degradation of virgin PU compared to PU samples subjected to biodegradation, providing a direct measure of structural and compositional changes.  

The method has also been applied in studies involving insect-mediated degradation. Specifically, TGA has been used to analyze PS and PE-1 samples, as well as the frass (excrement) of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, to compare thermal variations between the original feedstocks and the digested residues. This application demonstrates TGA’s utility in distinguishing between unprocessed materials and biologically transformed outputs. In related work, TGA has been used to assess the thermal degradation of mask materials after ingestion by mealworms, further underscoring its role in evaluating the impact of biological processes on synthetic polymers.  

Overall, Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) serves as a critical tool for characterizing weight loss, thermal stability, and degradation patterns of polymers and other materials under thermal stress. Its application in biodegradation studies provides valuable information on how materials such as plastics, foams, and protective equipment change structurally and chemically after biological interaction, making it an essential method for understanding material performance, environmental impact, and potential recycling or disposal strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) is a method used to detect thermal modifications and decomposition patterns in frass samples from larvae fed with PP.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385277</data>
</node>
<node id="Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC)">
  <data key="d0">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is an analytical technique widely used in polymer science for determining the molecular weight and molecular weight distribution of polymers and plastics. As a form of size-exclusion chromatography, GPC separates polymer chains according to their hydrodynamic volume, enabling precise characterization of polymer structure, chain length, and degradation behavior. This makes it a fundamental tool for evaluating polymer synthesis, processing, recycling, and environmental impact.  

A central application of GPC is in the study of polymer degradation and depolymerization. By quantifying changes in molecular weight, GPC provides direct evidence of chemical, thermal, or microbial breakdown of polymers. It has been employed to monitor decreases in molecular weight of polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foam fragments during depolymerization, as well as to track the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in biodegradation experiments. GPC has also been used to analyze residual polymer fractions and compare the stability of different polymer types, including expanded polystyrene (EPS) and high-impact polystyrene (HIPS). In biological studies, it has been applied to detect degraded polystyrene products in insect frass compared to control materials, confirming depolymerization processes.  

Beyond polystyrene, GPC has been extensively applied to investigate the depolymerization and biodegradation of other plastics such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Its ability to measure both average molecular weight (Mw) and molecular weight distribution (MWD) provides insights not only into the extent of degradation but also into the uniformity of the process across different polymer chains. This dual capability allows researchers to better understand how degradation mechanisms affect polymers at the molecular level.  

In environmental and biological contexts, GPC has been used to quantify molecular weight changes in polymers subjected to insect-mediated degradation, such as mask materials and Styrofoam ingested by mealworms. These applications demonstrate its value in linking laboratory-scale polymer analysis with real-world biodegradation and recycling studies. Additionally, GPC has been employed with specific instrumentation, such as the 1260 Infinity II GPC/SEC system, to analyze molecular weight changes in PS and PVC polymers after biodegradation.  

Overall, Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is regarded as a standard and indispensable method in polymer analysis. By delivering accurate molecular weight data and distributions, it supports the evaluation of polymer synthesis, degradation pathways, recycling efficiency, and environmental persistence. Its versatility across different polymer types and experimental contexts establishes GPC as a cornerstone technique in materials science, polymer chemistry, and sustainability research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
