<node id="Aspergillus flavus">
  <data key="d0">Aspergillus flavus</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Aspergillus flavus is a fungal species isolated from the gut of greater wax moth larvae, potentially contributing to PE degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus that contributes to PE degradation in the wax moth Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus isolated from the guts of wax moths, studied for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a lignocellulose-degrading fungus isolated from wax moth, which upregulates laccase-like multicopper oxidases under plastic stress.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspergillus flavus is a fungus isolated from the gut of the greater wax moth, involved in polyethylene (PE) degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382160</data>
</node>
<node id="Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2">
  <data key="d0">Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain identified within the genus *Exiguobacterium* and is notable for its capacity to degrade polystyrene (PS), a widely used plastic that is otherwise resistant to natural decomposition. The strain was originally isolated from the digestive tract of yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) in Beijing, where it was found to play a role in the insects’ ability to consume and metabolize polystyrene. Research led by Yang et al. first reported its isolation from mealworm intestines and highlighted its potential in plastic biodegradation studies.  

Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2, deposited under the accession number CGMCCC 10521, has been shown to utilize polystyrene as its sole carbon source. Laboratory experiments demonstrated that the bacterium can degrade polystyrene in both solid and liquid media, with measurable effects such as a 7.4% reduction in polystyrene weight over a 60-day period. During this process, the strain converts ingested polystyrene into carbon dioxide (CO₂) and microbial biomass, indicating its ability to metabolize the polymer rather than merely fragment it.  

The discovery of Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is significant because it provides a biological pathway for addressing plastic pollution, particularly the environmental persistence of polystyrene. Its association with mealworm digestion suggests a symbiotic role in enabling the insects to subsist on plastic-based diets, while its independent cultivation and degradation activity in controlled conditions demonstrate its potential for biotechnological applications. As such, Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 represents an important microbial resource for advancing research into sustainable plastic waste management and biodegradation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial strain isolated from mealworm guts that can degrade polystyrene by forming biofilms and altering its surface.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a plastic-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of the mealworm Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterium isolated from yellow mealworm, involved in polystyrene (PS) degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain living in the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, enabling the insect to biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 is a bacterial strain isolated from mealworm gut that can degrade polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388193</data>
</node>
<node id="Pseudomonas sp.">
  <data key="d0">Pseudomonas sp.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Pseudomonas sp. is a bacterial species belonging to the family *Pseudomonadaceae* that has been isolated from the gut microbiome of superworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae). This bacterium has attracted scientific interest due to its potential role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene (PS). Several studies have demonstrated that strains of *Pseudomonas sp.* obtained from superworms are capable of contributing to the breakdown of polystyrene, a widely used but environmentally persistent plastic.  

Research on *Pseudomonas sp.* has focused on its enzymatic pathways, especially those involved in the transformation of styrene, a monomer of polystyrene, into phenylacetate, which can then enter central metabolic routes. This metabolic capability highlights the genus *Pseudomonas* as a candidate for biotechnological applications in plastic waste management, given its previously documented styrene-degrading abilities. Beyond polystyrene, *Pseudomonas sp.* has also been studied for its potential in degrading other synthetic polymers, such as flexible polyurethane foams derived from castor oil, further underscoring its versatility in polymer biodegradation.  

Overall, *Pseudomonas sp.* represents an important microbial resource for advancing sustainable strategies in plastic waste reduction. Its isolation from the gut of superworms provides insights into insect–microbe interactions that facilitate plastic digestion, while its enzymatic machinery offers promising avenues for the development of bio-based solutions to address plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. is a bacterial species reported to degrade PE wax (Mn of 1.7 kDa) in sterilized compost conditions after 80 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. is a bacterial species from the Gammaproteobacteria class identified as overrepresented in PS samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacterial species isolated from the gut of superworms (larvae of Zophobas aratus), studied for biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas sp. is a bacterial species reported in previous studies as being capable of degrading polystyrene from the intestines of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;An unclassified Pseudomonas sp. was able to degrade high-impact polystyrene, though with limited efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386794</data>
</node>
<node id="Acinetobacter">
  <data key="d0">Acinetobacter</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Acinetobacter is a bacterial genus that has been widely studied for its role in plastic biodegradation and its association with insect gut microbiomes. Members of this genus have been isolated from the red flour beetle (*Tribolium castaneum*) and its larvae, where they have demonstrated the ability to degrade polystyrene (PS). Research has also identified Acinetobacter as a likely contributor to the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE), as reported by Cassone et al. (2020). In addition, Acinetobacter has been detected in the gut microbiomes of insects fed on various polyolefins, including polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP). In these contexts, it has been shown to play a significant role in gut microbiome assembly and adaptation to plastic-based diets. Specifically, Acinetobacter has been found to be dominant in PP-fed groups, accounting for approximately 9.65% of the microbial community, and is similarly enriched in other polyolefin-fed groups. Collectively, these findings highlight Acinetobacter as a key microbial genus involved in the degradation of synthetic polymers and in shaping the gut microbiota of insects exposed to plastic substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Acinetobacter is a bacterial genus found in the intestine of Aedes albopictus.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterium isolated from insect larvae and studied for polystyrene degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386822</data>
</node>
<node id="Tenebrio molitor (T. molitor)">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor (T. molitor)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the yellow mealworm, is the larval stage of a darkling beetle species that has gained significant scientific attention for its unique ability to biodegrade synthetic polymers. Research has demonstrated that T. molitor larvae can survive on and metabolize a wide range of plastics, including polyurethane (PU) foam, polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene (PE), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). This capacity is closely linked to the activity of its gut microbiota, which plays a critical role in the depolymerization and degradation processes.  

Studies on Tenebrio molitor have explored the influence of various environmental and dietary factors on its plastic-degrading efficiency. Variables such as diet composition, temperature, humidity, and rearing density have been shown to affect the rate and extent of biodegradation. Comparative experiments have also been conducted to assess gut cell health and cytotoxicity when larvae are fed plastics such as styrofoam versus conventional diets, providing insights into the physiological impacts of plastic ingestion.  

The gut microbial community of T. molitor has been a focal point of investigation, as it is believed to harbor key organisms and enzymes responsible for breaking down recalcitrant polymers. Analyses of these microbial populations under different feeding conditions, including plastic-based diets, have revealed shifts in community structure that correlate with biodegradation activity.  

Overall, Tenebrio molitor represents a promising biological model for studying plastic biodegradation and the role of insect–microbe interactions in environmental remediation. Its demonstrated ability to metabolize multiple types of plastics highlights its potential application in sustainable waste management strategies and the development of biotechnological solutions to plastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386527</data>
</node>
<node id="Galleria mellonella (G. mellonella)">
  <data key="d0">Galleria mellonella (G. mellonella)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Galleria mellonella larvae, also known as wax moth larvae, show preferences for pristine plastics and can degrade them with the help of gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae species used for gut microbial community analysis, also known as greater wax moths.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384507</data>
</node>
<node id="Polystyrene (PS)">
  <data key="d0">Polystyrene (PS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum through the polymerization of styrene monomers. It is one of the six most widely used plastics worldwide, accounting for approximately 6–7% of global plastic production, with an annual output of around 33 million tons. Structurally, polystyrene consists of a hydrocarbon backbone with attached phenyl groups, which impart high chemical stability, durability, and resistance to natural degradation. These characteristics make it valuable across industries but also contribute to its persistence in the environment.  

Polystyrene is manufactured in multiple forms, including rigid plastic, expanded polystyrene (EPS), extruded polystyrene (XPS), high-density polystyrene, and foam products, commonly marketed under the trade name Styrofoam. Its physical properties include low density, thermal insulation capacity, radiation resistance, and suitability for pyrolysis, which can yield high oil outputs. These features underpin its versatility and widespread use.  

Applications of polystyrene span numerous sectors. In packaging and food service, it is used for disposable cups, trays, containers, protective packaging, and takeout boxes. In construction, it serves as an effective insulation material. In the automotive and medical industries, it is employed in lightweight components, laboratory equipment, and specialized devices. Additional uses include household appliances, fisheries equipment, and scientific research, where it is studied in forms such as films, emulsions, powders, and foams. Polystyrene beads are also used in nanopolystyrene synthesis.  

Despite its economic importance, polystyrene presents significant environmental challenges. It is non-hydrolysable, exhibits an extremely slow biodegradation rate, and is recycled at very low levels. Consequently, it accumulates in landfills, soils, waterways, and oceans, where it is a major contributor to plastic debris and microplastic pollution. Its persistence has raised concerns about long-term ecological and human health impacts, making it a central focus of sustainability and waste management research.  

Recent studies have investigated biological pathways for polystyrene degradation. Certain insect larvae, including yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas morio), Alphitobius diaperinus, and black soldier fly larvae (Zophobas atratus), have demonstrated the ability to ingest and partially degrade polystyrene foam and brick forms. Mealworms have been observed chewing through polystyrene, leaving visible holes, while superworms exhibit even higher consumption rates. Analytical methods such as attenuated total reflectance Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) confirm chemical modifications, including reductions in molecular weight, indicating polymer breakdown. Gut microbiota play a key role in these processes, with bacterial strains such as Hafnia-Obsumbacter sp. in Z. atratus and Acinetobacter sp. in mealworms implicated in metabolizing polystyrene into simpler compounds.  

In addition to insect-mediated degradation, several microbial strains—including Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2, Pseudomonas sp., Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Priestia megaterium S1, and Achromobacter species—have been shown to degrade polystyrene independently. These microbes often form biofilms on polystyrene surfaces, leading to structural deterioration and measurable weight loss. Techniques such as FT-IR spectroscopy are widely used to monitor molecular and structural changes during microbial degradation.  

In summary, polystyrene (PS) is a petroleum-based polymer valued for its durability, versatility, and insulating properties, with extensive applications across packaging, construction, medical, and consumer industries. However, its resistance to degradation makes it a major contributor to plastic waste and microplastic pollution. Ongoing research into insect- and microbe-mediated degradation highlights potential biological solutions for mitigating its environmental impact, positioning polystyrene as a critical focus in global efforts to address plastic pollution and develop sustainable waste management and bioremediation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a plastic material widely used in packaging and insulation, resistant to degradation, and the subject of biodegradation studies with mealworms.</data>
