<node id="Polyethylene (PE)">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene (PE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene (PE) is a petroleum-derived synthetic polymer and the most widely produced and consumed plastic globally, accounting for a significant share of total polymer production and demand. Classified as a thermoplastic, polyethylene consists of a simple carbon–carbon backbone without aromatic rings, a structure that imparts high chemical stability, resistance to hydrolysis, durability, and versatility. These properties make it lightweight, strong, waterproof, corrosion-resistant, and suitable for a wide range of applications. Commercially, polyethylene is manufactured in several grades, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), medium-density polyethylene (MDPE), and high-density polyethylene (HDPE). Differences in molecular branching and density across these grades determine their flexibility, strength, and mechanical performance. Because of its low cost and favorable physical properties, polyethylene is extensively used in packaging films, plastic bags, bottles, agricultural mulching films, construction materials, protective foams, toys, consumer goods, and specialized products such as flame-retardant materials. Its global production volumes surpass those of other common plastics such as polystyrene.  

Despite its economic importance, polyethylene presents major environmental challenges. Its chemically stable structure and absence of hydrolyzable functional groups make it highly resistant to microbial or enzymatic degradation. As a result, polyethylene persists in terrestrial and marine ecosystems, contributing significantly to plastic pollution and microplastic accumulation. This persistence has made it a central focus of environmental research, particularly in the context of biodegradation and sustainable waste management.  

Scientific studies have investigated biological pathways for polyethylene degradation, with particular attention to insect-mediated processes. Mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), dark mealworms, superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), wax moths (*Galleria mellonella*), and flour beetles (*Tribolium confusum*) have demonstrated the ability to ingest and partially degrade polyethylene foams and films. Experimental results have shown processes such as oxidation, depolymerization, and reductions in molecular weight and crystallinity. For instance, mealworm studies have reported up to 49% conversion of polyethylene into CO₂, alongside measurable decreases in polymer molecular weight. Lesser waxworms have been observed chewing holes in polyethylene films, leading to material weight loss. However, degradation is typically incomplete, leaving residual plastic particles. Ingestion can also cause physiological stress in larvae, including oxidative stress, inhibited growth, and metabolic imbalances, with dose-dependent effects reducing efficiency and increasing toxicity.  

In addition to insect-based pathways, microbial degradation of polyethylene has been explored. Certain bacterial strains, such as *Klebsiella pneumoniae* Mk-1 and *Priestia megaterium* S1 isolated from waxworm guts, have shown limited capacity to degrade polyethylene. Genes such as *alkB* in *Pseudomonas* species have also been implicated in hydrocarbon and polyethylene breakdown. While these microbial processes suggest potential for biotechnological applications, current methods remain inefficient and are not yet scalable for industrial waste management.  

In summary, polyethylene (PE) is a highly versatile and economically vital thermoplastic that underpins modern industrial and consumer applications. However, its persistence in the environment and resistance to natural degradation make it one of the most problematic plastic pollutants. Ongoing research into insect- and microbe-mediated degradation provides promising but still preliminary insights, highlighting the urgent need for innovative and sustainable strategies to address polyethylene waste and its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a petroleum-based polymer and one of the microplastics studied for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE), including LLDPE, LDPE, and HDPE, is a plastic type that mealworms can ingest and biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a type of microplastic fed to mealworms, which induced oxidative stress and elevated ROS, SOD, and MDA levels.&lt;SEP&gt;PE is one of the six major petroleum-derived polymers with high demand.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is a plastic substrate tested for degradation by mealworm larvae.</data>
