<node id="Low-density polyethylene (LDPE)">
  <data key="d0">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used type of polyethylene characterized by its low density and flexible structure, commonly produced through molding processes. It is frequently employed in the manufacture of everyday plastic products such as cling films, fruit bags, and other packaging materials. Due to its extensive use and persistence in the environment, LDPE has become a focus of scientific research on plastic biodegradation and microplastic pollution.  

Studies have investigated the biodegradation of LDPE in both microbial and invertebrate systems. In microbial contexts, LDPE has been tested as the sole carbon source in incubation experiments to evaluate its potential for biological breakdown. Several microorganisms, including *Priestia megaterium* S1, *Aspergillus clavatus* strain JASK1, and various marine bacteria, have been studied for their ability to degrade LDPE. These investigations highlight the role of microbial metabolism in reducing the environmental persistence of this plastic.  

In addition to microbial degradation, LDPE has been examined in relation to insect-mediated breakdown. Research has shown that larvae of *Zophobas atratus* (superworms) and *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) are capable of ingesting and partially depolymerizing LDPE. The degradation efficiency appears to be influenced by the molecular weight of the polymer, with lower molecular weight LDPE being more readily broken down. These findings suggest that insect gut microbiota and digestive processes may contribute to the transformation of LDPE into smaller, more manageable compounds.  

LDPE has also been studied as one of the microplastics tested for its metabolic effects on mealworms, providing insights into both the ecological risks of plastic ingestion and the potential for biological systems to mitigate plastic waste. Collectively, these studies position low-density polyethylene as a critical subject in biodegradation research, bridging material science, microbiology, and environmental biology in efforts to address plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a plastic associated with biodegradation by Acinetobacter sp. in T. molitor and G. mellonella larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a type of polyethylene plastic whose biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae is influenced by molecular weight.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic studied for biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae and in dark- versus yellow-mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic tested in the study, commonly used in commercial bags.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic studied for biodegradation by Pseudomonas species.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a form of polyethylene studied for biodegradation by indigenous bacteria and fungi.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386266</data>
</node>
<node id="Polypropylene (PP)">
  <data key="d0">Polypropylene (PP)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polypropylene (PP) is a synthetic thermoplastic polymer derived from petroleum and classified as a fossil-based polyolefin plastic. It is one of the six most common plastic polymers globally, accounting for approximately 18.9–19.7% of total plastic production. Its widespread use is attributed to its durability, versatility, and resistance to chemical, physical, and biological stresses. These properties make polypropylene integral to diverse applications, including packaging materials, consumer goods, automotive components, textiles, medical devices, and industrial products. Notably, it is the primary material used in disposable medical masks, forming all three layers of their structure.  

From a chemical standpoint, polypropylene is characterized by a carbon–carbon backbone and an iso-paraffin structure, which renders it a non-hydrolysable polymer. This molecular configuration provides high resistance to hydrolysis and microbial attack, contributing to its environmental persistence. Its high molecular weight further enhances its stability, making it a significant contributor to plastic waste accumulation and microplastic pollution. While its structure shares some similarities with natural polymers such as lignin, polypropylene remains largely resistant to natural biodegradation pathways.  

Despite this resistance, research has shown that polypropylene is not entirely impervious to biological breakdown. Laboratory studies have demonstrated that larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and *Zophobas atratus* can ingest and partially degrade polypropylene in forms such as foam and commercial bags. Mealworms have been observed chewing polypropylene substrates and retaining mass when fed on them, though at lower efficiency compared to other plastics like polystyrene. The degradation process appears to involve the larvae’s gut microbiota, with bacterial strains such as *Klyuyera* sp., *Stenotrophomonas*, *Bacillus*, and *Rhodococcus* implicated in facilitating breakdown. However, the precise mechanisms remain under investigation, and evidence suggests that degradation results from a complex interaction between the larvae and their associated microbes rather than microbial activity alone.  

The extent of biodegradation varies depending on the molecular weight and grade of polypropylene, with some forms more susceptible to breakdown than others. While current findings demonstrate only limited degradation under experimental conditions, they highlight the potential for biological and microbial strategies to contribute to sustainable waste management solutions.  

In summary, polypropylene (PP) is a petroleum-based, non-hydrolysable thermoplastic polymer that plays a critical role in modern industry due to its strength, versatility, and recyclability. However, its resistance to natural degradation processes makes it a major environmental concern as a persistent source of plastic waste and microplastic pollution. Ongoing research into its partial biodegradation by insect larvae and associated microbes underscores both the challenges and opportunities in developing effective strategies for mitigating polypropylene’s environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;A plastic polymer mentioned as one of the major global plastic products.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene microplastics were found in animal-based traditional medicinal materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a petroleum-based plastic that yellow mealworms can ingest and biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a subject for future biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a target for future follow-up research on plastic biodegradation and oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;PP is one of the six major petroleum-derived polymers with high demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
