</node>
<node id="Polylactic acid (PLA)">
  <data key="d0">Polylactic acid (PLA)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polylactic acid (PLA) is a bio-based, biodegradable polymer that has attracted significant attention for its potential role in sustainable materials and waste management. Derived from renewable resources such as corn starch or sugarcane, PLA is considered an environmentally friendly alternative to conventional petroleum-based plastics. However, its biodegradation typically requires specific conditions, such as industrial composting environments, which has limited its broader application in natural settings.  

Recent studies have explored the biodegradation of polylactic acid by biological agents, particularly insects. Research has shown that yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) are capable of consuming and metabolizing PLA, making it one of the plastics tested in insect metabolism studies. Among the plastics examined, PLA demonstrated the highest biodegradation rate and the lowest toxicity to T. molitor larvae, highlighting its relative compatibility with insect-based degradation processes. This finding suggests that PLA may be more amenable to biological recycling pathways compared to other synthetic polymers.  

The ability of mealworms to consume and degrade PLA has prompted interest in circular waste management approaches. In such systems, insect larvae could be used to process PLA waste, simultaneously producing insect biomass that may serve as a resource for other applications, such as animal feed or bio-based products. This dual benefit positions PLA as a promising candidate for integration into innovative recycling and bioconversion strategies.  

Overall, polylactic acid (PLA) is a renewable, biodegradable plastic that, while requiring controlled conditions for conventional degradation, has shown strong potential for biological breakdown through insect-mediated processes. Its favorable biodegradation profile with Tenebrio molitor larvae underscores its relevance for future studies on sustainable plastic management and circular economy solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;A biodegradable plastic studied for degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polylactic acid (PLA) is a degradable plastic mentioned as a target for future follow-up research on plastic biodegradation and oxidative stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
