<node id="Mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworms, the larval stage of the darkling beetle *Tenebrio molitor* Linnaeus, are insect larvae of notable ecological, nutritional, and scientific significance. They undergo complete metamorphosis through egg, larva, pupa, and adult stages and are characterized by their adaptability, resilience, and high feeding capacity. Historically regarded as agricultural pests, mealworms are now increasingly recognized for their potential contributions to sustainability, food security, waste management, and environmental innovation.  

Ecologically, mealworms function as decomposers, consuming a wide range of organic matter. A particularly important discovery is their ability to ingest and biodegrade synthetic plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polyurethanes (PU). Through mechanical fragmentation and biochemical transformation, they convert plastics into partially degraded residues excreted as frass. Experimental studies have demonstrated that mealworms can survive on plastic-only diets, though survival, growth, and pupation rates vary depending on polymer type and the presence of supplemental feed such as wheat bran or soy protein. Feeding trials have shown measurable weight loss, averaging around 10% over 15 days on plastic diets. Importantly, their plastic-degrading ability persists across multiple generations, with larvae completing their life cycle while maintaining degradation capacity.  

The biodegradation of plastics in mealworms is closely linked to their gut microbiome. Symbiotic bacteria, particularly from the Enterobacteriaceae family, play a central role in breaking down resistant polymers. Strains such as *Exiguobacterium* sp. YT2 and various Enterobacter spp. have been isolated from their gut and frass and shown to facilitate polystyrene degradation. Plastic diets alter microbial composition, enriching strains that enhance degradation efficiency. Antibiotic treatments and dietary variations further confirm microbial involvement. In addition to microbial activity, oxidative digestive mechanisms contribute to polymer breakdown. Beyond plastics, mealworm gut microbiota have also been studied for nitrogen fixation, highlighting their broader ecological versatility.  

Nutritionally, mealworms are rich in protein, fats, and essential micronutrients, making them valuable as a sustainable food and feed source. Their biochemical composition is diet-dependent; for example, polystyrene diets reduce lipid and carbohydrate levels while maintaining protein content. They are already used as feed for fish, poultry, and reptiles, and are increasingly considered for human consumption within sustainable food systems and circular bioeconomy frameworks. Their lipid content has also been investigated for biodiesel production, underscoring their potential role in renewable energy research.  

In scientific research, mealworms serve as model organisms for studying plastic degradation, metabolic pathways, and survival under varied dietary and environmental conditions. Controlled experiments with plastics such as PS, PE, and PVC have measured ingestion rates, physiological responses, oxidative stress, and antioxidant performance. These studies demonstrate depolymerization, chemical modifications such as oxygen incorporation, and partial mineralization of plastics into biomass, carbon dioxide, and fecula. Mealworms have also been studied for their ability to degrade lignocellulose, providing insights into broader biodegradation processes.  

Overall, mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) represent a unique convergence of ecological function, nutritional value, and environmental innovation. Their dual role as nutrient-rich biomass producers and as biological agents capable of microbiome-supported plastic and nitrogen biodegradation underscores their significance in addressing global challenges. Positioned at the intersection of sustainable agriculture, renewable energy, and plastic waste management, mealworms are increasingly recognized as promising contributors to future strategies for food security and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are described as plastic-eating organisms involved in biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are the larvae of T. molitor, studied in this context for survival, growth, and reproduction when fed with PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are insects studied for their potential to biodegrade polystyrene, though their ability to complete life cycles on PS is doubtful.</data>
