<node id="Superworms">
  <data key="d0">Superworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Superworms, the larval stage of darkling beetles such as *Zophobas atratus* and *Zophobas morio*, are increasingly recognized for their unique biological and ecological roles, particularly in the context of plastic degradation and agricultural applications. These larvae have been extensively studied for their capacity to ingest and partially biodegrade synthetic plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyurethane (PU), materials that are otherwise highly resistant to natural decomposition. Experimental research has shown that superworms can survive on diets composed solely of plastics such as Styrofoam, with measurable effects on survival rates, pupation, body weight, and consumption levels. Compared to other insect larvae, they demonstrate relatively high cumulative consumption of plastics, making them a promising model organism for investigating biological approaches to plastic waste management.  

The ability of superworms to degrade plastics is closely linked to their gut microbiome. Studies have identified bacterial strains such as *Aeromonas sp.*, *Klebsiella pneumoniae*, *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, and members of the Enterobacteriaceae family as contributors to the breakdown of polystyrene. These microbial communities undergo significant shifts when the larvae are fed plastic-based diets, with both bacterial and fungal populations adapting to facilitate degradation. The metabolic processes involved can convert polystyrene into low molecular weight products and carbon dioxide, underscoring the potential of superworms and their symbiotic microbes as a biological system for advancing bioremediation strategies.  

Diet composition has been shown to influence both larval health and degradation efficiency. Experiments supplementing plastics with bran have revealed that mixed diets can affect survival, growth, and microbial activity, highlighting the importance of nutritional balance in optimizing plastic biodegradation. Additionally, the frass (excrement) produced by superworms has been investigated as a potential growth medium for plants, such as cacti, suggesting an extended ecological and agricultural relevance beyond waste reduction.  

Beyond their role in biodegradation research, superworms are widely used as feed for fish, poultry, and reptiles due to their relatively large size, heavy body mass, and favorable nutritional content. Importantly, studies indicate that their nutritional profile remains relatively stable even when they are raised on plastic-based diets, in contrast to other larvae such as mealworms. Their ability to be bred across multiple generations further enhances their practicality as a resource for both scientific research and agricultural industries.  

In summary, superworms (*Zophobas atratus* and *Zophobas morio* larvae) are notable for their dual significance as both a biological model for plastic degradation and a valuable resource in animal feed and agriculture. Their demonstrated ability to ingest and partially degrade persistent plastics, supported by the degradative potential of their gut microbiome, positions them as an important organism in the search for sustainable solutions to plastic pollution. At the same time, their role in feed production and potential contributions to plant growth highlight their broader ecological and economic importance.&lt;SEP&gt;Another type of worm referenced in a previous study (Yang et al., 2020a) on depolymerization of PP plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms are insects belonging to the tenebrionid group, known to degrade polystyrene at a rate of up to 0.58 mg/d.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, larvae of Zophobas atratus, are studied for their ability to biodegrade polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, the larvae of Zophobas morio, are also known to feed on expanded polystyrene, though less efficiently than mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
