</node>
<node id="Wax moth">
  <data key="d0">Wax moth</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wax moth larvae, also known as Galleria mellonella, are capable of consuming plastics and harbor microbes that aid in degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370600</data>
</node>
<node id="Snails">
  <data key="d0">Snails</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Snails, specifically Achatina fulica, have been identified as plastic-consuming organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Snails are species in which an increase in Enterobacteriaceae was observed when exposed to PS or PE feeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Invertebrates mentioned as organisms in which digestive biofragmentation of plastics into micro- and nano-plastics has been observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Snails are soil invertebrates tested for their ability to degrade polystyrene, but no biodegradation was observed.&lt;SEP&gt;Snails were reported to ingest and contain microplastics in studies cited in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385250</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut microbiota of Tenebrio larvae">
  <data key="d0">Gut microbiota of Tenebrio larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The gut microbiota of Tenebrio larvae assists in the mineralization and degradation of plastics such as polystyrene and polypropylene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370601</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworm feces">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm feces</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm feces are a byproduct of plastic consumption by mealworms and can be managed as fertilizer in circular waste management approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370601</data>
</node>
<node id="Agricultural products">
  <data key="d0">Agricultural products</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Agricultural products are proposed as feedstock for PLA production in circular waste management approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370602</data>
</node>
<node id="Waste rigid polyurethane (WRPU)">
  <data key="d0">Waste rigid polyurethane (WRPU)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">WRPU is a type of waste plastic tested with Galleria mellonella larvae, which showed a preference for pristine plastics instead.&lt;SEP&gt;WRPU is a type of waste plastic tested in larval feeding preference studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370602</data>
</node>
