<node id="Yang et al.">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang et al. are a group of researchers widely recognized for their pioneering contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation by insects and their associated microbiota, with their most influential work conducted between 2014 and 2021. Their research established a scientific foundation for understanding how biological systems, particularly insect larvae, can contribute to the breakdown of synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE).  

A central focus of Yang et al.’s investigations was the role of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus larvae) in consuming and degrading plastics. Through a series of landmark studies, they demonstrated that mealworms are capable of ingesting PS and other polymers, fragmenting the material, and facilitating partial mineralization and biodegradation within their digestive systems. Their experiments provided direct evidence that PS degradation occurs in vivo, as indicated by altered polymer structures, reduced molecular weights, and the incorporation of degraded carbon into insect lipids. They also showed that PS consumption could contribute to the energy needs of mealworms through CO₂ mineralization, while simultaneously examining the biological effects of plastic diets on insect development, including pupation success.  

One of their most significant contributions was the isolation and characterization of Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 from the gut microbiota of yellow mealworms. This bacterial strain was identified as a key agent in PS biodegradation, offering one of the earliest microbiological explanations for insect-mediated plastic metabolism. Their findings highlighted the critical role of gut symbionts in facilitating polymer breakdown and advanced broader understanding of microbial pathways involved in plastic degradation.  

Beyond polystyrene, Yang et al. expanded their research to polyethylene degradation, investigating both low-density polyethylene (LDPE) microplastics and high-density polyethylene (HDPE). They demonstrated that lower molecular weight PE underwent greater depolymerization, underscoring the influence of polymer characteristics on biodegradation efficiency. Their studies also extended to other insect species, such as Plodia interpunctella (the Indianmeal moth), thereby broadening the scope of insect-mediated plastic degradation research.  

To characterize chemical and structural changes in plastics after insect digestion, Yang et al. employed advanced analytical techniques including Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), gel permeation chromatography (GPC), and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). They also investigated the fate of plastic residues in mealworm frass, providing critical insights into the persistence of microplastic fragments and the environmental implications of insect-mediated degradation. Their findings on frass characteristics have been widely cited in subsequent studies.  

The methodologies developed by Yang et al. have been adopted and adapted by numerous researchers, particularly in preparing PS films for microbial degradation experiments. Collectively, their systematic investigations into insect-plastic interactions, microbial involvement, and degradation mechanisms have positioned Yang et al. as foundational contributors to the field. Their work has significantly shaped scientific understanding of how insects and their gut microbiota can contribute to mitigating global plastic pollution challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are cited in 2020 studies on Zophobas atratus larvae and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are researchers cited in multiple studies (2015b, 2018a, 2019a, 2019b, 2021) related to gut microbiota and plastic degradation in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are researchers who studied plastic biodegradation by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are cited researchers who studied plastic biodegradation processes in insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are researchers who studied the biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae and their gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. are authors of previous studies (2015a, 2018a) referenced in the text for comparative purposes.</data>
