<node id="PE">
  <data key="d0">PE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene (PE) is a petroleum-derived synthetic polymer and one of the most widely produced and utilized plastics worldwide. It exists in several structural forms, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), which differ in their branching and molecular configurations. PE is valued for its versatility and durability, but these same properties contribute to its high chemical stability and environmental persistence, making it a major contributor to global plastic pollution. Its resistance to natural degradation processes has positioned PE as a central focus of research into plastic biodegradation and depolymerization.  

Studies have explored the potential for biological systems to degrade PE, with particular emphasis on insect larvae such as Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworm). Experimental trials have shown that mealworms can ingest and partially degrade PE, both in vivo and in vitro. Degradation efficiency is generally higher in living organisms than in controlled laboratory settings, but PE provides little nutritional value due to its carbon-rich and nitrogen-deficient composition. Feeding experiments have reported mass reductions of less than 70%, with degradation occurring over timescales ranging from weeks to years. Importantly, frass (excrement) from larvae did not contain nanoplastics, suggesting that PE undergoes partial breakdown rather than complete mineralization.  

Morphological and chemical analyses of PE before and after larval digestion reveal significant structural changes. Smooth surfaces with fine pores and flaky layers develop wrinkles, cracks, and bubble-like protrusions after passage through the insect gut, indicating both physical and chemical modification. Trace elemental components such as aluminum, calcium, and minor amounts of hafnium have been detected in PE samples, which may influence degradation dynamics.  

The role of microbial communities in PE degradation has also been highlighted. Symbiotic microorganisms in the gut microbiome of T. molitor, including isolates such as PM S1, appear to assist in polymer breakdown, suggesting a cooperative mechanism between insect digestion and microbial activity. Beyond insect-associated systems, environmental bacterial isolates have also been studied for their ability to depolymerize PE, reflecting broader interest in microbial pathways for addressing plastic waste.  

Comparative studies of PE alongside other plastics, such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polystyrene (PS), consistently demonstrate that PE is more resistant to biodegradation. While partial biodegradation is possible under specific biological or environmental conditions, PE remains highly durable, with degradation half-lives extending from weeks to years depending on the context. In many cases, photo-oxidation or thermo-oxidation is required before microbial activity can effectively act on the polymer.  

In summary, polyethylene (PE) is a chemically diverse and environmentally persistent plastic polymer that poses significant ecological challenges due to its resistance to degradation. Research involving insect larvae, microbial isolates, and comparative analyses has shown that PE can undergo partial biodegradation, particularly in association with mealworms and their gut microbiota. However, its limited nutritional value, slow degradation rates, and structural resilience underscore its persistence in natural environments, while also pointing to the potential—but still limited—role of biological systems in mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;PE (polyethylene) is mentioned as a compound fed to mealworms, influencing gut microbiome composition.&lt;SEP&gt;PE (polyethylene) is a plastic polymer whose biodegradation involves depolymerization and mineralization into CO2 and H2O.&lt;SEP&gt;PE refers to polyethylene, a type of polymer studied for biodegradation and chemical modification in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene (PE) is a type of plastic polymer studied in the experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PE (polyethylene) is a type of plastic tested in the study for biodegradation by mealworms, with residual microplastic particles measured in frass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
