<node id="PP">
  <data key="d0">PP</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polypropylene (PP) is a synthetic plastic polymer that has been extensively studied in the context of biodegradation, particularly through experiments involving *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) larvae and associated microbial communities. As a highly crystalline and non-hydrolyzable material, PP exhibits exceptional resistance to biological breakdown, making it one of the most difficult plastics to degrade in experimental trials. When provided as a diet to *T. molitor* larvae, PP consistently showed very low levels of consumption compared to other plastics, with cumulative intake remaining minimal. In its rigid form, PP was not consumable by the larvae, and even when ingested in small amounts, it provided negligible nutritional value, failing to support larval growth, survival, or weight gain across 10-, 20-, and 30-day feeding intervals.  

In biodegradation studies, PP is often used as a benchmark material to highlight the challenges of breaking down crystalline polymers. While some limited mass reduction has been reported in certain trials—generally less than 70%—the overall degradation efficiency remains poor, reinforcing its classification as an unfavorable substrate for insect-mediated plastic processing. The depolymerization processes of PP are either severely inhibited or nearly halted under biological conditions, underscoring its persistence in natural and experimental environments.  

Research has also explored the microbial dimension of PP degradation. Specific bacterial strains, such as PELW2042, have been identified as potential candidates for further investigation, and associations with microbial taxa including *Lactococcus* and *Morganella* have been noted in insect gut studies. These findings suggest that while PP is highly resistant, certain microbial communities may play a role in its partial breakdown, though the efficiency and mechanisms remain poorly understood.  

Beyond degradation efficiency, PP has been suggested for future testing to evaluate its physiological impacts on organisms exposed to it, reflecting broader concerns about its environmental persistence and biological interactions. Its inclusion in experimental designs serves both as a comparative control to assess the relative degradability of other plastics and as a target for ongoing research aimed at developing strategies to overcome its resistance to biodegradation.  

In summary, Polypropylene (PP) is a durable, crystalline plastic polymer that remains highly resistant to insect- and microbe-mediated degradation. It provides little to no nutritional benefit to mealworms, shows minimal breakdown in controlled studies, and continues to represent a significant challenge for sustainable plastic waste management. Its role in research is both as a resistant benchmark material and as a focus for future efforts to identify biological or microbial pathways capable of addressing its environmental persistence.&lt;SEP&gt;PP (polypropylene) is a material composed only of hydrogen and carbon, lacking essential nutrients, and was tested as a diet for larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP (polypropylene) is the main plastic studied for biodegradation by insect larvae and their gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;PP refers to polypropylene, a type of plastic material that undergoes depolymerization and biodegradation, though with limited extent and lower consumption rates by certain beetle larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP (polypropylene) is a type of plastic reported to be degraded by Enterobacteriaceae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41</data>
