<node id="PS">
  <data key="d0">PS</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic, petroleum-derived plastic polymer that is widely used in consumer products but is highly resistant to natural decomposition, making it a major contributor to plastic pollution and a central focus of biodegradation research. As a hydrophobic and non-hydrolysable material, PS exhibits low degradability and a long environmental half-life compared to other plastics such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET). Its persistence, low recycling rate, and tendency to fragment into microplastics exacerbate its ecological impact.  

PS has been studied in multiple physical forms—including beads, foam, plates, and samples of varying molecular weights (PS-L, PS-M, PS-H)—to evaluate its chemical, structural, and biological transformations. Laboratory investigations have demonstrated that PS undergoes limited degradation through biological systems, particularly insect larvae and their associated microbial communities. *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) larvae were the first insects shown to degrade PS, consuming both foam and raw plates and inducing partial depolymerization, molecular weight reduction, and visible surface erosion. Microscopic analyses reveal that smooth PS surfaces become rough and irregular after digestion, indicating both physical and chemical alterations. Comparative studies across larvae from different regions, such as Tai’an, Shenzhen, and Guangzhou, suggest that PS degradation can also involve modifications in crystalline structure.  

Despite these transformations, PS provides poor nutritional value. Larvae fed exclusively on PS experience weight loss, reduced survival, inhibited growth, dehydration, and oxidative stress, although these effects are less severe than those caused by polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Feeding efficiency is influenced by larval density, and in some cases, PS depolymerization is inhibited or halted, including under antibiotic treatments such as gentamicin, which disrupt microbial symbionts. Other insect species, including *Galleria mellonella* (wax moth) larvae and *Zophobas atratus* (superworms), have also been reported to degrade PS, though at varying rates.  

Microbial symbionts play a critical role in PS degradation. Gut-associated bacteria in *T. molitor*, including unclassified Enterobacteriaceae, and external isolates such as strains PM S1 and PELW2042, have been implicated in PS breakdown. Fungal genera such as *Rhodotorula* and *Cryptococcus* have also been associated with PS degradation in mealworms. While in vitro microbial degradation is generally less efficient than in vivo larval degradation, microbial activity contributes to substrate weight loss, surface erosion, and molecular transformations. Metagenomic datasets, such as MG-RAST accession 4785884.3, have been generated to characterize microbial communities involved in PS degradation and to identify the genetic basis of these processes.  

The degradation pathway of PS involves oxidative reactions, aromatic ring cleavage, and conversion into long-chain fatty acids, as well as the production of aliphatic and aromatic alcohols. PS degradation has been studied under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, with some strains (e.g., LG3) showing two-stage anaerobic biodegradation involving oxidation and depolymerization. Despite these processes, PS remains highly persistent, with studies reporting relatively low mass loss (e.g., 1.48% in some experiments) and less than 70% reduction overall. Elemental analyses have detected bromine at relatively high concentrations, along with trace elements such as hafnium, which may influence its resistance to degradation and environmental impact. Thermal decomposition studies and NMR analyses of frass residues have further characterized its molecular transformations.  

In summary, polystyrene (PS) is a durable, non-hydrolysable plastic polymer that serves as a benchmark material in biodegradation studies. While insect larvae and their microbial symbionts can induce partial depolymerization and structural modification, PS remains an inadequate nutritional substrate and a persistent pollutant. These findings underscore the importance of advancing biological and microbial strategies to mitigate PS accumulation and address the broader challenges of plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;PS refers to polystyrene, a type of plastic material used as food for mealworms in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;PS is a feeding variant used in the study of enzyme activity in larvae and bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;PS is a food component fed to larvae, whose effects on survival, growth, and reproduction are being studied.&lt;SEP&gt;PS refers to larvae fed with Styrofoam chips (polystyrene), which are used for safe transport packaging.&lt;SEP&gt;PS refers to polystyrene, a plastic material tested as a food source for larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PS refers to a diet group where larvae were fed polystyrene, which was found to be insufficient to maintain life due to lack of essential nutrients.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e12