<node id="PVC">
  <data key="d0">PVC</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a synthetic, petroleum-derived plastic polymer widely used for its durability, hydrophobicity, and resistance to biodegradation. These same properties, however, make PVC one of the most environmentally persistent and problematic plastics, contributing significantly to global plastic pollution. Its chemical structure, characterized by chlorine-containing monomers, renders it non-hydrolyzable and highly resistant to natural breakdown processes.  

Research has extensively investigated the biodegradation potential of PVC, particularly through biological systems such as insects and microorganisms. One of the most studied models involves *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) larvae, which have been tested with PVC as a sole dietary source or in combination with other substrates like bran. While larvae are capable of ingesting PVC, the polymer provides little to no nutritional value and does not support larval growth effectively compared to other plastics, such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET). Experimental results consistently show that PVC undergoes very limited degradation in the larval gut, with depolymerization often severely inhibited or nearly halted. Reported mass reduction rates are low, typically less than 70%, and structural alterations to the polymer are minimal.  

The physiological effects of PVC ingestion on *T. molitor* larvae are severe. Breakdown of PVC in the digestive tract can lead to oxidative depolymerization, releasing chlorinated intermediates and hydrogen chloride. These byproducts induce high oxidative stress, toxicity, and oxidative damage, making PVC one of the most harmful plastics tested in insect-based biodegradation studies. Among various polymers, PVC consistently causes the most severe physiological stress and negative health outcomes in larvae. Some geographic populations of *T. molitor*, such as those from Tai’an and Shenzhen, have shown limited structural modification of PVC, while others, such as those from Guangzhou, exhibit little to no degradation, suggesting that environmental or biological factors may influence outcomes.  

Beyond insect models, microbial degradation of PVC has also been explored. Certain bacterial strains, such as PELW2042, have been investigated for their ability to attack PVC. However, microbial systems face similar challenges, as the polymer’s chemical stability and the toxic byproducts generated during breakdown severely limit degradation efficiency.  

Overall, PVC is recognized as a highly persistent plastic that poses significant challenges for biological degradation. While ingestion and partial modification of PVC are possible in both insect and microbial systems, its low degradability, high toxicity, and severe physiological impacts on degrading organisms underscore the difficulty of addressing PVC pollution through biological means alone. This makes PVC a critical focus in environmental research and waste management, highlighting the urgent need for alternative strategies to mitigate its ecological impact.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride) is a type of plastic mentioned in relation to biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC (polyvinyl chloride) is a type of plastic that, when fed to mealworms, caused the highest increase in oxidative stress and damage.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC (polyvinyl chloride) is a type of plastic reported to be degraded by Enterobacteriaceae.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC refers to polyvinyl chloride, a plastic material used as food for mealworms in the experiment. It was provided in medical-grade tubing form.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
