<node id="Frass">
  <data key="d0">Frass</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frass is the excrement or solid waste material produced by insect larvae, most notably species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), *Zophobas morio* (superworm), *Tenebrio obscurus*, the Greater Wax Moth (*Galleria mellonella*), and *Achroia grisea*. It is generated during the digestion of a wide range of substrates, including natural feed sources, agricultural residues, and synthetic polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and even components of surgical masks. As a biological byproduct, frass is composed of undigested feed particles, insect metabolic waste, body residues, microbial content, and, when plastics are consumed, residual or partially degraded polymer fragments. These fragments may include nanoplastics, oligomers, depolymerization intermediates, and oxidative transformation products, providing molecular-level evidence of insect-mediated plastic degradation.  

In scientific research, frass plays a central role in studying insect-driven biodegradation of plastics. Its chemical and structural composition varies depending on larval species and diet, making it a key indicator of degradation efficiency and mechanisms. For example, frass from *T. molitor* larvae fed on PS or EPS has been shown to contain reduced polymer residues and oxidative transformation products, while waxworm frass from PE diets contains extractable fractions of residual or chemically modified polymers. Quantitative studies have demonstrated that residual polymers in frass can account for 31.7% to 45.4% of ingested material, with reductions over time suggesting progressive degradation. Specific compounds, such as 1,3-di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene, have been detected in frass from PS-fed larvae, confirming chemical transformation. Analyses of frass have also revealed sub-micron and microplastic particles, undegraded high-molecular-weight polymers, and changes in polymer dispersity (PDI), all of which serve as measurable indicators of plastic breakdown.  

To characterize these processes, researchers employ advanced analytical techniques. Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is widely used to detect chemical modifications and altered polymer structures by comparing absorbance patterns between frass and untreated polymers. Additional methods include molecular weight distribution analysis, isotopic and mass balance studies, and assessments of lipophilic and polar fractions, proteins, nitrogen, cations, glycerol, and microbial activity. Frass collected after antibiotic treatments has been analyzed to evaluate the role of gut microbiota in polymer degradation, while studies of starvation-fed mealworms have shown that frass can also influence gut microbial diversity.  

Beyond its role in biodegradation studies, frass has ecological and agricultural significance. It is naturally rich in nitrogen, chitin, proteins, minerals, and bioactive compounds, making it a promising organic fertilizer and soil amendment. Research has shown that frass can enhance plant growth, improve soil health, and influence microbial communities, with successful applications in crops such as dragon fruit cacti. It also contributes to nutrient cycling and metal bioavailability in soils, supporting sustainable agriculture and circular bioeconomy strategies. Importantly, frass derived from larvae fed on EPS has been reported to be free of harmful residues, supporting its potential for safe agricultural use, though in some cases toxic substances may persist and pose ecological risks.  

In summary, frass is the fecal byproduct of insect larvae that serves as both a natural consequence of feeding and a critical material for scientific, environmental, and agricultural applications. It functions as a measurable indicator of insect-mediated plastic degradation, offering molecular evidence of polymer breakdown, while also providing nutrient-rich properties that support sustainable farming practices. By encompassing residual plastics, degradation intermediates, and biologically valuable compounds, frass occupies a unique position at the intersection of biodegradation research, environmental remediation, and bioresource utilization.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass refers to the waste material produced by mealworm larvae, considered in degradation efficiency calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Frass refers to the excreted residue from mealworms after consuming polystyrene.</data>
