<node id="SEM">
  <data key="d0">SEM</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is an analytical imaging technique widely employed to investigate surface morphology and structural changes in a variety of materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation and material characterization. SEM provides high-resolution visualization of surface features, enabling researchers to detect alterations such as pits, cracks, and other degradation patterns that occur during biological or chemical processes.  

In studies of polymer degradation, SEM has been used to characterize the surface morphological changes of polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foams, including those exposed to mealworms, thereby demonstrating evidence of biodegradation. Similarly, SEM has been applied to examine polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms, revealing distinct surface alterations that indicate microbial or enzymatic activity. The technique has also been utilized to compare morphological differences among original plastics, frass (insect excrement), and floating plastics, highlighting degradation features such as surface erosion and cracking.  

Beyond insect-mediated degradation, SEM has been employed to observe the surface topography of polyethylene (PE) mulching films following bacterial treatment, providing insights into microbial interactions with synthetic polymers. It has also been used to characterize nanopolystyrene powders, offering detailed information on particle morphology and size distribution.  

Overall, SEM serves as a critical tool in material science and biodegradation research, enabling precise visualization of surface changes that occur in polymers and other materials under biological or environmental influences. Its ability to capture detailed morphological characteristics makes it indispensable for understanding degradation mechanisms, assessing microbial activity, and characterizing both bulk and nanoscale materials.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) was used to measure the size and length of bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) was used to observe the surface morphology of PS films after microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) was used to observe erosion on the surface of degraded PS films.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM (scanning electron microscope) was used to observe micro- and submicroplastic particles.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d043948723ae25a365e297c98300be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d</data>
