  <data key="d0">USA</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States of America (USA) is a central hub of scientific, technological, industrial, and environmental activity, with a significant global presence across multiple domains. In science and technology, the USA is the country of origin for many leading companies and research instruments that play a pivotal role in advancing laboratory and industrial applications worldwide. Prominent organizations based in the USA include Thermo Scientific, Agilent Technologies Inc., Waters, Applied Biosystems (ABI), Illumina, TA Instruments, Mettler Toledo, Omega Inc., and Minitab Inc. These companies are recognized for producing advanced analytical and laboratory equipment such as scanning electron microscopes (SEM), thermal analysis instruments, elemental analyzers (e.g., Vario EL), sequencing kits, and high-throughput genetic sequencing platforms like the Illumina NovaSeq 6000 system. The USA is also associated with the GeneAmp 9700 system and the TGA5500 analyzer, further underscoring its role in the development and distribution of high-precision scientific tools.  

In addition to its technological contributions, the USA is home to Foam Factory, Science Signpost Publishing, and well-known commercial brands such as Milwaukee and Bob’s Red Mill, reflecting its broader industrial and commercial influence. The country is also the location of Western Michigan University, which contributes to research and academic advancement.  

From an environmental perspective, the USA is notable for both its challenges and its research-driven responses. It generates approximately 33 million tons of plastic waste annually, positioning it as a major contributor to global plastic pollution and a focal point in discussions on sustainability and waste management. At the same time, the USA has been a key site for pioneering biological and environmental research aimed at addressing these issues. Studies conducted in the country have investigated the potential of Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae to consume and degrade polystyrene (PS) foam, a common form of plastic waste. Research in the USA has examined mealworm consumption rates, degradation processes, and survival under optimal temperature conditions (25–28°C), highlighting the nation’s role in exploring innovative biological solutions to plastic pollution.  

Overall, the United States of America emerges as a global leader in scientific innovation, technological manufacturing, publishing, and commercial enterprise. While it faces significant environmental challenges, particularly in relation to plastic waste, it is also at the forefront of experimental research and technological development aimed at advancing sustainability and addressing pressing global issues.&lt;SEP&gt;USA is the country where Agilent Technologies, Inc. and Thermo Fisher Scientific are based.&lt;SEP&gt;USA is the country where companies such as Thermo Scientific, Applied Biosystems, Invitrogen, and Promega are based.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where Stanford University is located.&lt;SEP&gt;The USA is a country where Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae have been studied for plastic biodegradation and are commercially sold as food sources.&lt;SEP&gt;The USA is mentioned as generating around 33 million tons of plastic waste each year.</data>
