<node id="Yang">
  <data key="d0">Yang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang is a researcher whose work between 2001 and 2021 has made significant contributions to the study of polymer degradation, plastic biodegradation, and the analytical methods used to investigate these processes. Yang’s early research, beginning with a 2001 publication, focused on the fundamental mechanisms of polymer degradation, providing a foundation for later studies on microbial and biological pathways of plastic breakdown. By 2014, Yang co-authored studies on polyethylene (PE)-degrading bacteria, emphasizing the role of microbial cultures and oxidative processes, such as oxygen free radicals, in addressing plastic waste.  

A central theme of Yang’s research has been the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae, particularly the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and *Tenebrio obscurus*. In 2015, Yang and collaborators published landmark findings demonstrating that mealworms could survive on and degrade expanded polystyrene (EPS, commonly known as Styrofoam). Isotopic labeling experiments confirmed that degraded polystyrene was assimilated into larval biomass, establishing a biological pathway for plastic transformation. Subsequent studies in 2015 and 2018 expanded this work by examining the mechanisms of polystyrene degradation, the chemical composition of Styrofoam, and the broader implications for sustainable plastic waste management. Yang’s research also extended to other plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene (PP) foam, further demonstrating the potential of insect larvae as biological agents for degrading diverse plastic types.  

In addition to biological studies, Yang has contributed to the physicochemical characterization of plastic degradation. Research published in 2015 and 2021 employed advanced analytical techniques such as thermal analysis and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) to investigate structural and chemical changes in plastics during degradation. These studies provided critical insights into transformation pathways, degradation products, and the influence of physicochemical properties on biodegradation efficiency. Yang also co-authored work in 2021 that explored incubation conditions for mealworms, reflecting an interest in optimizing experimental parameters to enhance biodegradation outcomes.  

Collectively, Yang’s body of work integrates microbiology, entomology, and materials science to advance both the fundamental understanding of plastic biodegradation mechanisms and the applied potential of biological systems in waste management. By combining microbial and insect-based degradation experiments with rigorous physicochemical analyses, Yang has established a multidisciplinary framework for addressing the global challenge of plastic pollution. This sustained and diverse research trajectory positions Yang as a key figure in the emerging field of biological plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang is cited as an author in a 2018 study referenced in the text.</data>
