<node id="Illumina">
  <data key="d0">Illumina</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Illumina is a biotechnology company based in San Diego, California, USA, recognized as a leading provider of sequencing technologies and related products. In the context of microbial community analysis and genomic studies, Illumina supplied several key sequencing platforms, including the MiSeq (e.g., MiSeq PE300) and NovaSeq (e.g., NovaSeq PE250) systems, which were employed for high-throughput DNA sequencing. The company is also the manufacturer of the MiSeq sequencer and associated reagent kits, which are widely used for generating high-quality sequencing data.  

Beyond sequencing platforms, Illumina provided essential sample preparation tools and kits that supported library construction and sequencing workflows. These included the TruSeq Nano DNA LT Sample Preparation Kit, used for preparing DNA libraries, and the mRNA-Seq sample preparation kit, designed for transcriptome analysis. Illumina’s sequencing technology also incorporated adapter sequences necessary for library indexing and multiplexing, further enabling efficient and scalable sequencing experiments.  

In addition to hardware and reagents, Illumina contributed to bioinformatics resources by modifying the Greengenes v13.5 reference sequence database, which is used for bacterial classification in microbial studies. This highlights the company’s role not only in generating sequencing data but also in supporting downstream analysis and taxonomic assignments.  

Overall, Illumina is identified as the central technology provider in the study, supplying sequencing platforms, reagents, sample preparation kits, and reference database modifications that collectively enabled comprehensive microbial community analysis and broader genomic research applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Illumina is the provider of the MiSeq sequencing platform used for paired-end sequencing of the V3-V4 region of the 16S rRNA gene.&lt;SEP&gt;Illumina is the company whose sequencing technology was used to analyze microbial communities in the study.</data>
