<node id="Mealworm">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm is the common name for the larval stage of the darkling beetle *Tenebrio molitor*, an organism of notable ecological, agricultural, and scientific importance. Widely recognized as a high-protein feed source, mealworms have long been used in poultry, aquaculture, reptile husbandry, and other animal production systems, making them a valuable component of sustainable food and feed strategies. Their nutritional profile has also attracted increasing attention in the context of food security and the development of alternative protein sources for human consumption.  

Beyond their agricultural role, mealworms have become a focal point of environmental and microbiological research due to their unique ability to ingest and partially degrade synthetic polymers. Studies have demonstrated that mealworms can consume plastics such as polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS or Styrofoam), high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), and certain microplastics. However, their degradation capacity is limited, as they are unable to effectively process more crystalline or resistant polymers, including linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) and rigid polypropylene (PP). The biodegradation process is closely linked to the mealworm gut microbiota, which provides enzymatic activity and produces emulsifying agents that facilitate polymer breakdown. Specific microbial strains, such as *Bacillus* sp. PELW2042, have been isolated from the mealworm gut, highlighting the importance of microbial symbiosis in enabling plastic degradation.  

Experimental findings indicate that while mealworms can metabolize part of the ingested plastic into biomass, a considerable fraction is excreted as undigested residues, reflecting incomplete degradation. Their ability to process plastics appears to improve with age, potentially due to shifts in gut microbial communities or physiological adaptations. Nevertheless, mealworms cannot survive on plastics alone. Supplemental nutrition, such as barley bran or other natural feed sources, is essential for sustaining their growth, development, and successful metamorphosis. Without additional nutrients, mealworms exhibit reduced growth, impaired pupation, and higher mortality, particularly when exposed to plastics with higher crystallinity. Co-feeding with bran not only improves survival but also reduces the negative physiological effects of plastic ingestion.  

Mealworms have also been studied as a model for exploring nitrogen-fixing activity within their gut microbiome under different dietary conditions, including Styrofoam, bran, and antibiotic-treated diets. This research underscores the complexity of their microbial community and its role in both nutrition and biodegradation. Controlled experiments have further examined the effects of bedding materials, nutrient supplementation, and plastic pre-treatment on EPS consumption and degradation, consistently reinforcing the idea that mealworms represent a promising biological system for investigating plastic waste mitigation.  

In summary, mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) occupy a dual role of economic and scientific significance. They serve as a sustainable protein source in agriculture and animal husbandry while simultaneously providing a biological model for studying plastic biodegradation and microbial symbiosis. Although their plastic-degrading abilities are promising, they remain limited and insufficient for large-scale waste management applications. Ongoing research is focused on optimizing their diet, microbial interactions, and environmental conditions to enhance their biodegradation potential and expand their role in sustainable food and environmental systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm is the common name for the larval stage of Tenebrio molitor, used in experiments for polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
