<node id="Yellow Mealworm">
  <data key="d0">Yellow Mealworm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Yellow Mealworm (Tenebrio molitor larva) is a protein-rich insect larva that has attracted significant scientific and commercial interest due to its dual role as both a sustainable food source and a potential agent for plastic biodegradation. Recognized as safe for human consumption by the European Food Safety Agency, the yellow mealworm offers nutritional benefits, particularly its high protein content, making it a promising alternative protein source for food and feed industries.  

In addition to its nutritional value, the yellow mealworm has been extensively studied for its ability to degrade various plastics, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foam. Experimental observations have shown that mealworms can consume and metabolize these synthetic polymers, sometimes causing visible structural damage to the materials. Among these plastics, polystyrene degradation has been most widely studied, with yellow mealworms demonstrating continuous transformation of PS and exhibiting high degradation efficiency. This process is believed to be facilitated by unique microbial activity within the larvae’s gut microbiome, which undergoes notable changes when the insects are fed plastic-based diets.  

However, the capacity of yellow mealworms to survive and thrive on plastic diets varies depending on the type of polymer consumed. Studies have reported differences in survival rates, weight changes, mortality, and biomass production when larvae are fed plastics compared to conventional feed. For example, while yellow mealworms are capable of consuming polystyrene, they often exhibit reduced survival rates and altered growth performance under such feeding conditions.  

The dual significance of the yellow mealworm lies in its potential economic and environmental benefits. On one hand, it represents a sustainable protein source for human and animal nutrition; on the other, it offers a biological pathway for mitigating plastic pollution through biodegradation. As such, the yellow mealworm is increasingly studied not only for its nutritional applications but also for its role in advancing circular economy strategies and environmental sustainability.  

In summary, the yellow mealworm is a versatile insect larva valued for its high protein content, safety as a food source, and remarkable ability to degrade synthetic plastics, particularly polystyrene. Its unique biological and microbial mechanisms make it a subject of ongoing research at the intersection of food security, waste management, and environmental conservation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
