</node>
<node id="Polyether-PU Foam">
  <data key="d0">Polyether-PU Foam</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyether-PU foam was used as a feeding material for mealworms, which caused structural damage through chewing and gnawing.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a type of polyurethane tested as a diet for mealworms, showing no negative impact on survival and being partially degraded during digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a polymeric material studied for its biodegradation and thermal decomposition properties, including urethane, urea, and ether bonds.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyether-PU foam is a thermoset polyurethane material studied for its degradation and digestion in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370611</data>
</node>
<node id="Bran">
  <data key="d0">Bran</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bran is a nutrient-rich wheat product that serves as a fundamental dietary component in experimental research involving *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and *Zophobas morio* (superworms). In these studies, bran is most commonly employed as the primary control diet, providing a nutritionally sufficient baseline against which alternative feeding substrates—particularly synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), and microplastic spheres (MPS)—are evaluated. Its composition, which includes carbohydrates, starch, soluble and insoluble fiber, protein, fat, vitamins, and trace elements, supports high larval survival, normal growth, biomass accumulation, and successful development. These outcomes are not consistently achieved with plastic-only diets, making bran an indispensable reference condition for assessing the biological, ecological, and physiological impacts of experimental feeding materials.  

Beyond its role as a control, bran is frequently used as a co-diet or nutritional supplement in experiments where larvae are exposed to plastics. Supplementation with bran provides essential nutrients that enhance larval survival, growth, pupation, and resilience. Research has shown that bran can mitigate oxidative stress, enzyme activity disruptions, and developmental impairments associated with plastic ingestion. In some cases, bran supplementation improves survival rates in PS-fed larvae and reduces stress responses in microplastic-exposed larvae. Its presence has also been linked to enhanced PS degradation rates in both mealworms and superworms, in some experiments nearly doubling efficiency. However, other findings suggest a trade-off: while bran improves larval health, it may slightly reduce plastic biodegradation efficiency by diverting feeding activity away from plastics.  

Bran also plays a critical role in microbiome research. Larvae fed exclusively on bran establish characteristic gut microbial communities, including associations with fungal taxa such as *Hyphopichia* and *Debaryomyces*. These stable microbial profiles serve as essential baselines for distinguishing natural diet-driven microbial dynamics from shifts induced by plastic ingestion. When combined with plastics, bran has been shown to influence gut microbiota composition and function, providing insights into how diet modulates microbial responses to synthetic polymers.  

Experimental designs incorporate bran in diverse ways to evaluate its effects on consumption, degradation, and physiological outcomes. Examples include its use alongside 13C-labeled PS in agar jelly feeding experiments, comparative trials with Styrofoam to assess survival and mass changes in superworms, and mixed diets such as “Diet 2” (bran plus PS), which was associated with altered cholesterol levels. Bran-fed larvae have also been compared against PU-fed larvae, with bran diets supporting higher enzyme activity. Commercially available bran, such as that sourced from Bob’s Red Mill, is commonly used as both the original food source for worms and as a dietary additive in controlled studies. In some experiments, natural wheat bran without additives was specifically used as the feedstock for control groups.  

Overall, bran functions as a versatile and indispensable dietary component in insect research. It serves as a reliable control diet in survival, physiology, and microbiome studies; a nutritional supplement that offsets the negative impacts of plastic ingestion; and a comparative substrate for evaluating the effects of alternative feeding materials. Its consistent use across experimental contexts underscores its importance in advancing understanding of mealworm and superworm biology, gut microbiome dynamics, and the potential role of insects in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bran is a diet type for mealworm larvae that influences gut microbiota composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Bran was used as a dietary supplement in Yang et al. (2018a), which improved PS consumption by larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded64