<node id="PS (Polystyrene)">
  <data key="d0">PS (Polystyrene)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic, petroleum-derived plastic polymer composed of a linear carbon backbone with benzene ring side groups, consisting only of hydrogen and carbon atoms. It is one of the most widely produced plastics worldwide and is the primary material in Styrofoam, as well as in numerous consumer and industrial products. PS is valued for being lightweight, durable, and versatile, but it also poses a major environmental challenge due to its chemical stability, resistance to hydrolysis, and strong resistance to microbial and enzymatic degradation. Compared with other plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET), PS is particularly difficult to break down, making it a persistent pollutant and a central focus of plastic pollution and biodegradation research.  

In scientific studies, polystyrene is frequently used as a model substrate to explore biological strategies for plastic waste reduction. It has been tested as a carbon source in microbial metabolism experiments and as a feeding substrate in biodegradation trials involving soil invertebrates and insect larvae. Species such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), wax moth larvae (Galleria mellonella), and Tenebrio obscurus have been shown to ingest PS, including expanded polystyrene (EPS) containing additives like talc, calcium stearate, and butane. Among several plastics tested, PS was consumed the most by mealworms, both when provided as the sole diet and when supplemented with codiets such as corn flour or bran. Feeding trials have demonstrated measurable PS consumption over 10-, 20-, and 30-day intervals.  

Within insect digestive systems, PS undergoes partial depolymerization and chemical modification. Some of the material is metabolized into larval biomass, while residual microplastic fragments are excreted in frass. Analytical methods such as tetrahydrofuran (THF) extraction are used to assess molecular weight reduction and structural changes during degradation. The process is strongly influenced by gut microbiota, including bacteria and fungi, which play a critical role in PS breakdown. Antibiotic treatments that suppress microbial communities, such as gentamicin, inhibit depolymerization, underscoring the importance of symbiotic microorganisms. Microbial strains isolated from insect guts have been shown to degrade PS under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, though the process remains slow and incomplete.  

Degradation pathways of PS can yield products such as styrene, carbon dioxide (CO₂), fecula, and compounds like 1,3-di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene. Feeding trials with Tenebrio molitor larvae have also revealed physiological impacts, including increased protein levels, altered fatty acid synthesis, weight loss, and reduced survival rates when PS is consumed as the sole diet. Because PS provides little to no usable energy or mass for larvae, supplementation with conventional nutrient sources improves larval health and enhances biodegradation efficiency, suggesting that co-feeding strategies may facilitate plastic breakdown. Enzyme activity assays have further correlated PS diets with changes in alkaline phosphatase, acid phosphatase, and esterase-lipase C8 activity, indicating metabolic adjustments during PS ingestion.  

Overall, polystyrene (PS) represents both a widely used industrial material and a significant environmental pollutant. Its persistence in ecosystems has made it a key subject in biodegradation research, particularly in studies involving insect larvae and their gut microbiota. As both a contributor to microplastic pollution and a critical experimental substrate, PS continues to play an important role in advancing understanding of biological degradation mechanisms and in guiding the development of innovative strategies to mitigate plastic waste and its ecological impacts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092ae