<node id="PE (Polyethylene)">
  <data key="d0">PE (Polyethylene)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene (PE) is a synthetic polymer and one of the most widely produced and utilized plastics worldwide. It is composed of long chains of carbon–carbon bonds, giving it high molecular weight, hydrophobicity, and exceptional resistance to chemical and biological degradation. These properties make PE highly durable but also environmentally persistent, as it resists hydrolysis and biodegradation under natural conditions. Structurally, PE is similar to polypropylene (PP) but lacks the methyl groups present in PP subunits. It exists in multiple forms, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), each with distinct molecular arrangements that influence their mechanical strength, flexibility, and applications.  

Due to its versatility, PE is extensively used in packaging films, plastic bags, bottles, containers, foams, and a wide range of consumer and industrial products. Its widespread use, combined with its resistance to degradation, has made it a major contributor to global plastic pollution. Comparative studies with other plastics such as polyurethane (PU), polystyrene (PS), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) consistently show that PE degrades more slowly, reinforcing its classification as a non-degradable plastic in natural environments.  

Despite its persistence, PE has been the subject of intensive biodegradation research. Experimental studies have demonstrated that certain insect larvae, including mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*), Indianmeal moth larvae (*Plodia interpunctella*), and superworms, can chew, ingest, and fragment PE films and foams. Feeding experiments with *T. molitor* and waxworms have shown that ingestion of PE alters the composition of their gut microbiota, suggesting that symbiotic microorganisms contribute to partial degradation. In some cases, PE has been used as the primary diet for larvae, with results indicating limited but measurable breakdown into long-chain alkanes and oxygen-containing compounds. Studies have also reported that PE foams ingested by mealworms can be digested and partially mineralized, highlighting the role of insect–microbe interactions in initiating depolymerization.  

Microbial degradation of PE has also been documented, though with low efficiency. Several bacterial strains, including *Rhodococcus ruber*, *Citrobacter* sp., *Kosakonia* sp., *Enterobacter absurcis* YT1, *Enterobacter* sp. strain D1, *Enterobacter cloacae* AKS7, and SCGB1, have been identified as capable of initiating oxidative or enzymatic modifications of PE surfaces. Many of these strains have been isolated from insect digestive tracts, reinforcing the hypothesis that insect–microbe symbioses play a key role in PE biodegradation. LDPE films are frequently used as model substrates in controlled experiments to study these microbial interactions, enzymatic pathways, and the mechanisms underlying polymer fragmentation.  

In summary, Polyethylene (PE) is a chemically robust and widely used plastic polymer that underpins modern materials applications but also poses significant environmental challenges due to its persistence and resistance to natural degradation. While PE remains one of the most durable and polluting plastics in ecosystems, emerging research on insect- and microbe-mediated biodegradation provides valuable insights into potential biotechnological strategies for mitigating PE pollution and advancing sustainable plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;PE is one of the three tested microplastics used in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;PE is a type of microplastic fed to mealworms. It showed limited depolymerization and an increase in molecular weight values after digestion.</data>
