<node id="PP (Polypropylene)">
  <data key="d0">PP (Polypropylene)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polypropylene (PP) is a widely used synthetic polymer and one of the most common plastic materials, valued for its durability, chemical resistance, and non-hydrolysable nature. It is extensively employed in applications such as packaging films, containers, and foams, where its resistance to hydrolysis and environmental degradation makes it particularly useful. However, this same durability also contributes to its persistence as a pollutant, raising concerns about its accumulation in the environment and prompting research into potential biodegradation pathways.  

Biodegradation studies on polypropylene (PP) have focused on both insect- and microbe-mediated processes. Insects such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), and Zophobas morio have been shown to chew, ingest, and partially degrade PP when used as a sole diet. Experimental setups often involve feeding larvae with PP foams or films and subsequently analyzing their frass (excrement) for residual polymer content. These studies have revealed limited depolymerization of PP in the insect gut, suggesting that digestion is aided by gut-associated microbial communities. For example, Enterobacter species isolated from Z. atratus larvae have been linked to the degradation process, indicating a symbiotic role of microbes in facilitating polymer breakdown.  

Microbial degradation of PP has also been investigated independently. Certain bacterial strains, such as Bacillus sp. strain 27, have demonstrated measurable but modest degradation of PP microplastics, with reported rates around 4.0%. Research further indicates that the molecular weight of PP influences its susceptibility to degradation, with lower molecular weight fractions being more prone to breakdown compared to higher molecular weight forms.  

Analytical techniques such as Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) are commonly used to characterize chemical changes in PP during biodegradation experiments. Solvent extraction methods, including the use of 1,2-dichlorobenzene (1,2-DCB) and dichloromethane (DCM), are employed to isolate and quantify residual PP, providing insights into the extent of degradation.  

Comparative feeding studies have shown that while mealworms consume PP, it is ingested in smaller amounts relative to other plastics such as polystyrene (PS). Nonetheless, PP remains one of the five major plastics tested in insect-mediated biodegradation experiments and continues to be a focus of research due to its environmental persistence.  

In summary, polypropylene (PP) is a durable, non-hydrolysable plastic polymer that poses significant environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural degradation. Emerging evidence suggests that certain insect larvae and their gut microbiota, as well as specific bacterial strains, can contribute to its partial breakdown. Although degradation rates remain limited, these findings highlight PP as both a problematic pollutant and a promising target for future studies aimed at developing biological strategies for plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PP is a linear hydrocarbon resin with formula (C2H4)n, capable of forming atactic (aPP), isotactic (iPP), and syndiotactic (sPP) chain structures. It is a major source of plastic solid waste and pollutants such as microplastics (MPs) and nanoplastics (NPs).&lt;SEP&gt;PP (Polypropylene) is a plastic material studied for biodegradation by bacteria and insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic polymer tested in this study for biodegradation by Z. atratus and T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic polymer studied for its depolymerization by larvae such as T. molitor and Z. atratus, with the process being dependent on gut microbes.&lt;SEP&gt;PP is a plastic polymer that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP is a type of plastic mentioned as a potential target for future biodegradation studies with Tenebrio obscurus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
