<node id="Beijing, China">
  <data key="d0">Beijing, China</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Beijing, China is a major center for scientific research and technological development in the field of plastic biodegradation, particularly studies involving mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and microbial strains. The city has played a pivotal role in advancing knowledge of polystyrene and Styrofoam degradation, as it served as the geographic source of yellow mealworm larvae used in multiple experimental studies. Research conducted with mealworms originating from Beijing provided convincing evidence that these larvae can consume and degrade polystyrene, contributing to a deeper understanding of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation. In addition, Beijing was the site where the Styrofoam-degrading bacterial strain YT2 was first isolated, further highlighting its importance in microbial biodegradation research. Studies conducted in the city have also documented the ability of T. molitor larvae to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), expanding the scope of plastic degradation research beyond polystyrene.

Beijing hosts a range of institutions, companies, and research platforms that support and facilitate this work. Aoboxing Biotechnology Co., Ltd. has been associated with biodegradation studies, while Allwegene Technology Inc. and its Allwegene Cloud platform provide technological and analytical resources for scientific investigations. Other relevant organizations located in Beijing include Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., Foring Science Instrument Technology Development Co., Ltd., SINOPEC Beijing Yanshan Company, and Daxing Insect Breeding Plant, all of which contribute to the city’s role as a hub for scientific materials, instrumentation, and experimental resources. Beihang University, a prominent academic institution in Beijing, also contributes to the city’s research ecosystem, supporting studies in biodegradation and related scientific fields.

Collectively, these resources and institutions establish Beijing, China as a critical hub for experimental research, technological support, and scientific breakthroughs in the study of plastic biodegradation. Its role as both a source of biological material, such as mealworm larvae, and a site of microbial strain isolation underscores its importance in advancing global understanding of sustainable solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Beijing, China is the location of SINOPEC Beijing Yanshan Company.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
