<node id="Brandon et al., 2018">
  <data key="d0">Brandon et al., 2018</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon et al., 2018 is a widely cited research study that investigated the biodegradation of synthetic polymers by mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), with a particular focus on expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and related foams. The study is recognized as a foundational contribution to the field of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, providing both experimental findings and methodological approaches that have informed subsequent research.  

A central finding of Brandon et al., 2018 was that mealworms were capable of consuming and degrading plastics, and that their plastic ingestion rates increased when bran was provided as a co-feed. This suggested that nutritional supplementation plays an important role in enhancing both feeding behavior and the potential for polymer breakdown. The study also emphasized the influence of diet on gut microbial ecology, reporting shifts in microbial community composition when larvae were fed polyethylene, polystyrene, and bran. These results highlighted the potential role of gut microbiota in facilitating or supporting plastic degradation.  

Methodologically, Brandon et al. contributed to the field by developing and applying approaches for gut microbial sampling and DNA extraction, which have since been adopted in later studies examining microbial contributions to polymer degradation. Their work also included the detection and analysis of polystyrene degradation using analytical methods, further supporting evidence of partial breakdown of plastics within the insect digestive system. Additionally, the study identified gut-associated bacteria, including Lactococcus, as part of the microbial community potentially linked to gut health and degradation processes.  

Beyond its experimental findings, Brandon et al., 2018 has been referenced in discussions of microplastic degradation, larval feeding behavior, and the broader role of insects in addressing plastic waste. It has also served as a basis for experimental diets in later studies and as a methodological reference for analyzing gut microbiomes in biodegradation research. While the study has been influential, subsequent research has reported mixed results regarding the consistency and mechanisms of plastic degradation by mealworms and their gut microbes, reflecting ongoing debate in the field.  

Overall, Brandon et al., 2018 is regarded as a significant and pioneering study that advanced understanding of plastic biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae, particularly through its exploration of feeding behavior, the effects of co-feeding with bran, the role of gut microbial communities, and the application of molecular and analytical techniques to study polymer breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon and colleagues conducted a study in 2018 on polyethylene consumption by mealworms and analyzed gut chemical compositions.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al., 2018 is a cited study that investigated microorganisms involved in plastic consumption and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon and colleagues conducted a study in 2018 on biodegradation of plastics by T. molitor and Z. atratus.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al., 2018 is a cited research study referenced for biodegradation of PS, PE, and PP polymers in insect larvae.</data>
