<node id="Yang et al., 2021">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al., 2021</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang et al., 2021 refers to a research study conducted by Yang and colleagues that investigated the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae, with a particular focus on mealworms. The study examined the ability of larvae to consume and degrade different types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS) and polypropylene foam, and assessed the survival rates of the larvae under these feeding conditions. Over a 35-day experimental period, the researchers monitored plastic consumption and degradation, providing key insights into the potential of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation.  

In addition to measuring degradation rates, Yang et al., 2021 also explored the role of gut microbiota in the process by conducting antibiotic suppression tests. These experiments aimed to determine the contribution of microbial communities within the larvae to the breakdown of plastics. The study further included nutritional comparisons of larvae feeding on plastics versus other diets, thereby contextualizing the physiological and survival impacts of plastic consumption.  

The findings of Yang et al., 2021 have been widely cited in subsequent research as a reference point for methodologies and results related to plastic biodegradation, larval survival, and gut microbiota suppression. The study is frequently used for comparative purposes in biodegradation research, particularly in evaluating the efficiency of insect larvae as biological agents for managing plastic waste. Overall, Yang et al., 2021 represents a significant contribution to the field of plastic biodegradation and insect-based waste management strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
