<node id="Figure 2">
  <data key="d0">Figure 2</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 2 is a composite and multifaceted representation of experimental findings that collectively illustrate the interactions between mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus* and *Zophobas morio*), and synthetic polymers, with emphasis on plastic biodegradation, biological responses, and associated biochemical processes. The figure integrates ecological, microbiological, biochemical, and material science perspectives to provide a comprehensive view of plastic consumption and transformation in insect-based systems.  

At the ecological and physiological level, Figure 2 presents data on survival, pupation, weight, and consumption rates of mealworms and superworms when fed various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foam, and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). These measurements are reported both per larva and per unit biomass, often at five-day intervals, with statistical analyses applied to assess treatment differences. The figure also compares cumulative consumption patterns under different feeding conditions, such as microplastic substrates (MPS) alone or co-fed with bran, highlighting the influence of diet composition on larval performance.  

From a materials characterization perspective, Figure 2 incorporates multiple analytical techniques to assess structural and chemical changes in plastics during and after digestion. These include X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra comparing pristine polymers (PS, PVC, LDPE) with frass produced by mealworms, nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectra, and thermal analysis of Styrofoam and fecula decomposition. Additional evidence of oxygen incorporation into degraded polymers is presented, suggesting oxidative processes during biodegradation. Surface-level changes are also documented, including scratches observed in LDPE bags and deterioration of PS film topography following incubation with the bacterial strain YT2, indicating microbial or enzymatic contributions to polymer breakdown.  

At the biochemical and microbial level, Figure 2 highlights gut-associated processes and microbial community dynamics. It presents hydrophobicity measurements of mealworm gut supernatant fractions using contact angle analysis, which reveal surface-active properties. An emulsifying agent localized in the 30–100 kDa fraction is identified, suggesting the presence of bioactive compounds that may facilitate plastic degradation. Cytotoxicity assays of extracts from *Z. morio* larvae fed Styrofoam show reduced cytotoxic effects at most concentrations, pointing to potential detoxification mechanisms. The figure also includes a heat map of biochemical traits expressed by larval microbiomes, with variables shown as deviations from the mean, as well as analyses of gut microbial communities in *T. molitor*, including bacterial abundances and correlation-based network structures. Furthermore, experiments with the antibiotic gentamicin demonstrate the role of microbial activity in the depolymerization of PS and PE substrates.  

Overall, Figure 2 synthesizes diverse datasets—ranging from larval survival and feeding behavior to polymer structural changes, microbial community interactions, and biochemical assays—to provide a holistic understanding of how mealworms and superworms consume and transform synthetic plastics. It underscores the ecological feasibility, microbial involvement, and biochemical mechanisms underlying insect-mediated plastic degradation, while also addressing potential environmental and toxicological implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2 is referenced as showing the number of pupae formed and hatched individuals in the second phase of the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2 shows changes in MP surface morphology after being fed to larvae, including bite marks.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 2 presents the characterization of biodegradation and oxidation of ingested PS and LDPE polymers by mealworms, including molecular weight changes, isotope signatures, and FTIR spectra.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4</data>
