<node id="Figure 3">
  <data key="d0">Figure 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3 is a central figure that integrates multiple experimental datasets to characterize the biodegradation of synthetic polymers by *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae and related microbial processes, while also exploring ecological implications of the degradation products. The figure combines physical, chemical, microbial, and ecological analyses to provide a comprehensive view of plastic transformation and its downstream applications.  

At the physical level, Figure 3 presents cumulative weight loss measurements of various polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyether-based polyurethane foams (PU1 and PU2), and polyethylene (PE). These data, obtained through systematic weighing of plastic pieces over time, demonstrate the extent of plastic consumption by mealworm larvae. Complementary analyses of frass (excreta) provide evidence of chemical modification of ingested plastics.  

On the chemical level, the figure incorporates Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), attenuated total reflectance FTIR (ATR-FTIR), thermogravimetric (TG/DTG) analysis, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). These techniques reveal oxidation, cleavage, depolymerization, and altered thermal stability of polymers after digestion, confirming that ingestion by *T. molitor* leads to structural transformation of plastics. Additional gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analyses further characterize molecular weight changes in degraded polymers.  

Figure 3 also highlights microbial contributions to plastic degradation. It presents microbial respiration data showing the conversion of PS into CO₂, including isotopic signatures and carbon recovery proportions, thereby linking polymer breakdown to mineralization. The figure further illustrates changes in surface hydrophobicity and chemical composition of PS films after incubation with bacterial strain YT2, supporting the role of specific microbes in polymer modification. Gut microbiome analyses of *T. molitor* and *T. obscurus* larvae fed with different diets (LDPE, HDPE, LLDPE, and wheat bran) are also included, showing shifts in microbial community composition and abundance associated with plastic ingestion. These results underscore the interplay between host larvae and their gut microbiota in facilitating plastic degradation.  

Beyond degradation, Figure 3 explores ecological applications of the byproducts. It presents plant growth experiments in which frass derived from plastic-fed larvae was used as a soil amendment. Comparisons with tea leaf-based media demonstrate that frass can support cactus growth, with error bars representing standard error across replicates. This component links plastic biodegradation to potential agricultural reuse of frass as a nutrient substrate.  

In summary, Figure 3 synthesizes polymer weight loss data, spectroscopic and thermal analyses, microbial respiration and isotopic tracing, gut microbiome profiling, and frass-based plant growth experiments. Together, these results provide strong evidence that *T. molitor* larvae and their associated microbiota can degrade and transform synthetic polymers, while also highlighting the potential ecological utility of the degradation products.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 is a figure showing the change in mealworm mass versus mealworm viability rate from Source A for 10, 20, and 30 days with five types of plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 presents data on the amount of plastic consumed by mealworms at different time intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 shows SEM images of larval intestines after MP feeding, confirming ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 shows the characterization of the size-frequency distribution of residual plastics in frass, including SEM images and particle size distributions at different weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3 illustrates the DEB model growth curve fitting results for different groups of larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
