<node id="FTIR">
  <data key="d0">FTIR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique widely used to investigate chemical structures, functional groups, and compositional changes in a broad range of materials, with particular importance in polymer science and biodegradation research. FTIR operates by measuring the infrared absorption spectra of a sample, which provides information about the vibrational modes of chemical bonds. This allows researchers to detect the presence, modification, or disappearance of specific functional groups, thereby revealing chemical transformations within the material.  

In the context of polymer degradation and biodegradation studies, FTIR is a key tool for monitoring structural and chemical changes. It enables the detection of oxidation processes, chain scission, and the introduction of oxygen-containing groups such as hydroxyl, carbonyl, and carboxyl functionalities. By comparing spectra before and after exposure to degrading agents—including microorganisms, larvae, or environmental conditions—FTIR provides qualitative evidence of polymer breakdown. This has been demonstrated in studies of polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), and polypropylene (PP), where FTIR confirmed the occurrence of oxidation, partial degradation, and the formation of new chemical groups.  

FTIR has also been applied to the analysis of frass (insect excrement) to investigate how biological systems interact with and modify synthetic polymers. For example, in studies involving larvae such as *A. grisea*, FTIR analysis of egested frass revealed the presence of new functional groups, confirming that polymers like polyethylene had undergone chemical transformation during digestion. This application highlights FTIR’s role in linking biological activity with material degradation pathways.  

Beyond biodegradation, FTIR is often used in conjunction with complementary techniques such as thermogravimetric (TG) analysis to provide a more comprehensive understanding of thermal stability, compositional changes, and degradation mechanisms. Instruments such as the Nicolet Magna IR-8700 exemplify the advanced capabilities of FTIR in both standalone and integrated analytical workflows.  

Overall, FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a versatile and powerful method for analyzing chemical modifications, oxidation processes, and functional group transformations in polymers and related materials. Its ability to qualitatively confirm biodegradation and track molecular-level changes makes it an essential tool in environmental science, materials research, and studies of polymer–organism interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is an analytical method used to confirm the breakdown of benzoic rings in polystyrene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR refers to Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, an analytical technique used to confirm differences in biodegradation ability.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is the analytical method used to study the spectra of PE, PS, and frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) was used to analyze chemical modifications and incorporation of oxygen in biodegraded plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) is an analytical technique used to confirm chemical and physical modifications during LDPE biodegradation.</data>
