<node id="Yang et al., 2015a">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al., 2015a</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang et al., 2015a is a widely cited scientific study that investigates the biodegradation of synthetic plastics by insects, with a particular focus on the yellow mealworm (Tenebrio molitor). The research is considered foundational in demonstrating that insect larvae are capable of ingesting and metabolizing plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyether-based polyurethane (polyether-PU). The study provided direct evidence that mealworms not only consume these polymers but also transform them, as indicated by weight loss in the plastics, the presence of degraded polymer residues in frass, and chemical modifications in the remaining material.  

A key contribution of Yang et al., 2015a lies in its methodological rigor and use of advanced analytical techniques. The researchers employed isotopic labeling to trace the incorporation of plastic-derived carbon into the metabolic systems of mealworms, including fatty acid profiles, thereby confirming assimilation of polymer carbon into insect biomass. Solid-state ^13C cross-polarization/magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (CP/MAS NMR) spectroscopy was used to detect new functional groups in the degraded residues, providing molecular-level evidence of chemical transformations during the biodegradation process. The study also introduced methodological refinements such as starvation periods in feeding trials, which helped distinguish between plastic-derived signals and pre-existing metabolic activity.  

In addition to insect-driven degradation, Yang et al., 2015a explored the role of gut microbiota in facilitating polymer breakdown. The study reported that antibiotic treatments, such as gentamicin administration, inhibited PS depolymerization in mealworms, thereby implicating bacterial symbionts as essential contributors to the degradation process. This finding positioned the work as an important reference for subsequent research into microbial involvement in plastic biodegradation.  

The publication is frequently cited across multiple contexts: as evidence of soil invertebrates’ ability to transform plastics, as a reference for bacterial-driven transformations of PS, and as a methodological source for procedures such as antibiotic suppression tests in larvae. It is also used comparatively in studies on Styrofoam degradation and in broader discussions of insect larvae as biological agents for plastic waste management.  

Overall, Yang et al., 2015a is recognized as a seminal study that integrates biological, chemical, and isotopic evidence to advance understanding of how insects and their associated microbiota contribute to the degradation of synthetic polymers. It remains a cornerstone reference for both experimental protocols and conceptual frameworks in the emerging field of plastic biodegradation by living organisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
