<node id="Spiroplasmataceae">
  <data key="d0">Spiroplasmataceae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family within the order *Entomoplasmatales* that plays a significant role in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly species of the genus *Tenebrio* such as *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*. This family has been consistently identified as a predominant microbial group in studies investigating the gut communities of mealworms exposed to various diets, including conventional substrates like bran as well as synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polypropylene (PP), and polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foams.  

The abundance of Spiroplasmataceae within the gut microbiome is highly diet-dependent. When mealworms are fed bran, this family is dominant, but its relative abundance decreases when the diet shifts to polyether-PU foam. Conversely, Spiroplasmataceae populations increase significantly when larvae are fed with plastics such as PET, PS, and PP. In particular, studies have reported that Spiroplasmataceae can constitute over half of the gut microbial community in PP-fed groups, reaching levels as high as 53.34%. This strong association with plastic-based diets suggests a potential role in facilitating or adapting to the biodegradation of synthetic polymers.  

Within this family, *Spiroplasma velicicrescens* has been specifically identified as a representative species in experimental studies. The consistent presence and sometimes dominant abundance of Spiroplasmataceae across different Tenebrio gut microbiomes highlight its ecological importance and possible functional contributions to the digestion or transformation of both natural and synthetic dietary components.  

Overall, Spiroplasmataceae is characterized as a gut-associated bacterial family that responds dynamically to dietary shifts in mealworms, particularly in the context of plastic biodegradation. Its prevalence in polymer-fed larvae underscores its potential involvement in microbial processes linked to the breakdown of recalcitrant materials, making it a focal point of interest in studies of insect-mediated plastic degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387694</data>
</node>
<node id="Enterococcaceae">
  <data key="d0">Enterococcaceae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family within the order *Lactobacillales* that plays a prominent role in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly species of the genus *Tenebrio* such as *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*. This family is consistently identified as one of the most abundant and dominant microbial groups in the digestive tracts of these insects, especially when they are fed on bran-based diets. Its prevalence, however, is influenced by dietary conditions: Enterococcaceae abundance is high in bran-fed mealworms, reduced in those fed on polyurethane (PU), and notably increased in larvae consuming polyethylene terephthalate (PET).  

Research has also highlighted the association of Enterococcaceae with polystyrene (PS) diet shifts, suggesting a role in the microbial community dynamics that support plastic biodegradation in mealworms. This indicates that members of the family may contribute to the metabolic processes enabling insects to adapt to and degrade synthetic polymers. Within experimental studies, Enterococcaceae has been represented by species such as *Enterococcus hirae* and *Bacillus velezensis*, reflecting its taxonomic and functional diversity in insect gut ecosystems.  

Overall, Enterococcaceae is recognized as a key gut-associated bacterial family in *Tenebrio* species, with its abundance and functional roles closely tied to dietary inputs and potential contributions to plastic degradation pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family common in all groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family whose relative abundance was negatively affected by microplastic feeding in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae are a family of bacteria whose abundance decreased in response to polymer additives.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family found in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio larvae and linked to LDPE biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386521</data>
</node>
<node id="Streptococcaceae">
  <data key="d0">Streptococcaceae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family within the order *Lactobacillales* that plays a notable role in the gut microbiome of mealworms, particularly *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*. This family is consistently present across different dietary groups of mealworms and is considered one of the most abundant bacterial families in their gut communities. In experimental studies, Streptococcaceae has been represented by species such as *Lactococcus garvieae* and *Lactococcus taiwanensis*.  

Dietary conditions strongly influence the relative abundance of Streptococcaceae in mealworm gut microbiota. The family has been observed to increase significantly in mealworms fed with polyether-based polyurethane (PU) foam, as well as in those fed aged microplastics, suggesting a potential role in plastic degradation processes. Similarly, higher abundances of Streptococcaceae have been recorded in middle-fed and inner-fed yellow mealworms compared to bran-fed controls, indicating that substrate type and feeding location within the substrate can shape microbial community composition.  

Overall, Streptococcaceae is a core and adaptable bacterial family in mealworm gut ecosystems, with its abundance enhanced under conditions involving synthetic or aged plastic substrates. This highlights its potential ecological and functional importance in the degradation of complex materials within insect-associated microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio larvae and associated with LDPE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family found in the intestines of mealworms in previous metagenomic studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family dominant in the PS-L group, largely represented by Lactococcus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387684</data>
</node>
<node id="Enterobacteriaceae">
  <data key="d0">Enterobacteriaceae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterobacteriaceae is a diverse bacterial family within the order *Enterobacteriales* that plays a central role in the gut microbiota of insects, particularly in *Tenebrio* species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and *Tenebrio obscurus*. It is consistently reported as one of the dominant microbial groups in mealworm gut ecosystems, both under natural dietary conditions (e.g., bran) and when larvae are exposed to alternative substrates, including synthetic polymers. This prevalence highlights its ecological importance, metabolic versatility, and adaptability to different nutritional environments.  

Dietary studies have demonstrated that the abundance of Enterobacteriaceae varies significantly depending on the substrate consumed. In control groups fed on bran, unclassified Enterobacteriaceae were already dominant, but their populations expanded further when larvae were fed on plastics such as polyether-polyurethane (PU) foam, polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), and ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA). Similar increases were observed in *T. obscurus* when exposed to PS diets. The consistent enrichment of Enterobacteriaceae under plastic-based feeding conditions suggests that members of this family may actively participate in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers.  

Experimental evidence supports this association. Specific strains within Enterobacteriaceae, including *Klebsiella pneumoniae* and *Enterobacter absuriae* YTI, have been identified as capable of degrading PS and PE, respectively. Other genera such as *Citrobacter* and *Kosakonia* have also been detected in mealworm gut communities, underscoring the taxonomic diversity of this family in insect digestive systems. One study isolated 13 distinct Enterobacteriaceae strains from *T. molitor* larvae, highlighting both the richness and functional potential of this bacterial group. Beyond mealworms, Enterobacteriaceae has been detected in the gut microbiota of other insect larvae exposed to PS, further linking it to polymer degradation processes.  

The family includes species shown to degrade a wide range of plastics, including PS, EPS, PU, PE, LDPE, PP, high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), and polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA). Its ability to increase in abundance under plastic-based diets, including those involving aged microplastics, strongly implicates it in the metabolic pathways that enable insects such as mealworms to survive on otherwise recalcitrant substrates. Enterobacteriaceae has also been mentioned as part of commensal bacteria that can interact with microplastics in the gastrointestinal tract, suggesting a broader ecological role in host–plastic interactions.  

In summary, Enterobacteriaceae is a core component of the gut microbiota in *Tenebrio* larvae and other insects, characterized by its dominance under natural diets and functional enrichment under plastic-based feeding conditions. Its taxonomic diversity, demonstrated plastic-degrading capabilities, and ecological adaptability position it as a key microbial partner in insect digestion and an important contributor to the emerging field of plastic biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is one of the bacterial families classified under Gammaproteobacteria, identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is one of the bacterial families within Gammaproteobacteria identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is a bacterial family found in the intestines of mealworms in previous metagenomic studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is a bacterial family associated with biodegradation of plastics including PS, PP, PVC, and LDPE in Tenebrionidae insects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
