<node id="Shannon Index">
  <data key="d0">Shannon Index</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The **Shannon Index** is an ecological diversity metric widely used in microbiome research to quantify both species richness (the number of different species present) and evenness (the relative abundance of those species) within a community. In gut microbiome studies, it serves as a key measure of **alpha diversity**, providing insight into the complexity and balance of microbial populations. The index has been applied extensively to evaluate microbial communities in the gastrointestinal tracts of insects such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and superworms, particularly in experiments assessing the impact of different diets.  

Research using the Shannon Index has demonstrated that dietary composition significantly influences microbial diversity. For example, larvae fed with conventional diets such as bran exhibited higher Shannon Index values, reflecting greater microbial richness and evenness, compared to those fed with alternative or plastic-based diets, such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), polystyrene (PS), or corn starch (CS). In these cases, the index revealed a reduction in bacterial richness and overall diversity, indicating that less natural diets can disrupt the balance of gut microbial communities.  

Overall, the Shannon Index provides a standardized and sensitive tool for comparing microbial diversity across experimental conditions. Its application in gut microbiome studies highlights how environmental factors, particularly diet, shape the structure and diversity of microbial ecosystems. By capturing both the number of microbial taxa and their distribution, the Shannon Index offers a comprehensive measure of community health and stability in ecological and microbiological research.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a diversity estimator used to measure gut fungal community diversity in yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index is a diversity measure used to estimate species richness and evenness in microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon index was used to measure the diversity of the gut microbiome in mealworms under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The Shannon Index was used to measure richness and diversity of the gut microbiome in mealworms under different diet conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387681</data>
</node>
