<node id="China">
  <data key="d0">China</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">China is a country that plays a central role in global scientific research, industrial development, and environmental studies, with particular prominence in biotechnology, materials science, and environmental science. It is both a leading producer of plastics and a hub for innovative research aimed at addressing the environmental challenges associated with plastic waste. In 2019, China accounted for approximately 31% of global plastic production, highlighting its dual position as a major contributor to plastic generation and as a key driver of sustainability-focused research and technological solutions.  

The country’s research ecosystem is supported by robust institutional and financial frameworks. Major funding bodies such as the National Key R&amp;D Program, the National Natural Science Foundation of China, and the Jiangsu Province Foundation provide extensive support for scientific investigations across disciplines. China also hosts influential corporations and research institutions, including SINOPEC, Eyelslet, and the Guangdong Academy of Sciences, which contribute significantly to industrial innovation and environmental research.  

Geographically, China’s research and industrial activities are concentrated in major cities such as Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Shenzhen, Dongguan, Tai’an, and Nanchang, while provinces including Jiangxi and Jiangsu serve as important regional bases for affiliated institutions. Leading universities such as Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Lanzhou University, the Harbin Institute of Technology, and Northeastern University are central to the country’s scientific output. Specialized facilities, including the Shandong Sishui-Limin Insect Breeding Plant and the Daxing Insect Breeding Plant, further demonstrate China’s extensive infrastructure for experimental and applied research.  

China has emerged as a global leader in the study of biological plastic degradation, particularly through pioneering work on the ability of mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) to consume and metabolize polystyrene (PS). At least six independent studies conducted in Chinese laboratories have confirmed that mealworms can digest expanded polystyrene under controlled conditions. These experiments have examined degradation rates, larval survival, and environmental parameters, with findings showing that T. molitor larvae thrive and exhibit optimal plastic-degrading activity at temperatures between 25 and 28°C. For example, one experiment incubated 500 mealworms in 1200 mL containers to monitor polystyrene consumption, generating valuable data for global efforts to develop biological solutions to plastic waste. China has also been a source of yellow mealworms and T. obscurus larvae used in comparative studies of polystyrene consumption, with insect breeding plants across the country supplying larvae for biodegradation tests.  

Beyond laboratory-based biodegradation studies, China has conducted investigations into microplastic pollution in wastewater treatment plants, including analyses of sewage sludge for microplastic content. These efforts reflect the country’s broader commitment to understanding and mitigating the environmental impacts of plastic production and disposal. Additionally, China is home to LanXess, a supplier of polystyrene beads used in biodegradation research, further linking its industrial and scientific contributions.  

Overall, China stands out as both a leading producer of plastics and a global research hub dedicated to advancing environmental science and sustainability. Its combination of strong institutional support, extensive research infrastructure, and experimental contributions—particularly in the field of mealworm-mediated plastic degradation—underscores its pivotal role in shaping scientific and industrial responses to global environmental challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Country where Harbin and Beijing are located and where some of the institutions are based.&lt;SEP&gt;China is a country where Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus larvae have been studied for plastic biodegradation and are commercially sold as food sources.&lt;SEP&gt;China is the country where Tongji University and East China University of Science and Technology are located.&lt;SEP&gt;The country where Tongji University and its affiliated institutions are located.&lt;SEP&gt;China is one of the locations where biodegradation of commercial PS foams by mealworms was observed.&lt;SEP&gt;China is the country where Tongji University and the State Key Laboratory of Pollution Control and Resource Reuse are located.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
