<node id="Europe">
  <data key="d0">Europe</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Europe is referenced in multiple contexts relating to scientific research, sustainability, and industrial production. It is the region where the MIX-UP project received funding support from the European Union’s Horizon 2020 program, highlighting its role in advancing research and innovation in areas such as plastic biodegradation and circular economy solutions. Europe is also a significant hub for the commercial rearing of mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor), which are cultivated to produce sustainable protein and animal fats for both livestock feed and human consumption. Research in Europe has shown that T. molitor larvae exhibit optimal viability at temperatures between 25 and 28°C, and experimental studies across countries including Finland, France, Germany, Poland, Slovenia, Spain, Turkey, and the United Kingdom have observed mealworms consuming polystyrene (PS) foam, suggesting potential applications in waste management and biodegradation.

In terms of plastics, Europe plays a major role in global production and waste management. In 2019, global plastic production exceeded 359 million tons, with Europe contributing approximately 16% of this total. The region has developed structured systems for handling plastic waste, balancing recycling, energy recovery, and landfill disposal. In 2018, Europe collected 17.8 million tons of plastic waste, of which 42% was recycled, 39.5% was converted into energy, and 18.5% was landfilled. These figures reflect Europe’s efforts to reduce environmental impact through improved recycling rates and energy recovery practices, although landfill use remains a component of waste management. Additionally, Europe is noted for its market distribution of plastic polymers, with significant shares in materials such as polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene (PE), underscoring the diversity of plastic applications and industries in the region.

Overall, Europe emerges as a region at the intersection of scientific innovation, sustainable food production, and plastic management. It demonstrates leadership in funding research projects, advancing insect-based protein industries, and implementing structured approaches to plastic production and waste treatment, while also serving as a key site for experimental studies on biodegradation and material recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is the region considered for natural gas and electricity supply and consumption in GWP calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is mentioned as a region where consumer attitudes towards insect consumption have been negative but are gradually changing.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is referenced in the context of polymer demand statistics.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is a region where Tenebrio molitor is authorized for use as food for cattle, farmed fish, and humans under EU Regulation 2017/89.&lt;SEP&gt;Europe is the region where Styrofoam production reaches nearly 2 million tons per year.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388193</data>
</node>
<node id="LDPE (Low-Density Polyethylene)">
  <data key="d0">LDPE (Low-Density Polyethylene)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used plastic polymer and a common form of polyethylene that plays a significant role in both commercial applications and scientific research. It is a major component of everyday plastic products such as films, foams, and carrier bags, making it one of the most prevalent synthetic polymers in circulation. Due to its extensive use and environmental persistence, LDPE has become a focal material in studies of plastic biodegradation and microplastic pollution.  

Research has shown that LDPE can serve as a substrate for biological degradation, particularly through interactions with insect larvae and their associated microbial communities. Experimental studies have demonstrated that insect species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), *Zophobas atratus*, and *Tenebrio obscurus* are capable of consuming and partially degrading LDPE. In these systems, LDPE is depolymerized into lower molecular weight compounds and oxidized intermediates, indicating that the polymer undergoes chemical transformation during digestion. However, the extent of consumption is relatively small compared to other plastic types, and degradation rates vary across time intervals and experimental conditions.  

The role of gut microbes in LDPE degradation has been a central focus of investigation. In some cases, microbial symbionts appear to be essential for effective breakdown, as evidenced by experiments where the introduction of antibiotics inhibited degradation. For example, studies involving Indian mealmoth larvae identified *Enterobacter* species isolated from the gut as contributors to LDPE degradation. Conversely, in *T. molitor* larvae, LDPE depolymerization has been reported as less dependent, or even independent, of gut microbes, suggesting that both host enzymatic activity and microbial interactions may contribute to the process.  

Analytical methods have been employed to assess the extent of LDPE degradation, including the examination of insect frass (excrement) for residual polymer content. Solvents such as 1,2-dichlorobenzene (1,2-DCB) and dichloromethane (DCM) have been used to extract and analyze LDPE residues, providing evidence of partial breakdown and transformation of the material during digestion.  

Overall, LDPE is not only a ubiquitous plastic in consumer products but also a model material in biodegradation research. Its study in controlled feeding experiments with insect larvae, combined with microbial and chemical analyses, has advanced understanding of plastic degradation pathways. These findings highlight the potential of insect–microbe systems as biological tools for mitigating plastic pollution, while also underscoring the complexity and limitations of LDPE biodegradation in natural and experimental environments.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a specific form of polyethylene plastic foam that was ingested and degraded by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a form of polyethylene polymer that undergoes limited depolymerization when ingested by insects such as mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
