<node id="Brandon, A.M.">
  <data key="d0">Brandon, A.M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon, A.M. is a researcher recognized for his significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insects and their gut-associated microbial communities in breaking down synthetic polymers. His work has been instrumental in demonstrating that certain insect larvae, especially yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), are capable of ingesting and metabolizing persistent plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and mixtures of different polymer types.  

A central theme of Brandon’s research is the biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms. He has co-authored multiple influential studies, including a 2018 publication in *Chemosphere*, which provided clear evidence that mealworms can ingest and metabolically process polystyrene waste. These investigations have gone beyond documenting the physical degradation of plastics to explore the underlying biological mechanisms, particularly the role of the insect gut microbiome. By analyzing changes in microbial communities during plastic digestion, Brandon has highlighted the importance of insect–microbe symbiosis in enabling the breakdown of otherwise highly resistant polymers.  

In addition to his work on mealworms, Brandon has contributed to expanding the scope of insect-mediated biodegradation by studying superworms. A 2020 co-authored study demonstrated that *Zophobas atratus* larvae are also capable of processing synthetic polymers, thereby showing that the ability to degrade plastics is not limited to a single insect species. This finding broadened the potential applications of insect-based systems for plastic waste management and suggested that multiple insect taxa may play a role in addressing plastic pollution.  

Brandon’s research integrates insights from digestion, microbial ecology, and polymer chemistry to advance scientific understanding of biologically mediated pathways for plastic degradation. His studies emphasize both the fundamental biological processes that allow insects and their symbiotic microbes to metabolize plastics and the applied potential of these systems for sustainable waste treatment. By demonstrating that insect larvae and their gut microbiomes can contribute to the breakdown of persistent plastics, his work provides promising avenues for mitigating the global challenge of plastic accumulation.  

Overall, Brandon, A.M. is an established author and co-author of multiple studies on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene and polyethylene, in insect larvae. His contributions have been pivotal in shaping current knowledge of insect-driven and microbially mediated plastic degradation, positioning his research at the intersection of biotechnology, microbiology, and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon, A.M. is an author of a study on biodegradation of plastics in Tenebrio genus.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon, A.M. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on enhancing bioenergy production with carbon capture of microalgae.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon, A.M. is one of the authors of a 2018 study (details truncated in text).</data>
