<node id="Gao, S.H.">
  <data key="d0">Gao, S.H.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gao, S.H. is a researcher recognized for significant contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of insects in breaking down synthetic polymers. Gao has co-authored and authored multiple studies examining the capacity of mealworms, specifically yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae), to digest and biodegrade plastics that are otherwise resistant to natural degradation. This body of work has demonstrated the ubiquity and effectiveness of polystyrene digestion in mealworms, showing that these larvae can metabolize and fragment polystyrene into smaller components, thereby contributing to its biodegradation. In addition to polystyrene, Gao has investigated the biodegradation of polyethylene, one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics, providing evidence that mealworms can also contribute to its breakdown under certain conditions.  

Beyond Tenebrio molitor, Gao has extended research to other insect species, including Zophobas atratus larvae (superworms), in studies on the biodegradation of foam plastics. These investigations highlight the broader potential of insect-mediated plastic degradation across different species and plastic types. Gao’s work has also addressed the biodegradation of plastic mixtures, reflecting real-world scenarios where multiple polymers coexist in waste streams.  

Collectively, Gao, S.H.’s research has advanced scientific understanding of insect-driven plastic degradation processes and their environmental implications. By demonstrating that insects such as mealworms and superworms can metabolize synthetic polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene, Gao’s studies provide valuable insights into potential biological strategies for mitigating plastic pollution. This research contributes to the growing field of sustainable waste management and offers promising avenues for developing eco-friendly solutions to the global plastic waste crisis.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, S.H. co-authored a referenced study on polyethylene and plastic biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, S.H. is a co-author of research on biodegradation of polyethylene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, S.H. is one of the authors of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, S.H. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, S.H. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
