<node id="Yang, S.S.">
  <data key="d0">Yang, S.S.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang, S.S. is a researcher recognized for pioneering contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insects in breaking down synthetic polymers. Their work has been instrumental in demonstrating that certain insect larvae, including yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), and wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*), are capable of ingesting and metabolizing persistent plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene.  

A central theme in Yang, S.S.’s research is the use of yellow mealworms as a biological model for plastic degradation. Through multiple studies, Yang, S.S. has shown that mealworms can consume and process plastics, providing clear evidence of insect-mediated biodegradation pathways. A notable example is a 2018 publication in *Chemosphere* that examined the biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms, highlighting their ability to metabolize plastic waste. Subsequent studies expanded this work to include polyethylene and plastic mixtures, demonstrating that mealworms are capable of degrading a broader range of synthetic polymers. Complementary investigations into the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* have provided insights into the microbial contributions that facilitate plastic breakdown, shedding light on the ecological and biochemical mechanisms underlying these processes.  

Beyond mealworms, Yang, S.S. has co-authored studies on other insect species with plastic-degrading potential. Research on superworms has revealed their ability to consume and partially degrade polystyrene and polypropylene, while studies on wax moth larvae have shown their capacity to degrade polyethylene and polystyrene. These comparative investigations underscore the diverse metabolic capabilities of different insect species and highlight their potential applications in mitigating plastic pollution.  

In addition to experimental studies, Yang, S.S. has contributed to bibliometric analyses of the scientific literature on plastic biodegradation, mapping the growth and development of this emerging field. Their publications from 2020 and 2021 have advanced understanding not only of the biological mechanisms of insect-mediated plastic degradation but also of the broader ecological implications of employing insects as agents of waste reduction.  

Collectively, Yang, S.S.’s body of work has established a scientific foundation for insect-based biodegradation as a potential strategy to address global plastic waste challenges. By integrating research on insect physiology, microbial symbiosis, and polymer degradation, Yang, S.S. has positioned themselves as a key figure in advancing the field of plastic biodegradation by insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the 2020 study on biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene by greater wax moth larva.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, S.S. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, S.S. is an author of studies on biodegradation of plastics in Tenebrio genus.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, S.S. is an author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, S.S. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
