<node id="Zhou, J.">
  <data key="d0">Zhou, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhou, J. is a researcher whose work bridges environmental microbiology, entomology, and biodegradation science, with a strong focus on developing biological strategies to address plastic pollution. Zhou is particularly recognized for pioneering studies on insect-mediated plastic degradation, especially through research on the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*). In a series of influential investigations, Zhou and collaborators demonstrated that mealworms can ingest, digest, and metabolize persistent synthetic polymers, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polyurethane. These studies provided some of the earliest and most compelling evidence that insect larvae are capable of breaking down not only single polymer types, such as polystyrene waste, but also more complex plastic mixtures, including polyurethane foams. This body of work has significantly advanced scientific understanding of insect-based biodegradation and highlighted the potential of mealworms as biological agents for mitigating plastic waste accumulation.  

Zhou has co-authored multiple landmark studies in this area, including research on the biodegradation of polyethylene in mealworms, the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms, and the capacity of insect larvae to process plastic mixtures. More recent work, such as a 2022 study, has examined the feeding preferences of insect larvae for different types of waste plastics, further reinforcing the potential of insect-based systems as sustainable tools for waste management.  

Beyond insect-focused research, Zhou has also contributed to microbial biodegradation studies. A notable example is a 2009 study on the isolation of a biphenyl-degrading bacterial strain, reflecting an interest in microbial pathways for breaking down environmental contaminants. Zhou has also made methodological contributions to microbial ecology, including work on improving amplicon sequencing accuracy on the Illumina MiSeq platform for environmental microbial community analysis. Earlier in their career, Zhou co-authored a 1996 study on DNA recovery from soils, underscoring a longstanding engagement with molecular techniques in environmental microbiology.  

Taken together, Zhou, J.’s research portfolio reflects an interdisciplinary approach that integrates microbiology, entomology, and environmental science. By investigating both insect and microbial mechanisms of polymer degradation, Zhou has contributed to the development of innovative biological solutions for plastic pollution and environmental remediation. This work has deepened scientific understanding of biodegradation processes and laid a foundation for future applications of biological systems in addressing global waste challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene and plastic mixtures in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhou, J. is an author of studies on biodegradation of plastics and microbial processes.</data>
