<node id="Wu, W.M.">
  <data key="d0">Wu, W.M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wu, W.M. is an internationally recognized researcher and author whose pioneering work has significantly advanced the scientific understanding of plastic biodegradation, particularly through biological and microbial processes. His research has focused on some of the most persistent and environmentally problematic synthetic polymers, including polystyrene, polyethylene, polylactic acid, and expanded polystyrene. By investigating how these plastics can be degraded and mineralized, Wu has contributed foundational knowledge to the field of environmental biotechnology and waste management.  

A central theme of Wu’s research is the role of insects and their gut microbiota in facilitating plastic biodegradation. He has co-authored multiple influential studies demonstrating that mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) are capable of ingesting and biodegrading polystyrene, with evidence of both physical fragmentation and microbial enrichment processes that enable plastic breakdown. His work has shown that the gut microbiome of these insects plays a critical role in the degradation and mineralization of plastics. Building on these findings, Wu has extended his investigations to other organisms, including superworms (Zophobas atratus larvae), waxworms, and land snails, as well as to specific gut bacteria involved in polyethylene degradation. Collectively, these studies highlight the potential of insect–microbe systems as natural agents for reducing plastic waste.  

Wu has authored and co-authored numerous studies that have become key references in the field. These include research on polyethylene and plastic mixtures in mealworms, polystyrene degradation by both dark and yellow mealworms, and microbial contributions to plastic biodegradation. His 2021 study on the yellow mealworm gut microbiome provided important insights into microbial pathways of plastic degradation, while his earlier 2014 study on polystyrene biodegradation by bacterial strains from waxworms underscored his long-standing contributions to uncovering microbial mechanisms. He has also co-authored studies addressing the prevalence of microplastics in the environment and their ecological impacts, further broadening the scope of his work.  

Beyond organismal and microbiological studies, Wu has integrated perspectives from entomology, microbiology, and environmental science to address broader environmental challenges. His research framework connects fundamental mechanisms of biodegradation with applied strategies for mitigating plastic pollution, offering potential pathways toward sustainable waste management solutions.  

Overall, Wu, W.M. has established a significant body of work that provides foundational evidence for the role of insects, microorganisms, and their interactions in mitigating plastic pollution. His contributions not only advance scientific understanding of plastic biodegradation pathways but also offer promising strategies for addressing one of the most urgent environmental challenges of the modern era.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu, W.M. is an author of multiple studies on microplastics pollution, reduction strategies, and biodegradation of plastics in Tenebrio genus.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu, W.M. is a co-author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on polystyrene biodegradation by bacterial strains from waxworms.</data>
