<node id="Criddle, C.S.">
  <data key="d0">Criddle, C.S.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Criddle, C.S. is a researcher and author recognized for his influential contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insect larvae in breaking down synthetic polymers. His work has centered on species such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), dark mealworms, and superworms, which have demonstrated the ability to ingest and degrade plastics including polystyrene, polyethylene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and mixed plastic waste. By investigating these processes, Criddle has examined both the physiological mechanisms within insect larvae and the microbial enrichment in their digestive systems that enable the transformation of otherwise highly persistent synthetic materials.  

He has co-authored multiple landmark studies that have advanced this field of research. These include a 2018 study on the biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures in mealworms, a 2019 study on polystyrene degradation by mealworms, and a 2020 study on PVC biodegradation in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. In 2021, he collaborated on research exploring the broader potential of insect larvae as biological agents for plastic biodegradation, and in subsequent years expanded this scope to include superworms as effective degraders of plastics. Collectively, these publications provide detailed analyses of how insect larvae can ingest, digest, and metabolize synthetic polymers, offering insights into both the biological and microbial processes that drive biodegradation.  

Beyond experimental studies, Criddle has also contributed to research addressing the wider issue of microplastic pollution and strategies for its reduction. His co-authorship of a 2023 bibliometric analysis of publications on plastic biodegradation reflects his engagement not only in laboratory-based research but also in mapping the development, trends, and knowledge gaps within this emerging scientific field.  

Through this body of work, Criddle, C.S. has significantly advanced scientific understanding of the interactions between plastics, insect larvae, and their associated microbial communities. His research underscores the potential of leveraging biological systems as innovative, nature-based strategies for reducing the environmental impact of synthetic polymers. By integrating experimental findings with broader analyses of plastic pollution, his contributions highlight promising pathways toward sustainable solutions for global challenges in plastic waste management and ecological health.&lt;SEP&gt;Criddle, C.S. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the polystyrene biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;Criddle, C.S. is a co-author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
