<node id="Gao, J.">
  <data key="d0">Gao, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gao, J. is a researcher whose work focuses on the environmental impacts of plastic pollution and the potential for biological systems to contribute to plastic biodegradation. A central theme of Gao, J.’s research is the study of synthetic polymer degradation by insects, particularly the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms). Through multiple co-authored studies, Gao, J. has investigated the ability of these larvae to consume and metabolize various plastic materials, including expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene foams. This body of work has provided important evidence that *T. molitor* larvae can digest and degrade plastics, suggesting a possible biological pathway for mitigating plastic waste and contributing to sustainable waste management strategies.  

Among Gao, J.’s notable contributions are a 2018 study demonstrating the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in yellow mealworms, a 2020 publication on the biodegradation of EPS and polyethylene foams by *T. molitor* larvae, and a 2021 article in *Chemosphere* that further explored the biodegradation of EPS and polyethylene foams in the same species. These studies collectively advance scientific understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and its potential applications in environmental remediation.  

In addition to research on biodegradation, Gao, J. has also examined the ecological consequences of microplastic ingestion in other species. One significant study co-authored by Gao, J. demonstrated that polystyrene microplastic consumption increases honeybee susceptibility to viral infections, highlighting the broader ecological risks of plastic pollution to pollinator health and ecosystem stability.  

Through this integrated research agenda, Gao, J. has established expertise at the intersection of plastic pollution, biodegradation, and ecological health. By contributing to both fundamental scientific knowledge and applied approaches, Gao, J. has played a key role in advancing understanding of how biological systems may help address the global challenge of plastic waste while also illuminating the ecological consequences of microplastic contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, J. is an author of a study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene and polyethylene foams in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, J. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene and polyethylene foams in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
