<node id="Li, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Li, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Li, Y. is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of plastics, with particular emphasis on polystyrene and its breakdown through biological and ecological processes. A central theme of Li, Y.’s research is insect-mediated plastic degradation, especially through species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworms) and *Zophobas atratus* (superworms). Li, Y. has co-authored multiple studies demonstrating that these insects can consume and metabolize polystyrene and polyethylene, providing compelling evidence that insect digestion can contribute to reducing persistent plastic waste.  

A significant component of this research involves examining the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* during polystyrene biodegradation. Li, Y. has investigated how microbial communities and enzymatic processes within the insect gut facilitate the breakdown of synthetic polymers, thereby clarifying the biological mechanisms that enable insect-mediated plastic digestion. This work also extends to studying how dietary shifts influence the gut microbiome of mealworms, offering insights into the ecological and metabolic dynamics that underpin plastic biodegradation in insects.  

Beyond insect-focused studies, Li, Y. has contributed to broader research on the environmental implications of plastics. This includes co-authoring studies on the environmental fate, toxicity, and risk management of nanoplastics, expanding the scope of inquiry from degradation mechanisms to the ecological and health risks associated with plastic pollution. Li, Y. has also investigated the biodegradability of alternative polymer materials, such as crude glycerol-based polystyrene foams, reflecting an interest in developing more sustainable and environmentally degradable plastics.  

Complementing this work, Li, Y. has co-authored studies on the biodegradation of hydrolyzed polyacrylamide and has conducted research on microbial metabolism beyond plastics, including investigations into protein metabolic pathways during anaerobic digestion of waste activated sludge. These contributions highlight a broader engagement with microbial processes relevant to waste treatment and environmental sustainability.  

Overall, Li, Y. is recognized for advancing scientific understanding of both natural and engineered pathways for plastic biodegradation. Through research on insect digestion, microbial interactions, nanoplastic risk assessment, and the development of biodegradable polymers, Li, Y. has made significant contributions to knowledge that supports innovative strategies for mitigating the environmental impact of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Li, Y. is a co-author of a 2021 study on the response of yellow mealworm gut microbiome to diet shifts during polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
