<node id="Polystyrene">
  <data key="d0">Polystyrene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene is a synthetic, petroleum-derived plastic polymer that is among the most widely produced and utilized plastics globally, with an estimated annual production of about 35 million tons and a growth rate of approximately 5% per year. It is valued for its durability, hydrophobic nature, structural stability, and resistance to degradation, which make it highly versatile across industrial and consumer applications. Common uses of polystyrene include packaging, insulation, electrical equipment, and disposable products. Its expanded foam form, known as expanded polystyrene (EPS) and often marketed under the trade name Styrofoam, is especially prevalent in food containers and protective packaging.  

Despite its utility, polystyrene presents significant environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural decomposition. It accumulates in ecosystems as a persistent pollutant, is classified as hazardous household waste, and contributes substantially to global plastic pollution. This persistence has made polystyrene a central focus of research into sustainable waste management, recycling, and pollution mitigation strategies.  

Scientific studies have increasingly explored biodegradation as a potential solution to polystyrene’s environmental impact. Microbial degradation has shown promise, with bacteria such as *Acinetobacter*, *Klebsiella oxytoca*, and *Azotobacter beijerinckii*, as well as fungi including *Aspergillus* species, initiating depolymerization processes that fragment the polymer into smaller compounds. Enzymatic pathways, both microbial and insect-associated, play a critical role in these transformations and represent potential routes for reducing polystyrene’s ecological footprint.  

Insect-mediated degradation has emerged as a particularly innovative area of research. Several species, including yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), dark mealworms (*Tenebrio obscurus*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), waxworms, greater wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*), and even land snails, have demonstrated the ability to ingest and metabolize polystyrene. Among these, *T. molitor* has been the most extensively studied. Research shows that mealworms can consume polystyrene as a dietary substrate, with their gut microbiota facilitating partial biodegradation and mineralization of the polymer. Feeding trials indicate that polystyrene consumption can support larval development and metamorphosis, though it is associated with high mortality due to its low nutritional value. Studies have documented changes in protein content, fatty acid composition, nitrogen metabolism, and gut microbial communities in larvae reared on polystyrene. Compared to other feed types such as whey or potatoes, polystyrene-fed larvae exhibit lower nutrient intake and reduced fatty acid values, though they still excrete altered fatty acid profiles and fecula with distinct chemical properties, reflecting partial polymer breakdown.  

Polystyrene has also been studied for its toxic effects on soil and aquatic organisms, further underscoring its environmental risks. Overall, polystyrene represents both a critical material in modern industry and a pressing ecological concern. Its widespread use and persistence highlight the urgent need for improved waste management and recycling strategies, while ongoing research into microbial, enzymatic, and insect-mediated degradation pathways offers promising avenues for mitigating its long-term environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymer material studied in the context of degradation, recycling, and biodegradation.</data>
