<node id="Lee, S.">
  <data key="d0">Lee, S.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lee, S. is a researcher and author whose work spans multiple studies in microbiology, biodegradation, and host-microbe interactions. A significant portion of Lee’s research focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly plastics. Lee, S. has co-authored studies investigating the ability of *Pseudomonas* species, including *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* isolated from superworms, to degrade polystyrene and other plastics. These studies contribute to the growing body of research on microbial solutions for plastic waste management and highlight the potential of insect-associated bacteria in bioremediation processes.  

In addition to work on plastic biodegradation, Lee, S. has conducted research on the holobiont biology of the greater wax moth, *Galleria mellonella*. This includes studies on the hologenome of *Galleria mellonella* and its capacity to metabolize beeswax, a long-chain hydrocarbon. The research emphasizes the role of the insect’s microbiome in facilitating the breakdown of complex natural substrates, providing insights into host-microbe metabolic cooperation and the ecological functions of insect-associated microbial communities.  

Beyond these areas, Lee, S. is also listed as an author on a study examining nitrogen-fixing populations in ocean metagenomes. This work explores the diversity and ecological significance of nitrogen-fixing microorganisms in marine environments, contributing to a broader understanding of global biogeochemical cycles.  

Overall, Lee, S. is recognized for contributions to studies on microbial biodegradation of plastics, the metabolic interactions within insect holobionts such as *Galleria mellonella*, and the ecological roles of nitrogen-fixing microbes in marine systems. This body of work reflects an interdisciplinary approach that integrates microbiology, ecology, and environmental biotechnology.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene by Pseudomonas sp. from superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a 2020 study on biodegradation of polystyrene by Pseudomonas sp. isolated from the gut of superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lee, S. is an author of a study on the Galleria mellonella holosome and its metabolism of beeswax.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386541</data>
</node>
<node id="Li, J.">
  <data key="d0">Li, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Li, J. is a researcher whose work focuses on environmental toxicology, biodegradation, and the ecological impacts of plastics and persistent pollutants. A significant portion of Li, J.’s research centers on the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly through microbial activity associated with insects. Li, J. has co-authored multiple studies investigating the role of *Pseudomonas* species, including *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*, in breaking down polystyrene. Several of these studies specifically examined bacteria isolated from the gut microbiota of superworms, highlighting their potential in facilitating oxidative degradation of polystyrene and contributing to plastic waste management strategies.  

In addition to microbial degradation, Li, J. has contributed to research on the environmental and biological effects of microplastics. This includes a 2022 study evaluating the gut toxicity of polystyrene microplastics in the earthworm *Eisenia fetida*, which provided insights into how soil organisms are affected by plastic pollution. Li, J. has also investigated the role of microplastics as vectors for hydrophobic organic contaminant (HOC) bioaccumulation in earthworms, emphasizing the ecological risks posed by the interaction between microplastics and chemical pollutants in terrestrial ecosystems.  

Beyond plastics, Li, J. has explored the phytotoxic effects of perfluorinated compounds, specifically perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) and perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS). This research addressed the potential hazards these persistent organic pollutants pose to plants, contributing to a broader understanding of their environmental impact.  

Overall, Li, J.’s body of work integrates studies on microbial biodegradation, soil ecotoxicology, and pollutant-plant interactions. By examining both the potential for biological solutions to plastic pollution and the risks associated with emerging contaminants, Li, J. contributes to advancing knowledge on environmental sustainability and pollutant mitigation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
