<node id="Peng, B.Y.">
  <data key="d0">Peng, B.Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng, B.Y. is a researcher recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation by insects, with a particular focus on mealworms and related species. Their work has centered on investigating the ability of insect larvae, including yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), dark mealworms (*Tenebrio obscurus*), and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), to digest and degrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene, low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC).  

Between 2019 and 2020, Peng, B.Y. authored and co-authored multiple influential studies that examined the mechanisms underlying polystyrene digestion in mealworms, the ubiquity of this process across different species, and comparative biodegradation capacities among insect larvae. These studies provided evidence that insect-based systems could play a role in mitigating plastic pollution by breaking down persistent synthetic polymers. In addition to experimental findings, Peng, B.Y.’s research has contributed to broader discussions on the potential integration of insect-mediated plastic degradation into sustainable waste management strategies.  

Peng, B.Y. has collaborated on several key publications that explored both the biological and microbial processes involved in plastic degradation, highlighting the interactions between insect gut microbiota and polymer breakdown. Their work on dark and yellow mealworms, as well as superworms, has been widely cited and has helped establish a foundation for further research into biological solutions for plastic waste.  

Through this body of research, Peng, B.Y. has advanced scientific understanding of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, positioning insect larvae as promising biological agents for addressing global plastic pollution challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng, B.Y. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene by dark and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of a 2019 study on polystyrene biodegradation by dark and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng, B.Y. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng, B.Y. is a co-author of a 2023 study on adsorption and desorption mechanisms of oxytetracycline on microplastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
