<node id="Galleria mellonella">
  <data key="d0">Galleria mellonella</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Galleria mellonella, commonly known as the greater wax moth or waxworm, is a lepidopteran insect belonging to the family Pyralidae. It is closely associated with honeybee colonies, where its larvae feed on beeswax. This feeding behavior, while ecologically significant, makes the species a major pest in apiculture, as infestations can cause extensive damage to beehives. At the same time, the ability of Galleria mellonella larvae to metabolize beeswax has provided important insights into the digestion of complex hydrocarbons.  

Beyond its role as a pest, Galleria mellonella has become an important model organism in scientific research. Its larvae have gained particular attention for their ability to biodegrade synthetic polymers, including polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polystyrene (PS), even at the microplastic scale. This biodegradation capacity is supported by multiple mechanisms: enzymatic activity from larval secretions such as saliva, metabolic processes within the insect itself, and contributions from its gut microbiota. Together, these processes form an integrated system often described as a holobiont or hologenome, enabling the species to metabolize both natural hydrocarbons like beeswax and synthetic plastics. Physiological adaptations, including modifications in lipid metabolism, may further enhance this unique capability.  

Galleria mellonella has also served as a biological reservoir for the isolation of plastic-degrading microorganisms. Strains such as Enterobacter sp. D1, Massilia sp. PS1903, and Aspergillus flavus PEDX3 have been identified from its gut or associated microbial communities, underscoring its importance in the discovery of novel biodegradation agents with potential applications in environmental remediation and sustainable waste management.  

In addition to biodegradation studies, Galleria mellonella is widely used as a model organism in microbiology, infection biology, and comparative physiology. Its larvae provide a versatile system for studying insect–microbe interactions, host–pathogen dynamics, and metabolic adaptations. Because of its practicality and ethical advantages, it is often employed as an alternative to vertebrate models in experimental research.  

Overall, Galleria mellonella occupies a dual role as both an agricultural pest and a valuable subject of scientific inquiry. Its ability to metabolize natural substances such as beeswax alongside synthetic polymers like polyethylene and polystyrene places it at the intersection of entomology, microbiology, and environmental science. Research on this species not only advances understanding of insect physiology and microbial symbiosis but also opens promising avenues for biotechnological innovation, particularly in addressing global challenges such as plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, also known as the wax moth, is a species whose caterpillars are capable of biodegrading polyethylene.</data>
