<node id="Qiu, R.">
  <data key="d0">Qiu, R.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Qiu, R. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the environmental impacts of plastics, particularly their biodegradation and the occurrence of microplastics. Qiu, R. has co-authored multiple studies investigating the biodegradation and disintegration of synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene and polyethylene, with a specific emphasis on their breakdown by land snails. This line of research contributes to understanding potential biological pathways for mitigating plastic pollution through natural degradation processes. In addition to biodegradation studies, Qiu, R. has also co-authored research on the prevalence of microplastics in traditional medicinal materials, including a 2020 study that examined animal-based products. Collectively, Qiu, R.’s work highlights both the environmental persistence of plastics and possible biological mechanisms for their degradation, as well as the implications of microplastic contamination in materials used for human health.&lt;SEP&gt;Qiu, R. is an author of multiple studies on microplastics, including their prevalence in medicinal materials and biodegradation by snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Qiu, R. is one of the authors of a study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387746</data>
</node>
<node id="Hu, J.">
  <data key="d0">Hu, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Hu, J. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the environmental impacts and biological interactions of plastics and microplastics, particularly in relation to terrestrial snails. Hu, J. has co-authored multiple studies investigating the biodegradation and disintegration of synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene and polyethylene by land snails, contributing to the understanding of potential biological pathways for plastic degradation in terrestrial ecosystems. In addition to biodegradation research, Hu, J. has examined the uptake and adverse effects of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) microplastics in snails, highlighting the physiological and ecological risks associated with microplastic exposure in terrestrial invertebrates. Beyond snail-focused studies, Hu, J. has also co-authored research on the occurrence of microplastics in traditional medicinal materials, published in 2020, which underscores the broader implications of microplastic pollution for human health and cultural practices. Collectively, Hu, J.’s body of work integrates ecological, toxicological, and environmental perspectives, with a particular emphasis on the interactions between microplastics and terrestrial organisms, as well as the potential for biological systems to contribute to plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Hu, J. is one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387746</data>
</node>
<node id="Li, X.">
  <data key="d0">Li, X.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Li, X. is a researcher whose work centers on the environmental fate, degradation, and occurrence of plastics and microplastics, with a particular emphasis on biological and environmental mechanisms that influence their persistence. A significant portion of Li, X.’s research investigates the biodegradation and disintegration of synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene and polyethylene. Within this area, Li, X. has explored the role of biological organisms, including land snails, mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), and superworms, in facilitating the breakdown of plastics. These studies examine not only the ingestion preferences of such organisms but also the associated microbial and biochemical processes, including nitrogen fixation and diazotrophic community dynamics in plastic-eating mealworms. This research highlights the potential of biological systems to contribute to alternative strategies for mitigating plastic pollution.  

In addition to biodegradation, Li, X. has conducted research on abiotic degradation pathways, particularly the photolytic degradation of polylactic acid microplastics. This work investigates how light-driven processes affect the breakdown of biodegradable plastics, underscoring the role of natural environmental factors in shaping the persistence and transformation of microplastics.  

Li, X. has also contributed to studies on the prevalence, distribution, and ecological implications of microplastics in diverse environmental and anthropogenic contexts. This includes investigations of microplastics in sewage sludge from wastewater treatment plants in China, which demonstrate how wastewater systems act as both reservoirs and potential sources of microplastic pollution. Further extending this line of inquiry, Li, X. co-authored a 2020 study documenting the occurrence of microplastics in traditional medicinal materials, thereby linking microplastic contamination to human health and cultural practices. Additional research has examined bacterial communities associated with soil microplastics at Guyui, as well as the presence of marine debris in the northern South China Sea, contributing to a broader understanding of how plastics interact with ecosystems across terrestrial and marine environments.  

Beyond plastics-focused research, Li, X. has also co-authored studies on environmental biogeochemical processes, including a 2019 investigation into nitrate reduction and Fe(II) oxidation under anoxic conditions. This work reflects a broader interest in microbial and chemical interactions in environmental systems.  

Overall, Li, X.’s body of work integrates biological, chemical, and ecological perspectives to advance knowledge on the degradation, distribution, and impacts of plastics and microplastics. By examining both natural degradation mechanisms and pathways of environmental contamination, Li, X. contributes to the development of scientific strategies for addressing the global challenge of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Li, X. is an author of multiple studies on microplastics, including their prevalence in medicinal materials and biodegradation by snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Li, X. is a co-author of a 2020 study on biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polystyrene by land snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on plastic ingestion preferences of superworm and yellow mealworm.&lt;SEP&gt;Li, X. is one of the authors of the study on breast cancer mice and polybrominated diphenyl ether-47.&lt;SEP&gt;Li, X. is one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails.</data>
