<node id="Zhang, X.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, X.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, X. is a researcher whose work centers on the biodegradation of plastics, microplastic pollution, and their ecological and toxicological impacts. A significant portion of Zhang, X.’s research investigates the capacity of various organisms to degrade synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene and polyethylene. This includes studies on the role of land snails in the biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polystyrene, as well as research on Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), which explores both their ability to consume and degrade plastics and the nutritional and toxicological consequences of such processes. These investigations also address the persistence of residual plastics and potential toxicity during and after biodegradation.  

In addition to invertebrate-mediated degradation, Zhang, X. has contributed to microbial studies, including the identification of Klebsiella pneumoniae Mk-1, a soil bacterium capable of degrading polyethylene. This line of research highlights the potential of microbial pathways as sustainable strategies for addressing plastic waste. Zhang, X. has also examined the effects of microplastics on environmental systems, including studies on the interactions between polystyrene micro/nanoplastics and soil protists, as well as the influence of polystyrene microplastics on seawater microbial communities. These works provide insights into how microplastics affect ecological processes and microbial dynamics.  

Beyond plastics, Zhang, X. has co-authored research on the occurrence of microplastics in traditional medicinal materials, published in 2020, which underscores the widespread nature of microplastic contamination and its implications for human health and environmental safety. Additional contributions include studies on the impacts of quaternary ammonium compounds on microorganisms and research on azo dye wastewater decolorization using microbial electrochemical systems, reflecting a broader interest in environmental microbiology and pollutant remediation.  

Overall, Zhang, X.’s body of work integrates biological, ecological, and toxicological perspectives to advance understanding of plastic pollution and its mitigation. By investigating both natural organisms and microbial agents capable of breaking down persistent synthetic polymers, as well as the environmental risks posed by microplastics, Zhang, X. contributes to the development of sustainable approaches for managing plastic waste and safeguarding ecosystems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
