<node id="Chen, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Chen, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen, Y. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the environmental impacts, biological interactions, and potential biodegradation of plastics and microplastics. Their research contributions include studies on the biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polystyrene and polyethylene by land snails, highlighting the potential role of terrestrial organisms in mitigating plastic pollution. Chen, Y. has also investigated the biological effects of microplastics on soil invertebrates, co-authoring studies on defense responses in earthworms exposed to polyethylene microplastics, which provide insights into the ecological risks posed by plastic contamination in terrestrial ecosystems.  

In addition to biodegradation research, Chen, Y. has contributed to studies examining the occurrence of microplastics in traditional medicinal materials, with a notable publication in 2020 that emphasized the widespread presence of microplastic contamination in products intended for human use. Their work also extends to toxicological studies, including a 2021 co-authored study demonstrating that exposure to polystyrene microplastics can induce male reproductive toxicity in mice, thereby underscoring the potential health risks of microplastic exposure in mammals.  

Overall, Chen, Y.’s body of research integrates environmental science, toxicology, and biodegradation studies, with a particular emphasis on understanding how microplastics interact with living organisms across different ecosystems and how biological processes may contribute to mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Chen, Y. is a co-author of a 2020 study on biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polystyrene by land snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Chen, Y. is one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails.</data>
