<node id="Wang, Z.">
  <data key="d0">Wang, Z.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, Z. is a researcher and scientific author whose work centers on microbial interactions with plastics, environmental pollutants, and biodegradation processes. His research is characterized by an interdisciplinary approach that integrates microbiology, environmental science, and materials degradation, with a particular focus on the ecological and microbial mechanisms underlying the breakdown of synthetic polymers, nanomaterials, and pollutants across diverse ecosystems.  

A significant portion of Wang, Z.’s research has focused on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene. He co-authored a 2020 study that identified and characterized a polystyrene-degrading *Acinetobacter* bacterium isolated from the larvae of *Tribolium castaneum*. This work highlighted the potential role of insect-associated microbes in facilitating plastic degradation. Wang, Z. has also contributed to studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene by insect larvae more broadly, including a 2021 study that further demonstrated the capacity of insect gut microbiota to process synthetic polymers.  

Extending beyond plastics, Wang, Z. has investigated the biodegradation of graphene oxide by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, as reported in a 2022 study. This research expanded the scope of insect-microbe systems to include nanomaterials, underscoring the potential of biological processes in mitigating emerging contaminants.  

In addition to insect-based systems, Wang, Z. has explored the ecological interactions of micro- and nanoplastics in soil environments. His work includes studies on the interactions between polystyrene micro/nanoplastics and soil protists, which provided insights into the ecological dynamics and potential risks of microplastic contamination in terrestrial ecosystems. He has also examined the behavior of aged microplastics in shoreline environments, particularly their interactions with oil, thereby contributing to understanding the environmental fate and transport of plastics in coastal systems.  

Wang, Z. has also addressed the intersection of plastic pollution and public health. He co-authored research investigating how microplastics may interact with viral genetic material, specifically fragments of SARS-CoV-2 RNA. This work raised important questions about the role of plastics as potential vectors or reservoirs for viral material in the environment.  

His research further extends to aquatic and sedimentary ecosystems. In a 2021 study, Wang, Z. co-authored work on anaerobic degrading bacteria in mangrove sediments, emphasizing the role of microbial communities in pollutant breakdown under oxygen-limited conditions. Earlier in his career, he also co-authored a 2018 study on the impacts of quaternary ammonium compounds on microorganisms, reflecting his broader interest in the interactions between pollutants and microbial systems.  

Overall, Wang, Z. is recognized for advancing knowledge on the microbial and ecological processes involved in the degradation and environmental interactions of plastics, nanomaterials, and chemical pollutants. His contributions span terrestrial, aquatic, and sedimentary environments, with a consistent emphasis on the role of microbial communities and insect-microbe systems in mitigating the impacts of anthropogenic contaminants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2</data>
