<node id="Zhang, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, Y. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of plastics and the development of biologically based strategies to address global plastic pollution. Their research encompasses both conventional synthetic polymers, such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and bio-based polymers like polylactic acid (PLA). A central theme of Zhang, Y.’s scholarship is the integration of microbial and insect-mediated approaches to plastic degradation, with particular attention to mechanisms, efficiency, and environmental implications.  

In the area of microbial degradation, Zhang, Y. has co-authored studies on bacterial species capable of breaking down plastics. This includes research on *Acinetobacter* strains, such as one isolated from *Tribolium castaneum* larvae, and *Klebsiella pneumoniae* Mk-1, isolated from soil. These studies demonstrated that microbial systems can degrade synthetic polymers through biochemical and oxidative pathways, offering insights into molecular-level processes that could be applied to sustainable waste management.  

Complementing this microbial focus, Zhang, Y. has made extensive contributions to insect-mediated plastic degradation. Much of this work centers on mealworm species, including *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), *Tenebrio obscurus* (dark mealworm), and *Zophobas atratus* (superworm). Zhang, Y. has co-authored multiple studies showing that these larvae can consume and metabolize plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene, PVC, and PLA, thereby reducing waste volume. Research has also examined oxidative degradation of polystyrene in superworms and the role of gut-associated bacteria in *Tenebrio molitor*, highlighting the importance of microbial symbionts in facilitating insect-driven plastic degradation.  

A significant component of Zhang, Y.’s work involves investigating the mechanisms and influencing factors of biodegradation. Studies have analyzed how polymer size and physical properties affect the efficiency of polystyrene breakdown, as well as the environmental safety of insect-mediated degradation. This includes assessments of residual microplastics and potential toxicity following digestion by mealworms, reflecting a broader concern with ecological impacts and sustainability.  

Beyond plastics, Zhang, Y. has contributed to related environmental research. This includes studies on the reduction of lignocellulosic crop waste using mealworms, the adsorption of pharmaceutical contaminants such as levofloxacin hydrochloride by microplastics, and the presence and effects of microplastics in agricultural soils. Zhang, Y. has also co-authored work on sustainable material alternatives, such as molded fiber and pulp products, and on enhancing bioenergy production with carbon capture using microalgae, where they are listed as a co-author on a 2022 study.  

Collectively, Zhang, Y.’s research portfolio integrates microbial and insect-based approaches to plastic degradation, evaluates both efficiency and environmental safety, and extends to broader issues of waste reduction, resource recovery, and sustainable alternatives. Through these contributions, Zhang, Y. is recognized as a significant figure in advancing scientific understanding of plastic biodegradation and in developing innovative, ecologically responsible strategies to mitigate plastic pollution and promote environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang, Y. is an author of studies on plastic biodegradation and gut microbiota in insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang, Y. is listed as an author in multiple studies, including bioenergy production with microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots, and other research works.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang, Y. is also listed as an author of a study on mixture effects between emerging organic contaminants on cytotoxicity.</data>
