<node id="Tribolium castaneum">
  <data key="d0">Tribolium castaneum</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">**Tribolium castaneum Summary**  

Tribolium castaneum, commonly known as the red flour beetle, is a species of darkling beetle traditionally recognized as a major stored-product pest but increasingly studied for its ecological and biotechnological significance. Beyond its role in infesting and damaging stored grains and flour, this beetle has attracted scientific attention for its interactions with synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene and its expanded form, Styrofoam. Both larvae and adults of Tribolium castaneum have been observed chewing and damaging plastic materials, with some studies indicating that the species can survive on diets composed largely of plastics.  

A key discovery in this context is the role of the beetle’s gut microbiota in plastic degradation. The larvae of Tribolium castaneum host microbial communities, including strains of *Acinetobacter*, that are capable of breaking down polystyrene. The isolation of polystyrene-degrading *Acinetobacter* from the larvae provides evidence that the beetle’s biodegradation potential is not limited to mechanical fragmentation but also involves microbiological processes. This symbiotic relationship highlights the importance of gut microbes in enabling the insect to metabolize otherwise persistent plastic waste.  

Tribolium castaneum has also been hypothesized to share biodegradation mechanisms with other beetle species, such as *Tenebrio molitor*, which are known for their ability to degrade polyethylene terephthalate (PET). This suggests that the red flour beetle may contribute to broader insights into insect-mediated plastic biodegradation pathways.  

In addition to its relevance in biodegradation research, Tribolium castaneum has been studied for its life cycle, weight variations, and potential as an edible insect within sustainable food systems. Its dual significance as both a pest and a potential contributor to plastic waste management underscores its complex ecological role. The species is now considered a promising model organism for exploring biotechnological approaches to plastic packaging management, with particular emphasis on the synergistic role of its larvae and associated gut microbiota in degrading polystyrene.  

In summary, Tribolium castaneum is a darkling beetle species notable not only for its pest status but also for its emerging importance in studies of plastic biodegradation. Its ability to damage and metabolize plastics, coupled with the presence of polystyrene-degrading *Acinetobacter* in its larvae, positions it as a valuable subject for research into sustainable solutions for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Species of insect larvae within the darkling beetle family found to biodegrade plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium castaneum is an insect larva mentioned as capable of feeding on and degrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium castaneum is a beetle species whose larvae were found to host polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium castaneum is a species of darkling beetle confirmed to biodegrade PS plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium castaneum is an insect species from which Acinetobacter sp. AnTc-1 was isolated.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae from which a polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated.</data>
