<node id="Yang, J.">
  <data key="d0">Yang, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang, J. is a researcher and scientific author recognized for pioneering contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of insects and their associated microorganisms in breaking down synthetic polymers. A central focus of Yang, J.’s work has been the study of biological systems capable of degrading environmentally persistent plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene, which are among the most widely used and problematic contributors to global plastic waste.  

One of Yang, J.’s most notable achievements was the discovery and characterization of bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms that can biodegrade polyethylene. This research, including a landmark 2014 study, provided some of the earliest evidence that insect-associated microorganisms could facilitate the decomposition of plastics that are otherwise highly resistant to natural degradation. The findings demonstrated the critical role of gut microbiota in enabling polymer breakdown and opened new avenues for exploring insect–microbe interactions as potential biological solutions to plastic pollution.  

In addition to polyethylene, Yang, J. has co-authored influential studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae). Publications in journals such as *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* (2015) and *Chemosphere* (2018) showed that mealworms are capable of ingesting and metabolizing polystyrene, leading to partial biodegradation and mineralization of the plastic. These studies highlighted the importance of host–microbe interactions in the process and provided a biological foundation for considering insects as a tool in managing plastic waste streams. Yang, J. has also contributed to research on superworms, further broadening the scope of insect-based plastic degradation studies.  

Beyond organism-level investigations, Yang, J. has advanced molecular-level understanding of microbial mechanisms involved in plastic breakdown. This includes genome sequencing of *Bacillus* sp. YP1, a bacterial strain associated with polyethylene degradation, which provided insights into the genetic and enzymatic pathways underlying microbial plastic biodegradation.  

Yang, J.’s contributions extend beyond experimental research to include broader analyses of the scientific field. For example, a 2023 bibliometric study co-authored by Yang, J. mapped the development, trends, and research dynamics of plastic biodegradation studies, reflecting an engagement with both knowledge synthesis and community-level scientific progress. Additionally, Yang, J. has co-authored studies outside the plastic biodegradation domain, such as a 2022 investigation into *Enterobacter ludwigii* and its role in colitis protection, demonstrating a broader interest in microbiology and host–microbe interactions.  

Through this body of work, Yang, J. has significantly advanced scientific understanding of the ecological and biotechnological implications of insect- and microbe-mediated plastic degradation. Collectively, these contributions underscore both the natural capacity of biological systems to mitigate plastic accumulation and the potential for developing sustainable waste management strategies based on these mechanisms. Yang, J.’s publications have established a foundation for future innovations in biotechnological approaches to addressing the global challenge of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of a study on polystyrene biodegradation by bacterial strains from waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, J. is one of the authors of a 2018 study (details truncated in text).&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, J. is an author of a study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
