<node id="Yang, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Yang, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang, Y. is a researcher and author recognized for pioneering contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of insects and their associated microorganisms in breaking down persistent synthetic polymers such as polyethylene and polystyrene. Yang, Y.’s research has significantly advanced scientific understanding of how insect hosts and their gut microbiota contribute to mitigating plastic pollution through natural degradation and mineralization processes.  

A landmark achievement occurred in 2014, when Yang, Y. co-authored a groundbreaking study that provided the first clear evidence of insect-associated microbial plastic degradation. This work demonstrated that bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms were capable of degrading polyethylene. Complementing this discovery, Yang, Y. contributed to the genome sequencing of *Bacillus* sp. YP1, a bacterium implicated in polyethylene degradation, which revealed important genetic and metabolic mechanisms underlying microbial plastic breakdown.  

Building on these findings, Yang, Y. co-authored several influential studies in 2015 that established mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) as a model system for insect-mediated plastic degradation. A publication in *Chemosphere* reported that mealworms could ingest and metabolize polystyrene, converting it into simpler compounds, while a complementary study in *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* reinforced these results and highlighted the ecological and environmental significance of insect-based biodegradation pathways. These studies, which demonstrated both biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, positioned Yang, Y. as a leading contributor to the emerging field of insect-driven plastic degradation.  

Yang, Y. has continued to expand this research to other insect species. In 2021, Yang, Y. co-authored a study showing that superworms (*Zophobas atratus*) are capable of biodegrading foam plastics, thereby broadening the range of insect taxa known to contribute to plastic degradation. In 2022, Yang, Y. co-authored a study investigating nitrogen fixation and diazotrophic microbial communities in plastic-eating mealworms, providing new insights into the ecological and metabolic processes that enable insects to survive on plastic-based diets.  

Beyond insect-focused studies, Yang, Y. has contributed to broader research on microbial degradation and valorization of plastic wastes. A 2020 study emphasized the potential of microbial communities in sustainable plastic waste management, underscoring the complementary roles of insects and microorganisms in developing biologically driven strategies to address global plastic pollution. Yang, Y. has also authored a review specifically on polyethylene biodegradation, consolidating knowledge on microbial and insect-mediated pathways for breaking down this widely used polymer.  

Overall, Yang, Y.’s body of work highlights the promise of integrating entomological and microbiological approaches to develop innovative and sustainable solutions for plastic waste management. By documenting the plastic-degrading capabilities of waxworms, mealworms, and superworms, along with their microbial symbionts, Yang, Y. has provided critical insights into the mechanisms of plastic biodegradation and has contributed significantly to advancing environmentally friendly strategies for addressing one of the most pressing environmental challenges of the modern era.</data>
