<node id="Zhao, J.">
  <data key="d0">Zhao, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhao, J. is a researcher specializing in environmental biotechnology and ecotoxicology, with a primary focus on plastic biodegradation and the ecological impacts of microplastic pollution. Zhao, J.’s work has been central to advancing scientific understanding of how biological systems, particularly insects and their associated microbial communities, can contribute to the breakdown of persistent synthetic polymers such as polyethylene and polystyrene.  

A major theme in Zhao, J.’s research is the role of insect-associated bacteria in facilitating plastic degradation. In 2014, Zhao, J. co-authored a pioneering study that provided some of the earliest evidence of polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms. This was followed by a 2015 study published in *Environmental Science &amp; Technology*, which demonstrated that mealworms and their gut microbes could biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene. These findings were groundbreaking in showing that insects and their symbiotic microorganisms are capable of transforming plastics into simpler compounds, thereby offering a novel biological pathway for addressing plastic waste.  

Building on these foundational studies, Zhao, J. has co-authored multiple investigations into the efficiency, mechanisms, and ecological implications of insect- and microbe-mediated plastic degradation. This includes genome sequencing of Bacillus sp. YP1, a bacterium implicated in polyethylene degradation, and research on the biodegradation of graphene oxide by Tenebrio molitor larvae. Zhao, J. has also contributed to studies examining the growth performance and nutrient composition of mealworms consuming plastics, further expanding knowledge of insect-plastic interactions.  

Beyond biodegradation, Zhao, J. has investigated the environmental risks associated with microplastic contamination. This includes co-authorship of a study on oysters exposed to microplastics, which assessed oxidative stress biomarkers and physiological responses, demonstrating that microplastic exposure can impair marine organism health. Zhao, J. has also co-authored research on the adsorption behavior of oxytetracycline on polylactic acid microplastics, highlighting the potential for microplastics to act as vectors for pollutants in aquatic environments.  

Taken together, Zhao, J.’s research integrates two complementary dimensions of plastic pollution science: (1) the development of biologically based strategies for plastic waste management through insect- and microbe-mediated degradation, and (2) the assessment of toxicological and ecological risks posed by microplastic contamination in aquatic ecosystems. Through these contributions, Zhao, J. has significantly advanced scientific understanding of both potential solutions to the persistence of plastics in the environment and the broader ecological consequences of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
