<node id="Jiang, L.">
  <data key="d0">Jiang, L.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jiang, L. is a researcher known for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insects and their associated microbial communities in breaking down persistent synthetic polymers. In 2014, Jiang, L. co-authored a pioneering study that examined the biodegradation of polyethylene by bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms, insects recognized for their ability to consume and degrade plastic materials. This research provided some of the earliest evidence that insect-associated microbiota could facilitate the breakdown of polyethylene, a plastic that is otherwise highly resistant to natural degradation processes.  

Building on this foundation, Jiang, L. co-authored several subsequent studies investigating the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms. A notable 2015 publication in *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* demonstrated that mealworms are capable of ingesting and metabolizing polystyrene, with their gut microbiota playing a central role in the degradation process. These findings offered critical insights into the biological mechanisms underlying polystyrene breakdown and highlighted the potential of insect-microbe systems as natural solutions to the growing problem of plastic pollution. Jiang, L.’s work in this area has been widely referenced, particularly in studies emphasizing the capacity of mealworms to degrade and mineralize polystyrene.  

Beyond plastics research, Jiang, L. has also contributed to studies on soil bacterial communities, including investigations into microbial responses to wildfire disturbances. This broader line of inquiry reflects a consistent research interest in microbial ecology and its environmental applications, extending Jiang, L.’s expertise beyond plastic degradation to encompass wider ecological processes.  

Through this body of work, Jiang, L. has advanced scientific understanding of how insects and their symbiotic bacteria can contribute to the degradation of plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene. The research underscores the potential of leveraging natural biological systems to address the environmental challenges posed by the accumulation of synthetic polymers, offering promising directions for sustainable waste management and pollution reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Jiang, L. is a co-author of the study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on polystyrene biodegradation by bacterial strains from waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Jiang, L. is an author of a study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
