<node id="Polyethylene">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene is a synthetic polymer and one of the most widely manufactured plastics in the world, valued for its durability, versatility, and resistance to natural degradation. It exists in multiple forms, including high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and is extensively used in packaging, construction, and consumer products. These same properties that make polyethylene indispensable in industry also contribute to its persistence in the environment, making it a major source of plastic pollution and a central focus of research into sustainable waste management.  

Scientific studies have increasingly explored the biodegradation of polyethylene, examining both physicochemical ageing processes and biological pathways. A wide range of organisms, including insects, fungi, and bacteria, have demonstrated the ability to degrade or metabolize polyethylene under certain conditions. Insects such as waxworms (*Galleria mellonella*), mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*) have been shown to ingest and partially metabolize polyethylene. This process is often mediated by their gut microbiota, with bacterial symbionts such as *Enterobacter* sp. d1 and *Bacillus* sp. YP1 contributing to depolymerization and metabolic breakdown. Notably, *Galleria mellonella* has exhibited the capacity to metabolize polyethylene even when its gut microbiome is suppressed, suggesting that both host physiology and microbial partners play roles in the degradation process. Experimental findings also indicate that the form of polyethylene—such as oxidized (PE-oxo) or regranulated (PE-reg)—and the insects’ diet can influence degradation efficiency and larval development.  

Beyond insects, fungi and bacteria have been identified as important agents in polyethylene degradation. Fungal species such as *Alternaria alternata* and *Penicillium simplicissimum* can attack polyethylene surfaces, while bacterial strains including *Rhodococcus ruber* are capable of colonizing polyethylene, forming biofilms, and inducing structural and chemical changes. Other bacterial isolates, such as strain PELW2042, as well as soil bacteria, recombinant cells, and bacterial co-cultures, have been reported to reduce polyethylene’s molecular weight and promote oxidative or enzymatic modifications. Marine microbes have also been studied for their potential role in polyethylene biodegradation, expanding the scope of biological systems under investigation.  

The ecological implications of polyethylene ingestion have been studied in parallel with degradation research. For example, experiments with yellow mealworms have examined not only their ability to digest polyethylene but also the downstream effects on food webs, such as potential risks to fish that consume these insects. Such findings highlight both the promise and the complexity of biological degradation pathways, as they may mitigate plastic pollution while simultaneously introducing new ecological considerations.  

In summary, polyethylene is a critical industrial material whose environmental persistence has spurred extensive research into biodegradation. Studies involving insects, fungi, and bacteria—including waxworms, mealworms, superworms, *Penicillium simplicissimum*, *Alternaria alternata*, and *Rhodococcus ruber*—have demonstrated diverse mechanisms of polyethylene breakdown, ranging from microbial biofilm activity to insect–microbe symbioses. These discoveries provide valuable insights into potential biologically based strategies for addressing plastic pollution, underscoring the importance of integrating ecological, microbiological, and material science perspectives in the pursuit of sustainable polyethylene waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic studied for biodegradation by fungi, bacteria, and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic studied for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is a type of plastic studied for biodegradation by greater wax moth larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is a plastic material that affects the microbiota composition in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene is a type of polymer waste tested in the study for its effects on T. molitor larvae microbiome and enzymatic activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
