<node id="Polypropylene">
  <data key="d0">Polypropylene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polypropylene is a widely used synthetic polymer and type of plastic that has been extensively studied for its structural, physical, and environmental properties. Classified as a vinyl plastic, it is chemically similar to polyethylene and is characterized by high durability, resistance to microbial breakdown, and persistence in the environment. These qualities have made polypropylene valuable for industrial and consumer applications, such as packaging films, but they have also contributed to concerns about plastic waste accumulation and long-term environmental impact.  

Scientific research on polypropylene has focused on both its material properties and its potential for biodegradation. From a materials science perspective, studies have examined its isotactic structure, crystallinity, and thermal behavior, including how radiation exposure influences its melting properties and structural stability. These investigations provide important insights into the polymer’s performance under different environmental and experimental conditions.  

In parallel, polypropylene has been the subject of biodegradation research aimed at addressing plastic pollution. Traditionally considered resistant to microbial decomposition, it has more recently been shown to undergo partial biodegradation by certain biological agents. Notably, insect larvae such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*) have demonstrated the ability to consume and metabolize polypropylene, with evidence suggesting that their gut microbiomes play a role in breaking down the polymer. In addition to insects, studies have also explored the potential of mesophilic bacteria and other microorganisms to degrade polypropylene, expanding the scope of biological pathways for managing this persistent plastic.  

Polypropylene has also been investigated in the context of packaging materials, where its resistance to insect penetration is an important factor in product protection. At the same time, its vulnerability to partial degradation by insects highlights both challenges and opportunities in balancing material performance with environmental sustainability.  

Overall, polypropylene is a versatile and durable plastic polymer that remains central to industrial applications while also serving as a focal point for biodegradation research. Its resistance to natural degradation underscores the urgency of developing effective waste management strategies, while emerging findings on insect- and microbe-mediated breakdown suggest promising avenues for mitigating its environmental impact.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of plastic studied for biodegradation in yellow mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene is a type of plastic studied for its biodegradation by yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802</data>
