<node id="Waymouth, R.M.">
  <data key="d0">Waymouth, R.M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Waymouth, R.M. is a researcher recognized for co-authoring studies on the biodegradation of plastics, with a particular focus on polystyrene waste. His work has centered on the role of yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) in the digestion and breakdown of polystyrene, a persistent plastic pollutant. In 2018, Waymouth, R.M. co-authored a study that investigated the capacity of mealworms to ingest and metabolize polystyrene, providing evidence for the biodegradation of this material within their digestive systems. This research contributed to the growing body of knowledge on biological approaches to plastic waste management, highlighting the potential of insect-mediated processes as a means of addressing environmental challenges posed by synthetic polymers. Through these studies, Waymouth, R.M. has been identified as an author advancing the understanding of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation in yellow mealworms, underscoring the significance of biological systems in exploring sustainable solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the polystyrene biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;Waymouth, R.M. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
