<node id="Wang, H.">
  <data key="d0">Wang, H.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, H. is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the environmental impacts of plastics and their interactions with biological systems. He has contributed extensively to studies on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene, by insects such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms. His research has demonstrated the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in yellow mealworms, providing evidence that these organisms can ingest and metabolize plastic materials, thereby offering potential biological solutions to plastic waste management. In addition, Wang, H. has investigated the ingestion preferences of both superworms and *Tenebrio molitor*, furthering understanding of how different insect species interact with and process synthetic polymers.  

Beyond insect-mediated biodegradation, Wang, H. has also examined the toxicological effects of plastics and associated chemicals on aquatic organisms. His work includes studies on the impacts of polystyrene microplastics and phthalates on zebrafish, highlighting the ecological and health risks posed by plastic pollutants in aquatic environments. Furthermore, he has contributed to research on micro- and nanoplastics as pollutant vectors, emphasizing their role in transporting harmful substances through ecosystems and potentially into food chains.  

Overall, Wang, H.’s body of work integrates perspectives from biodegradation, toxicology, and environmental pollution, with a particular emphasis on polystyrene and micro(nano)plastics. His research contributes to advancing knowledge on both the risks of plastic pollution and the potential for biological organisms to mitigate its environmental burden.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the polystyrene biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a 2018 study on ubiquity of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, H. is an author of a study on gut microbiome changes due to microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, H. is a co-author of a study on aging behavior of microplastics accelerated by oxidation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on plastic ingestion preferences of superworm and yellow mealworm.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
