<node id="Li, P.">
  <data key="d0">Li, P.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Li, P. is a researcher and co-author recognized for contributions to studies on plastic biodegradation and environmental toxicology. Li, P. has been prominently involved in research examining the ability of yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) to digest and biodegrade polystyrene, a common and persistent plastic pollutant. This work, including a 2018 study, highlighted the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in mealworms and provided important insights into the potential of biological systems to mitigate plastic waste. In addition to research on plastic biodegradation, Li, P. has also co-authored studies investigating the environmental and biological impacts of chemical pollutants, specifically focusing on the phytotoxicity induced by perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) and perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS). These studies contribute to a broader understanding of how synthetic materials and pollutants interact with living organisms and ecosystems. Through this body of work, Li, P. has advanced knowledge in the fields of environmental science, biodegradation, and ecotoxicology, with particular emphasis on the biological processing of plastics and the toxicological effects of persistent organic pollutants.</data>
