<node id="Xing, D.">
  <data key="d0">Xing, D.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xing, D. is a researcher whose work is centered on the biodegradation of plastics, with a particular focus on the role of insect larvae in breaking down synthetic polymers. Through a series of studies, Xing, D. has contributed to advancing scientific understanding of how species such as yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*) are capable of consuming and metabolizing plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene. This research highlights the potential of insect larvae as biological agents for mitigating plastic pollution and developing sustainable waste management strategies.  

A significant portion of Xing, D.’s work has focused on yellow mealworms and their ability to ingest and degrade polystyrene. As a co-author of a 2018 study, Xing, D. helped document the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion across mealworm populations, demonstrating that this capacity is not limited to isolated cases but is widespread. These studies provided evidence that the degradation process involves both physical fragmentation of plastics and biochemical contributions from gut microbial and enzymatic activity.  

In addition to polystyrene, Xing, D. has also investigated polyethylene biodegradation. This includes research on the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae during polyethylene consumption, where microbial communities were shown to play a role in carbon and nitrogen metabolism. Such findings shed light on the metabolic pathways and microbial interactions that enable larvae to process plastics, offering mechanistic insights into biodegradation.  

Xing, D. has further extended this line of inquiry to wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*), co-authoring a 2020 study that demonstrated their ability to biodegrade both polyethylene and polystyrene. This comparative research across insect species underscores the broader potential of insect larvae as a biological solution to plastic waste.  

More recently, Xing, D. co-authored a 2021 study examining the gut microbiome response of *Tenebrio molitor* to diet shifts during plastic biodegradation. This work emphasized the adaptability of microbial communities in the insect gut and their role in facilitating plastic digestion under varying dietary conditions.  

Taken together, Xing, D.’s research contributions span experimental demonstrations of plastic consumption by insect larvae, comparative analyses across species, and microbiome-focused studies that elucidate the metabolic and microbial mechanisms underlying biodegradation. This integrated body of work positions Xing, D. as an active and influential contributor to the emerging field of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, with important implications for addressing global plastic waste challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on gut microbiome response to diet shifts during plastic biodegradation.</data>
