<node id="Picard, C.J.">
  <data key="d0">Picard, C.J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Picard, C.J. is a researcher recognized for contributions to studies on the biodegradation of plastics by insects, particularly the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*). Picard, C.J. is listed as a co-author of a 2018 study that investigated the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms, providing evidence that these larvae are capable of breaking down polystyrene, a common and persistent plastic. This research highlighted the potential of mealworms as a biological agent for mitigating plastic waste. In addition to this work, Picard, C.J. is also credited as an author of research related to the yellow mealworm genome, which supports broader scientific efforts to understand the genetic and biological mechanisms underlying the species’ ability to process synthetic polymers. Through these contributions, Picard, C.J. has played a role in advancing knowledge on insect-mediated plastic degradation and the genomic foundations that may enable such processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Picard, C.J. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f</data>
