</node>
<node id="He, L.">
  <data key="d0">He, L.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">He, L. is a researcher and author whose scholarly work is centered on environmental science, biodegradation, and sustainable waste management. A significant focus of He, L.’s research is the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly polypropylene and polystyrene terephthalate, through the use of insect larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and *Zophobas atratus* (superworm). By investigating the capacity of these organisms to consume and break down plastics, He, L. has contributed to advancing biological solutions for mitigating plastic pollution. This line of research highlights the potential of insect-mediated processes as innovative strategies to address the global challenge of plastic waste.  

Beyond plastics, He, L. has also explored broader sustainability issues, including the valorization of agricultural residues. Research co-authored by He, L. has examined the use of *Tenebrio molitor* to reduce lignocellulosic crop waste, aligning with circular economy principles that emphasize resource recovery and repurposing of byproducts. This demonstrates a broader commitment to developing environmentally sustainable approaches that integrate waste reduction with ecological resilience.  

In addition to experimental studies, He, L. has contributed to bibliometric analyses, including a 2023 study that mapped global research trends in the biodegradation of plastics. Such work provides valuable insights into the evolution of the field, identifying key themes, research gaps, and emerging directions in plastic biodegradation studies.  

He, L.’s research interests also extend to environmental chemistry, as evidenced by co-authored work on chloride ions in wastewater, which reflects engagement with water quality and pollution control.  

Overall, He, L.’s body of work spans experimental, analytical, and applied research in biodegradation, waste management, and environmental sustainability. By combining investigations into insect-based plastic degradation, agricultural waste utilization, and bibliometric assessments of scientific progress, He, L. has established a multidisciplinary profile that contributes to advancing sustainable solutions for pressing ecological challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of a study on biodegradation of polypropylene by yellow mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of 2021 study on biodegradation of polypropylene by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the polypropylene biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;He, L. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of polypropylene by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
