<node id="Xing, D.F.">
  <data key="d0">Xing, D.F.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xing, D.F. is a researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insects such as yellow mealworms and superworms. Xing has co-authored multiple studies investigating the degradation of synthetic polymers, including polypropylene and polyethylene, by these insects. This body of work explores both the direct biodegradation of plastics and the microbial processes occurring within the insect gut that facilitate this breakdown.  

In 2021, Xing, D.F. contributed to a study examining the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms and its role in enabling plastic biodegradation, highlighting the importance of microbial communities in the digestive systems of insects for processing otherwise persistent synthetic materials. Xing’s research has consistently emphasized the potential of insect-based systems as a biological solution to plastic waste management, with studies demonstrating that mealworms and superworms can consume and degrade common plastics such as polypropylene and polyethylene.  

Through these investigations, Xing, D.F. has helped advance understanding of insect-mediated biodegradation pathways, the ecological and microbiological mechanisms underlying plastic decomposition, and the potential applications of these processes in addressing global plastic pollution. Overall, Xing’s work contributes to the growing field of sustainable waste management and environmental biotechnology by exploring innovative biological strategies for mitigating plastic accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation of polypropylene and polyethylene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Xing, D.F. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene by Galleria mellonella larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the 2020 study on biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene by greater wax moth larva.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of multiple biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Xing, D.F. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms and superworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
