</node>
<node id="Zhao, L.">
  <data key="d0">Zhao, L.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhao, L. is a researcher and co-author whose work is centered on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers and organic waste through insect-mediated processes. A significant focus of Zhao, L.’s research has been on the ability of insect larvae, particularly yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms, to degrade persistent plastics such as polypropylene, polyethylene, polystyrene, and low-density polyethylene. These studies have demonstrated that larvae can consume and metabolize otherwise recalcitrant polymers, highlighting the potential of insect-based systems as a sustainable approach to reducing plastic waste.  

Beyond plastics, Zhao, L. has also contributed to research on the bioconversion of lignocellulosic crop residues using *Tenebrio molitor*, thereby extending the application of insect larvae to broader waste management challenges. This work underscores the versatility of insect-mediated degradation in addressing both synthetic and organic waste streams.  

Another important dimension of Zhao, L.’s research involves the gut microbiome of mealworms, with particular attention to microbial associations involved in carbon and nitrogen metabolism during polyethylene degradation. By examining the microbial communities that support polymer breakdown, Zhao, L. has provided insights into the biological and biochemical mechanisms underlying insect-mediated biodegradation. This microbiome-focused research not only advances understanding of how larvae process plastics but also informs potential strategies for developing scalable, biologically inspired waste treatment technologies.  

Overall, Zhao, L. is recognized for significant contributions to the emerging field of insect-based biodegradation. Through studies spanning polymer degradation, crop residue reduction, and gut microbiome analysis, Zhao, L. has advanced knowledge of how insect larvae and their associated microbes can be harnessed to address pressing environmental challenges in waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene in dark- versus yellow-mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of multiple biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhao, L. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation of polypropylene by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhao, L. is a co-author of a study on confirmation of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
