<node id="GPC Analysis">
  <data key="d0">GPC Analysis</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
  <data key="d2">**GPC Analysis Summary**

GPC Analysis, short for Gel Permeation Chromatography analysis, is an established analytical technique primarily used to determine the molecular weight and molecular weight distribution of polymers. Within the field of biodegradation and depolymerization research, GPC analysis plays a central role in characterizing the structural changes that occur when synthetic polymers are subjected to biological processes, particularly digestion by insect larvae such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and darkling beetle larvae (Tenebrio obscurus).  

The technique has been widely applied to study plastics including expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polystyrene (PS) foams, polyurethane (PU), and mask materials. By comparing molecular weight distributions before and after polymer ingestion, GPC analysis provides direct evidence of depolymerization and biodegradation. For example, in studies of PS degradation, GPC analysis has revealed significant reductions in molecular weight and shifts in distribution profiles after passage through larval digestive systems, confirming that gut activity contributes to the breakdown of otherwise persistent plastics.  

Beyond intact polymer samples, GPC analysis has also been employed to examine residual polymers extracted from frass (larval excrement). This application enables researchers to trace the fate of polymer fragments and verify whether molecular weight reduction occurs during digestion. In some experiments, GPC analysis was conducted at the conclusion of extended feeding trials, such as 31-day tests, to assess the cumulative extent of depolymerization. It has also been used in studies involving antibiotic-treated larvae, where analysis of frass provided insights into the role of gut microbiota in polymer degradation.  

Overall, GPC Analysis serves as a critical tool for confirming polymer depolymerization and biodegradation in biological systems. Its quantitative assessment of molecular weight reduction and distribution shifts provides robust evidence of structural changes in plastics during larval digestion. By enabling detailed characterization of polymer breakdown, GPC analysis contributes significantly to understanding the potential of insect larvae and their associated microbiota in addressing plastic waste degradation and advancing sustainable recycling strategies.</data>
