<node id="Expanded polystyrene">
  <data key="d0">Expanded polystyrene</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, foam form of polystyrene that has been the subject of multiple studies investigating its potential for biodegradation. As a widely used plastic material, EPS poses significant environmental challenges due to its persistence and resistance to natural decomposition. Research has explored the capacity of various organisms to break down EPS, with particular focus on invertebrates such as land snails and insect larvae. For example, the land snail *Achatina fulica* has been studied for its ability to consume and degrade EPS. More extensively, larvae of the darkling beetle *Tenebrio molitor*, commonly known as mealworms, have been shown to ingest and biodegrade EPS foam.  

In controlled experiments, EPS has been provided as the sole nutrient source for mealworm larvae, demonstrating that these organisms can survive on the material while contributing to its breakdown. Such studies have also revealed that different feeding strategies influence the efficiency of EPS degradation by mealworms. Furthermore, investigations into the gut microbiota of these larvae have led to the isolation of bacteria capable of metabolizing EPS, suggesting that microbial activity plays a central role in the biodegradation process.  

Overall, expanded polystyrene is recognized as a form of polystyrene plastic that, despite its environmental persistence, can undergo partial biodegradation through biological interactions with certain organisms, particularly mealworm larvae and their associated gut microbes. These findings highlight the potential for biological approaches to address the environmental burden of EPS waste.&lt;SEP&gt;A plastic material studied for biodegradation by mealworm larvae under different feeding strategies and conditions.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387469</data>
</node>
