<node id="Yang, L.">
  <data key="d0">Yang, L.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-352650a946ab941cec3f7b9813faeee1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang, L. is a researcher whose work is primarily focused on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly expanded polystyrene (EPS) and polyethylene (PE), through biological processes involving both insects and microorganisms. A central theme of Yang, L.’s research has been the role of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (yellow mealworms) in breaking down plastic materials. Between 2018 and 2021, Yang, L. co-authored multiple studies that systematically investigated the ability of mealworm larvae to ingest and degrade EPS and low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foams. These studies demonstrated that insect larvae can metabolize plastics, thereby contributing to a growing body of evidence supporting insect-mediated biodegradation as a potential strategy for sustainable waste management and environmental remediation.  

Among these contributions, Yang, L. co-authored a 2021 article in *Chemosphere* that provided detailed insights into the mechanisms and efficiency of EPS and PE foam degradation by *T. molitor* larvae. Earlier work in 2020 reinforced these findings by examining degradation under varied experimental conditions, highlighting the robustness of the biological processes involved. In addition to experimental studies, Yang, L. has also contributed to a review on the biodegradation of macro- and microplastics, reflecting an interest in synthesizing knowledge across different scales of plastic pollution.  

Beyond insect-based systems, Yang, L. has explored microbial pathways for plastic degradation. This includes research on *Klebsiella pneumoniae* strain Mk-1, a soil-isolated bacterium capable of degrading polyethylene. This line of work underscores Yang, L.’s broader interest in identifying and characterizing diverse biological agents that can contribute to mitigating the environmental challenges posed by persistent plastic waste.  

In addition to research on plastic biodegradation, Yang, L. has co-authored studies in the field of bioenergy. Notably, in 2022, Yang, L. contributed to a study on enhancing bioenergy production and carbon capture in microalgae using ultraviolet spectrum conversion facilitated by graphene oxide quantum dots. This work highlights an interdisciplinary dimension of Yang, L.’s research, extending beyond waste degradation to encompass renewable energy and carbon management technologies.  

Overall, Yang, L. has established a research profile centered on the biodegradation of plastics, with a particular emphasis on insect- and microbe-mediated processes, while also contributing to advancements in bioenergy and carbon capture. This body of work has advanced scientific understanding of biological plastic degradation and its potential applications in addressing plastic pollution, while also engaging with broader sustainability challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, L. is an author of a study on bioenergy production with carbon capture of microalgae.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang, L. is one of the authors of a study on enhancing bioenergy production with carbon capture of microalgae using ultraviolet spectrum conversion via graphene oxide quantum dots.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387716</data>
</node>
<node id="Bo-Yu Peng">
  <data key="d0">Bo-Yu Peng</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bo-Yu Peng is a researcher affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, College of Environmental Science and Engineering at Tongji University in Shanghai, China. His work primarily focuses on the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly the degradation of microplastics and synthetic polymers by insect larvae. He has contributed as an author to multiple studies investigating the capacity of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) to degrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene, polystyrene, and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). These studies explore the digestion and biodegradation processes within yellow mealworms, highlighting their potential role in mitigating plastic pollution.  

In his research contributions, Bo-Yu Peng has been involved in several key aspects of the scientific process, including conceptualization, methodology design, formal analysis, experimental investigation, and the preparation of original manuscripts. His work has advanced understanding of how biological systems, particularly insect larvae, can contribute to the breakdown of persistent plastic pollutants, offering insights into sustainable waste management and environmental remediation strategies.  

Through his authorship of multiple publications on plastic biodegradation in mealworms, Bo-Yu Peng has established himself as an active contributor to the field of environmental science, with a focus on innovative biological approaches to addressing global plastic pollution challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Bo-Yu Peng is one of the authors of the study on polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Author affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, Tongji University.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
