<node id="Ying Sun">
  <data key="d0">Ying Sun</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ying Sun is a researcher affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, within the College of Environmental Science and Engineering at Tongji University in Shanghai, China. Ying Sun has contributed significantly to scientific studies focused on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly microplastics, through biological processes involving mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor). As an author of multiple research papers in this field, Ying Sun has been involved in investigations examining the capacity of mealworms to degrade different types of plastics, including polyethylene and polystyrene. These studies explore the potential of insect-mediated biodegradation as a sustainable approach to addressing plastic pollution. Within this body of work, Ying Sun’s contributions include formal analysis and validation, ensuring the accuracy and reliability of the research findings. Through these efforts, Ying Sun has played an important role in advancing knowledge on innovative methods for mitigating the environmental impacts of plastic waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
