<node id="Mealworm larvae">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor), the larval stage of beetles in the family Tenebrionidae, are widely studied for their unique ability to ingest and partially biodegrade synthetic polymers, particularly polyethylene (PE) microplastics and expanded polystyrene, as well as biodegradable plastics such as polylactic acid (PLA) and natural substrates like cellulose. They have become a model organism in experimental research aimed at understanding biological plastic degradation, with studies focusing on their feeding behavior, physiological responses, and the role of gut microbiota in breaking down recalcitrant materials.  

In controlled laboratory experiments, mealworm larvae are typically provided with diets consisting of pure plastics, plastics supplemented with nutritional additives such as wheat bran, or bran alone. These dietary variations allow researchers to measure plastic consumption rates, weight changes, survival outcomes, and biochemical responses. Findings consistently show that while larvae are capable of consuming and fragmenting plastics, the process imposes significant physiological costs. When fed exclusively on polyethylene or polystyrene, larvae experience oxidative stress, lipid peroxidation, weight loss, reduced biomass, and lower survival rates. Supplementation with bran improves survival and biomass retention, but it can also influence the efficiency of plastic degradation, highlighting a trade-off between nutritional support and biodegradation performance.  

The ability of mealworm larvae to process plastics is closely linked to their multi-stage digestive system and the activity of gut microbial communities. These microbes are thought to play a central role in depolymerizing and metabolizing synthetic polymers, enabling the larvae to derive limited energy from otherwise resistant materials. However, the physiological stress associated with plastic ingestion underscores the limitations of relying solely on larvae for large-scale biodegradation.  

Overall, mealworm larvae are recognized as a promising biological model for investigating the mechanisms and potential applications of plastic biodegradation. Their demonstrated capacity to ingest and partially degrade plastics provides valuable insights into possible biotechnological and ecological strategies for mitigating plastic pollution. At the same time, research emphasizes the importance of balancing biodegradation efficiency with the health and survival of the organisms, as plastic ingestion is not without detrimental effects on larval physiology.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade expanded polystyrene under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are capable of digesting synthetic and natural waste materials such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and cellulose through a multi-stage digestive process involving gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are organisms studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene, polyethylene, and other plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
