<node id="Zhang">
  <data key="d0">Zhang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang is a researcher and author whose work spans multiple studies on plastic degradation, food safety, and the biological impacts of micro- and nanoplastics. Zhang has been cited in research from 2010 through 2022, contributing to the understanding of how various organisms and environmental factors interact with plastics.  

In early work, Zhang was involved in a 2010 study examining the ability of superworms to chew and consume Styrofoam, an important contribution to the field of plastic biodegradation. This line of inquiry continued in subsequent years, with Zhang cited in a 2017 study investigating the interactions between mealworms and microplastics, further advancing knowledge of insect-mediated plastic degradation. Zhang’s research also extended to Zophobas morio (superworms), where studies demonstrated their capacity to degrade Styrofoam, highlighting the potential of insect larvae as biological agents for plastic waste management.  

Zhang’s contributions include methodological developments, such as protocols for treating larvae with alcohol and sterile water prior to dissection, which are important for ensuring experimental accuracy in biodegradation studies. Beyond insect-based degradation, Zhang has also studied environmental factors influencing plastic breakdown, including the role of ultraviolet (UV) radiation in accelerating the aging and degradation of plastics.  

In addition to biodegradation research, Zhang has contributed to the field of food safety. A 2019 study co-authored by Zhang addressed food safety testing standards, underscoring the researcher’s broader engagement with public health and environmental safety issues. More recently, in 2022, Zhang was cited as an author in studies examining the biological impacts of micro- and nanoplastics, reflecting an ongoing commitment to understanding the ecological and health consequences of plastic pollution.  

Overall, Zhang’s body of work integrates biological, environmental, and safety perspectives, with a particular emphasis on the degradation of plastics by insect larvae and the environmental processes that influence plastic persistence. This research has provided valuable insights into both the risks posed by microplastics and potential biological strategies for mitigating plastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
