<node id="High-density polyethylene (HDPE)">
  <data key="d0">High-density polyethylene (HDPE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a widely used type of polyethylene and a common plastic polymer produced through molding processes. It is known for its durability and is frequently employed in the manufacture of commercial products such as plastic bags and other packaging materials. Due to its extensive use and environmental persistence, HDPE has become a focus of scientific research on biodegradation and plastic waste management.  

Studies have investigated the potential for both microorganisms and insects to degrade HDPE. Several bacterial strains, including *Priestia megaterium* S1, *Micrococcus luteus* strain CGR112, and species of *Achromobacter* such as *Achromobacter xylosoxidans*, have been tested for their ability to utilize HDPE as a carbon source and contribute to its breakdown. In addition to microbial activity, insect species such as the lesser waxworm (*Achroia grisella*) and mealworms have been studied for their capacity to degrade HDPE, often in combination with associated gut microbiota.  

In experimental settings, HDPE has been used as the sole carbon source to evaluate the biodegradation potential of these organisms. The findings from such studies highlight the possibility of biological pathways for reducing HDPE waste, although the efficiency and scalability of these processes remain under investigation.  

Overall, High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is both an essential industrial material and a significant environmental challenge, prompting ongoing research into microbial and insect-mediated biodegradation as potential solutions for plastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
