<node id="Earthworms">
  <data key="d0">Earthworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Earthworms are soil-dwelling macroinvertebrates that play an important role in soil ecosystems but have been increasingly studied in the context of plastic pollution. Research has shown that earthworms can physically fragment plastics through digestive processes, breaking them down into smaller particles such as microplastics and nanoplastics. However, despite this mechanical biofragmentation, earthworms are not considered true plastic-degrading organisms, as they lack the biochemical capacity to biodegrade most petroleum-based plastics, including polystyrene. Experimental studies have confirmed that no significant biodegradation of polystyrene occurs within their digestive systems.  

Some evidence suggests that earthworms may contribute to the biodegradation of certain degradable or biopolymer-based plastics, though this capacity is limited and not comparable to microbial or enzymatic degradation pathways. Their gut microbiota has been investigated for potential roles in polyethylene decay and soil restoration, indicating that associated microorganisms may have more relevance for plastic breakdown than the worms themselves.  

Exposure to plastics has been shown to negatively impact earthworm health. When subjected to high concentrations of microplastics, earthworms experienced increased mortality rates and significant reductions in body weight. Additionally, ingestion of plastic residues has been documented, with particles found in their stomach contents. Beyond physical impacts, plastics have been associated with physiological disruptions in earthworms, including disturbances in lipid homeostasis, which may impair their overall metabolic balance.  

Overall, earthworms are frequently used as model organisms in soil ecotoxicology to assess the environmental impacts of plastic pollution. While they can fragment plastics into smaller particles and may harbor gut bacteria with limited plastic-degrading potential, earthworms themselves exhibit poor or negligible ability to biodegrade conventional petroleum-based plastics. Instead, their significance lies in their role as indicators of soil contamination and as vectors that redistribute microplastics within terrestrial ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms were reported to ingest and contain microplastics in studies cited in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are macroinvertebrates with poor plastic-degrading ability, leading to biofragmentation of ingested plastics into nanoparticles.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil-dwelling organisms studied for their ability to degrade polylactic acid bioplastics and their responses to pollutants such as phenanthrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387469</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworm Larvae">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm Larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm larvae, the larval stage of *Tenebrio molitor*, are widely studied as biological subjects in research focused on plastic degradation and physiological adaptation. These larvae have been shown to ingest, fragment, and biodegrade various plastics, including polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), as well as PE microplastics. Their ability to process synthetic polymers has made them a model organism for investigating potential biological solutions to plastic waste management.  

In experimental studies, mealworm larvae are used to assess growth and morphological parameters such as body mass and length when fed with different plastic substrates. Researchers also examine their physiological responses to plastic ingestion, including changes in metabolism, mineral content, and overall health. The larvae’s digestive tract microbiome is of particular interest, as microbial community composition and bacterial abundance are analyzed to better understand the role of gut microorganisms in facilitating plastic degradation.  

Beyond biodegradation capacity, mealworm larvae are studied under varying feeding conditions to evaluate how different diets influence their survival, development, and biochemical processes. These investigations provide insights into the interactions between diet, gut microbiota, and host physiology. Collectively, research on mealworm larvae highlights their significance as a model system for exploring both the biological mechanisms of plastic degradation and the broader ecological and physiological impacts of consuming synthetic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) are the subject of the study, with their fatty acid profiles and growth affected by different feeds.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae are the organisms whose digestive tract microbiomes were analyzed under different feeding substrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174</data>
