<node id="Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC)">
  <data key="d0">Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a widely used synthetic plastic polymer and thermoplastic that accounts for approximately 10–10.4% of global plastic production, making it one of the most common and versatile general-purpose plastics. Derived from petroleum, PVC is classified as one of the six major plastic polymers and represented about 10.2% of polymer demand in Europe in 2018. Its popularity is attributed to its durability, versatility, and cost-effectiveness, which have led to extensive applications across construction materials, industrial products, household goods, artificial leather, pipes, wires, cables, and packaging films.  

From a scientific perspective, PVC is a non-hydrolysable plastic, meaning it is highly resistant to microbial and environmental degradation compared to other polymers such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET). This resistance contributes to its persistence in the environment and makes it a significant focus of biodegradation research. PVC’s environmental impact is compounded by its potential toxicity and the difficulty of breaking it down naturally, raising concerns about pollution, microplastic accumulation, and associated health risks. For example, PVC microplastics have been detected in animal-based traditional medicinal materials, underscoring its widespread environmental presence.  

Research into biological degradation of PVC has highlighted the role of insects, particularly the larvae of the mealworm beetle (*Tenebrio molitor*). Experimental studies have shown that these larvae can chew and ingest PVC, though the process is limited and often incomplete. Degradation is not solely mechanical but is strongly influenced by gut-associated microorganisms, with specific bacterial consortia contributing to partial breakdown of the polymer. Despite this, PVC exhibits lower conversion rates compared to other plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS), reflecting its relative resistance to biodegradation.  

Feeding trials with *T. molitor* larvae have demonstrated both the potential and the limitations of insect-mediated PVC degradation. Mealworms exposed to PVC often chew the edges of materials, such as plastic bags, but rarely penetrate the center, suggesting limited palatability. Moreover, ingestion of PVC has been associated with higher larval mortality, reduced nutritional value, and incomplete degradation, leaving behind residual fragments that may still pose environmental and toxicological risks. These findings highlight the challenges of relying on biological systems for effective PVC waste management, even though they provide valuable insights into possible microbial pathways for future biotechnological applications.  

Overall, Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) represents both a valuable industrial material and a significant environmental challenge. Its widespread use across multiple sectors underscores its economic importance, while its resistance to natural decomposition and potential toxicity make it a pressing concern for sustainability. Ongoing research, particularly involving *Tenebrio molitor* larvae and their gut microbiota, illustrates the promise of biological approaches to PVC recycling but also emphasizes the considerable obstacles that remain. As such, PVC continues to be a critical subject of study at the intersection of materials science, environmental sustainability, and biotechnology.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a plastic type that mealworms can ingest and biodegrade.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
