<node id="Plastic Degradation Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Degradation Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The experiment involved feeding mealworm larvae with different diets (PE, PE + bran, bran, and unfed) to study plastic consumption, biodegradation, and physiological responses.&lt;SEP&gt;The experimental design involved feeding yellow mealworms different types and amounts of plastics (PE, PS, PVC) to study degradation efficiency and nutritional effects.&lt;SEP&gt;The experiment described involves feeding yellow mealworms with different plastics (PE, PS, PVC) under controlled conditions to measure degradation rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378662</data>
</node>
<node id="Brandon">
  <data key="d0">Brandon</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon is a researcher and author whose work has been widely cited in the scientific literature on plastic biodegradation, particularly in relation to mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor). He is recognized for his contributions to studies published in 2018 and 2020 that investigated the ability of mealworms to consume and degrade plastics, including polyethylene (PE). Brandon’s research has provided evidence that the gut microbiome of T. molitor larvae plays a critical role in facilitating and accelerating the biodegradation of plastics, highlighting the importance of microbial communities in this process. His 2018 peer-reviewed study is frequently referenced for documenting the capacity of mealworms to ingest and metabolize PE, offering one of the earlier demonstrations of insect-mediated plastic degradation. In addition, Brandon’s work has been cited for its methodological contributions, particularly in the use of analytical approaches to study polymer degradation and microbial interactions. Collectively, Brandon’s research has advanced understanding of insect-plastic interactions, gut microbiome dynamics, and the potential for biologically driven solutions to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Researcher cited for work on PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon is an author cited in studies related to plastic biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon is cited as an author in a 2018 study referenced in the text.</data>
