<node id="PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride)">
  <data key="d0">PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a synthetic plastic polymer widely used for its durability, resistance to degradation, and versatility in applications. It is often produced in film form and may contain additives such as plasticizers, flame retardants, and adhesives, which further influence its physical and chemical properties. Due to its persistence in the environment, PVC has been studied extensively as a representative microplastic, particularly in the context of biodegradation by insect larvae such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus).  

Experimental studies have shown that mealworms are capable of chewing and ingesting PVC films, causing physical damage to the material. However, compared to other plastics such as polystyrene (PS), PVC is consumed in smaller amounts and demonstrates significantly lower biodegradability. The polymer’s chemical structure, combined with its additives, makes it highly resistant to biological breakdown. While ingestion occurs, true metabolic degradation is minimal, and PVC remains largely persistent after passage through the insect digestive system.  

Research has highlighted the role of gut microbes in influencing the limited degradation of PVC. When microbial activity is suppressed, for example through antibiotic treatment, the already low levels of biodegradation are further reduced, underscoring the importance of microbial symbionts in any potential transformation process. Analytical methods, such as tetrahydrofuran (THF) extraction, have been employed to examine residual PVC in insect frass (excrement), providing evidence that most of the polymer remains intact after ingestion.  

Although partial degradation and limited mineralization have been observed, these processes are incomplete and can generate toxic byproducts, including chlorinated intermediates and hydrogen chloride. Such byproducts highlight the environmental risks associated with PVC persistence and its resistance to safe biodegradation pathways.  

Overall, PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride) is a common and environmentally persistent plastic that has been tested in biodegradation studies with insect larvae. While mealworms and related species can ingest and physically alter PVC, the polymer’s resistance to microbial and enzymatic breakdown makes it one of the least degradable plastics studied. Its persistence, combined with the potential release of harmful byproducts, underscores the significant environmental challenges posed by PVC as a microplastic pollutant and its importance as a target for future biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC is a type of plastic tested as a food source for mealworms. It was not greatly eaten or degraded by mealworms, but results suggest it may be biodegradable by gut bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387872</data>
</node>
<node id="Wheat bran">
  <data key="d0">Wheat bran</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wheat bran is a natural agricultural byproduct that has been widely employed as a nutritional feed and experimental control in studies involving yellow mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor). In these experiments, wheat bran served multiple important roles. Primarily, it functioned as a control diet, providing a nutritionally rich baseline against which the effects of alternative diets, such as plastics, could be compared. Its higher nutritional value relative to synthetic polymers supported improved larval growth, survival, and physiological stability, thereby ensuring a reliable standard for assessing experimental outcomes.  

Beyond its role as a control, wheat bran was also used as a co-diet in combination with plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE). When included alongside these polymers, wheat bran enhanced larval feeding activity and survival rates, which in turn facilitated greater consumption and degradation of the plastics. This demonstrated that the presence of a natural, nutrient-rich supplement could significantly improve the efficiency of plastic utilization by mealworms. The bran’s contribution to maintaining larval homeostasis and supporting physiological performance was a key factor in enabling the insects to process otherwise nutritionally deficient materials.  

In practical terms, wheat bran was sourced from suppliers such as Binzhou Mealworm Co. and Exotic Nutrition for use in these controlled feeding experiments. Its consistent application across studies underscores its importance as both a standard dietary supplement and an experimental variable. By providing essential nutrients and serving as a benchmark for comparison, wheat bran played a critical role in evaluating how natural dietary components influence the capacity of Tenebrio molitor larvae to survive, grow, and degrade synthetic polymers.  

In summary, wheat bran is not only a valuable agricultural byproduct but also a central experimental tool in mealworm research, functioning as a control diet, a nutritional supplement, and a co-diet that enhances the biological and ecological relevance of studies on plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is a type of feed used in the experiment, with measured crude protein and fat content in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran was used as the natural feedstock for the control group in the experiment.</data>
