<node id="Gut microbiome">
  <data key="d0">Gut microbiome</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The **gut microbiome** refers to the diverse microbial community residing in the digestive tract of insect larvae, particularly species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and *Galleria mellonella*. This microbial community has been extensively studied for its role in the digestion and biodegradation of synthetic polymers, including polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). Research demonstrates that the gut microbiome contributes to the breakdown of these plastics by mediating metabolic processes, particularly those involving carbon and nitrogen cycling, which are essential for polymer degradation.  

In *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, the gut microbiome has been shown to shift in composition, abundance, and functional capacity depending on dietary inputs, including the presence of plastics and co-diet supplementation. Antibiotic treatments and variations in feeding conditions further influence the structure and activity of the microbial community, highlighting its adaptability and functional plasticity. Similarly, in *Galleria mellonella* larvae, the gut microbiome has been investigated in the context of plastic biodegradation, with studies focusing on how co-diets and polymer properties affect microbial activity and degradation efficiency.  

Overall, the gut microbiome of insect larvae plays a central role in facilitating plastic biodegradation, with its composition and metabolic functions being shaped by host species, diet, and environmental conditions. This microbial ecosystem not only supports larval nutrition but also provides a biological basis for the potential application of insects and their associated microbes in bioremediation strategies targeting plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of insect larvae such as Tenebrio molitor plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics and responds to diet shifts.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbial community in mealworm digestive systems studied for its role in plastic biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387147</data>
</node>
<node id="Galleria mellonella larvae">
  <data key="d0">Galleria mellonella larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Galleria mellonella larvae, commonly known as the larvae of the greater wax moth, have attracted significant scientific interest due to their unique biological and ecological properties. These insect larvae are notable for their reported ability to degrade synthetic polymers, particularly plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). Research has shown that Galleria mellonella larvae can break down PS, with measurable changes in polymer characteristics such as number-average molecular weight (Mn) and weight-average molecular weight (Mw). They have also been studied for their capacity to degrade PS microplastics and microbeads, highlighting their potential role in mitigating plastic pollution.  

The plastic-degrading ability of Galleria mellonella larvae has been linked not only to their own metabolic processes but also to the activity of gut-associated microorganisms. For example, the fungus *Aspergillus flavus* strain PEDX3, isolated from these larvae, has demonstrated the capacity to degrade polyethylene. Similarly, gut bacteria from the larvae have been investigated for their role in polystyrene degradation, suggesting a complex interplay between host physiology and microbial symbionts in facilitating plastic breakdown.  

Beyond their relevance to plastic degradation, Galleria mellonella larvae have been used in comparative studies of fatty acid metabolism under varying dietary conditions, providing insights into insect physiology and metabolic adaptation. Their ability to metabolize plastics has often been compared to that of mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*), another insect species recognized for plastic biodegradation, positioning Galleria mellonella as a valuable model organism in this emerging field.  

Due to their demonstrated plastic-degrading features, Galleria mellonella larvae have been proposed as a model system for future research on the biological and ecological impacts of microplastics. In particular, they are considered promising for studies investigating microplastic-induced oxidative stress and related physiological responses. This dual role—as both a potential agent of plastic degradation and a model for assessing the biological effects of microplastic exposure—underscores their importance in environmental and biomedical research.  

In summary, Galleria mellonella larvae are insect larvae of the wax moth that serve as a key organism in studies of plastic biodegradation, microbial symbiosis, and metabolic adaptation. Their unique ability to degrade plastics, combined with their utility as a model for studying the effects of microplastics, makes them a significant subject of ongoing scientific investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella larvae, also known as greater wax moth larvae, are studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
