<node id="Peng">
  <data key="d0">Peng</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng is a researcher and author whose work is frequently cited in the field of plastic biodegradation, particularly in relation to insect larvae. Peng has contributed to multiple studies, including those published in 2019 and 2022, that investigate the ability of insect species such as mealworms and dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus) to degrade synthetic polymers like polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and Styrofoam. In these studies, Peng has been recognized for advancing understanding of how insect larvae can metabolize or fragment plastics, thereby providing insights into potential biological solutions for plastic waste management.  

Peng’s research has also addressed methodological aspects, such as the application of thermogravimetric analysis to study degradation processes, and has examined the role of nutrient limitations when larvae are exposed to exogenous polymers. Additionally, Peng has contributed to the taxonomic and biological characterization of Tenebrio obscurus, describing it as a darkling beetle whose larvae are commonly referred to as mini mealworms, which are capable of consuming and breaking down plastics.  

Overall, Peng’s body of work is significant in the emerging field of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, combining experimental studies on polymer degradation with analytical methods and biological insights into insect species that demonstrate plastic-degrading capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
