<node id="Eisenia fetida">
  <data key="d0">Eisenia fetida</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Eisenia fetida is a species of earthworm widely used as a model organism in ecotoxicological research, particularly for studying the biological impacts of pollutants in soil ecosystems. Classified as a macroinvertebrate, it plays an important role in soil health and nutrient cycling, making it a relevant indicator species for assessing environmental contamination. Research has shown that Eisenia fetida is negatively affected by exposure to various types of microplastics, including polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE). When exposed to these materials, the earthworms exhibit signs of oxidative stress, DNA damage, and gut toxicity, highlighting their vulnerability to plastic pollution.  

Studies have also demonstrated that Eisenia fetida can fragment polyethylene microplastics into smaller particles, including nanoplastics, which in turn cause additional biological damage. Despite this interaction, the species is generally considered to have poor plastic-degrading ability, meaning it does not effectively break down plastics in a way that mitigates their environmental persistence. Instead, its ingestion and processing of microplastics often exacerbate the problem by generating smaller, more biologically active particles.  

Beyond microplastic exposure, Eisenia fetida has also been studied for its responses to other environmental contaminants, such as phenanthrene, a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon. Laboratory experiments have examined its defense mechanisms and stress responses under such conditions, further establishing its role as a sentinel species for soil pollution studies.  

Overall, Eisenia fetida serves as a key organism for understanding the ecological and toxicological effects of emerging pollutants, particularly microplastics, in terrestrial ecosystems. Its sensitivity to contaminants, combined with its ecological importance, makes it a valuable bioindicator for assessing soil health and the broader impacts of anthropogenic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
