<node id="Biomass">
  <data key="d0">Biomass</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Biomass, in the context of polystyrene (PS) and expanded polystyrene (EPS, commonly known as Styrofoam) biodegradation, refers to the organic material generated and incorporated into living organisms—primarily insects such as mealworms and microorganisms such as bacteria—during the breakdown of synthetic polymers. It represents the fraction of carbon from degraded plastics that is assimilated into biological tissues or cellular material, serving as a key indicator of carbon conversion efficiency in biodegradation processes.  

In mealworms, biomass specifically denotes the insect body mass formed from assimilated carbon derived from consumed PS or EPS. Studies show that only a very small proportion of polystyrene carbon, approximately 0.5%, is incorporated into mealworm biomass, while the majority is released as carbon dioxide or remains as degradation residues. This biomass includes larval tissues composed of proteins, fats, carbohydrates, and other components, and is often analyzed for crude protein content to evaluate nutritional outcomes. Importantly, analyses of mealworm biomass have revealed no detectable residues of PS monomers or oligomers, but instead identified bioactive compounds such as acids, vitamins, and amides, suggesting metabolic transformation of plastic-derived carbon into biologically useful substances.  

The growth and survival of mealworms under different diets are frequently assessed through biomass measurements, with average larval weight serving as a proxy. Comparisons indicate that larvae fed on polystyrene exhibit reduced biomass levels compared to those fed on natural substrates like wheat bran, underscoring the limited nutritional value and low assimilation efficiency of plastic-derived carbon. Biomass in this context also functions as a measurable outcome in carbon balance studies, helping to quantify the extent of plastic-to-organic matter conversion.  

Beyond insect-mediated degradation, biomass is also generated during microbial processes. For example, the bacterium *Priestia megaterium* S1 produces biomass alongside carbon dioxide, water, ATP, and other metabolites during the biodegradation of polystyrene. In microbial systems, biomass refers to the accumulation of cellular material derived from the utilization of plastic-derived carbon sources, reflecting microbial growth and metabolic activity.  

Overall, biomass in the domain of polystyrene and Styrofoam degradation represents the transformation of synthetic polymer carbon into living organic matter, whether in insect tissues or microbial cells. It serves as a critical parameter for evaluating the efficiency of biological systems in assimilating plastic-derived carbon, providing insights into the potential of insects and microbes for plastic waste management, resource recovery, and the development of sustainable biodegradation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the small fraction of LDPE carbon assimilated by larvae during digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the mass of larvae, which increased in control groups and decreased in starvation groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomass refers to the organic matter built into larvae from PS feeding, with only 5% of PS incorporated.</data>
