<node id="Residual plastic particles">
  <data key="d0">Residual plastic particles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Residual plastic particles are the undigested fragments of PE found in the frass of mealworm larvae after biodegradation. They range in size from 5–300 μm, with submicron particles also observed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370859</data>
</node>
<node id="Nanoplastics">
  <data key="d0">Nanoplastics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles that originate from the breakdown of larger plastics, including microplastics, and are increasingly recognized as a significant environmental and biological concern. Due to their nanoscale size, nanoplastics exhibit high mobility in ecosystems and can readily interact with biological systems, leading to potential toxicity. Research has shown that they can bind to biomolecules, increase cell membrane permeability, and induce cellular toxicity, raising concerns about their capacity to disrupt physiological processes across a wide range of organisms.  

In aquatic environments, nanoplastics have been studied for their toxicological effects, including genotoxicity and intestinal damage, as observed in freshwater clams. Their ability to interfere with biological systems makes them a focus of ecotoxicological research, particularly in relation to their persistence, bioavailability, and potential to accumulate in food webs.  

Nanoplastics are also studied in the context of biodegradation and bioremediation. Mealworm larvae, for example, have been shown to ingest plastics, and within their gut microcosms, nanoplastics can be efficiently removed. Interestingly, nanoplastics were not found to accumulate in the frass (excrement) after polyethylene (PE) biodegradation by mealworm larvae, suggesting that the larvae’s digestive processes or associated microbial communities may play a role in breaking down or transforming these particles. Additionally, the absorption of nanoplastics in the mealworm gut can be inhibited by corona proteins, which may reduce their biological uptake and toxicity.  

Beyond invertebrate systems, nanoplastics are also a subject of bacterial degradation studies, with the aim of developing bioremediation strategies to mitigate their environmental impact. Their persistence and potential to cause harm underscore the importance of understanding their interactions with living organisms and ecosystems.  

Overall, nanoplastics represent a critical area of environmental and toxicological research. They are defined as nanoscale plastic particles that arise from the degradation of larger plastics, pose risks due to their toxicity and mobility, and are being investigated both for their harmful effects on organisms and for potential biological pathways that could mitigate their presence in the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Category referring to extremely small plastic particles studied for their ingestion effects on terrestrial snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are defined as plastic particles smaller than 100 nm, analyzed in the frass samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are smaller plastic particles formed from microplastics, studied for their potential hazards in aquatic and terrestrial systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Extremely small plastic particles studied for their interactions with plant species.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are studied for their toxicological effects, including mitochondrial damage in human cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
