<node id="Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Depolymerization is the process by which long polymer chains are broken down into smaller molecular units, serving as the first and essential step in the biodegradation of plastics. This process typically occurs through mechanisms such as oxidation and hydrolysis, which reduce complex polymers into simpler structures that can be further metabolized or degraded. Depolymerization has been observed in a variety of synthetic polymers, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and mask polymers (MPS).  

Experimental studies have demonstrated depolymerization during the biodegradation of plastics by insect larvae, particularly mealworms such as *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*. In these organisms, ingested polymers undergo molecular breakdown in the gut, resulting in smaller fragments that indicate the onset of biodegradation. For example, PE and PS plastics show evidence of depolymerization after digestion by mealworm larvae, while PET and PP polymers also undergo similar breakdown into smaller molecular structures. In the case of mask polymers, depolymerization has been specifically observed in the digestive systems of yellow mealworms.  

Research has also highlighted that depolymerization can vary depending on environmental or experimental conditions. For instance, in the case of mask polymers (MPS), depolymerization was observed in mealworms at one experimental condition (OLS) but not at another (MLS), suggesting that factors such as diet, microbial communities, or gut environment may influence the efficiency of the process.  

Overall, depolymerization represents a critical molecular transformation in the degradation of synthetic plastics. By fragmenting long polymer chains into smaller, more manageable units, it enables subsequent microbial or enzymatic processes that contribute to the complete biodegradation of otherwise persistent plastic materials. This makes depolymerization a central focus of research into biological and environmentally sustainable methods for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Depolymerization is a process facilitated by enzymes and microbial activity that breaks down aged MPs into smaller monomers.&lt;SEP&gt;Depolymerization refers to the breakdown of ingested plastics into smaller molecular structures during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Depolymerization refers to the breakdown of polymers into smaller units. It occurs more readily in amorphous fractions of polymers than crystalline fractions and is a key process in mealworm-mediated plastic degradation.</data>
