<node id="Wei-Min Wu">
  <data key="d0">Wei-Min Wu</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wei-Min Wu is a professor in the Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Stanford University and is affiliated with the William &amp; Cloy Codiga Resource Recovery Center. He is internationally recognized for his pioneering research in environmental engineering, particularly in the areas of plastic biodegradation and microbial ecology. His work has significantly advanced scientific understanding of how synthetic polymers, including polystyrene and low-density polyethylene, can be biologically degraded. A central focus of his research has been the role of yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and their gut microbiomes in facilitating the breakdown of plastics, providing critical insights into insect- and microbe-mediated pathways for addressing plastic pollution.  

As both an author and corresponding author on multiple influential studies, Wu has contributed extensively to research on molecular-weight-dependent degradation of plastics, biodegradation of microplastics, and the mechanisms underlying microbial and insect-driven plastic decomposition. His contributions span conceptualization, methodology design, validation, data curation, formal analysis, investigation, supervision, and manuscript preparation, including drafting, reviewing, and editing. He has also provided essential guidance and mentorship to collaborators and students, underscoring his role as a scientific leader in the field.  

Wu’s research activities extend beyond the laboratory through his involvement in international collaborations and funding initiatives. Notably, he has served as an international collaborator under a grant from the National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC Grant 51373006), supporting cross-border research on environmental sustainability. His leadership in securing resources and fostering global partnerships reflects his commitment to advancing interdisciplinary solutions to pressing environmental challenges.  

Overall, Wei-Min Wu’s work integrates environmental engineering, microbiology, and sustainability science, positioning him as a key figure in the study of plastic biodegradation. Through his authorship, mentorship, and international collaborations, he has played a central role in shaping research directions that address the environmental impacts of plastic waste while exploring innovative strategies for resource recovery and ecological resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Professor Wei-Min Wu from Stanford University provided suggestions during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Wei-Min Wu is one of the authors of the study on polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Corresponding author affiliated with Stanford University, Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, William &amp; Clay Codiga Resource Recovery Center, and Center for Sustainable Development &amp; Global Competitiveness.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
