<node id="Microplastics">
  <data key="d0">Microplastics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microplastics are small plastic particles, generally defined as ranging in size from approximately 100 nanometers or 1 micrometer up to 5 millimeters, that have become a pervasive and persistent environmental contaminant. They are considered anthropogenic in origin and arise from two primary sources: the fragmentation of larger plastic debris through physical, chemical, or biological processes, and the intentional manufacture of microscopic plastics for use in consumer and industrial products such as cosmetics, cleaning agents, and abrasives. Common polymer types found in microplastics include polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). Their small size, large surface area, and strong adsorption capacity give them unique physicochemical properties that influence their environmental behavior and interactions with other pollutants.  

Microplastics are now distributed across virtually all ecosystems, including oceans, rivers, lakes, soils, sediments, agricultural fields, wastewater treatment systems, and even the atmosphere. Their persistence and resistance to natural degradation make them difficult to remove once released. In aquatic systems, they are extensively studied for detection, quantification, and toxicological effects, though their irregular shapes and varied compositions complicate standardized monitoring. Increasing attention is also being paid to terrestrial environments, where microplastics can affect soil health, crop productivity, and interactions with contaminants such as heavy metals and pharmaceuticals.  

The ecological and biological impacts of microplastics are a growing concern. They are readily ingested by a wide range of organisms, including fish, crustaceans, copepods, oysters, insects, and terrestrial invertebrates such as mealworms. Ingestion can cause intestinal blockage, physical damage, oxidative stress, inflammatory responses, reduced nutrient absorption, and altered energy balance. Studies have shown that microplastics can accumulate in digestive systems, impair health, and disrupt feeding behavior. Importantly, microplastics can bioaccumulate and transfer through food webs, raising concerns about exposure of higher predators, including humans, to both the particles themselves and the chemical additives or adsorbed pollutants they may carry. This trophic transfer underscores potential long-term ecological and public health risks.  

Research into the degradation of microplastics has identified certain insect larvae, such as yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), as capable of partially degrading polymers like PE, PS, and PET. However, this process often results in secondary microplastic residues in their waste. Microbial communities are also being investigated for their potential to degrade plastics, with metagenomic and biochemical studies exploring enzymatic pathways. Despite these advances, effective large-scale biodegradation strategies remain under development, and the persistence of microplastics continues to pose significant challenges.  

Microplastics are increasingly recognized as a global environmental and public health issue. Their ubiquity, persistence, and capacity to disrupt ecosystems and food chains highlight the urgency of mitigation strategies. Proposed approaches include reducing plastic production and consumption, improving waste management and recycling systems, and advancing biodegradation technologies. Ongoing research continues to focus on their occurrence, toxicity, degradation pathways, and interactions with other environmental stressors, reflecting the recognition of microplastics as one of the most significant emerging contaminants of the modern era.</data>
