<node id="Bertocchini, F.">
  <data key="d0">Bertocchini, F.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Bertocchini, F. is a researcher recognized for pioneering contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insects in breaking down synthetic polymers. Bertocchini is best known as one of the authors of the landmark 2017 study that demonstrated the ability of caterpillars of the wax moth (*Galleria mellonella*) to biodegrade polyethylene, one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics. This discovery provided the first evidence that biological systems, specifically wax moth caterpillars, could contribute to mitigating plastic waste, thereby opening new perspectives for sustainable waste management strategies.  

Building on this foundational work, Bertocchini co-authored subsequent studies, including research published in 2019, which further explored polyethylene biodegradation in the context of holobiont metabolism. These investigations examined the complex interactions between the caterpillars and their associated microbiota, contributing to a deeper understanding of the biological and ecological mechanisms involved in plastic degradation.  

In addition to these studies, Bertocchini has also been involved in research that identified and characterized enzymes present in wax worm saliva capable of degrading polyethylene. This line of inquiry has been particularly significant, as it shifted attention from the whole organism to specific biochemical agents, offering promising avenues for the development of enzymatic or biotechnological solutions to address global plastic pollution.  

Through this body of work, Bertocchini, F. has played a central role in advancing scientific knowledge of insect-mediated plastic degradation. Their research has not only demonstrated the potential of wax moth caterpillars and their enzymes in breaking down persistent plastics but has also laid the groundwork for innovative approaches to bioremediation and the development of environmentally friendly technologies to combat plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Bertocchini, F. is a co-author of research on polyethylene biodegradation by wax moth caterpillars.&lt;SEP&gt;Bertocchini, F. is an author of a referenced study on polystyrene biodegradation by caterpillars of the wax moth Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Bertocchini, F. is listed as an author of a study on polyethylene biodegradation by caterpillars.&lt;SEP&gt;Bertocchini, F. is an author of a study on polyethylene biodegradation by caterpillars of the wax moth Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Bertocchini, F. is an author of a referenced study on polyethylene biodegradation by caterpillars of the wax moth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
