<node id="Wu, W.-M.">
  <data key="d0">Wu, W.-M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wu, W.-M. is an environmental biotechnology researcher internationally recognized for his pioneering contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of insect larvae and their associated microbial communities. His work has demonstrated that several insect species, including mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms (*Zophobas atratus*), and waxworms, are capable of consuming and metabolizing synthetic polymers such as polystyrene, polyethylene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and mixed plastic wastes. By systematically documenting these processes, Wu has provided critical insights into the mechanisms of insect-mediated plastic breakdown, showing how polymer type, size, and composition influence degradation efficiency.  

A frequent co-author of landmark studies in the field, Wu has published extensively in leading journals such as *Chemosphere* and *Environmental Science &amp; Technology*. His research has revealed that both yellow and dark mealworms can biodegrade polystyrene, that mealworms are capable of processing polyethylene and low-density polyethylene, and that waxworms harbor bacterial strains capable of polyethylene degradation. He has also investigated the gut microbiome of insect larvae, elucidating its role in carbon and nitrogen metabolism during plastic biodegradation. For example, he co-authored a 2021 study on the gut microbiome response of *Tenebrio molitor* to diet shifts during plastic consumption, highlighting the microbial contributions to polymer degradation.  

Beyond insect-based systems, Wu has advanced microbial biodegradation research by co-authoring studies on bacterial strains isolated from waxworms, including polyethylene-degrading microbes, and contributing to the genome sequencing of *Bacillus* sp. YP1. This work broadens the scope of biological strategies for mitigating plastic waste by integrating entomology, microbiology, and environmental science, and emphasizes the potential of combined insect–microbe approaches to accelerate plastic degradation and mineralization. His contributions also extend to bibliometric and analytical studies, including a 2023 bibliometric analysis of global publications on plastic biodegradation, reflecting his role in shaping the research landscape of this emerging field.  

Wu has also co-authored studies addressing broader environmental concerns, such as the environmental fate, toxicity, and risk management strategies of nanoplastics, further underscoring his commitment to understanding the ecological impacts of synthetic polymers. His recurring authorship across multiple influential studies, including a 2020 investigation into PVC biodegradation in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae and research on expanded polystyrene and polyethylene foams, highlights his leadership in exploring the biodegradation of diverse polymer types.  

Through his interdisciplinary approach, Wu, W.-M. has emerged as a leading authority on plastic biodegradation. His research has significantly advanced scientific understanding of how biological systems—particularly insect larvae and their microbiomes—can contribute to sustainable solutions for plastic waste management. By bridging fundamental research and applied environmental science, Wu continues to influence the development of innovative strategies aimed at reducing the ecological impact of plastics and addressing one of the most pressing environmental challenges of the modern era.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu, W.-M. is an author of multiple studies on plastic biodegradation by insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu, W.-M. is one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d390237