<node id="Wang, J.">
  <data key="d0">Wang, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, J. is a researcher and scientific author whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics and the microbial and ecological processes that facilitate this transformation. His research integrates perspectives from entomology, microbiology, and environmental science, with the overarching goal of identifying biological pathways and organisms capable of mitigating plastic pollution.  

A central theme in Wang, J.’s work is the study of insect-mediated plastic degradation, particularly the role of superworms (Zophobas atratus) and mealworms (Tenebrio molitor L.) in breaking down persistent polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene. He has authored and co-authored multiple studies demonstrating that superworms are capable of consuming and degrading polystyrene, a widely used but environmentally resistant plastic. These investigations not only document the physical fragmentation of the polymer but also explore the biochemical and microbial pathways that enable its mineralization, highlighting the potential of insect-based systems as a sustainable solution for plastic waste management.  

In addition to polystyrene, Wang, J. has contributed to research on polyethylene degradation. One notable study involved the bacterial strain Enterobacter sp. D1, which was shown to facilitate the breakdown of polyethylene, thereby expanding the range of microbial candidates for plastic biodegradation. His work also extends to the ecological and microbial dynamics in mealworms consuming plastics, with particular attention to nitrogen fixation and the role of diazotrophic microbial communities in the insect gut. These studies underscore the importance of insect–microbe symbioses in enabling the metabolism of otherwise recalcitrant synthetic polymers.  

Beyond insect and microbial systems, Wang, J. has investigated the environmental impacts of plastic pollution more broadly. He is an author of a study on microplastics contamination in inland freshwater systems in China, contributing to the understanding of how plastic waste disperses and persists in natural ecosystems. He has also examined the physiological responses of earthworms exposed to polyethylene microplastics, providing insight into the ecological risks associated with microplastic pollution in soil environments.  

Wang, J.’s contributions include studies on the ingestion preferences of superworms and mealworms when exposed to different plastics, co-authorship of a 2022 study on nitrogen fixation in plastic-eating mealworms, and multiple publications on the biodegradation of polystyrene by Zophobas atratus superworms. Collectively, his body of work advances knowledge of insect- and microbe-mediated plastic degradation, identifies novel biological pathways for polymer breakdown, and informs strategies for sustainable waste management and environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, J. is an author of a study on defense responses in earthworms exposed to polyethylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the nitrogen fixation study in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, J. is an author of a study on microplastics as vectors for HOC bioaccumulation in earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, J. is a co-author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene by superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, J. is a co-author of multiple studies on microplastics, including toxic effects in earthworms and oxytetracycline adsorption.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on plastic ingestion preferences of superworm and yellow mealworm.</data>
