<node id="Zhang, Z.R.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, Z.R.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, Z.R. is a researcher recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insects in breaking down polyethylene. As an author and co-author of multiple scientific studies, Zhang has investigated the capacity of mealworms (larvae of *Tenebrio* species) to degrade polyethylene, including low-density polyethylene, a common and persistent plastic pollutant. His research has explored the biological and microbiological mechanisms underlying this process, with particular attention to the gut microbiome of mealworms and its role in facilitating plastic degradation. Zhang has also contributed to comparative studies examining differences in biodegradation efficiency between dark mealworms and yellow mealworms, thereby providing insights into species-specific variations in plastic consumption and breakdown. Collectively, Zhang, Z.R.’s work advances understanding of insect-mediated biodegradation as a potential strategy for mitigating plastic waste, highlighting both the ecological significance and applied potential of mealworm-based systems in addressing environmental pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
