<node id="Ren, N.">
  <data key="d0">Ren, N.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ren, N. is a researcher whose work centers on the biodegradation of plastics and the biological and chemical processes that enable this transformation. Their research contributions have been particularly influential in exploring the capacity of insect larvae, such as the greater wax moth (*Galleria mellonella*) and the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*), to degrade synthetic polymers including polyethylene and polystyrene. Through these studies, Ren, N. has helped demonstrate the potential of insect-based systems as a novel and sustainable approach to mitigating plastic pollution.  

A significant focus of Ren, N.’s work lies in uncovering the biological mechanisms underlying plastic degradation, with particular emphasis on the role of the gut microbiome. Their studies have examined how microbial communities within the digestive systems of larvae contribute to the breakdown of plastics, linking microbial activity to key metabolic processes such as carbon and nitrogen cycling. For example, Ren, N. has co-authored research investigating the gut microbiome response of *Tenebrio molitor* to diet shifts during plastic biodegradation, as well as studies on how polyethylene degradation in mealworms is facilitated by microbial interactions and metabolic pathways. Similar investigations have been conducted on the gut microbiome of *Galleria mellonella* larvae, further highlighting the importance of host-microbe interactions in enabling plastic digestion.  

In addition to insect- and microbiome-based biodegradation, Ren, N. has also contributed to research on advanced chemical processes relevant to environmental remediation. This includes work on the electrogeneration of hydroxyl radicals, highly reactive species that play a role in the degradation of pollutants. By integrating microbiological, entomological, and chemical perspectives, Ren, N.’s research provides a multidisciplinary framework for addressing the global challenge of plastic waste.  

Overall, Ren, N. has established a body of work that advances understanding of both biological and chemical strategies for plastic degradation. Their contributions highlight the potential of insect larvae and their gut microbiomes as natural agents of polymer breakdown, while also exploring complementary chemical methods, thereby offering valuable insights into sustainable approaches for reducing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
