</node>
<node id="Tenebrio larvae">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene with crop residues as co-diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae are macroinvertebrates capable of degrading plastics, directly exposed to PE microplastics during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae, including T. molitor and T. obscurus, are recognized as effective tools for plastic degradation and as a protein source for livestock and humans.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae are insect larvae studied for their gut microbiome functions and ability to degrade plastics such as PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio larvae are studied as model organisms for plastic degradation and oxidative stress responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae of Tenebrio species studied for gut microbiome associations during polyethylene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380140</data>
</node>
<node id="Crop residues">
  <data key="d0">Crop residues</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Crop residues are used as co-diets in studies of polyethylene biodegradation in Tenebrio larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756370883</data>
</node>
<node id="Geyer, R.">
  <data key="d0">Geyer, R.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">R. Geyer is a researcher and author widely recognized for his influential work on plastics, with a particular focus on their production, use, waste generation, and environmental impacts. He is a co-author of the landmark 2015 *Science* article *“Plastic waste inputs from land into the ocean”*, which quantified the scale and pathways of plastic waste entering marine environments and has become a foundational reference in the study of ocean plastic pollution. In addition to this contribution, Geyer has co-authored highly cited research on the global production, usage patterns, and ultimate fate of plastics, including the comprehensive study that examined the production, use, and fate of all plastics ever made. His work provides critical insights into the lifecycle of plastic materials, highlighting the challenges of waste management and the long-term implications of plastic accumulation for environmental sustainability. Through these studies, R. Geyer has played a central role in advancing scientific understanding of plastic pollution and its ecological consequences, making his scholarship a cornerstone in the field of environmental research on plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385304</data>
</node>
<node id="Jambeck, J.R.">
  <data key="d0">Jambeck, J.R.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jambeck, J.R. is a researcher recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic pollution, particularly in relation to its production, use, and environmental fate. She is one of the authors of the influential article *“Plastic waste inputs from land into the ocean”* published in *Science* in 2015, which provided groundbreaking estimates of the scale of plastic waste entering marine environments from terrestrial sources. This work has been widely cited as a foundational study in understanding the pathways and impacts of plastic pollution in the world’s oceans.  

In addition to this landmark publication, Jambeck, J.R. has co-authored research examining the global production, use, and fate of plastics, including studies that quantify the total amount of plastics ever produced and assess how these materials are managed or mismanaged across their life cycle. Her work has contributed to advancing knowledge on the scale of plastic waste generation, the challenges of waste management, and the long-term environmental consequences of plastic accumulation.  

Through these studies, Jambeck, J.R. has played a central role in shaping scientific and policy discussions on plastic pollution, providing critical data and analysis that highlight the urgency of addressing plastic waste at both global and local levels. Her research continues to inform strategies for reducing plastic inputs into the ocean and for improving the sustainability of plastic use worldwide.&lt;SEP&gt;Jambeck, J.R. is an author of a referenced study on production, use, and fate of all plastics ever made.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582</data>
