<node id="Zhang, H.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, H.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, H. is a researcher and author whose work spans multiple scientific domains, including climate science, microbiology, and environmental toxicology. In the field of climate research, Zhang, H. contributed to the *Climate Change 2013* assessment, specifically as an author of a study examining anthropogenic and natural radiative forcing, which is a key component in understanding the drivers of global climate change.  

In microbiology, Zhang, H. has co-authored studies investigating the role of gut microorganisms in various organisms. This includes research on cellulolytic bacteria isolated from the gut of *Holotrichia parallela*, a scarab beetle species, as well as studies on the bacterial and fungal communities inhabiting the gut of *Bactrocera minax* larvae, a significant agricultural pest. These works contribute to the broader understanding of insect-microbe interactions, microbial diversity, and potential applications in biotechnology and pest management.  

Zhang, H. has also conducted research in medical and environmental microbiology. In 2022, Zhang, H. co-authored a study on *Enterobacter ludwigii*, which demonstrated protective effects against colitis, highlighting the potential therapeutic role of gut-associated bacteria in gastrointestinal health.  

In addition, Zhang, H. has been actively involved in the emerging field of nanoplastics and microplastics research, with a particular focus on their toxicological impacts on human health. This includes co-authorship of multiple studies examining how nanoplastics induce mitochondrial damage in human cells, with specific investigations into effects on liver and lung cells. These studies provide important insights into the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying nanoplastic toxicity, contributing to the growing body of evidence on the health risks associated with environmental plastic pollution.  

Overall, Zhang, H.’s body of work reflects a multidisciplinary research profile, integrating expertise in climate science, microbial ecology, and environmental health. Through contributions to global climate assessments, studies on insect and human microbiomes, and investigations into the biological impacts of micro- and nanoplastics, Zhang, H. has advanced scientific understanding across diverse but interconnected areas of environmental and life sciences.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhang, H. is an author of a study on the impact of polystyrene microplastics on cadmium toxicity in zebrafish larvae.</data>
