<node id="Liu, B.">
  <data key="d0">Liu, B.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Liu, B. is a researcher and co-author whose work is centered on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics by insect larvae and the microbial processes that facilitate this phenomenon. Their research has significantly contributed to understanding how persistent plastic polymers, particularly polyethylene and polystyrene, can be broken down through biological pathways involving insects and their gut microbiota.  

A major focus of Liu, B.’s work has been on the greater wax moth (Galleria mellonella) larvae, which have demonstrated the ability to degrade polyethylene and polystyrene. Through co-authored studies, Liu, B. has provided evidence that both the larvae themselves and their associated gut microbial communities play a role in decomposing these otherwise resistant plastics. This research has helped establish insect larvae as promising biological models for exploring plastic degradation mechanisms.  

In addition to wax moths, Liu, B. has contributed to studies on yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor). Their research has examined how the gut microbiome of mealworms responds to dietary shifts when consuming plastics, particularly polystyrene. These studies highlight the dynamic interactions between insect hosts and their gut microbes, underscoring the importance of microbial activity in facilitating plastic breakdown. By integrating physiological and microbial perspectives, Liu, B.’s work advances knowledge of how insect–microbiome systems can contribute to mitigating plastic pollution.  

Beyond insect-mediated plastic degradation, Liu, B. has also co-authored research on environmental toxicology, including a study investigating the phytotoxic effects of perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) and perfluorooctane sulfonate (PFOS). This work broadens their research scope to include the ecological impacts of persistent pollutants beyond plastics.  

Overall, Liu, B. is recognized for advancing the field of plastic biodegradation through insect models, with a particular emphasis on the interplay between insect larvae and their gut microbiomes in the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene, while also contributing to broader environmental toxicology research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
