<node id="Environ. Sci. Technol.">
  <data key="d0">Environ. Sci. Technol.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">*Environmental Science &amp; Technology* (commonly abbreviated as *Environ. Sci. Technol.*) is a leading peer-reviewed scientific journal that publishes cutting-edge research in the fields of environmental science and technology. Recognized as one of the most influential journals in its discipline, it serves as a central platform for disseminating pioneering studies on pollution, contaminants, environmental processes, and sustainable solutions. The journal has been instrumental in shaping global understanding of plastics, polymers, and their environmental impacts, consistently publishing landmark research that has advanced both scientific knowledge and policy development.  

Since its early contributions, such as Miller’s 1994 study on plastics reuse and Browne’s 2008 work on microplastic ingestion, *Environ. Sci. Technol.* has played a pivotal role in documenting the interactions of plastics with natural and biological systems. It has published foundational studies on the biodegradability of photodegraded polymers, including work by Guillet, Regulski, and McAnaney, as well as investigations into the mineralization of polystyrene and the mechanisms underlying polymer breakdown in environmental contexts.  

A significant focus of the journal has been on biological pathways for plastic degradation. Research published in *Environ. Sci. Technol.* has demonstrated the ability of insect larvae—including mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*), superworms, and wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*)—to degrade and mineralize plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and mixed plastic waste. Notable studies from 2014 and 2015 reported the biodegradation of polyethylene by bacterial strains isolated from waxworms and the mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms. A 2020 study further expanded this knowledge by examining the biodegradation of both polyethylene and polystyrene by *Galleria mellonella*, highlighting the critical role of gut microorganisms in facilitating plastic breakdown. These findings underscored the importance of microbial ecology, including the enrichment of microbial communities from insect gut microbiomes, in enhancing degradation efficiency.  

Beyond polymer degradation, *Environ. Sci. Technol.* has published influential research on the environmental fate and biological effects of plastic additives, such as hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD), as well as studies on the impacts of nanoplastics on human cells. The journal has also contributed significantly to understanding the ecological consequences of microplastics, including a 2016 study showing that microplastics can alter soil properties and affect plant performance, thereby influencing terrestrial ecosystem health. Additionally, it has published research on mixture effects between emerging organic contaminants and their cytotoxicity, further broadening its scope in environmental toxicology.  

By integrating research on biodegradation mechanisms, microbial interactions, contaminant dynamics, and sustainable remediation strategies, *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* has established itself as a cornerstone of environmental science scholarship. Its publications continue to inform scientific discourse, guide environmental policy, and shape global strategies for addressing pressing challenges related to plastic pollution, ecosystem resilience, and human well-being. Through these contributions, *Environ. Sci. Technol.* has solidified its reputation as a premier journal advancing knowledge and innovation in environmental science and sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Environ. Sci. Technol. is the journal that published research on HBCD fate in polystyrene-degrading mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Journal where the studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms were published.</data>
