<node id="Sci. Total Environ.">
  <data key="d0">Sci. Total Environ.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">*Science of the Total Environment* (abbreviated as *Sci. Total Environ.*) is a peer-reviewed international scientific journal that publishes research across the full spectrum of environmental science, with a particular emphasis on pollution, ecological impacts, and sustainability. It is widely recognized as a leading platform for advancing knowledge on pressing global environmental challenges, especially those related to plastic pollution, biodegradation, and the ecological consequences of synthetic materials.  

The journal has played a pivotal role in disseminating research on the degradation of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene, polypropylene, polyethylene, vulcanized styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR), tire crumb, and other oil-derived plastics. A significant body of work published in *Sci. Total Environ.* has focused on biological degradation pathways, highlighting both microbial and macro-organism contributions. Studies have examined the role of bacteria such as *Acinetobacter* and other polystyrene-degrading microbial strains, as well as the capacity of insect larvae—including mealworms, superworms, wax moths, and other species—to ingest, fragment, and mineralize plastics. Research has also explored ingestion preferences and biodegradation efficiencies among different larvae species, providing insights into their potential applications in waste management.  

In addition to insect- and microbe-driven degradation, the journal has published studies on other organisms, such as snails, that contribute to plastic breakdown in terrestrial ecosystems. It has also addressed the interactions between plastics and other pollutants, such as the combined effects of microplastics and chemical contaminants like atrazine on soil organisms, including earthworms. These investigations underscore the complexity of pollutant mixtures and their ecological risks.  

Beyond biodegradation, *Sci. Total Environ.* has provided a venue for research on the detection, prevalence, and distribution of microplastics in diverse environments. This includes studies on microplastics in terrestrial systems, marine ecosystems, and even in animal-based traditional medicinal materials. The journal has also published research on emerging waste streams, such as the environmental impacts of discarded COVID-19 face masks, and has contributed comprehensive reviews that synthesize knowledge on plastic waste sources, pathways, ecological risks, and mitigation strategies.  

The scope of *Sci. Total Environ.* extends well beyond plastics, encompassing broader environmental issues such as microbial diversity in contaminated soils, pollutant interactions, ecosystem health, and sustainability strategies. By integrating experimental studies, applied research, and critical reviews, the journal fosters a holistic understanding of environmental challenges and potential solutions.  

Overall, *Science of the Total Environment* is regarded as a leading international journal in environmental research. It is particularly noted for its influential contributions to the study of plastic pollution, biodegradation mechanisms, pollutant interactions, and the ecological impacts of synthetic materials, thereby supporting the development of sustainable approaches to address global environmental issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Sci. Total Environ. (Science of the Total Environment) is the journal where the study on biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails was published.</data>
