<node id="Liu, Q.">
  <data key="d0">Liu, Q.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Liu, Q. is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of plastics and the role of insect gut microbiomes in this process. Liu, Q. has contributed extensively to studies involving *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), particularly in examining how its gut microbiome responds to and facilitates the degradation of synthetic polymers such as polystyrene. This includes co-authorship of multiple studies investigating the microbial community dynamics within the mealworm gut during plastic consumption, as well as how shifts in diet influence microbial composition and function. In 2021, Liu, Q. co-authored research that provided detailed insights into the gut microbiome’s adaptive responses to plastic-based diets, highlighting the potential of mealworms as a biological model for plastic biodegradation. Beyond this focus, Liu, Q. has also contributed to research on analytical methods for the detection, analysis, and characterization of nanomaterials in environmental contexts, reflecting a broader interest in environmental science and material interactions. Collectively, Liu, Q.’s work advances understanding of insect-microbe interactions in plastic degradation and contributes to the development of methodologies for assessing environmental pollutants.&lt;SEP&gt;Liu, Q. is a co-author of a 2021 study on the response of yellow mealworm gut microbiome to diet shifts during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
