<node id="Peng, B.-Y.">
  <data key="d0">Peng, B.-Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng, B.-Y. is a researcher and author recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation by insect larvae, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus). Their work focuses on investigating how various types of plastics, including petroleum-based polymers such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), as well as biodegradable plastics like polylactic acid (PLA), can be biologically degraded through insect-mediated processes.  

A central theme of Peng, B.-Y.’s research is the exploration of the mechanisms and efficiency of plastic degradation in insect larvae. This includes examining how physical characteristics of plastics, such as polymer size, influence the biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms. By conducting comparative studies across different plastic types, Peng, B.-Y. has provided insights into the metabolic capacity of insect larvae to process both conventional and biodegradable polymers, thereby expanding the understanding of their ecological role in plastic waste reduction.  

Peng, B.-Y. has co-authored several influential studies in this field. These include a 2018 study on the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation in yellow mealworms, research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by both dark and yellow mealworms, and investigations into the ability of Zophobas atratus larvae to degrade polystyrene and polyethylene. Additionally, Peng, B.-Y. contributed to a 2020 study on the biodegradation of PVC in Tenebrio molitor larvae, further broadening the scope of insect-based plastic degradation research.  

Through this body of work, Peng, B.-Y. has played a key role in advancing the emerging field of biological plastic degradation. Their research highlights the potential of insect larvae as a sustainable and ecologically relevant approach to mitigating plastic pollution, offering valuable insights into alternative strategies for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng, B.-Y. is an author of a study on the biodegradation of polystyrene and low-density polyethylene by Zophobas atratus larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
