</node>
<node id="Chen, Z.">
  <data key="d0">Chen, Z.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen, Z. is a researcher and scientific author whose work integrates environmental biotechnology, entomology, and bioinformatics, with a particular emphasis on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics by insects. A central theme of Chen’s research is the ability of insect larvae—especially mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*, including both dark and yellow variants) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*)—to degrade persistent synthetic polymers. Chen has co-authored multiple studies demonstrating that these larvae can process and break down plastics such as polystyrene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene, and polylactic acid (PLA). These investigations not only document the extent of degradation but also explore the metabolic, microbial, and ecological mechanisms that enable insects to digest and transform these otherwise resistant materials.  

Among Chen’s notable contributions are studies showing the biodegradation of polystyrene by dark and yellow mealworms, the oxidative degradation of polyethylene in superworms, and the breakdown of PVC by *T. molitor* larvae, including a 2020 study that provided significant insights into insect-based strategies for plastic waste management. Research on PLA degradation further highlights Chen’s interest in both petroleum-derived and biobased plastics, reflecting a comprehensive approach to understanding insect-mediated plastic biodegradation.  

In addition to applied biodegradation research, Chen, Z. has contributed to the field of computational biology and genomics. This includes authorship of a referenced work on transcriptome assembly from RNA-Seq data, underscoring expertise in bioinformatics and molecular data analysis. This dual focus on experimental biodegradation and computational genomics positions Chen as a multidisciplinary scholar bridging environmental sustainability with biological data science.  

Chen’s research portfolio also extends to related environmental biotechnology topics, such as a 2018 study on hyperthermophilic composting and the removal of antibiotic resistance genes, further demonstrating engagement with microbial ecology and waste treatment processes.  

Overall, Chen, Z. is recognized as a frequent co-author of scientific studies that advance understanding of insect-driven plastic degradation and its potential applications in sustainable waste management. By combining experimental studies on insect larvae with contributions to transcriptomic methodologies, Chen has established a body of work that informs both practical environmental solutions and broader biological research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387152</data>
</node>
<node id="Chen, J.">
  <data key="d0">Chen, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Chen, J. is a researcher and co-author recognized for extensive contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of biological systems—especially insect larvae—in breaking down synthetic polymers. Their work has focused on understanding how species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), dark mealworms, and *Zophobas atratus* (superworms) can metabolize and degrade persistent plastics including polystyrene, polylactic acid (PLA), polyethylene, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC).  

A central theme in Chen, J.’s research is the biodegradation of polystyrene. They have co-authored multiple studies investigating how yellow and dark mealworms ingest and metabolize this widely used plastic, examining factors such as polymer size, physical properties, and structural characteristics that influence degradation efficiency. Their work has also extended to PLA, demonstrating that *Tenebrio molitor* larvae are capable of degrading this biodegradable polymer, thereby broadening the scope of insect-mediated processes beyond polystyrene. In addition, Chen, J. has contributed to research on PVC degradation in mealworms, further highlighting the potential of insect-based systems to address diverse types of plastic waste.  

Beyond insect-focused studies, Chen, J. has co-authored research on microbial contributions to plastic degradation. This includes genome sequencing of *Bacillus* sp. YP1, a bacterium capable of degrading polyethylene, which complements insect-based findings by illuminating microbial pathways that could be harnessed for waste management.  

Chen, J. has also investigated the environmental implications of these biodegradation processes. Their studies have explored the persistence of residual plastics, the generation of microplastics, and the potential toxicity associated with insect-mediated degradation. These findings underscore both the opportunities and challenges of using biological systems to mitigate plastic pollution. Additionally, Chen, J. has co-authored work on the effects of environmental contaminants, such as phenanthrene, on earthworms, reflecting a broader interest in soil ecology and pollutant interactions with invertebrates.  

Through these collaborative efforts, Chen, J. has advanced scientific understanding of insect- and microbe-based biodegradation pathways. Their research provides valuable insights into sustainable strategies for managing plastic waste and contributes to the development of innovative approaches in environmental biotechnology and circular economy solutions to address global plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Chen, J. is an author of a study on biodegradation of plastics by Zophobas atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Frequent co-author of studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on effects of phenanthrene on earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Chen, J. is an author of a study on the biodegradation of polystyrene and low-density polyethylene by Zophobas atratus larvae.</data>
