<node id="Zhou, X.">
  <data key="d0">Zhou, X.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhou, X. is a researcher and author recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, with a particular emphasis on the role of insect larvae as biological agents in this process. Their work has focused on evaluating the ability of various insect species, including dark and yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor and related larvae) as well as Zophobas atratus larvae, to degrade a wide range of synthetic polymers. These polymers include polystyrene, polyethylene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polylactic acid, which are among the most prevalent and environmentally persistent plastics.  

Through a series of co-authored studies, Zhou, X. has investigated the mechanisms, efficiency, and influencing factors of insect-mediated plastic degradation. This research has explored how variables such as polymer type, particle size, and larval age affect degradation rates, as well as the extent to which residual plastics persist after digestion. Zhou, X. has also examined the potential toxicity of microplastic breakdown products, thereby addressing both the biological and environmental implications of insect-based plastic metabolism.  

In addition to studies on mealworms and Zophobas atratus, Zhou, X. has contributed to research on the biodegradation of polystyrene and low-density polyethylene, providing evidence of the capacity of insect larvae to process different classes of plastics. Their work has highlighted both the promise and the limitations of using insect larvae as a sustainable waste management strategy, offering insights into the feasibility of applying this approach to mitigate global plastic pollution.  

Beyond insect-mediated plastic degradation, Zhou, X. has also co-authored research in related areas of environmental science, including a study on bioenergy production involving microalgae and graphene oxide quantum dots. This reflects a broader interest in sustainable technologies and environmental remediation strategies.  

Overall, Zhou, X.’s body of work has advanced scientific understanding of insect-based biodegradation and its potential role in addressing the pressing issue of plastic waste. By integrating perspectives from applied entomology, environmental science, and waste management, Zhou, X. has contributed valuable knowledge to the development of innovative and sustainable approaches for reducing plastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
