<node id="Sun, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Sun, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Sun, Y. is a researcher whose work spans environmental microbiology, biodegradation, and ecological interactions, with a strong emphasis on plastic pollution and its biological remediation. A central focus of Sun, Y.’s research is the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene and polyethylene, through the activity of insect larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) and *Zophobas atratus*. These studies investigate how insect digestive systems contribute to the breakdown of plastics, including the fragmentation of polymers, the persistence of residual microplastics, and the potential toxicity associated with their degradation. Sun, Y. has also co-authored research on the influence of polymer size and structural characteristics on the efficiency of biodegradation, providing mechanistic insights into how molecular properties affect biological processing of plastics.  

In addition to insect-mediated degradation, Sun, Y. has contributed to broader investigations of microplastics, including their environmental fate, bioaccumulation, and interactions with other pollutants. This includes studies on the adsorption and desorption behavior of oxytetracycline on different types of microplastics, such as polylactic acid, particularly under conditions of microbial adhesion and degradation. Sun, Y. has also co-authored a critical review on the interactions between microplastics and soil fauna, highlighting the ecological consequences of microplastic contamination in terrestrial ecosystems.  

Beyond plastics research, Sun, Y. has explored plant–microbe interactions, with work examining the role of the rhizomicrobiome in nutrient acquisition in wild rice, thereby linking microbial ecology to plant growth and nutrient cycling. Additionally, Sun, Y. has co-authored biomedical research, including a 2022 study on *Enterobacter ludwigii* and its protective role in colitis, reflecting an interest in host–microbe relationships beyond environmental contexts.  

Overall, Sun, Y.’s body of work integrates themes of environmental sustainability, waste management, and microbial ecology. By investigating both the biological pathways of plastic degradation and the ecological and health implications of microplastics, Sun, Y. contributes to advancing knowledge on pollution mitigation strategies and the broader interactions between microbes, plants, animals, and synthetic materials.</data>
