<node id="Ding, M.-Q.">
  <data key="d0">Ding, M.-Q.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ding, M.-Q. is a researcher and co-author whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics and organic waste by insect larvae, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus). Their research contributions span multiple studies investigating the capacity of these insects to degrade common plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene (including low-density polyethylene), and polypropylene. Ding, M.-Q. has co-authored studies that not only confirm the ability of mealworms to biodegrade these polymers but also explore the underlying biological and microbial mechanisms that facilitate this process.  

In addition to plastics, Ding, M.-Q. has contributed to research on the reduction of lignocellulosic crop waste using Tenebrio molitor, highlighting the broader potential of insect larvae in bioconversion and waste management. Their work also extends to examining the gut microbiome of Tenebrio larvae, with a particular focus on its associations with carbon and nitrogen metabolism during polyethylene biodegradation. This line of research provides insights into the microbial and metabolic pathways that support plastic degradation within insect digestive systems.  

Beyond experimental studies, Ding, M.-Q. is also a co-author of a 2023 bibliometric analysis that reviews and synthesizes the growing body of scientific literature on plastic biodegradation. This contribution reflects an interest not only in experimental findings but also in mapping the development, trends, and research directions of the field as a whole.  

Overall, Ding, M.-Q. is recognized for advancing knowledge on insect-mediated biodegradation of plastics and organic waste, with research that integrates experimental validation, microbial ecology, and bibliometric analysis to address environmental challenges associated with plastic pollution and agricultural residues.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the polypropylene biodegradation study.&lt;SEP&gt;Ding, M.-Q. is a co-author of a study on confirmation of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
