<node id="Xing, D.-F.">
  <data key="d0">Xing, D.-F.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xing, D.-F. is a researcher and co-author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly through the activity of insect larvae such as mealworms and superworms. Their contributions span several key studies that have advanced understanding of how different types of plastics can be biologically degraded. Xing, D.-F. has co-authored research confirming the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in mealworms, providing evidence that these larvae are capable of breaking down one of the most widely used plastics. In addition, they have contributed to studies demonstrating the biodegradation of polystyrene, another persistent plastic pollutant, further supporting the role of insect larvae in mitigating plastic waste.  

Beyond LDPE and polystyrene, Xing, D.-F. has also co-authored investigations into the biodegradation of polypropylene, including a 2021 study that examined the capacity of both mealworms and superworms to degrade this material. These studies collectively highlight the potential of insect-based biodegradation as a biological approach to addressing plastic pollution.  

In addition to experimental research, Xing, D.-F. has contributed to broader analyses of the field. Notably, they are a co-author of a 2023 bibliometric analysis of publications on plastic biodegradation, which provides an overview of research trends, key contributors, and emerging directions in this area of study.  

Overall, Xing, D.-F.’s body of work reflects a consistent focus on the biodegradation of plastics, with particular emphasis on the role of insect larvae as biological agents capable of breaking down synthetic polymers such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and polypropylene. Their contributions encompass both empirical confirmation of biodegradation processes and meta-level assessments of the scientific literature, positioning them as an active contributor to the growing field of plastic biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
