<node id="Ren, N.-Q.">
  <data key="d0">Ren, N.-Q.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ren, N.-Q. is a researcher and co-author recognized for contributions to the study of biodegradation and sustainable waste management strategies, with a particular focus on plastic degradation by insect larvae. Their work has prominently explored the role of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and polystyrene. Ren, N.-Q. has co-authored studies confirming that mealworms are capable of ingesting and breaking down these plastics, thereby providing evidence for the potential of insect-based approaches to mitigate plastic pollution. This body of research has highlighted both the ubiquity and effectiveness of polystyrene digestion in yellow mealworms and the broader implications of insect-mediated biodegradation for addressing global plastic waste challenges.  

In addition to research on plastic degradation, Ren, N.-Q. has contributed to studies examining chloride ions in wastewater, reflecting a broader interest in environmental engineering and sustainable waste treatment processes. Their work spans multiple investigations into waste biodegradation and the development of sustainable strategies for managing environmental pollutants. Collectively, Ren, N.-Q.’s research emphasizes innovative biological and ecological approaches to waste management, particularly through leveraging natural organisms such as insect larvae to address persistent environmental contaminants.&lt;SEP&gt;Ren, N.-Q. is one of the authors of a 2023 bibliometric analysis of publications on biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of multiple studies on plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Ren, N.-Q. is a co-author of a study on confirmation of polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.</data>
