<node id="Xia, M.">
  <data key="d0">Xia, M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xia, M. is a researcher and author whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of plastics and the environmental impacts of microplastics. Xia, M. has contributed to multiple scientific studies investigating the potential of biological systems to degrade synthetic polymers, with particular emphasis on polystyrene. A significant portion of this research centers on the role of superworms, specifically *Zophobas atratus*, in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene. These studies explore how superworms ingest and break down polystyrene, providing insights into the mechanisms and potential applications of plastic biodegradation in waste management and environmental remediation. Through co-authorship in several publications, Xia, M. has advanced understanding of the processes by which superworms contribute to the degradation of persistent plastics, highlighting both the biological pathways involved and the implications for reducing plastic pollution. In addition to this work, Xia, M. has also been involved in research addressing the toxicity of microplastics, further contributing to the broader field of plastic pollution and its ecological consequences. Collectively, Xia, M.’s research underscores the potential of biological organisms in mitigating plastic waste and provides a foundation for future studies on sustainable solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Xia, M. is a co-author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene by superworms.</data>
