<node id="Tongji University">
  <data key="d0">Tongji University</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
  <data key="d2">Tongji University is a comprehensive higher education institution located in Shanghai, China, recognized for its strong academic and research contributions. The university hosts a range of specialized schools and research centers, including the School of Materials Science and Engineering, the School of Environmental Science and Engineering, and the School of Life Science and Technology. It is also home to the State Key Laboratory of Pollution Control and Resource Reuse, a nationally significant research facility that supports advanced studies in environmental protection and sustainable resource management.  

The institution is closely affiliated with numerous researchers and authors who contribute to scientific studies across diverse fields. Notable individuals associated with Tongji University include Yalei Zhang, Bo-Yu Peng, Shaoze Xiao, Ying Sun, Jiabin Chen, and Xuefei Zhou. In addition to providing institutional support, Tongji University has been acknowledged for the contributions of its faculty and researchers, such as Mr. Boyu Peng, who offered valuable suggestions to ongoing research efforts.  

Through its academic schools, research laboratories, and collaborations, Tongji University plays a central role in advancing knowledge in materials science, environmental science, life sciences, and related disciplines. Its integration of education, research, and applied innovation positions it as a leading institution within Shanghai and an influential contributor to both national and international scientific communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Tongji University is the institution affiliated with multiple authors, including Yalei Zhang, Bo-Yu Peng, Ying Sun, Shaoze Xiao, Jiabin Chen, and Xuefei Zhou.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387695</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Plastic biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08</data>
  <data key="d2">Darkling beetle species are capable of biodegrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down plastics by insects and their gut microorganisms, studied through various analytical methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down plastics by microorganisms, insect larvae, and their gut microbiomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process by which macroinvertebrates, especially insect larvae, break down petroleum-based plastics and biodegradable polymers through gut microbial mechanisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation is the process of breaking down plastics by biological organisms, central to the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the microbial process of breaking down plastics, studied under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385660</data>
</node>
