<node id="Poland">
  <data key="d0">Poland</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Poland is a Central European country that serves as the geographic and institutional setting for a range of academic, industrial, and scientific activities highlighted in the study. It is home to several key cities and institutions that played a central role in the research, including Olsztyn, Lublin, and universities such as the University of Warmia and Mazury, Wrocław University of Science and Technology, and the Institute of Agrophysics of the Polish Academy of Sciences (PAS). These institutions represent important centers of higher education and scientific research in Poland, and they were directly affiliated with the authors and contributors to the study.  

In addition to its academic infrastructure, Poland hosts several companies and organizations that provided industrial and commercial context for the research. These include Bugstore, Swisspor, Kupiec, and the Cricket Farm, as well as the Polish operations of Sigma Aldrich, a global supplier of laboratory reagents and chemicals. Together, these entities supplied materials, expertise, and practical applications that supported the scientific investigations.  

The study conducted in Poland focused on waste management and biodegradation processes, particularly examining the ability of mealworms to consume and degrade different types of plastics such as polystyrene (PS) foam, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polylactic acid (PLA). This research underscored Poland’s role in advancing sustainable approaches to plastic waste reduction and environmental protection. Beyond waste management, Poland has also been identified as a location where edible insects are studied as an alternative protein source in animal diets, reflecting the country’s engagement with innovative and applied environmental sciences.  

Overall, Poland is depicted as both the geographic setting and the institutional base for the study, integrating contributions from universities, research institutes, and private companies. It emerges as a hub of scientific inquiry and industrial collaboration in Europe, particularly in the fields of waste management, biodegradation, sustainable food systems, and applied environmental research.</data>
