<node id="Bacteroides">
  <data key="d0">Bacteroides</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacteroides is a taxonomic group of bacteria that has been described at multiple levels, including genus, class, and phylum, and is consistently recognized as an important component of insect gut microbiomes. In particular, Bacteroides is one of the dominant bacterial phyla identified in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms), where it plays a significant role in digestion and microbial community structure. Studies have shown that Bacteroides can occur in high abundance in mealworm gut ecosystems, and its prevalence has also been linked to specific dietary conditions, such as higher proportions observed in OAT and CEL variants and associations with Diet 3.  

Beyond mealworms, Bacteroides has also been isolated from termites, where it is associated with lignocellulose degradation, highlighting its functional role in breaking down complex plant materials. This metabolic capacity aligns with its broader ecological importance in aiding host organisms with digestion of otherwise recalcitrant substrates. Additionally, Bacteroides has been identified as part of the gut microbiome involved in plastic biodegradation, suggesting a potential role in novel metabolic processes that contribute to the breakdown of synthetic polymers.  

In research datasets, Bacteroides is often listed as a bacterial genus with measured abundance across different substrates, reflecting its adaptability and responsiveness to environmental and dietary conditions. Its consistent presence across diverse insect hosts and diets underscores its ecological versatility and functional significance in microbial communities.  

Overall, Bacteroides is a key bacterial taxon within insect gut microbiomes, particularly in mealworms and termites, where it contributes to nutrient processing, degradation of complex organic matter, and potentially to plastic biodegradation. Its abundance and distribution vary with diet and substrate, making it an important focus of microbiome and biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is one of the dominant bacterial phyla identified in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a bacterial class with higher proportions in OAT and CEL variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroides is a bacterial genus listed in the dataset with measured abundance across substrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387941</data>
</node>
<node id="Firmicutes">
  <data key="d0">Firmicutes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum that plays a central role in the gut microbiomes of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and superworms, where it is consistently detected across diverse dietary and experimental conditions. It is recognized as one of the dominant microbial groups in these insect digestive systems, with its relative abundance strongly influenced by the type of substrate or diet provided. Studies have shown that Firmicutes populations can increase under certain feeding conditions, such as diets based on bran, polyurethane (PU), corn starch (CS), and polyethylene (PE), particularly in oxidized PE (PE-oxo) variants. In contrast, their abundance may decline in larvae maintained on cellulose-based (CB) diets or under specific polystyrene (PS) feeding regimes, indicating that the phylum is highly responsive to nutritional and environmental factors.  

Functionally, Firmicutes is of particular interest because it encompasses bacterial classes such as Bacilli, which include strains capable of degrading synthetic polymers like polystyrene. This suggests that members of the phylum may contribute to the biodegradation of plastics within insect gut ecosystems, a finding that has attracted attention in research on sustainable waste management and insect-mediated plastic degradation. In addition to their potential role in polymer breakdown, Firmicutes also include species with nitrogen-fixing capabilities, further highlighting their ecological versatility and contribution to host nutrition.  

Experimental observations reinforce the importance of Firmicutes in plastic-fed larvae. For example, they have been reported as predominant after 20 days of PS feeding, with higher relative abundance in PS-fed groups compared to certain controls, though in some cases their levels decrease relative to bran-fed larvae. Such variability underscores the phylum’s adaptability and sensitivity to substrate type, as well as its dynamic role in shaping gut microbial community structure under different dietary pressures.  

Overall, Firmicutes is a key bacterial phylum in the gut microbiomes of mealworms and superworms. Its abundance is diet-dependent, and its inclusion of species with polymer-degrading and nitrogen-fixing functions positions it as a central microbial group of ecological and applied significance. This makes Firmicutes a focal point in studies of insect gut ecology, microbial adaptation to recalcitrant substrates, and the potential for biological contributions to plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum dominant in the gut microbiome of bran-fed and PS-L-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is one of the dominant bacterial phyla identified in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum present in the digestive tracts of mealworm larvae, with higher proportions in PE-oxo variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum listed in the dataset with abundance values across different substrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
