<node id="Actinobacteria">
  <data key="d0">Actinobacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Actinobacteria is a major bacterial phylum that plays a central role in the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae and related species such as *T. obscurus*. It is consistently identified as one of the dominant microbial groups inhabiting the digestive tract of these insects, with its abundance varying depending on dietary substrates. Studies have shown that Actinobacteria are particularly enriched when larvae are reared on substrates such as oatmeal waste, cellulose-based materials, and synthetic polymers including polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE). Their presence has also been documented in larvae fed on cardboard (CT) and high-density polyethylene (HDPE), with relative abundances typically ranging from 4–8% in some experimental groups.  

Within this phylum, several genera have been highlighted for their ecological and functional significance. *Rhodococcus* species, for example, contribute to host nutrition by supplying essential amino acids, while *Kocuria* species have been reported as overrepresented in mealworms fed with polystyrene. Other unclassified Actinobacteria, including members of Corynebacterium, have been associated with nitrogen metabolism in *T. obscurus* larvae, further underscoring the metabolic diversity of this group.  

Beyond their nutritional contributions, Actinobacteria are notable for their biodegradation potential. Certain genera within the phylum harbor genes such as *alkB*, which are involved in hydrocarbon degradation, and their sequences have been compared to those found in other degradative bacteria like *Klebsiella oxytoca*. Actinobacteria have also been implicated in the breakdown of styrene and the degradation of HDPE, highlighting their ability to metabolize both natural and synthetic recalcitrant compounds. This metabolic versatility positions them as key players in the biodegradation of plastics and other persistent environmental pollutants.  

Overall, Actinobacteria represents a versatile and ecologically important bacterial phylum within the mealworm gut microbiome. It contributes to host nutrition through amino acid and nitrogen metabolism, while simultaneously supporting environmental processes through the biodegradation of plastics and complex organic materials. This dual role makes Actinobacteria a microbial group of significant interest in the study of insect-microbe interactions, sustainable waste management, and bioremediation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a bacterial phylum dominant in mealworm gut microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is one of the dominant bacterial phyla identified in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a dominant bacterial phylum in the digestive tracts of mealworm larvae, more abundant in OAT and CEL variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a bacterial class listed in the dataset with measured abundance across different substrates such as oatmeal waste, cardboard, polyethylene, and polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a bacterial phylum, with species such as Rhodococcus sp., that supply insects with amino acids.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
