</node>
<node id="Lactococcus">
  <data key="d0">Lactococcus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Lactococcus is a bacterial genus within the phylum Firmicutes and a member of the lactic acid bacteria group, characterized as facultative anaerobic fermentative microbes. It is a prominent and frequently detected taxon in the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and related larvae, where it has been consistently identified across different gut regions, including the anterior gut. Its presence is strongly influenced by diet, making it a key genus in studies of diet-driven microbiome shifts in insects.  

In the context of synthetic polymer consumption, Lactococcus has been repeatedly associated with larvae fed on plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), and polypropylene (PP). It is particularly enriched in PS-fed superworms and yellow mealworms, where it often shows high relative abundance. Notably, Lactococcus was the only bacterial genus consistently linked to PU diets throughout experimental trials, suggesting a unique persistence and adaptability under these conditions. Its abundance also increased significantly when mealworms were fed combined diets, such as PE with wheat bran, but it was absent in wheat bran-only diet groups, highlighting its dependence on specific substrates. Beyond plastics, Lactococcus has also been observed to increase in abundance following the ingestion of anthropogenic materials such as face masks, further underscoring its role in microbial responses to synthetic compounds.  

The consistent enrichment of Lactococcus in plastic-fed larvae suggests that it may play a role in the microbial processes enabling mealworms to tolerate, and potentially metabolize, synthetic polymers. Previous studies have also associated Lactococcus with the degradation of polylactic acid (PLA), supporting its potential involvement in polymer breakdown. In addition to its possible role in biodegradation, Lactococcus likely contributes to gut health and stability within the mealworm microbiome. As a lactic acid bacterium, it can create favorable gut conditions that support microbial balance and digestive function, thereby maintaining overall gut ecosystem stability.  

Overall, Lactococcus represents a key microbial genus in the gut of *T. molitor* and related larvae. It is notable for its persistence across gut regions, its strong diet-dependent abundance patterns, its adaptability to synthetic and combined diets, and its potential dual role in both maintaining gut health and contributing to the biodegradation of synthetic polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
