<node id="Brandon et al. (2018)">
  <data key="d0">Brandon et al. (2018)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon et al. (2018) is a widely cited research study that examined the capacity of mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*) to degrade synthetic plastics, with a focus on polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene (PE). The study is considered one of the early empirical investigations into insect-mediated plastic biodegradation and has served as a foundational reference in this emerging field. Brandon and colleagues conducted controlled feeding experiments in which mealworms were provided with plastics either alone or in combination with bran, enabling the assessment of both consumption rates and physiological impacts on the larvae. They reported that mealworms were capable of depolymerizing PS and LDPE, suggesting a relatively broad ability to break down synthetic polymers.  

A key contribution of the study was its attempt to establish a full mass balance for the degradation of PE and PS mixtures, documenting plastic mass loss and proposing pathways of microbial and enzymatic involvement. However, this claim has been met with skepticism in later analyses, as subsequent research has questioned the completeness of degradation and the accuracy of the reported balance. Despite these debates, the degradation rates reported by Brandon et al. have been used as comparative benchmarks in later studies.  

The research also explored the biological consequences of plastic ingestion. Mealworms maintained on nutrient-poor, plastic-only diets exhibited reduced survival and fertility, highlighting the nutritional limitations of plastics as a food source. At the microbial level, Brandon et al. identified significant shifts in gut microbiota associated with plastic consumption. They reported an increased prevalence of *Spiroplasma* species and higher abundances of *Enterococcus* spp., *Listeria* spp., and *Nitrospira defluvii* in PS-fed larvae. Additionally, they identified *Citrobacter* sp. and *Kosakonia* sp. as bacterial taxa potentially linked to PE and PS degradation. These findings underscored the role of gut microbial communities in facilitating or enhancing the breakdown of synthetic polymers.  

Beyond microbial associations, the study also contributed insights into the structural changes of polystyrene during digestion, providing evidence of polymer modification within the insect gut. By linking bacterial metabolism to polymer degradation, Brandon et al. (2018) helped establish a framework for understanding the biological and biochemical mechanisms underlying insect-driven plastic processing.  

Overall, Brandon et al. (2018) represents a pivotal and influential study in the field of plastic biodegradation. While its conclusions regarding complete degradation and mass balance remain contested, the work provided critical early data on polymer depolymerization by insects, highlighted the importance of gut microbiota in the process, and raised important questions about the ecological and biotechnological potential of insect-mediated plastic degradation. It continues to serve as both a reference point and a subject of debate in ongoing research.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al. (2018) is a cited study reporting LDPE conversion by T. molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brandon et al. (2018) is a cited author group studying mealworm larvae degradation of plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
