<node id="Principal Component Analysis (PCA)">
  <data key="d0">Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a multivariate statistical method widely applied in biological and ecological research to explore complex datasets and uncover patterns of correlation among variables. In the context of microbiological and nutritional studies, PCA has been used to determine relationships between substrates, microorganisms, and enzymatic activity, as well as to analyze gut microbiome data and broader microbial community structures. By reducing data dimensionality while retaining the most significant sources of variation, PCA enables researchers to identify key factors driving differences among experimental groups.  

In dietary and feeding studies, PCA has been employed to assess discrimination between different feeding strategies and to test correlations between diets and biological parameters such as insect development, protein levels, fatty acid composition, and microbial presence. It has also been used to identify associations between diet groups and microbiome traits, providing insights into how nutritional inputs shape microbial communities and host physiology.  

Specifically, PCA has been applied to the study of microbial communities in superworms and yellow mealworms fed with bran or plastics, helping to reveal how alternative feeding substrates influence gut microbiota composition and metabolic activity. The method often relies on similarity measures such as the Pearson correlation-similarity matrix to evaluate relationships between observations, thereby facilitating the visualization of clustering patterns and group separations within complex datasets.  

Overall, Principal Component Analysis (PCA) serves as a powerful statistical tool for simplifying high-dimensional biological data, enabling the identification of meaningful correlations and distinctions across diets, microbial communities, and metabolic parameters. Its application in microbiome and feeding studies highlights its value in uncovering interactions between diet, host development, and microbial ecology.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used in the study for modeling differences between metabolic profiles of insect samples under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was used to evaluate PS biodegradation, biomass protein, frass nitrogen, and cation concentrations, showing good model fit.&lt;SEP&gt;A statistical method used to analyze gut bacterial community differences among diet groups.&lt;SEP&gt;PCA was used to analyze gut fungal microbiome shifts in yellow mealworms under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was used to analyze microbial community data.&lt;SEP&gt;Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to determine relationships between substrates, microorganisms, and enzymatic activity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387955</data>
</node>
