<node id="Bacteroidetes">
  <data key="d0">Bacteroidetes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum that plays an important role in the gut microbiomes of various organisms, including insects such as mealworms and termites. Within termite digestive tracts, Bacteroidetes contribute to nutrient cycling, highlighting their ecological significance in the breakdown and transformation of organic matter. In mealworm larvae, this phylum is consistently detected across different dietary treatments, though its relative abundance varies depending on the substrate consumed.  

Studies have shown that Bacteroidetes abundance is diet-dependent in mealworms. For example, their representation is lower in larvae fed a polyurethane (PU) diet, while an increase is observed in groups fed polystyrene (PS), corn starch (CS), ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA), and polypropylene (PP). In EVA-fed yellow mealworms, Bacteroidetes reached a relative abundance of 8.91%, indicating a notable dietary influence on microbial composition. Comparisons between PS- and cellulose-treated (CT) groups further demonstrate this variability: Bacteroidetes were present in both, but their abundance was substantially higher in CT samples, reaching 26%, while they were nearly absent in PS-fed samples.  

Overall, Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum that exhibits flexible abundance patterns in response to different feeding substrates, reflecting its adaptability and potential functional roles in digestion and nutrient processing. Its presence across diverse diets and host organisms underscores its ecological importance in microbial communities associated with decomposition and gut health.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum dominant in mealworm gut microbial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum present in the gut microbiome of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum found in the digestive tracts of mealworm larvae, with proportions differing across feeding variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum represented in the dataset with abundance values across multiple substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteroidetes is a bacterial phylum found in termites, involved in nutrient cycling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
