<node id="Gammaproteobacteria">
  <data key="d0">Gammaproteobacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class within the phylum **Proteobacteria**, encompassing diverse species such as *Pseudomonas* sp. and *Cronobacter* sp., and represented in some studies by the order *Enterobacteriales*. This class is notable for its ecological and metabolic versatility, including the ability to degrade complex organic compounds such as lignocellulose and petroleum hydrocarbons. Gammaproteobacteria has been observed in various experimental and natural contexts, often showing significant abundance and functional importance.  

In microbiome studies, Gammaproteobacteria is frequently detected as a dominant taxonomic group. For example, it is one of the major bacterial classes found in the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm), where it plays a role in digestion and nutrient processing. Its abundance varies depending on diet and substrate exposure: it is enriched in mealworms fed with polyvinyl chloride (PVC) and is dominant in polystyrene (PS) and control (CT) groups, with particularly high representation in PS samples, where it accounts for approximately 76% of the bacterial community. These findings highlight its adaptability to different feeding conditions and its potential involvement in plastic degradation or tolerance.  

In experimental datasets, Gammaproteobacteria is included as a tracked bacterial class with abundance values measured across multiple substrates, reflecting its relevance in comparative microbial ecology. It is also associated with specific feeding variants, such as PE-oxo and PE-reg, further underscoring its role in environments influenced by synthetic polymers.  

Overall, Gammaproteobacteria represents a metabolically diverse and ecologically significant bacterial class within Proteobacteria. Its presence across varied substrates, enrichment in plastic-fed insect gut microbiomes, and capacity to degrade both natural and anthropogenic compounds position it as an important group for understanding microbial adaptation, biodegradation, and ecosystem functioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class, with 11 strains identified from mealworm intestines as potential polystyrene degraders.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a class of bacteria, with 11 strains identified in the study, classified into five families: Enterobacteriaceae, Yersiniaceae, Aeromonadaceae, Pseudomonadaceae, and Moraxellaceae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class characteristic of PE-oxo and PE-reg feeding variants.&lt;SEP&gt;Gammaproteobacteria is a bacterial class included in the dataset with abundance values across substrates.</data>
