<node id="Enterococcus">
  <data key="d0">Enterococcus</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterococcus is a bacterial genus within the phylum Firmicutes that is consistently identified as a prominent component of the gut microbiome in mealworms, particularly in *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and black mealworm larvae. It has been repeatedly reported as one of the predominant genera across multiple studies examining insect gut microbial communities. Within the mealworm digestive tract, Enterococcus is notably absent from the foregut and anterior midgut but is well represented in other gut regions, where it contributes significantly to the microbial composition.  

The genus demonstrates remarkable adaptability to different dietary conditions. It has been detected in mealworms fed on conventional substrates as well as in those maintained on specialized diets, including synthetic polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), and polystyrene (PS). In these plastic-feeding experiments, Enterococcus frequently emerges as a dominant taxon, suggesting a potential role in the microbial processes underlying plastic degradation. Its consistent presence across diet groups and its variable relative abundance depending on substrate type highlight its ecological flexibility and functional importance.  

Functionally, Enterococcus is described as an esterase-producing genus, capable of contributing to the breakdown of complex organic compounds. This enzymatic activity is relevant not only to the digestion of natural dietary components but also to the degradation of anthropogenic compounds such as insecticides and synthetic polymers. In particular, its association with PE, PU, and PS diets has led to the hypothesis that Enterococcus participates in or supports microbial pathways involved in polymer degradation.  

Overall, Enterococcus represents a common, abundant, and functionally significant bacterial genus in the gut microbiome of mealworms and other insect larvae. Its consistent detection across diverse feeding conditions, coupled with its enzymatic potential, underscores its importance in both natural digestive processes and the biodegradation of synthetic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a bacterial genus listed in the dataset with measured abundance across substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterococcus is a bacterial genus mentioned as producing esterases responsible for degradation of indoxacarb insecticide.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
