<node id="Entomoplasmatales">
  <data key="d0">Entomoplasmatales</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Entomoplasmatales is an order of bacteria within the class **Mollicutes**, represented in some studies by the family **Spiroplasmataceae**. This bacterial order is primarily associated with insects, where it often forms symbiotic relationships, including roles within the insect gut microbiota. While some members of Entomoplasmatales are considered part of the natural microbial community of insects, they are also sometimes regarded as undesirable microflora depending on the context of host health and diet.  

In ecological and experimental studies, Entomoplasmatales has been observed as a component of microbial communities linked to processes such as nitrogen fixation, highlighting its potential functional significance beyond simple host association. Quantitative analyses of microbial abundance have shown that Entomoplasmatales can represent an average of 1.1% of the bacterial community, with notably higher levels (up to 3.7%) in insects fed on polyethylene-oxo (PE-oxo) diets. This suggests that dietary conditions can strongly influence the prevalence of this bacterial order, with enrichment observed in plastic-associated feeding environments.  

Overall, Entomoplasmatales is a bacterial order of importance in insect-associated microbiomes, both for its symbiotic interactions and its variable abundance under different environmental and dietary conditions. Its presence across diverse substrates and its potential roles in host physiology and microbial community function make it a relevant taxonomic group for studies of insect-microbe interactions and microbial ecology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387970</data>
</node>
<node id="Spiroplasma">
  <data key="d0">Spiroplasma</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Spiroplasma is a bacterial genus belonging to the family **Spiroplasmataceae** within the phylum **Tenericutes**, and it represents one of the most abundant and consistently detected microbial taxa in the gut microbiome of mealworm larvae (*Tenebrio molitor*). It is particularly prevalent in the posterior gut and has been observed across a wide range of dietary treatments, including natural substrates such as bran and corn starch, as well as synthetic polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), polypropylene (PP), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET).  

The relative abundance of Spiroplasma varies depending on the diet. It is often more dominant in bran-fed larvae compared to those consuming PE or PS, though in some cases higher levels have been reported in PS- and corn starch-fed groups. Notably, in polypropylene (PP)-fed larvae, Spiroplasma has been recorded as a dominant genus, accounting for over 50% of the gut microbial community. This variability suggests that Spiroplasma responds dynamically to different substrates, reflecting its adaptability within the mealworm gut ecosystem.  

Functionally, Spiroplasma has been associated with the degradation of synthetic polymers, particularly PE, PET, and PP, highlighting its potential role in plastic biodegradation. This association has made it a focal point of research into microbial contributions to sustainable waste management and the development of biodegradation pathways for recalcitrant plastics. Its consistent presence across both natural and synthetic diets underscores its ecological importance in the mealworm gut and its potential utility in biotechnological applications.  

Although Spiroplasma is generally known as a pathogenic genus in other insects, it does not appear to be harmful to *T. molitor* larvae. Instead, it functions as a stable and often dominant member of their gut microbiota. Overall, Spiroplasma is a key bacterial genus in the mealworm gut, characterized by its widespread occurrence across diverse diets, its variable but often high abundance, and its potential functional role in the microbial degradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma is a bacterial genus mentioned in the genus-level abundance analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma is a genus of bacteria found in the hindgut of mealworms under oxygen-limited conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Spiroplasma is a bacterial genus listed in the dataset with measured abundance across substrates.</data>
