<node id="Larvae">
  <data key="d0">Larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Larvae are the immature developmental stage of insects, such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) and *Galleria mellonella*, and are widely used as experimental organisms in studies of nutrition, metabolism, and biodegradation. In scientific research, larvae serve as a model system for investigating insect development, dietary adaptation, and the biological breakdown of synthetic materials. Their physiological and metabolic responses to different diets have been extensively studied, including natural feeds such as wheat bran and artificial substrates like polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), wax, and other synthetic polymers.  

Experimental studies involving larvae typically measure survival rate, relative weight, pupation rate, biomass, frass production, and cannibalism incidence to assess their developmental outcomes under varying dietary conditions. Researchers also analyze their protein and fat content, oxidative stress responses, and microbiome composition to better understand how diet influences insect physiology. In particular, larvae have been used to evaluate the effects of feeding on plastics, both alone and in combination with bran or other substrates, with assessments including reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels, digestive enzyme activity, and gut tissue analysis.  

A central focus of larvae research is their role in plastic biodegradation. Larvae have demonstrated the ability to consume and metabolize synthetic polymers such as PS, cellulose-based substrates (CS), and carbon black (CB), with their frass examined to determine degradation efficiency and environmental impact. Feeding and incubation trials with PS microplastics, bran, and jelly powder have provided insights into both nutritional outcomes and the potential for waste management applications. The gut microbiota of larvae is considered a key factor in facilitating polymer degradation, and studies often investigate microbial contributions to this process.  

Overall, larvae represent a critical biological model for exploring the intersection of insect physiology, diet, and environmental sustainability. Their capacity to adapt to diverse feeding conditions, including plastic-rich diets, makes them valuable for advancing knowledge of insect biology while also offering potential applications in bioremediation and plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the immature stage of Tenebrio molitor studied for survival, mortality, and nutritional changes under different feeding conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae were collected, reared, and fed with different diets in the experiments to measure mortality, growth, and nutritional outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the organisms whose digestive tracts were studied for enzyme activity under different diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the developmental stage of T. molitor being analyzed in the study, specifically in relation to growth trajectories and metabolism.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae are the developmental stage of T. molitor before pupation, used in experiments to study growth and pupation thresholds.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
