<node id="Pseudomonas">
  <data key="d0">Pseudomonas</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Pseudomonas is a diverse and metabolically versatile bacterial genus within the phylum Proteobacteria, widely recognized for its ecological and biotechnological significance. As a Gram-negative genus, Pseudomonas is notable for its ability to degrade a wide range of synthetic polymers and xenobiotic compounds, positioning it as a key microbial group in bioremediation and sustainable waste management. Its enzymatic capacity, particularly through esterase production, enables the breakdown of insecticides, chemical pollutants, and various plastics, underscoring its environmental relevance.  

One of the most studied features of Pseudomonas is its role in plastic biodegradation. Members of this genus have been shown to metabolize polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and bioplastics. Polystyrene, which is highly resistant to natural decomposition, can be degraded with the assistance of Pseudomonas, especially when present in insect gut microbiomes. Studies have identified Pseudomonas in the digestive systems of superworms, yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), and the greater wax moth, where it contributes to the insects’ ability to process and degrade PS. In T. molitor, Pseudomonas has been isolated from larval gut samples, including those fed with PS, suggesting a functional role in facilitating plastic breakdown. Its abundance, however, varies with diet, showing decreased levels in PVC-fed larvae and significant shifts in PS-fed groups.  

Beyond PS, Pseudomonas has been associated with the degradation of other plastics such as PP and PE. It has been identified as dominant in polyolefin-fed groups, including PP-fed T. molitor larvae, and has been used in incubation experiments with UV-pre-treated PP films. These findings highlight its adaptability to different polymeric substrates. Furthermore, Pseudomonas exhibits synergistic growth with other bacterial genera, such as Bacillus, enhancing its capacity to degrade polypropylene and potentially other plastics.  

At the biochemical level, Pseudomonas possesses specialized enzymatic pathways for the utilization of styrene, the monomer of polystyrene, as a sole carbon source. This process involves membrane-bound styrene oxide isomerases (SOIs), which catalyze key steps in styrene catabolism. Through these pathways, Pseudomonas converts styrene and related compounds into intermediates that can be assimilated into central metabolic processes, effectively integrating plastic-derived substrates into its natural metabolism.  

Collectively, Pseudomonas represents a bacterial genus of considerable environmental and industrial importance. Its presence in insect gut microbiomes, dominance in plastic-fed microbial communities, enzymatic machinery for styrene and polymer degradation, and synergistic interactions with other microbes make it a central organism in the study of microbial biodegradation. These attributes position Pseudomonas as a promising candidate for advancing strategies in plastic waste reduction, environmental remediation, and the development of sustainable biotechnological applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a bacterial genus with nifH gene similarity to nitrogen-fixing bacteria found in mealworm gut microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a bacterial genus found in the intestine of Aedes albopictus.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a bacterial genus mentioned as producing esterases responsible for degradation of indoxacarb insecticide.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
