</node>
<node id="Bacillus">
  <data key="d0">Bacillus</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacillus is a bacterial genus within the family Bacillaceae, characterized as Gram-positive and widely recognized for its metabolic versatility and ecological significance. Members of this genus are capable of producing esterases and have been shown to degrade a variety of synthetic compounds, including insecticides, polystyrene (PS), styrene, and other plastics under aerobic conditions. Some Bacillus strains can utilize polystyrene as their sole carbon source, underscoring their potential role in plastic biodegradation and their relevance for biotechnological applications aimed at mitigating environmental pollution.  

Ecologically, Bacillus is frequently associated with the gut microbiota of insects, particularly in species such as Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), Tenebrio obscurus, and the greater wax moth. It has been consistently reported as one of the most common bacterial isolates in these systems, suggesting a central role in digestion and nutrient cycling. In mealworms, Bacillus abundance is influenced by diet, with higher prevalence in bran- and polystyrene-fed larvae compared to those fed on polyvinyl chloride (PVC). In T. obscurus larvae, Bacillus has been linked to nitrogen metabolism, while in greater wax moth larvae fed on PS diets, it is strongly associated with gut microbial activity related to plastic degradation.  

Beyond mealworms and wax moths, Bacillus has also been isolated from termite guts, where it is implicated in lignocellulose degradation, further highlighting its ecological adaptability and functional diversity. Phylogenetic and dendrogram analyses have confirmed its close relationships within the Bacillaceae family, including links to isolates such as Prestia megaterium S1 (PM S1).  

Overall, Bacillus represents a metabolically diverse and ecologically important bacterial genus. Its widespread presence in insect digestive systems, combined with its demonstrated ability to degrade plastics and other recalcitrant compounds, positions it as a key microbial group of interest for both ecological studies and applied research in biodegradation and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus refers to bacterial strains used in the incubation experiments with UV-pre-treated PP films.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a bacterial genus studied for its synergistic growth with Pseudomonas and its ability to degrade polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a bacterial genus found in mangrove and landfill environments that can degrade plastics such as polypropylene and polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a bacterial species found in landfill sediment that can decompose polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterium studied in co-culture with Acinetobacter for polyethylene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus is a bacterial genus mentioned as producing esterases responsible for degradation of indoxacarb insecticide.</data>
