<node id="T. molitor larvae">
  <data key="d0">T. molitor larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">T. molitor larvae, commonly referred to as mealworm larvae and representing the immature stage of the darkling beetle, have emerged as important biological models for the study of plastic biodegradation. These larvae are capable of chewing, ingesting, and metabolizing a wide range of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polylactic acid (PLA). In addition to synthetic polymers, they can also process natural lignocellulose-based materials. Their activity results in measurable reductions in polymer mass, visible structural alterations such as tunnels and holes, and the incorporation of plastic-derived carbon into their metabolism, underscoring their potential role in sustainable plastic waste management and environmental remediation.  

Experimental studies have shown that T. molitor larvae can survive and grow on plastic-based diets, though their growth performance, biomass accumulation, and pupation rates are generally reduced compared to those fed on nutritionally rich substrates like wheat bran or corn-based feed. For instance, larvae fed exclusively on PS or PP exhibit smaller body sizes and lower biomass, while PET degradation has been observed to be relatively more efficient, especially when supplemented with agar gels. Supplementation with natural feed sources consistently improves survival, growth, and degradation efficiency, indicating that plastics alone provide limited nutritional value.  

A key factor in the larvae’s plastic-degrading ability is the role of their gut microbiota. Studies comparing larvae fed on plastics, natural diets, or subjected to antibiotic treatments reveal that microbial communities contribute significantly to polymer depolymerization and biodegradation. Specific bacterial taxa, such as Lactococcus, have been strongly associated with PP degradation. Suppression of gut microbes through antibiotics often reduces degradation efficiency, although host digestive enzymes and gene expression changes also contribute independently, suggesting a synergistic interplay between host physiology and microbial metabolism.  

At the molecular and physiological level, exposure to plastics induces metabolic adaptations in T. molitor larvae. Transcriptomic analyses have identified changes in digestive enzyme activity, stress response pathways, and oxidative stress regulation, reflecting both defensive mechanisms against dietary stress and active participation in polymer degradation. These findings highlight the larvae’s capacity to adjust to plastic-based diets despite the physiological challenges they impose.  

T. molitor larvae have also been studied in comparison with other insect species, such as Zophobas atratus, with T. molitor generally being smaller but still effective in consuming and degrading plastics like PP and PS. They have been observed to process microplastics, including polyethylene (PE) films and PS foams, producing various degradation products.  

In summary, T. molitor larvae are insect larvae with demonstrated ability to biodegrade multiple synthetic polymers through a combination of mechanical chewing, ingestion, enzymatic activity, and microbial metabolism. While plastics alone do not fully sustain optimal growth, the larvae can survive on such diets and induce measurable structural and chemical changes in polymers. Their host–microbe system, metabolic flexibility, and demonstrated plastic-degrading potential make T. molitor larvae a valuable model organism for advancing eco-friendly strategies in plastic waste reduction and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae are one of the species used to consume and biodegrade PE foams.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae (mealworms) are studied for their nutritional potential and their ability to process biodegradable, hardly biodegradable, and even non-biodegradable plastics. They survive on plastics like PS, PVC, and PLA, though these are not efficient energy sources.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae (mealworm larvae) were the subject of the study, used to test the effects of polymer waste metabolism on microbiome and digestive enzymes.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae are the experimental organisms used in the study to test the effects of different diets and starvation conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae are mealworm larvae whose development depends on reserve dynamics and are studied for their ability to degrade polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fe