<node id="Plastic Waste">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Waste</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic Waste refers to discarded plastic materials, including common polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyurethane (PUR), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). It represents a major category of solid waste that contributes significantly to environmental pollution, landfill accumulation, and ecological degradation. Plastic waste is widely recognized as an urgent and pressing environmental problem, with its impacts further exacerbated during the COVID-19 pandemic due to increased consumption of single-use plastics. Beyond its persistence in the environment, plastic waste is associated with the release of toxic pollutants, posing risks to ecosystems and human health, and is often classified as hazardous due to its chemical composition and long-term effects.  

In scientific research, plastic waste is a central focus of biodegradation studies, particularly in exploring biological solutions to mitigate its environmental burden. Insects such as mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) and various fungi have been investigated for their ability to degrade different types of plastics. Experimental studies have tested the capacity of mealworm larvae to consume and break down plastics, with specific microbial communities, such as Lactococcus, being identified as unique to certain plastic-based diets in these organisms. These findings highlight the potential role of insect gut microbiota in facilitating plastic biodegradation.  

Overall, plastic waste encompasses both the environmental challenge of persistent synthetic polymers and the subject of ongoing research into sustainable degradation methods. It is simultaneously a pollutant of global concern and a target for innovative biological and ecological approaches aimed at reducing its accumulation and mitigating its harmful effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste refers to discarded synthetic plastics, including polystyrene, which have become a global environmental concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste refers to discarded plastic materials that accumulate in landfills or as microplastics due to lack of effective disposal methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste, particularly non-hydrolyzable vinyl plastics, is an environmental concern that mealworms may help manage through biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste refers to the large amount of discarded plastic generated globally, with only a small percentage recycled.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386797</data>
</node>
<node id="Cellulose">
  <data key="d0">Cellulose</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellulose is a natural polymer and the most abundant polysaccharide in the biosphere, composed of linear chains of glucose units. It serves as a structural component in plant cell walls and is a critical dietary substrate for many organisms. In biological systems, cellulose degradation is facilitated by specialized enzymes such as AXE1, FAEB, CES1, and Eb-G, which break down the polymer into glucose monomers. This process is central to the nutrition of various species and the functioning of microbial ecosystems.  

In nature, cellulose degradation is carried out by diverse microbial consortia, including those found in the digestive systems of ruminating mammals, where it provides a primary energy source. Similarly, Nasutitermes termites rely on symbiotic microbial phyla in their gut to efficiently degrade cellulose. In insects, cellulose has been studied extensively as a dietary substrate. For example, Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae are capable of biodegrading cellulose, and it has been used in controlled feeding experiments to investigate their digestive capabilities. The biodegradation of cellulose by T. molitor larvae has also been compared to their ability to degrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene, with cellulose serving as a natural reference substrate. Likewise, gut bacteria from Lepidiota mansueta larvae have been studied for their role in cellulose degradation, highlighting the widespread involvement of insect-associated microbiota in processing this polymer.  

Beyond biological systems, cellulose has also been investigated in industrial and environmental contexts. In particular, it has been studied in combination with polystyrene during pyrolysis to evaluate its influence on bio-oil properties, demonstrating its relevance in energy recovery and waste management research.  

Overall, cellulose is a fundamental natural polymer with ecological, biological, and industrial importance. Its degradation by enzymes, microbial consortia, and insect gut systems not only sustains diverse organisms but also provides valuable insights into biodegradation processes and potential applications in sustainable material and energy research.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a natural polymer studied for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a waste material biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;A material studied for biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a type of polymer waste tested in the study for its effects on T. molitor larvae microbiome and enzymatic activity.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387983</data>
</node>
