<node id="Springer">
  <data key="d0">Springer</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Springer is an international academic publishing organization that serves as the publisher of a wide range of scientific and technical works across disciplines. It is the publishing body responsible for disseminating numerous studies and reference materials, including research in environmental science, engineering, chemistry, and materials science. Springer published *The Handbook of Environmental Chemistry*, a notable series that included the 2017 study on freshwater microplastics, and it is also recognized as the publisher of *Environmental Science and Engineering* and related works on microbial degradation. In addition, Springer has published research on flexible films prepared from PLA/PBAT blends, as well as studies presented in *Characterization of Minerals, Metals, and Materials 2017*. Beyond scientific articles and conference proceedings, Springer has also released broader reference works such as *Fifty Materials That Make the World*. Headquartered in Frankfurt, Springer functions as the publishing organization associated with the referenced scientific works, providing a platform for the dissemination of scholarly research across multiple domains.&lt;SEP&gt;Springer is a publishing organization mentioned as the publisher of 'Microplastics in Terrestrial Environments'.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387721</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Microbes">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microbes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gut microbes are diverse communities of microorganisms residing in the digestive systems of insects, particularly larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm), *Galleria mellonella* (waxworm), and *Zophobas atratus*. These intestinal microbial populations have been extensively studied for their unique role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, including expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). In *T. molitor* larvae, gut microbes have been isolated and screened for their biodegradation potential, with their activity often observed directly on the surfaces of plastic polymers during experimental studies.  

Research has shown that these gut microbes contribute to plastic depolymerization and degradation through the production of specialized enzymes and metabolic pathways. In particular, microbial communities in *T. molitor* are enriched with genes and enzymes associated with PET degradation, enabling them to hydrolyze polymer bonds and facilitate downstream metabolic processes. Beyond plastic degradation, these microbes also participate in broader physiological functions, including lipid metabolism, nitrogen fixation, and chemotaxis, highlighting their multifunctional role in host metabolism.  

The significance of gut microbes extends beyond *T. molitor*, as similar microbial flora in other insect larvae, such as *Plesiophthalmus davidis* and waxworms, have also demonstrated plastic-degrading capabilities. Collectively, gut microbes represent a critical biological resource for understanding and harnessing natural mechanisms of plastic biodegradation, offering potential applications in environmental biotechnology and waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms in the digestive systems of mealworms and superworms that contribute to plastic depolymerization and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes in larvae play a role in the biodegradation of LDPE and other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbes are microorganisms in larvae that contribute to polymer depolymerization, though LDPE degradation was shown to be partially independent of them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386514</data>
</node>
