<node id="Low Density Polyethylene (LDPE)">
  <data key="d0">Low Density Polyethylene (LDPE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">LDPE is a type of polyethylene with the molecular formula -(CH₂-CH₂)n-. It appears as white beads or pellets, has a melt flow index of 20–30 g/10 min, and a melting point of 116°C.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a type of plastic polymer included in the list of feedstocks for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Low density polyethylene is a type of plastic studied for fluidised bed pyrolysis and in composite films with nano TiO2.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a form of polyethylene plastic studied for biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Low density polyethylene is a type of plastic that was less favorable for mealworm consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383497</data>
</node>
<node id="High Density Polyethylene (HDPE)">
  <data key="d0">High Density Polyethylene (HDPE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">HDPE is a type of polyethylene with the molecular formula -(CH₂-CH₂)n-. It appears as white beads or pellets, has a melt flow index of 12 g/10 min, and a melting point between 125–140°C.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE is a type of plastic polymer included in the list of feedstocks for biodegradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;High density polyethylene is a type of plastic that was less favorable for mealworm consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383497</data>
</node>
<node id="Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Biodegradation is the biological process by which complex organic and synthetic materials, including natural polymers and plastics, are broken down into simpler compounds through the activity of living organisms. This process is primarily mediated by microorganisms such as bacteria, fungi, and algae, which secrete enzymes capable of depolymerizing large molecules into smaller fragments. These fragments can then be further mineralized into fundamental substances such as carbon dioxide, water, and biomass. In addition to microbial activity, certain invertebrates, particularly insects like yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) and other larvae, contribute to biodegradation by consuming plastics such as polystyrene and polypropylene. In these cases, the gut microbiota of the insects plays a crucial role in polymer breakdown, supporting both carbon metabolism and nitrogen fixation during digestion.  

Scientific research has demonstrated that biodegradation can act on a wide range of synthetic polymers, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polypropylene (PP). For instance, bacteria capable of metabolizing PET and its intermediates have been identified, while microbial and enzymatic processes have been shown to degrade polyethylene and polystyrene. A notable example is the partial biodegradation of polystyrene by the bacterium *Rhodococcus ruber*, which was studied in association with biofilm formation. This research was published in 2008 in the journal *Biodegradation*. The same journal has also published studies on related topics, such as ozonation and biodegradation, highlighting its role as a key platform for disseminating findings in this field.  

The efficiency and extent of biodegradation depend on multiple factors. Polymer characteristics such as molecular weight, crystallinity, and the presence of chemical additives influence microbial accessibility and enzymatic activity. Environmental conditions, including temperature, oxygen availability, and moisture, further shape the rate and completeness of degradation. These variables explain why some plastics, despite being highly resistant, can still undergo biological breakdown under specific conditions.  

Biodegradation has been studied in both controlled laboratory settings and natural ecosystems, providing insights into its potential as a sustainable and environmentally responsible method for managing plastic waste. Unlike conventional disposal methods such as landfilling and incineration, which contribute to pollution and greenhouse gas emissions, biodegradation offers a pathway to reduce the persistence of synthetic polymers in the environment. This makes it a critical area of research for addressing global plastic pollution, advancing sustainability, and supporting circular economy solutions.  

Overall, biodegradation represents a vital ecological and technological process that enhances scientific understanding of material breakdown, informs the development of innovative waste management strategies, and supports the design of more environmentally friendly materials. The integration of microbial and insect-based biodegradation approaches holds particular promise for reducing the environmental impact of plastics and contributing to long-term ecological resilience.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation is described as an environment-friendly method for degrading microplastics with low energy consumption and no secondary pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation refers to the biological process of breaking down polystyrene by bacteria and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation is the process by which organisms, such as mealworms, consume and chemically break down plastics into simpler compounds, sometimes converting them into biomass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
