<node id="Bacteria">
  <data key="d0">Bacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacteria are single-celled microorganisms that play diverse and significant roles in natural and engineered environments. They are characterized by structural and functional features such as flagella, which not only facilitate motility but also enhance their adhesion capacity to surfaces, thereby influencing their ability to metabolize organic matter. Bacteria are widely studied for their ecological and biotechnological importance, including their potential in biodegradation processes. Certain bacterial species have been identified as capable of degrading plastics, although their efficiency varies, with some showing limited biodegradation capacity. This has been of particular interest in research on sustainable waste management, especially in relation to synthetic polymers such as polystyrene.  

In ecological contexts, bacteria are integral components of the gut microbiome, where their composition and activity are shaped by diet and environmental factors. Studies have highlighted their presence in the digestive systems of various organisms, including larvae such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor). In these systems, bacteria are thought to contribute to the breakdown of otherwise recalcitrant materials, including plastics. For example, bacterial species identified in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed polystyrene are considered potentially responsible for initiating or supporting the biodegradation of this material. This suggests a symbiotic relationship in which the host provides a habitat and substrate, while bacteria contribute metabolic capabilities that expand dietary possibilities.  

From a methodological perspective, bacteria have been analyzed alongside Archaea in microbiological studies, such as a 2014 investigation that employed a universal primer to enable simultaneous detection and characterization of both groups. Such approaches underscore the importance of bacteria in comparative microbial ecology and in understanding the broader microbial communities in which they function.  

Overall, bacteria are versatile microorganisms with roles that extend from fundamental ecological processes, such as nutrient cycling and gut microbiome maintenance, to applied functions, including the biodegradation of plastics. Their metabolic diversity, surface adhesion properties, and interactions with host organisms make them central subjects of research in microbiology, environmental science, and biotechnology.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria in the mealworm gut play a role in EPS biodegradation, with effectiveness reduced by gentamicin treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria capable of growing on NPS emulsion as a sole carbon source were tested for enzyme activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria are microbial agents implicated in the degradation of plastics within the mealworm gut.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria are studied for their role in biodegradation of polyethylene wax.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria are microorganisms that have been found capable of degrading plastic materials in previous studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384500</data>
</node>
<node id="Fungi">
  <data key="d0">Fungi</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fungi are a diverse group of microorganisms that play important roles in ecological processes, including interactions with other organisms and contributions to biodegradation. Within the context of plastic degradation, fungi have been identified as potential agents capable of breaking down synthetic polymers such as polystyrene, polyethylene wax, and polyester polyurethane. However, research consistently shows that their biodegradation capacity is generally limited, with many species exhibiting only poor or very slow degradation rates. For example, studies testing seventeen fungal species in axenic cultures demonstrated only minimal breakdown of polystyrene, highlighting the challenges of relying on fungi alone for efficient plastic biodegradation. Despite these limitations, fungi have been recognized as predominant degraders of soil-buried polyester polyurethane under varying soil water conditions, suggesting that environmental factors strongly influence their effectiveness.  

Beyond their role in biodegradation, fungi are also integral components of microbiomes. They are present in the gut microbiome, where their abundance and diversity are shaped by diet and environmental factors. Similarly, fungi have been detected in the gut microbiota of larvae, where they are studied in relation to host diet and microbial community composition. These findings underscore the ecological versatility of fungi, as they contribute both to host-associated microbial ecosystems and to environmental processes such as organic matter recycling and polymer degradation.  

Overall, fungi are microorganisms of significant scientific interest due to their dual roles in microbiome ecology and biodegradation research. While their capacity to degrade plastics is often limited compared to other microorganisms, they remain important subjects of study for developing sustainable strategies to address plastic pollution and for understanding the broader dynamics of microbial communities in diverse environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi are microorganisms that have been found capable of degrading plastic materials in previous studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Free-living fungi have been described as efficient in degrading synthetic polymers, though their role in insect gut degradation of polystyrene has not been investigated in detail.&lt;SEP&gt;Fungi is a kingdom mentioned in the table, with multiple genera such as Aspergillus, Penicillium, Trichoderma, and Hyphodermella identified as overrepresented in PS samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384765</data>
</node>
