<node id="LDPE">
  <data key="d0">LDPE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a synthetic plastic polymer belonging to the polyethylene family, characterized by its highly branched molecular structure that includes both long and short chain branches. This branching reduces its crystallinity, typically ranging between 45–60%, and lowers its density compared to other polyethylene variants such as linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) and high-density polyethylene (HDPE). These structural features give LDPE distinctive material properties, including flexibility, toughness, and strong resistance to chemicals, which make it a widely used material in diverse applications such as packaging films, toys, pipes, fuel storage tanks, and various consumer and industrial products. Together with LLDPE, LDPE represents approximately 17.4% of global plastic production, highlighting its importance in modern manufacturing and commerce.  

Despite its widespread utility, LDPE is environmentally persistent and resistant to natural decomposition, contributing significantly to plastic waste accumulation and long-term ecological pollution. Although it degrades somewhat faster than LLDPE and HDPE, its breakdown in natural environments remains slow and incomplete. This persistence has made LDPE a major focus of biodegradation research, with studies exploring microbial, fungal, soil-based, and insect-mediated pathways for its degradation.  

Microbial degradation studies often employ LDPE in bead or film form to test bacterial activity. Certain bacterial strains, including *Bacillus* species and isolates such as PM S1, have demonstrated partial degradation, as evidenced by weight loss, surface erosion, oxidative modifications, and early stages of depolymerization. Fungal species such as *Trichoderma viride* and *Aspergillus nomius* have also been shown to degrade LDPE under controlled laboratory conditions. Soil-based studies indicate that oxidative processes can contribute to LDPE breakdown, though the efficiency of these processes remains limited.  

Insect-mediated biodegradation has provided particularly promising insights. Larvae of species such as *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), *Tenebrio obscurus*, *Zophobas atratus* (superworm), and *Galleria mellonella* (wax moth) have been tested as consumers of LDPE. Among these, mealworms have been studied most extensively, with experiments monitoring their mass, survival, and physiological changes over feeding periods of 10, 20, and 30 days. Ingestion of LDPE by larvae has been linked to reductions in polymer molecular weight, transitions from crystalline to more amorphous structures, and evidence of depolymerization. Foam variants of LDPE, such as PE-1 and PE-2, have also shown distinct degradation patterns depending on foam type.  

The role of gut microbiota in insect-mediated degradation is particularly significant. Microbial communities within insect digestive systems, including unclassified Enterobacteriaceae in superworms, appear to facilitate LDPE breakdown through processes such as nitrogen fixation and enzymatic activity. These findings suggest that insect gut ecosystems may hold potential for developing biotechnological solutions to plastic waste management.  

In summary, LDPE is a widely used polymer valued for its flexibility, toughness, and chemical resistance, but it poses substantial environmental challenges due to its persistence and resistance to complete biodegradation. While microbial, fungal, soil-based, and insect-mediated studies have demonstrated partial depolymerization, oxidative modification, and structural transformation of LDPE, complete biodegradation remains elusive. Ongoing research into microbial strains, fungal species, and insect gut microbiomes offers promising avenues for advancing sustainable strategies to mitigate LDPE pollution and improve environmental remediation efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE (low-density polyethylene) is a polymer observed to undergo limited-extent depolymerization during biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE (low-density polyethylene) is a type of plastic reported to be degraded by Enterobacteriaceae.</data>
