<node id="HDPE">
  <data key="d0">HDPE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) is a synthetic plastic polymer belonging to the polyethylene family and is one of the most widely produced plastics globally, contributing approximately 12.9% of total plastic production. It is characterized by its high density, high crystallinity (around 55.3%), and structural stability, which give it superior durability and resistance to natural decomposition compared to other polyethylene variants such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE). These properties make HDPE a valuable material for industrial and consumer applications but also a significant environmental challenge due to its persistence in ecosystems.  

In biodegradation research, HDPE has been extensively studied as a model polymer to evaluate the potential of biological systems to break down recalcitrant plastics. Its high crystallinity and dense molecular structure make it more resistant to degradation than LDPE, positioning it as a critical target for studies on sustainable plastic waste management. Experimental approaches often involve using HDPE in the form of microplastic beads or as a dietary component for insects, particularly larvae of Tenebrio species such as *T. molitor* (mealworms) and *T. obscurus*. These organisms have been tested to assess both the physiological impacts of ingesting HDPE and the potential for biodegradation mediated by their gut microbiota.  

Research findings indicate that while HDPE is poorly degradable, certain bacterial strains isolated from the gut of mealworms, including PM S1 and PELW2042, can initiate partial depolymerization of the polymer. Evidence of microbial activity includes measurable weight loss of HDPE beads, surface erosion, reductions in molecular weight, changes in crystallinity, and chemical structure modifications. However, the extent of biodegradation remains limited, underscoring the polymer’s inherent resistance to biological breakdown.  

Studies also highlight the biological consequences of HDPE ingestion by invertebrates. Compared to LDPE, HDPE ingestion has been associated with stronger negative physiological effects in Tenebrio larvae, such as heightened oxidative stress and impairment of normal biological functions. These findings emphasize HDPE’s dual role in biodegradation research: it serves both as a test material for evaluating microbial and insect-mediated plastic degradation and as a model pollutant for understanding the ecological and physiological impacts of plastic ingestion on living organisms.  

Beyond biodegradation studies, HDPE has also been investigated for its structural properties, including oriented structure and anisotropy in blown films, which are relevant to its industrial applications. Overall, HDPE is recognized as a highly resistant plastic polymer that poses considerable environmental challenges. At the same time, it provides a critical platform for advancing research into microbial and insect-based strategies for plastic degradation, offering insights into both the limitations and potential pathways for mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) is a type of polyethylene plastic tested for its effects on mealworm mass and viability across 10-, 20-, and 30-day intervals.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) is a type of plastic tested in the experiment, showing similar effects on mealworm metabolism as PVC.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) is another type of polyethylene mentioned in relation to biodegradation by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) is another type of plastic mentioned in relation to biodegradation by insects.</data>
