<node id="Bacillaceae">
  <data key="d0">Bacillaceae</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacillaceae is a bacterial family within the class **Bacilli** and the order **Bacillales**, comprising a diverse group of genera such as *Bacillus*, *Metabacillus*, *Cytobacillus*, *Virgibacillus*, *Pseudogracilbacillus*, and *Prestia megaterium* S1. Members of this family are widely distributed in various environments and are notable for their ecological and functional diversity. In microbiological studies, representatives such as *Bacillus alkalinitrilicus* have been used to characterize the family’s traits.  

Bacillaceae plays an important role in host-associated microbial communities, particularly in insects. It is one of the most abundant bacterial families in the gut microbiome of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), where it is especially prevalent in the anterior gut. Its abundance has been shown to vary with diet, being enriched in bran-fed mealworm groups and significantly more abundant in samples associated with polystyrene (PS) feeding. These findings highlight the adaptability of Bacillaceae to different substrates and its potential contribution to digestion and nutrient processing in insect hosts.  

Overall, Bacillaceae represents a taxonomically and ecologically significant bacterial family, encompassing multiple genera and species that are relevant both in environmental contexts and in host-associated microbiomes, with particular importance in the study of insect gut ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillaceae is a bacterial family mentioned as part of commensal bacteria that can interact with microplastics in the gastrointestinal tract.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385603</data>
</node>
<node id="Agilent Technologies">
  <data key="d0">Agilent Technologies</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Agilent Technologies is a U.S.-based company recognized for its extensive portfolio of scientific instruments, software, and consumables that support advanced chemical and biological analysis. In the referenced study, Agilent Technologies played a central role by supplying a wide range of analytical equipment and resources. The company provided Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy instruments, which were employed to analyze low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and polystyrene (PS) beads before and after biodegradation. Additionally, Agilent supplied gas chromatography (GC) and mass spectrometry (MS) systems that were critical for chemical characterization and metabolite profiling.  

Specifically, Agilent Technologies furnished the 7890B gas chromatography system and the 7200 Accurate-Mass quadrupole time-of-flight (Q-ToF) mass spectrometer, along with the HP-5MS capillary column. These instruments were complemented by proprietary software solutions, including MassHunter 10.0 and Mass Profiler Professional (MPP) v.15.0, which facilitated data acquisition, processing, and interpretation. The company also provided the 1290 Infinity II Flex pump coupled with a 6470 liquid chromatography triple quadrupole mass spectrometer (LC-QQQ-MS), further enhancing the study’s analytical capabilities.  

Beyond instrumentation, Agilent Technologies contributed consumables and standards essential for experimental accuracy. This included internal standards used in the analyses, as well as cartridges and other materials required for metabolite extraction and sample preparation. The study also referenced the Agilent G1676AA Fiehn GC/MS Metabolomics RTL Library, published in 2013, which served as a resource for compound identification and metabolomic data interpretation.  

Overall, Agilent Technologies is presented as a key enabler of the research, providing both the hardware and software infrastructure necessary for comprehensive chemical and metabolomic investigations. Its contributions spanned multiple analytical platforms—FTIR, GC-MS, and LC-MS—demonstrating the company’s broad technological reach and its importance in supporting high-precision scientific studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies, located in Palo Alto, CA, USA, provided equipment for gel permeation chromatography (GPC) analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies is mentioned as the manufacturer of the Agilent PL - GPC220 system used in the experiment, located in Palo Alto, CA, USA.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies is a company mentioned in relation to analytical software such as Masshunter 10.0 and Mass Profiler Professional v.15.0, as well as mass spectrometry products like 'all ion MS-MS'.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies is a company based in the USA whose software products Masshunter 10.0, Mass Profiler Professional (MPP) v.15.0, and Agilent MassHunter Lipid Annotator were used for data processing, chemometrics, and lipid annotation in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Agilent Technologies is a company based in Santa Clara, CA, USA, associated with the HP-88 chromatographic column.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384271</data>
</node>
