<node id="CO2">
  <data key="d0">CO2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon dioxide (CO2) is a gaseous compound that functions as both a fundamental metabolic byproduct and a key indicator of degradation processes in biological and chemical systems. In the context of polymer biodegradation, CO2 has been extensively studied as a primary end product when organisms such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms metabolize synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). During these processes, ingested plastics are partially assimilated into larval biomass, while a significant portion of their carbon content is mineralized and released as CO2. For example, in mealworm-mediated degradation of polystyrene, approximately 47.7% of the ingested carbon has been measured to convert into CO2 within 16 days, whereas in superworm studies, conversion rates of up to 36.7% have been reported. This transformation is facilitated by microbe-dependent gut activities that enable the breakdown of otherwise recalcitrant polymers.  

Beyond insect-mediated degradation, CO2 is also produced during the digestion of other carbon-rich substrates, such as Styrofoam and lignin hydrocarbons, in the guts of mealworms and termites. In microbial studies, CO2 is consistently used as a direct marker of metabolic activity and mineralization. For instance, in laboratory experiments, CO2 released during Styrofoam degradation has been trapped in sodium hydroxide (NaOH) solutions and precipitated as barium carbonate (BaCO3) for quantification. In microbial respiration assays, CO2 is measured in the headspace of sealed serum bottles, while gas chromatography and infrared (IR) detection methods are employed to analyze CO2 generated during elemental transformations, such as the oxidation of carbon monoxide (CO).  

In addition to insect gut systems, CO2 has been identified as the final metabolite in anaerobic biodegradation pathways. For example, bacterial strains such as LG3 produce CO2 as the terminal gaseous product during polystyrene degradation under oxygen-limited conditions. This highlights the role of microbial communities in facilitating polymer mineralization and underscores CO2’s importance as a measurable endpoint in biodegradation research.  

Overall, carbon dioxide (CO2) serves as the main gaseous product of polymer mineralization and a critical marker of biological and microbial degradation processes. Its quantification provides valuable insights into the extent of plastic breakdown and the efficiency of biological systems in converting synthetic materials into simpler compounds. However, while CO2 production demonstrates the feasibility of biological plastic degradation, it also underscores a limitation: CO2 is a terminal byproduct with no direct reuse value in waste management, representing a release of carbon rather than a pathway to material recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbon dioxide produced as a result of LDPE digestion by Tenebrio larvae, accounting for nearly 40% of ingested LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 (carbon dioxide) is a mineralization product of plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is a product of LDPE digestion by larvae, with nearly 40% of ingested carbon converted into CO2.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is the main product formed during the biodegradation of LDPE by Tenebrio larvae, as indicated by carbon balance analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 is carbon dioxide, produced as a result of polystyrene degradation in mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;CO2 was found to be a product of PS metabolism in larvae, with 47.7% of PS converted into CO2 in the gut.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
