</node>
<node id="NA Medium Plate">
  <data key="d0">NA Medium Plate</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NA medium plate (Nutrient Agar medium plate) was used to grow bacterial isolates to test their ability to degrade PE and PS beads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756371417</data>
</node>
<node id="16S rRNA Sequencing">
  <data key="d0">16S rRNA Sequencing</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">16S rRNA Sequencing was performed on five mealworm gut bacterial isolates (S1–S5) to screen for their potential in degrading PE and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA Sequencing was used to identify bacterial isolates from mealworm gut microbiome, confirming PM S1's relation to Bacillus genera.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing is a genetic analysis method used to identify PS-degrading bacterial species from microbial enrichment.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing was used to identify bacterial species from microbial colonies involved in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing refers to sequencing performed on DNA samples to analyze bacterial communities.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing was used for molecular barcoding and bacterial identification.&lt;SEP&gt;16S rRNA sequencing was used to taxonomically identify bacterial colonies isolated from mealworm intestines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386810</data>
</node>
<node id="Jayan et al. (2023)">
  <data key="d0">Jayan et al. (2023)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jayan et al. (2023) reported that Bacillus cereus NJDI strain from a dumpsite degraded LDPE films by 43% in 120 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756371417</data>
</node>
<node id="Mohan et al. (2016)">
  <data key="d0">Mohan et al. (2016)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mohan et al. (2016) investigated Bacillus strains that degraded brominated high-impact PS (HIPS) with 23% weight loss in 30 days.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohan et al. are cited authors who described the methodology for turbidity assay using NPS emulsion.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohan et al. (2016) are cited researchers who showed that some Enterobacteriaceae species can degrade plastics such as HIPS, PHA, and EPS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384792</data>
</node>
<node id="Bacillus cereus NJDI">
  <data key="d0">Bacillus cereus NJDI</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacillus cereus NJDI is a bacterial strain obtained from a long-term dumpsite, capable of degrading LDPE films by 43% in 120 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756371418</data>
</node>
<node id="HIPS">
  <data key="d0">HIPS</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">HIPS (High-Impact Polystyrene) is a brominated form of polystyrene tested for biodegradation by Bacillus strains.&lt;SEP&gt;HIPS (High Impact Polystyrene) is one of the experimental plastic diets fed to the worms.&lt;SEP&gt;HIPS is a type of amorphous plastic that showed the highest cumulative consumption by mealworms in the study. It is rigid in structure.&lt;SEP&gt;HIPS is a type of plastic group used in the experiment to feed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;HIPS refers to high-impact polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;HIPS (High Impact Polystyrene) is a type of plastic that some Enterobacteriaceae species can degrade.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384790</data>
</node>
<node id="BOD Test">
  <data key="d0">BOD Test</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) test measured oxygen consumption by PM S1 during degradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756371418</data>
</node>
<node id="Carbon Source">
  <data key="d0">Carbon Source</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbon source refers to LDPE, HDPE, and PS plastics provided as the sole carbon source for bacterial growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic is described as being used as a source of carbon in the degradation process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
