<node id="Peng BY">
  <data key="d0">Peng BY</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng BY is a researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insects such as mealworms and superworms in this process. Their work has centered on understanding how different types of plastics, including polystyrene and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), can be digested and broken down by insect larvae, with a strong emphasis on Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms).  

In 2018, Peng BY co-authored a study that demonstrated the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in yellow mealworms, highlighting the widespread potential of these insects to degrade this persistent plastic. Building on this, Peng BY contributed to a 2019 study that further examined the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, providing evidence of their ability to consume and metabolize the material. Additional research by Peng BY has explored the influence of polymer size on the efficiency of polystyrene biodegradation, offering insights into the factors that affect the rate and extent of plastic breakdown in insect digestive systems.  

Beyond polystyrene, Peng BY has also investigated the biodegradation of other plastics, including PVC, within Tenebrio molitor larvae. This work expanded the scope of insect-based biodegradation research to more chemically resistant polymers, underscoring the potential of biological systems to address a wider range of plastic waste. Furthermore, Peng BY has contributed to studies on plastic degradation in superworms, broadening the understanding of how different insect species may contribute to mitigating plastic pollution.  

Overall, Peng BY’s research has significantly advanced knowledge of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, demonstrating both the capacity and limitations of mealworms and related species in processing synthetic polymers. This body of work provides a foundation for exploring sustainable, biologically based solutions to global plastic waste challenges.</data>
