<node id="Wu WM">
  <data key="d0">Wu WM</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wu WM is a researcher and author recognized for extensive contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insect larvae. His work has centered on the capacity of mealworms, specifically *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, to degrade and mineralize synthetic polymers such as polystyrene. Wu WM has co-authored multiple studies that demonstrate how these larvae can ingest and biologically process plastics, offering insights into potential natural pathways for mitigating plastic waste.  

In addition to documenting the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, Wu WM has contributed to research on the broader issue of microplastics pollution, including strategies for its reduction. His studies have explored both the environmental implications of microplastics and the potential of biological systems to address this global challenge. Beyond degradation processes, his work has also examined the generation of biochar from yellow mealworm frass, highlighting the potential for value-added byproducts in the context of plastic biodegradation.  

Overall, Wu WM’s research integrates environmental science, waste management, and biological processes, with a particular emphasis on insect-mediated biodegradation of plastics. His publications have advanced understanding of how mealworms and related organisms can contribute to reducing plastic pollution and recovering useful materials, positioning him as a key contributor in the field of sustainable plastic waste management and biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu WM is one of the authors of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu WM is a co-author of a 2018 study on biodegradation of plastics in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu WM is an author of research on biodegradation of PVC in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Wu WM is an author of studies on polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms and superworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
