<node id="Yang SS">
  <data key="d0">Yang SS</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang SS is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of plastics and the development of sustainable solutions to plastic waste pollution. Their research contributions span multiple studies investigating the capacity of insects, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus), to degrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene. Yang SS has co-authored studies published in 2018 and 2021 that demonstrate the potential of these organisms to digest and metabolize plastic materials, offering insights into biological pathways for mitigating plastic waste.  

In addition to examining the direct biodegradation of plastics within insect digestive systems, Yang SS has contributed to research on the valorization of byproducts from this process. This includes studies on the generation of biochar from frass (insect excrement) produced by yellow mealworms fed with wheat straw, highlighting opportunities for resource recovery and circular economy applications. Their work also extends to microbial degradation processes, exploring how microbial communities associated with insects or other systems can contribute to the breakdown and transformation of persistent plastic polymers.  

Overall, Yang SS’s research emphasizes both the biological mechanisms of plastic degradation and the potential for integrating these processes into broader waste management and environmental remediation strategies. Through studies on insect-mediated digestion, microbial activity, and byproduct utilization, Yang SS has advanced scientific understanding of innovative, nature-based approaches to addressing global plastic pollution.</data>
