<node id="Microbiome">
  <data key="d0">Microbiome</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The term **Microbiome** has two distinct but related uses in scientific contexts: it refers both to a peer-reviewed scientific journal and to the microbial communities inhabiting specific environments, such as insect guts or plant rhizospheres.  

As a **scientific journal**, *Microbiome* publishes research on microbial communities and their ecological, functional, and biotechnological roles. Studies featured in the journal include investigations into the gut microbiome of mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) under different diets, the performance of microbiomes in plant cell wall deconstruction, and the rhizomicrobiome of wild rice. These publications highlight the journal’s focus on advancing knowledge of microbial diversity, interactions, and applications in biodegradation, agriculture, and environmental sustainability.  

As a **biological concept**, the microbiome refers to the collective microbial community, particularly bacteria, residing in host organisms or environments. In the case of mealworms, the gut microbiome has been studied extensively to understand its role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as well as in the digestion of natural diets like bran. Research has shown that distinct microbiomes emerge depending on dietary conditions, with differences observed in bacterial community composition, taxonomic orders, and associated biochemical properties. These microbial shifts reflect structural and physiological changes in the insect digestive system that enable adaptation to different food sources, including the breakdown of otherwise recalcitrant compounds like styrene derivatives.  

Together, the dual usage of the term underscores both the importance of the *Microbiome* journal as a platform for disseminating cutting-edge microbial research and the significance of the microbiome itself as a dynamic, diet-dependent microbial community with critical ecological and biotechnological functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific journal where the 2023 study on mealworm gut microbiome responses to polystyrene and corn straw diets was published.&lt;SEP&gt;The microbiome refers to the microbial community in the insects' gut, which varies depending on diet and influences PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Journal where the study on microbiota abundance quantification was published.</data>
