<node id="Environ Sci Technol">
  <data key="d0">Environ Sci Technol</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">**Environmental Science &amp; Technology (Environ Sci Technol)** is a peer-reviewed scientific journal that has served as a prominent platform for publishing research on plastic biodegradation, particularly involving mealworms and waxworms. The journal has featured multiple influential studies addressing the environmental fate of synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene. Among these, it published a 2012 review on microplastics, which provided an early synthesis of knowledge on the prevalence and impacts of microscopic plastic particles in the environment.  

In subsequent years, Environmental Science &amp; Technology became a key outlet for pioneering research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms. Notably, studies published in 2014 and 2015 reported on the ability of both mealworms and waxworms to ingest and metabolize polystyrene and polyethylene, demonstrating evidence of biodegradation and mineralization of these persistent plastics. These findings were significant in highlighting potential biological pathways for mitigating plastic pollution. The journal continued to advance this line of inquiry by publishing a 2019 study that further investigated the mechanisms and outcomes of polystyrene biodegradation in mealworms, reinforcing the role of insect gut microbiota in facilitating plastic breakdown.  

Through these contributions, Environmental Science &amp; Technology has played a central role in disseminating foundational research on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene, by insect species such as mealworms and waxworms. The journal’s publications in this area have provided critical insights into the potential for biological systems to contribute to solutions for global plastic waste challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific journal where multiple referenced studies on plastics and biodegradation were published.&lt;SEP&gt;Environ Sci Technol is a scientific journal that published multiple studies on nanomaterials, nanoplastics, and biodegradation.</data>
