<node id="Expanded Polystyrene">
  <data key="d0">Expanded Polystyrene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, foam form of polystyrene that is widely recognized as a persistent plastic material and a significant environmental pollutant. Due to its durability, low density, and resistance to natural degradation, EPS has become a focus of scientific research aimed at identifying potential biological pathways for its breakdown. In particular, studies have investigated the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by various organisms, most notably the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms). Research has demonstrated that these larvae are capable of consuming and partially degrading EPS, making them a promising biological model for addressing plastic waste. Different feeding strategies have been explored to optimize the biodegradation process in mealworm larvae, highlighting the potential influence of diet composition on the efficiency of EPS breakdown.  

In addition to mealworms, other invertebrates such as land snails have also been studied for their ability to degrade expanded polystyrene, suggesting that multiple biological systems may contribute to the disintegration of this material. While EPS remains a challenging pollutant due to its resistance to conventional recycling and environmental degradation, these studies indicate that certain organisms possess metabolic or digestive mechanisms that can facilitate its partial biodegradation.  

Overall, expanded polystyrene is a foam plastic material of environmental concern that has been extensively studied for biodegradation, particularly through the digestive activity of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, mealworms, and land snails. This line of research underscores the potential role of biological agents in mitigating plastic pollution and advancing sustainable waste management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is studied for biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene studied for biodegradation by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene was studied for its biodegradation and disintegration by land snails Achatina fulica.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52</data>
