<node id="Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE)">
  <data key="d0">Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used type of polyethylene plastic distinguished by its relatively low crystallinity and high degree of long- and short-chain branching. These structural characteristics give LDPE unique physical properties compared to other polyethylene variants, including greater flexibility but also reduced resistance to biodegradation. Because of its extensive use and persistence in the environment, LDPE has been the focus of numerous studies investigating its potential for biological degradation and its role in plastic pollution.  

Research has shown that LDPE can serve as a carbon source in biodegradation studies, and its interaction with living organisms has been of particular interest. A significant body of work has examined the ability of insect larvae, especially *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), to ingest and degrade LDPE. Mealworms have been observed to consume LDPE in various forms, including foams and plastic bags, leaving behind visible holes, scratches, and altered surface morphology. These changes suggest both mechanical wear from ingestion and biochemical modification. Experimental studies have further demonstrated that LDPE degradation in mealworms is not solely dependent on gut microbes, but rather involves a combination of the larvae’s digestive processes and the activity of their associated microbiota. For example, tests with LDPE foams of different molecular weights (130.6 kDa and 288.7 kDa) have indicated that polymer characteristics influence the efficiency of degradation.  

In addition to mealworms, other insect larvae such as wax moth larvae have been tested for their ability to ingest and degrade LDPE, with similar observations of surface damage and partial breakdown. Beyond insect-mediated processes, LDPE has also been studied for biodegradation by microbial communities, including mixed cultures of bacteria and fungi. These microorganisms have demonstrated the capacity to colonize LDPE surfaces and contribute to its gradual decomposition, further highlighting the potential for biologically mediated solutions to plastic waste.  

Overall, Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a form of polyethylene with distinctive structural features that make it more susceptible to biological degradation than other polyethylene types. It has been extensively studied in the context of biodegradation, particularly through ingestion by insect larvae such as mealworms and wax moths, as well as through microbial activity involving bacteria and fungi. These investigations provide valuable insights into possible biological pathways for mitigating LDPE pollution and advancing sustainable waste management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a plastic polymer studied for its biodegradation by T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE is a type of polyethylene plastic that Tenebrio molitor larvae can depolymerize.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of polyethylene plastic tested for biodegradation by mealworm larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene foam tested for biodegradation by mealworms in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a petroleum-based plastic tested in the study for biodegradation by yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
