<node id="Coleoptera">
  <data key="d0">Coleoptera</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Coleoptera is an order of insects commonly known as beetles, encompassing a vast diversity of species, including mealworms (the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*), superworms, and yellow mealworms. This order is characterized by its members’ hardened forewings (elytra) that protect the delicate hindwings and abdomen, a defining feature of beetles. Within scientific research, Coleoptera has been studied extensively for both its ecological roles and its physiological adaptations.  

In biodegradation studies, Coleoptera is particularly significant because certain larvae, such as those of *Tenebrio molitor*, have demonstrated the ability to consume and degrade synthetic polymers like expanded polystyrene (EPS or Styrofoam). This capacity is linked to gut microorganisms that facilitate the breakdown of otherwise persistent plastics, making beetle larvae a subject of interest in environmental biotechnology and waste management research. Both mealworms and superworms, as representatives of Coleoptera, have been investigated for their potential in mitigating plastic pollution through biological processes.  

Beyond biodegradation, Coleoptera has also been studied for its digestive physiology, including the presence of cysteine digestive proteinases in some species. These enzymes play a role in protein digestion and contribute to the understanding of insect nutrition and metabolism.  

Overall, Coleoptera represents a major insect order that not only includes agriculturally and ecologically important beetles but also serves as a model group in scientific studies ranging from digestive biochemistry to innovative approaches for addressing environmental challenges such as plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;An order of insects including edible species such as Tenebrio molitor, Zophobas morio, and Alphitobius diaperinus.&lt;SEP&gt;Coleoptera is an insect order whose gut microbiota includes commensals and parasites such as gregarines.&lt;SEP&gt;Coleoptera refers to beetles, whose gut may contain commensals or parasites such as gregarines.&lt;SEP&gt;Coleoptera are insects observed to consume plastics and decompose them in vivo.&lt;SEP&gt;Coleoptera are insects observed consuming and decomposing plastics in vivo.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386210</data>
</node>
<node id="Tenebrionidae">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrionidae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrionidae is a large family of beetles within the order *Coleoptera*, commonly referred to as darkling beetles. This family encompasses more than 20,000 described species worldwide and includes several well-known representatives such as *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as the mealworm beetle), *Zophobas morio* (often called the superworm beetle), and *Zophobas atratus*. Members of Tenebrionidae are notable for their ecological diversity and adaptability, occupying a wide range of habitats.  

The family is of particular scientific and applied interest due to the larvae of certain species, such as mealworms and superworms, which are widely used in research, animal feed, and waste management studies. Since 2015, Tenebrionidae beetles have also gained attention for their unique ability to biodegrade plastics, including polystyrene, through the activity of their gut microbiota. This discovery has positioned them as important organisms in the exploration of sustainable solutions to plastic pollution.  

Overall, Tenebrionidae represents a taxonomically diverse and ecologically significant group of beetles, with species that play roles in natural ecosystems, scientific research, and emerging environmental applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is a beetle family that includes larvae such as T. molitor, T. obscurus, and Zophobas atratus, which are studied for plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is a cosmopolitan family of beetles within Coleoptera, comprising more than 20,000 species, including superworms and yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is a beetle family, commonly known as darkling beetles, with more than 20,000 species. Tenebrio molitor belongs to this family.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is the insect family that includes both T. molitor and Alphitobius diaperinus Panzer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438</data>
