<node id="Achroia grisella">
  <data key="d0">Achroia grisella</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Achroia grisella, commonly referred to as the lesser waxworm or lesser waxmoth, is a species of moth belonging to the family Pyralidae. Traditionally associated with beehives, where its larvae feed on wax, this species has gained significant scientific attention for its unique ability to degrade synthetic plastics. In particular, the larvae of Achroia grisella have been shown to ingest and metabolize polyethylene (PE), including high-density polyethylene (HDPE), one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics.  

Research has demonstrated that the larvae are not only capable of breaking down polyethylene but can also complete their life cycle when sustained on a polyethylene-based diet. This remarkable capacity positions Achroia grisella as a promising biological model for exploring eco-friendly solutions to plastic pollution. Molecular taxonomy and phylogenetic studies have confirmed its identity and role in experimental investigations, further validating its potential as a natural agent in bioremediation.  

The biodegradation ability of Achroia grisella highlights its ecological and applied significance. While it is still primarily recognized as a waxworm due to its natural association with beeswax, the species is increasingly studied for its novel role in addressing global plastic waste challenges. Its capacity to metabolize plastics suggests potential applications in sustainable waste management strategies, where biological organisms are employed to reduce or eliminate pollutants.  

In summary, Achroia grisella is an insect species of the Pyralidae family, commonly known as the lesser waxworm, that has emerged as an important subject of research for its ability to biodegrade polyethylene, offering potential pathways for innovative bioremediation approaches to combat plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Achroia grisella is an insect species with plastic-degrading capacities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278</data>
