<node id="Plodia interpunctella">
  <data key="d0">Plodia interpunctella</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian meal moth, is a species of moth in the family Pyralidae (order Lepidoptera). It is widely recognized as a major pest of stored products, where its larvae infest and damage a variety of foodstuffs, including grains, cereals, nuts, and dried fruits. The larvae are also capable of penetrating packaging materials such as polyethylene, polyvinyl chloride, and polypropylene films, which makes them particularly problematic in food storage and distribution systems.  

Beyond its role as a pest, Plodia interpunctella has attracted significant scientific interest due to the unusual properties of its larvae in relation to synthetic polymers. Research has demonstrated that the larvae can chew, ingest, and fragment polyethylene (PE), including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), a plastic that is otherwise highly resistant to natural degradation. This process is thought to involve both mechanical action from chewing and biochemical contributions from the larvae’s associated microbiota. Notably, bacterial strains capable of degrading polyethylene, such as *Enterobacter absurcis* YT1, have been isolated from the gut of Plodia interpunctella larvae, highlighting the potential role of symbiotic microorganisms in plastic biodegradation.  

The species has therefore become an important model organism in studies of plastic degradation and waste management. Investigations into its midgut bacteria and enzymatic activity have provided insights into possible biological pathways for breaking down persistent synthetic materials. This has positioned Plodia interpunctella at the intersection of entomology, microbiology, and environmental science, with research exploring its potential applications in mitigating plastic pollution.  

In summary, Plodia interpunctella (Indian meal moth) is both a globally significant pest of stored food products and a subject of growing scientific interest for its larvae’s ability to penetrate packaging films and degrade synthetic polymers such as polyethylene. Its dual significance—as a destructive pest and as a potential contributor to plastic biodegradation—makes it a species of considerable importance in agriculture, materials science, and environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella is an insect larva mentioned as capable of feeding on and degrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Plodia interpunctella, also known as Indian-meal moth, is an insect species referenced in studies of plastic ingestion.</data>
