<node id="Citrobacter">
  <data key="d0">Citrobacter</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Citrobacter is a bacterial genus that has been identified in multiple ecological and biomedical contexts. Within insect gut microbiomes, Citrobacter is strongly associated with the digestion of synthetic polymers. It has been detected in superworms (Zophobas atratus larvae) fed diets containing polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP), as well as in mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) fed diets containing polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). In these studies, Citrobacter appears prominently in genus-level abundance analyses of microbial communities, where it is particularly enriched in the gut microbiome of PP-fed Z. atratus larvae.  

Beyond its association with plastic-degrading diets, Citrobacter has also been linked to nitrogen metabolism. It has been identified as a nitrogen-fixing bacterium within the guts of insects, including mealworms, suggesting a role in supporting host nutrition by contributing to nitrogen cycling. This dual involvement in both polymer-associated gut communities and nitrogen fixation highlights its ecological versatility and potential importance in insect-microbe symbioses.  

In addition to its ecological roles, Citrobacter is recognized in medical and pharmacological contexts as a bacterial genus associated with infections in humans. It is studied in clinical microbiology and pharmacology due to its pathogenic potential, underscoring its relevance across both environmental and biomedical domains.  

Overall, Citrobacter is a diverse bacterial genus notable for its presence in insect gut microbiomes linked to plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation, while also being clinically significant as a genus associated with human infections.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
