<node id="Klebsiella">
  <data key="d0">Klebsiella</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Klebsiella is a bacterial genus that has been identified in multiple ecological and experimental contexts, particularly in relation to insect gut microbiota and biodegradation processes. Within the gut microbiome of mealworms, Klebsiella has been consistently detected and is noted to occur in both control diets and polystyrene-fed conditions. Interestingly, higher counts of Klebsiella have been observed in mealworms maintained on control diets compared to those consuming polystyrene. Despite its prevalence, the genus has also been associated with negative gut health effects in larvae, suggesting a potentially detrimental role under certain conditions.  

Beyond its ecological presence, Klebsiella has been recognized for its functional capabilities in biodegradation. Strains belonging to this genus have been identified as capable of degrading bioplastics, and its association with mealworms consuming polystyrene highlights its potential involvement in plastic degradation pathways. This functional versatility underscores its relevance in studies of microbial contributions to waste management and environmental sustainability.  

From a taxonomic perspective, Klebsiella is well-characterized at the genetic level. For example, strain WJ2020 was determined to belong to this genus, clustering with 99% similarity in phylogenetic analysis. This close genetic relationship confirms its classification within Klebsiella and supports its role in the broader microbial community associated with mealworms.  

In summary, Klebsiella is a bacterial genus notable for its dual significance in both ecological and applied contexts. It is a common component of the mealworm gut microbiota, where it may influence host health, and it has demonstrated the ability to degrade synthetic polymers such as bioplastics and polystyrene. Its genetic characterization, including the classification of strain WJ2020, further establishes its identity and importance in microbiological and environmental research.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria identified as an important symbiont in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria isolated from the guts of nitrogen-fixing insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a bacterial genus belonging to the order Enterobacteriales, identified as an important nitrogen-fixing symbiont in Styrofoam-eating mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a bacterial genus identified as a nitrogen-fixing gut bacterium in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella is a bacterial genus found in the intestine of Aedes albopictus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
