<node id="Enterobacter">
  <data key="d0">Enterobacter</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterobacter is a bacterial genus that occupies an important ecological and biomedical role, with relevance in both environmental microbiology and human health. Within insect systems, Enterobacter has been consistently identified as a dominant and adaptable member of the gut microbiome, particularly in larvae such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor), superworms (Zophobas atratus), and the greater wax moth. Studies have shown that Enterobacter is present across different gut regions, including the posterior gut, and persists under a variety of diet conditions, ranging from conventional feed to plastic-based substrates. Its recurrent detection in microbial community and genus-level abundance analyses underscores its ecological significance in insect digestion.  

A notable aspect of Enterobacter’s role in insect microbiomes is its association with plastic degradation. In superworms, Enterobacter has been strongly linked to diets containing polypropylene (PP), while in greater wax moth larvae it has been enriched following polystyrene (PS) feeding. Certain strains of Enterobacter have been experimentally demonstrated to degrade styrene and polystyrene under both aerobic and anaerobic conditions, suggesting a functional contribution to the microbial processes that enable insects to metabolize synthetic polymers. However, dietary influences can modulate its abundance; for example, mealworms fed with polyvinyl chloride (PVC) exhibited a reduction in Enterobacter populations, indicating that different plastic substrates shape microbial communities in distinct ways. Despite such fluctuations, Enterobacter remains a recurrent and significant taxon across diet groups, highlighting its adaptability and potential role in insect-mediated plastic biodegradation.  

Beyond its ecological functions, Enterobacter is also recognized in clinical and pharmacological contexts. It is a facultative bacterial genus that includes strains capable of acting as probiotics in certain environments, but also as opportunistic pathogens in others. Enterobacter species have been associated with human infections and are studied in medical microbiology and pharmacology due to their clinical relevance. Identification of Enterobacter strains is commonly achieved through phylogenetic analysis of 16S rRNA sequences, which has been used to confirm their presence in both environmental and host-associated microbiomes.  

In summary, Enterobacter is a versatile bacterial genus with dual importance: it is a dominant and adaptable component of insect gut microbiomes, where it contributes to digestion and potentially to the biodegradation of plastics such as polypropylene and polystyrene, and it is also a clinically significant genus studied for its role in human health as both a potential probiotic and a pathogen. This combination of ecological adaptability and biomedical relevance makes Enterobacter a genus of considerable scientific interest.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria isolated from the guts of nitrogen-fixing insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacter is a bacterial genus found in the intestine of Aedes albopictus.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
