<node id="Plastic Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic biodegradation is the process by which plastic polymers are broken down through biological activity, primarily involving insects and microorganisms. This phenomenon has been observed in various contexts, including the ingestion and partial degradation of plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) by insect larvae and their associated gut microbiomes. Notably, species such as superworms and yellow mealworms (Tenebrio species) have demonstrated the ability to consume and degrade plastics including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and polystyrene.  

Research has shown that the gut microbiota of these insects plays a critical role in facilitating plastic biodegradation, with microbial communities contributing to the breakdown of otherwise persistent polymers. In addition to insect-mediated processes, bacterial strains have been identified that can degrade polystyrene films, further highlighting the role of microorganisms in this process. Studies have also examined the influence of gut microbes and emulsifying factors in enhancing the degradation efficiency of plastics within insect digestive systems.  

Overall, plastic biodegradation represents a biologically driven pathway for reducing the persistence of synthetic polymers in the environment. It encompasses both insect-based and microbial mechanisms, with a primary focus on widely used plastics such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and, to a lesser extent, polyvinyl chloride. This area of research is of growing interest due to its potential applications in mitigating plastic pollution through natural or bioengineered solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation is the central research theme, focusing on how insect larvae, fungi, and bacteria contribute to breaking down plastics such as polystyrene, polyethylene, polyurethane, and polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down plastics such as polyethylene and polypropylene through biological means, including gut microbes in insects like waxworms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic biodegradation refers to the process studied, where mealworms consumed PS and LDPE foams, and their frass was analyzed for polymer breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to the process of polystyrene breakdown by mealworms and their gut microorganisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387997</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Recycling">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Recycling</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic recycling refers to the process of reprocessing plastic waste, with only 10% of global plastic waste recycled as of 2019.&lt;SEP&gt;The process of reusing plastic waste, which currently accounts for only 14% of global plastic production, leaving 86% discarded or mismanaged.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic recycling is described as a critical process for achieving a sustainable and green society.</data>
