<node id="Plastic degradation experiment">
  <data key="d0">Plastic degradation experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental study where superworm and yellow mealworm larvae were fed with PE or PS foam plastics and bran controls for 45 days to study gut microbial communities and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The experiment studied the conversion efficiency, weight change, and mortality of yellow mealworms when fed different plastics (PE, PS, PVC) and wheat bran.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378669</data>
</node>
<node id="Frass samples">
  <data key="d0">Frass samples</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frass samples refer to the excreted waste material produced by larvae after consuming and metabolizing various polymer feedstocks, including polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). In experimental studies, these samples are systematically collected at different time points to evaluate the extent and mechanisms of plastic degradation occurring within the larval gut. The frass serves as a critical indicator of biodegradation processes, as it contains both residual polymer fragments and metabolic byproducts resulting from larval digestion.  

Analytical techniques such as gel permeation chromatography (GPC), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), and other chemical characterization methods are applied to frass samples to detect changes in molecular weight distribution, functional groups, and chemical structures. These analyses help confirm whether oxidation, depolymerization, or other biodegradation pathways have occurred during larval processing of plastics. For example, FTIR analysis of raw frass samples provides information on infrared absorption patterns, revealing the presence of new functional groups associated with oxidation and polymer breakdown. Similarly, GPC analysis of frass collected from Tenebrio molitor larvae enables the assessment of polymer chain length reduction over time.  

Frass samples are also used to assess carbon balance and track the fate of polymer-derived carbon, offering insights into how much of the ingested plastic is mineralized, assimilated, or excreted. By comparing frass derived from larvae fed on different polymers, researchers can evaluate the efficiency and specificity of larval biodegradation across various plastic types. In this way, frass samples function as a key experimental material for confirming plastic degradation, characterizing chemical transformations, and advancing the understanding of insect-mediated biodegradation pathways.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386488</data>
</node>
<node id="ATR-FTIR">
  <data key="d0">ATR-FTIR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is an analytical technique widely employed to investigate chemical and structural changes in polymeric and biological materials, with particular relevance to studies of plastic degradation. The method functions by detecting the vibrational modes of molecular bonds, which allows for the identification and characterization of major functional groups present in a sample. This capability makes ATR-FTIR a powerful tool for monitoring oxidation, bond cleavage, depolymerization, and the emergence of new chemical functionalities associated with both abiotic and biotic degradation processes.  

In environmental and microbiological research, ATR-FTIR has been extensively applied to assess the biodegradation of plastics. For example, it has been used to analyze frass samples resulting from the digestion of plastics by larvae, where spectral evidence revealed oxidation, depolymerization, and the formation of new functional groups, thereby confirming plastic biodegradation. The technique has also been employed to compare untreated and microbially treated polyethylene (PE) materials, including high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as well as polyethylene mulching films subjected to bacterial activity, where surface oxidation and chemical modifications were detected. Similarly, ATR-FTIR has been applied to study polystyrene (PS) films and nanopolystyrene powders, enabling the observation of chemical transformations during bacterial degradation and microbial growth. These applications highlight the versatility of the method in analyzing both bulk plastics and nanoscale materials.  

Beyond plastics, ATR-FTIR has been used to characterize chemical changes in mask layers and other polymeric materials, further demonstrating its broad applicability. Its ability to directly analyze solid and semi-solid samples without extensive preparation makes it particularly valuable for non-destructive testing. Instruments such as the Vertex70 (Brock Instruments, Germany) have been employed in these studies, offering high sensitivity and reproducibility for detecting subtle chemical transformations.  

Overall, ATR-FTIR serves as a critical analytical tool for elucidating the mechanisms of plastic weathering and biodegradation. By enabling the detection of functional group changes in plastics, frass, and related materials, it provides essential insights into chemical alterations induced by biological and chemical treatments. This makes ATR-FTIR indispensable for advancing the understanding of polymer degradation pathways and their environmental implications.&lt;SEP&gt;ATR-FTIR (attenuated total reflection Fourier transformed infrared spectroscopy) was used to evaluate oxidation and biodegradation of ingested polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387088</data>
</node>
<node id="TGA">
  <data key="d0">TGA</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) is an analytical technique widely applied in studies of polymer degradation and biodegradation, particularly in the context of insect-mediated plastic breakdown. It measures changes in the weight of a material as it is heated, providing insights into thermal stability, decomposition patterns, and chemical modifications. In biodegradation research, TGA has been used to analyze frass (insect excrement) samples to confirm the degradation of plastics within worm or larval digestive systems. Specifically, it has been employed to study the thermal stability of polystyrene (PS) before and after mealworm-mediated biodegradation, as well as to confirm polymer modifications during the biodegradation of PS and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Additionally, TGA has served as a key method to demonstrate the biodegradation of polyurethane (PU) and to verify oxidation and degradation of polypropylene (PP) in larvae. Beyond polymer-specific studies, TGA has also been applied to investigate the thermal stability and degradation of mask layers, further highlighting its versatility in assessing material transformations. Overall, TGA provides critical evidence of polymer breakdown, oxidation, and excretion, making it an essential tool for confirming and characterizing the biodegradation of plastics in biological systems.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) was used to study thermal properties of PE feedstocks and frass extracts.&lt;SEP&gt;TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) is an analytical technique used to confirm chemical and physical modifications during LDPE biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386517</data>
</node>
<node id="GPC">
  <data key="d0">GPC</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is an analytical technique widely employed to determine the molecular weight and molecular weight distribution of polymers, making it a critical tool in studies of polymer degradation and biodegradation. By separating polymer chains based on their size in solution, GPC provides detailed information on molecular weight averages and distribution profiles, which are essential for assessing structural changes in polymers.  

In the context of plastic biodegradation research, GPC has been extensively applied to evaluate the breakdown of synthetic polymers such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET), expanded polystyrene (EPS or Styrofoam), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polystyrene (PS). The technique has been used to analyze residual polymers in frass samples from insects such as worms and larvae, including Tenebrio species and superworms, after ingestion of plastics. By measuring reductions in molecular weight and shifts in molecular weight distribution, GPC provides direct evidence of polymer chain scission and depolymerization during gut passage. This application has also been extended to frass from antibiotic-treated insects, enabling researchers to investigate the role of gut microbiota in facilitating plastic degradation.  

Beyond insect-mediated biodegradation, GPC has been applied to study microbial degradation of PS films, the breakdown of Styrofoam, and the analysis of degraded polymer residues from discarded materials such as face masks. In each case, the method allows for quantification of molecular weight changes, thereby confirming the extent and nature of polymer degradation.  

Overall, Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) serves as a fundamental analytical method in polymer science and biodegradation studies. It enables precise measurement of molecular weight distributions, verification of polymer degradation processes, and evaluation of biological and microbial contributions to plastic depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC is an analytical method used to confirm the breakdown of benzoic rings in polystyrene during biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;GPC (gel permeation chromatography) analyses were performed in triplicate to study polymer properties.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
