<node id="Supporting Information">
  <data key="d0">Supporting Information</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">**Supporting Information** refers to supplementary material provided alongside a scientific article to enhance the main text by offering additional data, methodological details, and extended analyses. It is typically made available online through the publisher’s platform and serves as an important resource for readers seeking deeper insight into the study. Supporting Information may include detailed descriptions of experimental methods, such as sample preparation, analysis protocols, omics approaches, and specific methodological appendices like Method S1. It often contains extended datasets, figures, and tables that are too detailed for inclusion in the main article, such as morphology data, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, energy-dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping, and microbial analyses.  

In addition, Supporting Information can present supplementary experimental results, including antibiotic effects, bacterial growth patterns, phylogenetic trees, and chemical analyses, thereby providing a more comprehensive view of the research findings. It may also include calculations, extended figures, and other forms of data that support the conclusions of the study but are not essential to the primary narrative. Within the structure of a publication, Supporting Information is often indicated as a dedicated section heading, signaling the availability of these supplementary materials.  

Overall, Supporting Information functions as an integral extension of a scientific article, ensuring transparency, reproducibility, and completeness by making detailed methodologies, additional results, and supporting datasets accessible to the research community.&lt;SEP&gt;Supporting Information is referenced as the source for the full composition of vitamins and minerals and for the derivation of equations.&lt;SEP&gt;Supporting Information is referenced as containing full derivations and additional details for equations used in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Supporting Information contains additional descriptions of model parameter estimation steps and supplementary data such as Table S2 and Figures S3 and S4.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388207</data>
</node>
