<node id="Table S1">
  <data key="d0">Table S1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table S1 is a supplementary dataset that consolidates multiple lines of experimental evidence and analytical results related to the study of plastic degradation and its biological impacts in mealworms and associated microbial communities. It integrates chemical, biological, and ecological data to provide a comprehensive overview of the processes and outcomes observed under different experimental conditions.  

Specifically, Table S1 includes results from ATR-FTIR analyses that demonstrate oxidation and de-polymerization of plastics within larval guts, supporting evidence of plastic biodegradation. It also presents laboratory analysis of plastic materials, including information on additives, which helps contextualize the chemical transformations occurring during degradation.  

From a biological perspective, the table contains fitted death rate data, mortality outcomes, and larval age ranges for mealworms subjected to different feeding conditions and diets, offering insights into survival dynamics and the physiological impacts of plastic ingestion. It further details microbial community structure and abundance across dietary treatments, highlighting the role of gut microbiota in degradation processes.  

In addition, Table S1 provides sequencing-related data, including coverage and clean reads after quality control, as well as sequence information for bacterial isolates associated with polystyrene (PS) degradation. This is complemented by information on fungal diversity in larval guts, with species richness and diversity estimators such as OTUs, Shannon, and Simpson indices reported under varying dietary conditions.  

Overall, Table S1 serves as a comprehensive supplementary resource that integrates chemical analyses, mortality and developmental data, microbial sequencing results, and community diversity metrics. It provides critical supporting evidence for understanding the interactions between mealworms, their gut microbiota, and plastic materials during biodegradation studies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382781</data>
</node>
<node id="Table S2">
  <data key="d0">Table S2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table S2 is a supplementary dataset that consolidates multiple lines of experimental evidence related to plastic degradation processes in insect larvae and associated microbial activity. It presents results from ATR-FTIR analyses that demonstrate oxidation and depolymerization of plastics within larval guts, providing chemical evidence of polymer breakdown. Complementing this, the table reports statistical outcomes, including t-test results (p &lt; 0.05), which confirm significant decreases in molecular weight parameters (Mn and Mw) across all tested plastic sources, as well as reductions in Mw/Mn ratios in polymers recovered from the frass of *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus* larvae. These findings collectively indicate measurable structural changes in plastics following larval digestion.  

In addition to polymer characterization, Table S2 includes biological data such as worm weight changes under different dietary conditions, offering insights into the physiological impacts of plastic ingestion. It also documents sequencing results, specifically the number of valid reads per sample after removal of host contamination, which supports the reliability of microbial community analyses. The table further details identified microbial isolates and their associations with hydrocarbon and polymeric substrate degradation, with particular emphasis on the genus *Achromobacter*. Information is provided on the enzymatic activity of *Achromobacter* and its historical efficacy in degrading xenobiotic compounds, highlighting its potential role in plastic biotransformation.  

Finally, Table S2 incorporates laboratory analyses of plastic material purity, ensuring that observed degradation effects are attributable to biological activity rather than contaminants. Taken together, Table S2 serves as a comprehensive supplementary resource that integrates chemical, biological, and statistical evidence to support the study of plastic degradation in insect larvae and their associated microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Table S2 presents data on carbon conversion and digestion results of LDPE by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Table S2 provides data on larval density in the Control sample.&lt;SEP&gt;Table S2 contains data on larvae density and shape coefficient values used in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a</data>
