<node id="Frass Samples">
  <data key="d0">Frass Samples</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frass samples refer to the excreted waste material produced by larvae, particularly mealworms, during and after the consumption of plastic substrates such as polystyrene (PS). These samples are a key focus in studies investigating the biodegradation of plastics, as they provide direct evidence of how ingested polymers are processed and transformed within the larval digestive system. Collected typically on a weekly basis during feeding experiments, frass samples serve as measurable outputs for assessing both the extent of plastic consumption and the progression of biodegradation.  

The analysis of frass samples encompasses a wide range of techniques to capture chemical, physical, and biological changes in the material. Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is employed to detect chemical modifications in the plastics after digestion, while High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to evaluate shifts in molecular weight distribution, indicating polymer breakdown. Thermal property analyses further characterize the altered material, and cation content measurements provide insight into the elemental composition of the residues.  

Beyond chemical and physical characterization, frass samples are also used in microbiological studies. They serve as substrates for enrichment cultures, enabling the isolation and identification of microbial communities potentially involved in plastic degradation. In some experiments, frass collected after gentamicin suppression is specifically analyzed to distinguish microbial contributions from larval digestive processes.  

Overall, frass samples represent a critical analytical resource in plastic biodegradation research. By examining the excreted residues of plastics-fed larvae, researchers can trace the fate of synthetic polymers, identify intermediates and degradation products, and explore the synergistic roles of insect digestion and microbial activity in breaking down persistent plastic materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb</data>
