<node id="Oxidation">
  <data key="d0">Oxidation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxidation is a chemical process observed in the degradation of PE and PS plastics within larval guts, leading to the formation of new functional groups such as carbonyl and hydroxyl groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation refers to chemical modifications of MPS detected in both MLS and OLS mealworms through FTIR analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process observed in PS degradation, evidenced in worm frass in previous studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process observed on PS film surfaces during microbial degradation, confirmed by XPS and FTIR analyses.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation was identified in mask polymers after digestion by larvae, indicated by new C=O stretch peaks in ATR-FTIR analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process involved in the depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a chemical process occurring on the surface of aged MPs, reducing hydrophobicity and promoting microbial adhesion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386246</data>
</node>
<node id="Cannibalism">
  <data key="d0">Cannibalism</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Cannibalism, in the context of insect studies—particularly involving mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and darkling beetle larvae (Zophobas atratus)—refers to the behavior in which individuals consume conspecifics, including larvae, pupae, or adults. This phenomenon has been documented under experimental and controlled feeding conditions, where it can significantly influence both survival outcomes and the interpretation of biodegradation studies.  

In Tenebrio molitor, cannibalism has been observed when different developmental stages are housed together, with larvae preying on each other or adults consuming larvae. Such interactions reduce the efficiency of plastic degradation experiments, as cannibalism decreases the amount of plastic ingested and introduces additional biological residues into frass samples. These residues, derived from digested conspecific material, can confound measurements of plastic consumption and degradation. Researchers have therefore emphasized the importance of monitoring experimental populations, removing dead individuals and molting exoskeletons to prevent their consumption, and ensuring that results reflect true plastic biodegradation rather than nutritional recycling.  

The occurrence of cannibalism in mealworms is strongly influenced by environmental and dietary conditions. It has been linked to nutrient scarcity, where food limitation drives larvae to consume conspecifics as a survival strategy. This behavior has been interpreted as a means of accessing essential nutrients, including nitrogenous compounds that may support symbiotic microbial activity. Experimental findings show that cannibalism was absent when larvae were fed polystyrene alone, but it was observed in groups provided with wheat bran or dried whey, suggesting that diet composition plays a critical role. In some cases, cannibalism was also reported among polystyrene-fed or starved larvae, contributing to reduced survival rates.  

Cannibalism has been noted particularly during later stages of feeding trials, when dead larvae and exoskeletons become available and are consumed by surviving individuals. This behavior has been described not only as a confounding factor in biodegradation studies but also as a biological digestive process that contributes to decomposition products in frass samples. In Zophobas atratus, cannibalism has been observed among mature or large larvae under nutritional limitation, reinforcing its role as a context-dependent survival mechanism.  

Overall, cannibalism in Tenebrio molitor and related species is a complex, context-dependent behavior influenced by diet, nutrient availability, and developmental stage interactions. While it can serve as an adaptive strategy under resource-limited conditions, it poses challenges for experimental research on plastic biodegradation by altering survival rates, reducing plastic intake, and introducing confounding biological residues. Careful experimental design and management are therefore required to minimize its impact and obtain reliable data on insect-mediated plastic degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee</data>
