<node id="GenBank">
  <data key="d0">GenBank</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">GenBank is a comprehensive genetic sequence database maintained by the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI). It serves as a central repository for nucleotide sequences submitted by researchers worldwide and is widely used across diverse fields of biological and biomedical research. In various studies, GenBank has been employed as the primary platform for depositing Illumina MiSeq sequences, including those derived from gut bacterial microbiomes and gut fungi, ensuring that such data are accessible for future comparative and functional analyses.  

The database also plays a critical role in gene-specific investigations. For example, it has been used as a source of alkB nucleotide sequences, as well as for reporting the distribution of alkB and CYP153 genes in bacterial genomes. Researchers frequently rely on GenBank for sequence comparison and homology assessments, such as comparing alkB gene sequences to identify evolutionary relationships or functional similarities. Additionally, GenBank is integral to bioinformatics workflows, particularly in BLAST (Basic Local Alignment Search Tool) analyses, where it provides the reference dataset against which newly generated sequences are aligned and compared.  

Overall, GenBank functions as both a repository and a reference resource, supporting data deposition, retrieval, and comparative analysis of nucleotide sequences. Its role in storing diverse genetic information—from microbial communities to specific functional genes—underscores its importance as a foundational tool in genomics, molecular biology, and microbiome research.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a genetic sequence database where accession numbers MT459299 and MT459300 for strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 are stored.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a database used for comparing sequences during strain identification and phylogenetic analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a genetic sequence database where the sequence of the isolated bacterial strain was deposited.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a database where the 16 S rRNA sequences of the isolated bacterial strains were deposited.&lt;SEP&gt;GenBank is a genetic sequence database where the 16S rRNA and nifH gene sequences from the study were deposited.</data>
