<node id="OTUs">
  <data key="d0">OTUs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are a fundamental concept in microbial ecology and microbiome research, serving as a standardized way to classify and analyze microbial diversity. OTUs represent groups of closely related microorganisms, often used as proxies for microbial taxa when exact species-level identification is not feasible. They are widely applied in studies of bacterial and fungal communities, particularly in gut microbiome research, where they function as key measures of species richness and diversity.  

In gut microbiome analyses, OTUs are employed to estimate bacterial species richness and to evaluate community composition under varying conditions, such as different diets or environmental exposures. For example, in studies of yellow mealworms, OTUs were used to assess bacterial diversity across dietary treatments, providing insights into how diet influences gut microbial communities. Similarly, OTUs have been applied to fungal microbiome studies, where they serve as diversity estimators; in some cases, the number of OTUs was found to increase in experimental groups, such as mask-fed organisms, compared to controls.  

Beyond quantifying richness and diversity, OTUs are also used to evaluate community similarity across groups, enabling comparisons of microbial structure between experimental treatments. In certain studies, specific OTUs were significantly associated with particular dietary interventions, such as protein-enriched (PE) diets, highlighting their role in identifying microbial groups linked to functional or ecological shifts.  

Overall, OTUs provide a practical and widely adopted framework for classifying microbial groups, estimating species richness, and comparing community diversity and similarity. Their use in gut microbiome studies—across bacteria and fungi, in both insects and other hosts—makes them an essential tool for understanding how microbial communities respond to environmental and dietary factors.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
