<node id="Table S3">
  <data key="d0">Table S3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Table S3 is a supplementary dataset that consolidates diverse experimental results related to insect-mediated plastic degradation, gut microbiome characterization, and larval development. It encompasses multiple categories of information derived from biochemical, microbiological, and materials analyses. Specifically, Table S3 includes sequencing coverage data used to characterize the gut bacterial microbiome, providing insights into microbial diversity and potential functional roles in digestion. It also presents data highlighting the limited number of studies addressing the involvement of *Achromobacter* in polystyrene degradation, underscoring gaps in current research.  

From a materials science perspective, Table S3 reports molecular weight (Mn and Mw) values of feedstocks and frass samples from different sources, as well as detailed molecular weight changes in residual polymers, including high-density polyethylene (HDPE) and polypropylene, before and after larval digestion. Complementary to these measurements, the table contains Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis results of residual polymers, which help identify chemical modifications occurring during the degradation process. Additionally, gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS) data are included, with proposed chemical identities corresponding to peaks detected in worm frass, offering evidence of polymer breakdown products and metabolic transformations.  

Beyond polymer degradation, Table S3 also documents biological characteristics of larvae, such as instar stage, body weight, and sensitivity, providing context for how developmental factors may influence digestion and degradation efficiency. Together, these datasets make Table S3 a comprehensive resource that integrates microbiological, chemical, and biological perspectives, supporting the study of insect–plastic interactions, microbial contributions to polymer degradation, and the physiological traits of larvae involved in these processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Table S3 provides data on residual polymer fractions in frass samples compared to raw LDPE foams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
