<node id="Figure 4">
  <data key="d0">Figure 4</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 4 is a composite, multi-panel figure that integrates diverse analytical, molecular, and microbiological approaches to illustrate the biodegradation of synthetic plastics by insect larvae and their associated gut microbiomes. It combines structural, chemical, microbial, and transcriptomic evidence to provide a comprehensive view of how plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), and polyurethane (PU) are metabolized or degraded in larval systems.  

The figure presents physicochemical analyses of plastics before and after larval ingestion, including thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and gel permeation chromatography (GPC), which demonstrate changes in chemical stability and molecular weight distributions. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) micrographs further reveal surface alterations in plastic substrates, serving as visual evidence of degradation. Complementary characterization of PS degradation is provided through Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), contact angle measurements, and quantification of mass loss, all of which confirm structural and chemical modifications linked to microbial and enzymatic activity. Gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC–MS) data from superworms and mealworms fed with bran or polystyrene highlight the formation of chemical transformation products, indicating metabolic processing of plastic-derived compounds.  

Microbial community analyses form another major component of Figure 4. These include differential abundance and beta diversity analyses of gut microbiota in *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus* larvae under different diets, using methods such as principal coordinates analysis, nonmetric multidimensional scaling, and heatmap clustering. The results demonstrate significant shifts in microbial composition depending on the feeding substrate, underscoring the role of gut symbionts in plastic degradation. Metagenomic sequencing further identifies PET-degrading metabolic functions, with KEGG pathway enrichment, CAZy enzyme classification, and gene expression heatmaps providing functional insights into microbial contributions.  

Transcriptomic analyses of *T. molitor* larvae are also included, comparing gene expression profiles between polypropylene-fed and wheat bran-fed groups. Volcano plots of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) highlight host transcriptional responses to plastic diets, suggesting physiological adaptations to plastic ingestion.  

In addition to microbial and molecular data, Figure 4 reports quantitative measures of plastic consumption and degradation. These include comparative mass loss of PS in larvae fed with polystyrene alone versus polystyrene supplemented with carrots, demonstrating the influence of diet composition on degradation efficiency. It also presents data on weight loss and molecular weight reduction of PS samples inoculated with the bacterial strain YT2, providing evidence of microbial contributions beyond the insect host. Furthermore, feeding preference experiments show that polypropylene is the second most favorable plastic substrate consumed by mealworms, contextualizing degradation efficiency across different polymers.  

Overall, Figure 4 synthesizes a wide range of experimental evidence—spanning material characterization, microbial ecology, metagenomics, transcriptomics, and degradation assays—to support the conclusion that insect larvae and their gut microbiomes play a significant and multifaceted role in the biodegradation of synthetic plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 presents molecular weight (Mn, Mw, Mz) of PE and PS microplastics and frass of microplastic-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 4 illustrates digestive biofragmentation modeling of the size-reduction process of ingested PS and PE plastic particles in mealworms, including simulations and estimated removal times.</data>
