<node id="C.S. Criddle">
  <data key="d0">C.S. Criddle</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">C.S. Criddle is a scientific researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly through the role of insect larvae. Criddle has co-authored multiple publications investigating how mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and superworms can degrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene. This body of work includes studies on the microbial biodegradation of polystyrene using microbial communities enriched from the gut microbiome of mealworms, highlighting the potential role of gut-associated microorganisms in breaking down plastics.  

In addition to focusing on polystyrene, Criddle has contributed to research examining the biodegradation of polyethylene and mixed plastic waste in mealworms, with particular attention to the effects of plastic ingestion on the gut microbiome. These studies provide insights into both the biological mechanisms of plastic degradation and the ecological and physiological impacts on the insects involved.  

Criddle’s research, including a 2021 study on plastic biodegradation in insect larvae, has helped advance understanding of how insect-microbe systems may contribute to mitigating plastic pollution. By documenting the capacity of mealworms and superworms to process and metabolize plastics, Criddle’s work contributes to the broader field of sustainable waste management and the exploration of biologically based solutions to plastic waste challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386851</data>
</node>
<node id="W.M. Wu">
  <data key="d0">W.M. Wu</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">W.M. Wu is a researcher and author recognized for extensive contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly through the role of insect larvae. Wu has co-authored multiple studies investigating how mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and superworms can digest and biodegrade various synthetic polymers, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene. A significant focus of Wu’s work has been on the capacity of Tenebrio molitor larvae to consume and mineralize polystyrene, with a landmark 2015 study demonstrating that mealworms are capable of both biodegradation and mineralization of this plastic.  

Wu’s research has also explored the microbial mechanisms underlying this process, including the enrichment and activity of gut microbiota in Tenebrio molitor that facilitate the breakdown of polystyrene. In addition to polystyrene, Wu has co-authored studies on the biodegradation of polyethylene and polypropylene by insect larvae, further broadening the understanding of how different plastics can be biologically processed. More recent publications from 2020 and 2021 have continued to expand this line of inquiry, including investigations into the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene and polyethylene foams by mealworms.  

Overall, W.M. Wu’s body of work has been instrumental in advancing knowledge of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, highlighting both the digestive capabilities of larvae and the microbial communities that enable these processes. This research has provided important insights into potential biological solutions for mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;W.M. Wu is an author of a study on polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385036</data>
</node>
<node id="Y. Zhang">
  <data key="d0">Y. Zhang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Y. Zhang is a scientific researcher and author whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics and the microbial processes associated with this phenomenon. Zhang has contributed to multiple studies investigating the ability of insects, particularly mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and superworms, to degrade plastics such as polystyrene and polyethylene. These studies have explored both the direct consumption of plastics by the larvae and the role of their gut microbiota in facilitating degradation.  

A significant portion of Zhang’s research emphasizes the identification and characterization of gut-associated bacteria in *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, which are believed to play a critical role in breaking down otherwise recalcitrant polymers. This includes work on isolating and studying specific bacterial strains, such as polystyrene-degrading *Acinetobacter*, that contribute to the biodegradation process. By examining both the insect hosts and their microbial symbionts, Zhang’s research provides insights into the biological and ecological mechanisms that enable plastic degradation.  

Overall, Y. Zhang is recognized as an author of multiple scientific publications in this field, with contributions spanning studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene and polyethylene, the microbial ecology of insect guts, and the potential application of insect–microbe systems for mitigating plastic pollution. Zhang’s work is frequently cited in the literature on plastic biodegradation, underscoring its relevance to ongoing research into sustainable solutions for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Zhang is a co-author of a 2015 study on gut-associated bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of a scientific publication on QIIME 2 microbiome data science.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Zhang is a co-author of a study on gut-associated bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on a polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacterium isolated from larvae of Tribolium castaneum.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Zhang is an author of studies on nitrate-promoted antibiotic transformation and iron cycle tuning in Fenton reactions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387180</data>
</node>
<node id="Y. Li">
  <data key="d0">Y. Li</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Y. Li is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly through the role of insect larvae. Y. Li has co-authored multiple scientific studies investigating the capacity of organisms such as superworms and yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) to digest and degrade synthetic polymers, including polyethylene and polystyrene. This body of research explores both the biological mechanisms of plastic degradation and the ecological implications of using insect larvae as potential agents for mitigating plastic waste.  

Among the contributions, Y. Li is listed as an author of a 2018 study examining the biodegradation of polystyrene in yellow mealworms, which provided important insights into the ability of these larvae to consume and metabolize plastic materials. Additional studies co-authored by Y. Li have investigated the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms during plastic biodegradation, highlighting the role of microbial communities in facilitating the breakdown of otherwise persistent polymers. Research involving superworms has further expanded this line of inquiry, demonstrating that multiple insect species may contribute to the biodegradation of different types of plastics.  

Overall, Y. Li’s scholarly contributions emphasize the intersection of entomology, microbiology, and environmental science, with a particular focus on developing biological strategies for addressing plastic pollution. Through collaborative studies, Y. Li has advanced understanding of how insect larvae and their associated gut microbiota can participate in the degradation of common plastics, thereby offering potential pathways for sustainable waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Li is an author cited in the references for work on mechanical properties of expanded polystyrene concrete and brick.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278</data>
