<node id="A.M. Brandon">
  <data key="d0">A.M. Brandon</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A.M. Brandon is a researcher and author recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly through the use of insects and their associated microbiomes. Dr. Brandon has co-authored multiple studies investigating the capacity of mealworms, specifically *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (commonly known as yellow mealworms), to digest and biodegrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene. This body of work has explored both the direct degradation of plastics by the larvae and the role of their gut microbiota in facilitating this process.  

Brandon’s research includes studies on microbial enrichment derived from the gut microbiome of *T. molitor* larvae, which has been shown to play a critical role in the breakdown of polystyrene. These investigations have provided insights into the microbial and enzymatic mechanisms underlying plastic degradation, highlighting the potential of insect-microbe systems as a biological solution to plastic waste. In addition to polystyrene, Brandon has contributed to studies examining the biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures, further broadening the scope of research into different types of synthetic polymers.  

Several of Brandon’s works also address the effects of plastic consumption on the gut microbiome of mealworms, offering a more comprehensive understanding of the interactions between host organisms, their microbial communities, and synthetic materials. Collectively, these studies have established A.M. Brandon as a key contributor to the emerging field of plastic biodegradation research, with a focus on insect-based and microbiome-mediated approaches to mitigating plastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
