<node id="Y. Yang">
  <data key="d0">Y. Yang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Y. Yang is a researcher affiliated with Beihang University who is widely recognized for significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly through microbial and insect-mediated processes. Their research has focused on understanding how persistent synthetic polymers such as polyethylene and polystyrene can be biologically degraded and potentially valorized into less harmful or useful products.  

A central theme of Y. Yang’s work is the role of insects and their associated microbiota in plastic degradation. They have co-authored multiple studies demonstrating that Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) are capable of consuming and mineralizing polystyrene, providing experimental evidence that insect-based systems can contribute to mitigating plastic pollution. Similarly, Y. Yang has investigated the biodegradation of polyethylene by bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms, highlighting the importance of insect gut microbiomes as reservoirs of plastic-degrading microorganisms. These studies have been widely cited in the scientific literature and have advanced understanding of the biological mechanisms underlying plastic breakdown.  

In addition to insect-based research, Y. Yang has contributed to broader investigations into microbial and enzymatic pathways for polyethylene biodegradation, including reviews that synthesize current knowledge in the field. Their work has also extended to applied environmental contexts, such as co-authoring a study on the enhanced biodegradation of microplastics in sewage sludge using hyperthermophilic composting, which explores scalable approaches for managing plastic waste.  

Overall, Y. Yang’s body of research integrates microbiology, entomology, and environmental science to address the global challenge of plastic pollution. By examining both microbial strains and insect hosts capable of degrading synthetic polymers, Y. Yang has helped establish a scientific foundation for sustainable, biologically driven solutions to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Yang is an author of a study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation of foam plastics by Zophobas atratus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Y. Yang is an author cited in the references.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
