<node id="J. Yang">
  <data key="d0">J. Yang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">J. Yang is a researcher and academic affiliated with Beihang University who has made significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation. Their work focuses primarily on the microbial and insect-mediated degradation of synthetic polymers such as polyethylene and polystyrene. J. Yang has authored and co-authored multiple studies investigating the ability of organisms, including waxworms and mealworms, to consume and break down plastics, as well as the role of associated bacterial strains in facilitating this process.  

In particular, J. Yang is recognized as a co-author of a 2015 study that demonstrated the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, a finding that has been widely cited in subsequent research on biological solutions to plastic pollution. Beyond this landmark study, J. Yang has continued to publish on the mechanisms of plastic degradation, including the identification of bacterial strains from waxworms capable of degrading polyethylene and the broader ecological and biochemical pathways involved in the mineralization of plastics.  

Through these contributions, J. Yang has become a frequently cited figure in the field of environmental microbiology and biodegradation research. Their work has advanced scientific understanding of how insects and their gut microbiota can contribute to mitigating the environmental impact of persistent plastic waste. By combining experimental studies with microbial analysis, J. Yang’s research provides important insights into potential biotechnological applications for sustainable plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Yang is an author of a study on polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains from waxworm guts.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Yang is an author of a study on polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Yang is listed as an author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by waxworms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Yang is an author of multiple studies on biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Yang is an author cited in the references.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387875</data>
</node>
<node id="J. Zhao">
  <data key="d0">J. Zhao</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">J. Zhao is a researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the roles of insects and their associated microbial communities. Zhao has co-authored multiple studies investigating the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, including a notable 2015 study that demonstrated the ability of mealworms to consume and metabolize polystyrene, providing evidence of plastic degradation through biological processes. In addition to work on mealworms, Zhao has also conducted research on polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains isolated from waxworms, as well as studies on polystyrene-degrading bacteria derived from the gut of Galleria mellonella larvae. Collectively, Zhao’s publications contribute to the growing body of evidence that certain insect species and their gut microbiota play a significant role in breaking down synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene, offering potential pathways for sustainable plastic waste management. Zhao’s work is frequently cited in the scientific literature on plastic biodegradation, underscoring its importance in advancing understanding of biological solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Zhao is listed as an author of multiple studies on biodegradation of plastics by waxworms and mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Zhao is an author of a study on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;J. Zhao is an author cited in the references.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387876</data>
</node>
<node id="L. Jiang">
  <data key="d0">L. Jiang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">L. Jiang is a researcher and author whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation and mineralization of synthetic polymers, particularly plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene. Jiang has contributed to multiple studies investigating the ability of biological organisms, including waxworms and mealworms, to degrade these persistent materials. This research encompasses both the microbial processes involved, such as the role of bacterial strains isolated from waxworms in polyethylene biodegradation, and the direct consumption and breakdown of polystyrene by mealworms.  

Jiang’s studies on mealworms have examined not only the biodegradation of polystyrene but also its mineralization, providing insights into the extent to which the polymer can be converted into simpler compounds. These investigations often include detailed chemical and physical characterizations, as well as isotopic analyses, to track the transformation of plastic material and assess the environmental implications of such processes. By integrating biological, chemical, and physical perspectives, Jiang’s work contributes to a deeper understanding of how insect-based systems may offer potential solutions for mitigating plastic pollution.  

In addition to research on plastic biodegradation, L. Jiang has also been cited for contributions to studies on the mechanical properties of construction materials, including expanded polystyrene concrete and brick. This indicates a broader research scope that extends beyond biodegradation to encompass material science and engineering applications.  

Overall, L. Jiang is recognized as an author of multiple influential publications that explore innovative biological pathways for addressing plastic waste, with a particular emphasis on the roles of waxworms and mealworms in the degradation and mineralization of polyethylene and polystyrene. This body of work positions Jiang as a contributor to advancing sustainable strategies for plastic waste management while also engaging in research on the structural properties of polymer-based construction materials.&lt;SEP&gt;L. Jiang is an author cited in the references.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387876</data>
</node>
