<node id="Science of the Total Environment">
  <data key="d0">Science of the Total Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">**Science of the Total Environment** is a peer-reviewed scientific journal that publishes research across the full spectrum of environmental science, with a strong emphasis on pollution, ecological impacts, and sustainability. It serves as a leading platform for disseminating studies that address urgent environmental challenges and explore innovative solutions, particularly in the context of plastic pollution and biodegradation.  

The journal has become a prominent venue for experimental research on the biological degradation of plastics by insects and microorganisms. Notable studies published in *Science of the Total Environment* include investigations into the capacity of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae to degrade vulcanized styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR), tire crumb, and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Comparative research has also examined the ingestion preferences and degradation abilities of mealworms and superworms when exposed to different plastics such as polyethylene and polypropylene. In addition, the journal has featured studies on microbial contributions to plastic degradation, such as the role of *Acinetobacterium* isolated from *Tribolium castaneum*. Other published work has explored the biodegradation of micro-polystyrene and its developmental effects on *Tenebrio molitor*, thereby advancing understanding of both the mechanisms and ecological consequences of biological plastic breakdown.  

Beyond laboratory-focused biodegradation research, *Science of the Total Environment* has published influential studies on the environmental distribution, pathways, and impacts of plastics and microplastics. These include assessments of microplastic contamination in remote ecosystems, such as the Antarctic marine environment, as well as region-specific case studies, such as reviews of microplastic pollution in Yunnan Province, China. By addressing both global and localized perspectives, the journal highlights the widespread nature of plastic pollution and its implications for ecosystems and human health.  

Overall, *Science of the Total Environment* plays a critical role in advancing scientific knowledge on environmental issues, particularly those related to plastic pollution. By integrating experimental studies on biodegradation mechanisms with broader environmental assessments of microplastic distribution and impact, the journal contributes to the development of strategies for mitigating plastic waste and safeguarding ecosystems. It is widely recognized as a key publication venue for research at the intersection of pollution, ecology, and sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Science of the Total Environment is a scientific journal referenced in the text, publishing research on biodegradation of polyethylene and polypropylene by mealworms and superworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40</data>
