<node id="Environmental Science &amp; Technology">
  <data key="d0">Environmental Science &amp; Technology</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">**Environmental Science &amp; Technology** is a peer-reviewed scientific journal recognized as a leading platform for research in environmental science, with a strong reputation for publishing pioneering studies on plastic biodegradation. Commonly abbreviated as *Environ. Sci. Technol.*, the journal has played a central role in advancing scientific understanding of how biological systems—including insects and microorganisms—can degrade synthetic polymers such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and polypropylene.  

A landmark contribution published in the journal in 2014 demonstrated polyethylene biodegradation by bacterial strains isolated from waxworms, providing early evidence of microbial activity as a mechanism for breaking down this widely used plastic. Building on this foundation, *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* published influential studies in 2015 that documented the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, marking some of the first demonstrations that insect-based systems could contribute to plastic waste management. These studies highlighted the role of the gut microbiome in facilitating plastic degradation, opening new avenues for research into biological solutions to plastic pollution.  

Subsequent publications in the journal have expanded this line of inquiry. A 2019 article examined polystyrene biodegradation by two mealworm species, *Tenebrio obscurus* and *Tenebrio molitor*, offering comparative insights into species-specific degradation capabilities. Other research featured in the journal has addressed the breakdown of Styrofoam debris, including studies on gut bacteria such as strain YT2, and has explored the broader role of microbial communities in plastic degradation processes. More recent contributions have reported on polypropylene biodegradation by *T. molitor* larvae and microbial biodegradation of polystyrene in *T. molitor*, further broadening the scope of biological approaches to addressing plastic waste.  

The journal has served as the publication venue for influential researchers including Brandon, Gao, Tian, Ning, Yang, Zhou, Wu, and Criddle, underscoring its status as a trusted outlet for high-impact research in this domain. Collectively, these contributions have positioned *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* as a central forum for documenting and advancing knowledge on the interactions between insects, microorganisms, and synthetic polymers.  

Overall, *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* has established itself as a critical resource for disseminating scientific advances on environmental challenges, particularly those related to plastic pollution and biodegradation. By publishing foundational and ongoing research, the journal continues to provide valuable insights into potential biological solutions for mitigating the global problem of plastic waste.</data>
