<node id="Brandon et al.">
  <data key="d0">Brandon et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon et al. are researchers recognized for their influential contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation by insect larvae, particularly the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*). Their work, beginning with key publications in 2018 and extending into subsequent years, has provided foundational insights into the ability of mealworms to ingest, metabolize, and mineralize synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyethylene (PE), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC).  

Through controlled experimental studies, Brandon et al. demonstrated that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of consuming these plastics and converting them into simpler compounds. A notable finding was that polyethylene ingested by mealworms could be mineralized into carbon dioxide, with a reported conversion rate of 42.0 ± 1.4% within 32 days. This result underscored the potential of biological systems to achieve true biodegradation, rather than merely fragmenting plastics into microplastics. Their research also highlighted the role of the gut microbiome in facilitating this process, identifying eight unique microbial taxa—including *Citrobacter freundii*, *Serratia marcescens*, and *Klebsiella aerogenes*—that contribute to the depolymerization and assimilation of polystyrene.  

In addition to microbial involvement, Brandon et al. reported that mealworms secrete emulsifying agents that aid in the breakdown of plastic polymers, suggesting a synergistic mechanism between physiological secretions and microbial activity. Their studies further examined the survival, growth, and pupation of larvae maintained on plastic-based diets, showing that mealworms could sustain themselves on LDPE and EPS, albeit with varying nutritional and developmental outcomes.  

Beyond polymer degradation, Brandon et al. also investigated the interaction between mealworms and chemical additives in plastics, such as flame retardants. They studied the excretion and resistance of larvae to hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD), a common flame retardant, and explored the toxicological effects of polystyrene diets on larval health and development. These findings broadened the scope of their research by addressing not only the biodegradation of plastics but also the ecological and toxicological implications of plastic ingestion by insects.  

Overall, Brandon et al. have made significant contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation research. Their work established that *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, supported by their gut microbiota and biochemical secretions, can degrade and mineralize persistent synthetic polymers. By integrating studies on polymer breakdown, microbial ecology, larval physiology, and toxicological responses, Brandon et al. provided a comprehensive framework for understanding insect-based biodegradation systems. Their findings continue to be widely cited as evidence of the potential for biological solutions to address global plastic waste challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
