<node id="Peng et al.">
  <data key="d0">Peng et al.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng et al. are a group of researchers recognized for their extensive studies on the biodegradation of plastics by insects, particularly mealworms (larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*) and superworms (*Zophobas atratus*). Between 2019 and 2021, they published multiple works that advanced understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation, with a focus on polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and polyethylene. Their research demonstrated that mealworms and related larvae are capable of consuming and degrading synthetic polymers, contributing to the growing body of evidence supporting insect-based bioremediation strategies.  

In their 2019 study, Peng et al. investigated EPS biodegradation in mealworms using analytical techniques such as Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). These methods allowed them to characterize changes in fecula (excreta) composition and to quantify mass-loss ratios, providing evidence of polymer breakdown during digestion. Subsequent studies in 2020 and 2021 expanded on these findings by examining the degradation of plastics with different molecular weights and compositions, including polyethylene and polystyrene.  

A key focus of Peng et al.’s work was the role of the insect gut microbiome and metabolome in facilitating plastic degradation. They analyzed microbial community shifts and metabolic responses when larvae were fed PS polymers of varying molecular weights, revealing that gut microorganisms play an important role in the depolymerization and assimilation processes. Their findings aligned with other studies reporting similar microbiome adaptations in mealworms exposed to plastic diets.  

Peng et al. also explored the influence of co-feeding strategies on degradation efficiency. They observed that supplementing polystyrene with natural food sources such as bran or corn flour enhanced the rate of plastic consumption and breakdown, suggesting that nutritional support improves insect survival and metabolic activity during plastic degradation.  

Overall, Peng et al. made significant contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation by insects. Their research provided detailed insights into the physicochemical changes in plastics during digestion, the role of gut microbial communities, and the potential for optimizing degradation through dietary supplementation. These studies have been widely cited as foundational evidence for the feasibility of using mealworms and related larvae as biological agents for mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng et al. are cited in 2020 studies on Zophobas atratus larvae and plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng et al. are researchers cited in studies (2019, 2020a) related to gut microbiota and plastic degradation in larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng et al. are researchers who studied Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio species in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng et al. are cited researchers who studied insect larvae behavior and plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Peng et al. are researchers who showed that antibiotics can suppress gut microbiota and inhibit polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
