<node id="Bacillus sp. YP1">
  <data key="d0">Bacillus sp. YP1</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworm larvae, specifically *Plodia interpunctella*. This microorganism has attracted scientific interest due to its ability to degrade synthetic polymers, particularly polyethylene (PE) and its variants, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), as well as polystyrene. Identified and reported by Yang et al., Bacillus sp. YP1 was deposited in GenBank under the accession number KJ466897. Experimental studies have demonstrated its capacity to break down plastic materials, with one study showing that the strain was able to remove approximately 10.7% of LDPE films after 60 days of incubation.  

The strain’s isolation from the waxworm gut highlights the role of insect-associated microbiota in plastic biodegradation, suggesting that symbiotic gut bacteria may contribute to the host’s ability to digest or modify otherwise recalcitrant polymers. Bacillus sp. YP1 has been genome sequenced, providing a foundation for further exploration of its metabolic pathways and enzymatic mechanisms involved in polymer degradation. Its demonstrated activity against PE, LDPE, and polystyrene positions it as a promising candidate for biotechnological applications aimed at mitigating plastic pollution.  

In summary, Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene- and polystyrene-degrading bacterium derived from the gut of *Plodia interpunctella* waxworms, with documented biodegradation activity and genomic characterization, making it a significant organism for research into microbial plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. YP1 is a microbial strain capable of degrading commercial LDPE film.&lt;SEP&gt;A polyethylene-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene (PE)-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the waxworm gut.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterium isolated from waxworm's gut.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that degraded about 11.3% of commercial LDPE film with a Mw of 88.2 kDa in more than two months.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
