<node id="Expanded Polystyrene (EPS)">
  <data key="d0">Expanded Polystyrene (EPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid, and low-cost foam form of polystyrene, widely recognized under the trade name Styrofoam. It is extensively used in packaging, disposable food containers, protective cushioning for consumer goods, and as an insulation material in construction. Its popularity stems from its affordability, versatility, thermal insulation capacity, and shock-absorbing properties. Commercially, EPS is typically applied without complex processing, often being cut into shapes or dimensions suited for specific applications.  

Despite its utility, Expanded Polystyrene poses significant environmental challenges. As a durable, single-use plastic, EPS is highly resistant to natural degradation, leading to its accumulation in landfills and ecosystems. Its persistence contributes substantially to long-term plastic waste, and improper disposal methods, such as incineration, can release pollutants and exacerbate environmental harm. These issues have made EPS a central subject in debates on plastic waste management, recycling, and the development of sustainable alternatives.  

Scientific research has increasingly investigated the biodegradation potential of EPS, particularly through biological organisms. Studies have demonstrated that mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and certain beetles can chew, ingest, and partially metabolize EPS. The degradation process is facilitated by the larvae’s intestinal microbiota, which plays a role in breaking down the polymer. In experimental settings, EPS collected from sources such as electronic equipment boxes was used as a feed material to evaluate its effects on mealworm survival, growth, and reproduction. Results indicated that larvae not only consumed EPS but also exhibited significant weight gain, suggesting that EPS can serve as a partial energy source. Research has also examined EPS with a weight-average molecular weight of approximately 256.4 kDa as a substrate for microbial degradation studies.  

While these findings highlight promising biological pathways for mitigating EPS waste, practical and large-scale applications of such biodegradation methods remain under development. Current studies emphasize the potential of mealworms and their associated microbiota as a natural means of reducing EPS accumulation, but further research is needed to optimize and scale these processes.  

In summary, Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a widely used plastic material valued for its light weight, insulating properties, and cost-effectiveness, but it also represents a major environmental concern due to its persistence and resistance to decomposition. Ongoing research into biological degradation, particularly through mealworms and microbial activity, offers potential strategies for addressing EPS waste, though scalable solutions are still in progress.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene is a major form of polystyrene used in packaging, food contact materials, and aquaculture buoys.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight plastic material used in insulation and packaging, known for causing environmental pollution due to its resistance to decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a material provided by Woosung Resin Co., Ltd., consisting of 93–96% EPS, 4–7% pentane, and less than 1% 1,2,5,6,9,10-hexabromocyclododecane.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a plastic material ingested by mealworms in the study, leading to degradation and weight changes in the insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a commercial form of PS used in building insulation and packaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627</data>
