<node id="Plastics (PS, PE, PP, PVC, LDPE, LLDPE, EPS, HIPS)">
  <data key="d0">Plastics (PS, PE, PP, PVC, LDPE, LLDPE, EPS, HIPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Various plastics studied for degradation by mealworms and other insects, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and their subtypes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756371824</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Bacteria">
  <data key="d0">Gut Bacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gut bacteria are microbial communities residing in the digestive systems of mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and superworms, where they play a critical role in the biodegradation of synthetic plastics. Research has demonstrated that these bacteria are directly involved in the breakdown of materials such as polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). Antibiotic suppression experiments provide strong evidence of their importance, as the inhibition of gut bacteria significantly reduces the ability of mealworms to depolymerize and metabolize plastics.  

The mechanisms by which gut bacteria contribute to plastic degradation are multifaceted. They are suggested to secrete extracellular depolymerases, enzymes capable of breaking down complex polymer chains into smaller, more manageable molecules. In the case of PET, gut bacteria exhibit chemotaxis toward PET intermediates, metabolize depolymerized products, and produce bioemulsifying factors that enhance the degradation process. For EPS and PS, these microbial communities facilitate depolymerization and further biodegradation, converting portions of the consumed plastic into carbon dioxide and microbial biomass.  

Beyond enzymatic activity, gut bacteria also influence the host larvae’s digestive processes by altering gene expression, thereby supporting the overall capacity of the insect to utilize plastics as a carbon source. This symbiotic relationship highlights the essential role of gut microbiota in enabling mealworms and related larvae to process otherwise recalcitrant synthetic polymers.  

In summary, gut bacteria in mealworms and superworms are indispensable agents in plastic biodegradation. Through enzymatic depolymerization, metabolic processing of intermediates, and synergistic interactions with host physiology, these microorganisms transform persistent plastics into simpler compounds, contributing to both energy acquisition for the larvae and the partial mineralization of plastics into environmentally benign products.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in mealworms were studied for suppression using antibiotics and for their role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria are suggested as a possible factor in the plastic-degrading ability of Achroia grisella larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria are microbial communities in the larvae of T. obscurus and T. molitor, playing a critical role in PS depolymerization and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in yellow mealworms contributed to plastic biodegradation, with responses differing from gut fungi.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria in Tenebrio molitor larvae play a unique role in the biodegradation of polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
