<node id="LLDPE">
  <data key="d0">LLDPE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">LLDPE (Linear Low-Density Polyethylene) is a widely used polyethylene polymer distinguished by its linear molecular chain structure with fewer branches compared to low-density polyethylene (LDPE). This structural difference contributes to its unique physical properties, including a relatively high degree of crystallinity (approximately 51.9%) and distinct mechanical characteristics such as oriented structure and anisotropy in blown films. LLDPE accounts for an estimated 17.4% of total global plastic production, making it a significant material in the manufacture of diverse products and a major contributor to plastic waste streams.  

Due to its environmental persistence, LLDPE has been the focus of biodegradation and depolymerization studies, particularly involving invertebrate models such as Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae (commonly known as mealworms). In controlled experiments, LLDPE was provided as a dietary component to evaluate its biodegradation potential, nutritional effects, and physiological impacts on the larvae. Compared to LDPE, LLDPE was consumed less readily and degraded at a slower rate. Its breakdown was associated with increased physiological stress, including oxidative stress and impairment in the larvae, indicating that its molecular structure poses greater challenges to biological degradation processes.  

Experimental results also showed that feeding LLDPE to Tenebrio larvae led to significant increases in the molecular weights of residual polymers, suggesting limited depolymerization and incomplete breakdown. This contrasts with the relatively higher degradability of LDPE, underscoring the difficulty of processing LLDPE through biological means. Despite these challenges, LLDPE remains an important target for ongoing research into plastic biodegradation, given its widespread use, environmental persistence, and the urgent need to develop effective strategies for managing polyethylene microplastics.  

In summary, LLDPE (Linear Low-Density Polyethylene) is a globally significant plastic polymer characterized by its linear structure, high crystallinity, and industrial importance. While it has been studied extensively in biodegradation experiments with Tenebrio larvae, findings indicate that it is more resistant to biological breakdown than LDPE and induces greater physiological stress in organisms attempting to metabolize it. These properties highlight both the environmental challenges posed by LLDPE and its importance as a focus of future biodegradation and depolymerization research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383224</data>
</node>
<node id="EPS">
  <data key="d0">EPS</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">EPS (Expanded Polystyrene) is a lightweight, amorphous plastic foam widely used in commercial and industrial applications, particularly in packaging, insulation, and construction. It is valued for its low density, thermal insulation capacity, and versatility, but it is also recognized as a persistent synthetic polymer that poses environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural degradation. These characteristics have made EPS both a practical material in engineering contexts and a focal point of scientific research into polymer breakdown and biodegradation.  

In material performance studies, EPS has been tested for physical properties such as hardness, gloss, and mass loss. Results indicate that EPS exhibits relatively low hardness and gloss compared to other materials, along with a notable mass loss of around 19.29%. In construction-related experiments, both white and grey EPS have been applied as insulation materials, adhered to ceramic bricks with cement mortar, to evaluate their effectiveness under practical conditions. These investigations highlight EPS’s continued importance in building technologies, where its insulating properties are widely utilized.  

At the same time, EPS has become a model material in biodegradation research, where scientists explore its potential breakdown by biological systems. Several studies have demonstrated that insects, particularly larvae of Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms), can ingest EPS, with partial depolymerization occurring during digestion. Supplementing the larvae’s diet with bran has been shown to increase ingestion rates and influence physiological responses, including changes in larval weight and gene expression, suggesting that EPS consumption affects both metabolism and feeding behavior. Other insect species, such as Galleria mellonella larvae and barley worms, have also been tested as EPS consumers, with experiments leading to the isolation of microbial strains such as Masilia sp. FS1903.  

Microbial degradation of EPS has also been investigated, with certain bacterial groups, including Enterobacteriaceae and Acinetobacter spp., showing potential to degrade EPS. Mechanisms such as adhesion and autoaggregation have been proposed as contributing factors in microbial interactions with the polymer. These findings indicate that both insect gut microbiota and free-living microorganisms may play roles in EPS transformation, making it a valuable substrate for studying bioremediation strategies.  

Overall, EPS (Expanded Polystyrene) occupies a dual role: it is a widely used material in packaging and construction due to its functional properties, and it is also a significant subject of environmental and biodegradation research. Its persistence in the environment has prompted investigations into biological and microbial pathways for its breakdown, positioning EPS as a key material for advancing knowledge in both applied engineering and environmental science.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
