<node id="PLA">
  <data key="d0">PLA</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable, bio-based polymer derived from renewable resources and widely studied as a sustainable alternative to conventional plastics. Unlike petroleum-based polymers such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET), PLA demonstrates higher biodegradation rates, facilitated by enzymatic hydrolysis through polyester hydrolases, lipases, and carboxylesterases. Its relatively low molecular weight and crystallinity contribute to its degradability, and it has also been associated with microbial degradation, including by Lactococcus species.  

PLA has been a focus of biodegradation research involving insect larvae, particularly Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) and other insect models. Studies have shown that T. molitor larvae are capable of ingesting PLA at rates ranging from 45.2 to 202.7 mg per 100 larvae per day. When incorporated into their diet, PLA accelerated pupation but inhibited successful development into adult beetles, suggesting that while larvae can metabolize the polymer, it may interfere with normal life cycle progression. Importantly, among plastics tested in similar experiments, PLA caused the least oxidative stress and physiological damage to larvae, indicating it is less harmful than many synthetic alternatives. Furthermore, when PLA was provided in combination with a natural food source such as bran, larvae exhibited improved growth and physiological function, highlighting the role of co-dietary supplementation in supporting plastic-degrading organisms.  

In addition to insect-based studies, PLA has been tested as a plastic substrate in broader biodegradation experiments, where it has shown promising results compared to other polymers. In some contexts, insect larvae fed on PLA substrates performed well in terms of carbon conversion metrics (CCM) outputs, further underscoring its potential in biological degradation pathways.  

Overall, PLA represents a distinctive case among plastics: it is biodegradable, derived from renewable resources, relatively less toxic to organisms, and subject to enzymatic and microbial breakdown. These characteristics make PLA a promising candidate for sustainable material applications and for advancing research into biological plastic degradation. However, its observed effects on insect development emphasize the need for continued investigation into the ecological and biological consequences of PLA ingestion and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PLA refers to polylactic acid, a biodegradable plastic material used as food for mealworms in the experiment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
