<node id="RT-PCR">
  <data key="d0">RT-PCR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">RT-PCR (Reverse Transcription Polymerase Chain Reaction), also referred to as real-time PCR, is a molecular biology technique widely used for the quantification and validation of gene expression. It functions by converting RNA into complementary DNA (cDNA) through reverse transcription, followed by amplification of specific DNA sequences via polymerase chain reaction. This method is particularly valuable for confirming differential gene expression (DEGs) identified through high-throughput sequencing approaches such as RNA-Seq and metatranscriptomic analysis.  

In experimental studies, RT-PCR has been employed to validate the expression levels of various enzymes and genes involved in metabolic and degradative processes. For example, it has been used to measure the expression of enzymes such as acyl-CoA, acetyl-CoA acyltransferase, cytochrome P450 6A1, carboxylesterase, and glutathione S-transferases, which play roles in detoxification and metabolic pathways. Additionally, RT-PCR has been applied to assess the expression of plastic- and lignocellulose-degrading enzymes in insect larvae, particularly mealworms, which are capable of consuming and metabolizing synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS) and cellulose-based materials (CS). Through this application, RT-PCR has provided evidence for the upregulation of PS- and CS-degrading enzymes, thereby supporting the role of mealworms and their gut microbiota in biodegradation processes.  

Furthermore, RT-PCR has been used to analyze specific microbial gene expression within insect gut communities. For instance, it has been applied to detect and quantify transcribed nifH mRNA, a marker of nitrogen fixation, in the gut microbiota of Styrofoam-eating mealworms. This highlights its utility not only in host gene expression studies but also in characterizing the functional activity of symbiotic microbial populations.  

Overall, RT-PCR serves as a critical validation tool in molecular biology, bridging high-throughput sequencing data with precise quantification of gene expression. Its applications in studies of mealworms and their associated microbiota underscore its importance in elucidating the molecular mechanisms underlying plastic degradation, lignocellulose digestion, detoxification, and microbial symbiosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386000</data>
</node>
