<node id="Yang et al., 2015b">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al., 2015b</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">**Yang et al., 2015b** refers to a scientific study that made significant contributions to the understanding of microbial plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on polystyrene (PS). In this work, Yang and colleagues investigated the role of gut microbiota in the degradation of plastics, with a specific emphasis on bacteria isolated from the digestive tract of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. The study was among the first to identify and characterize bacterial strains within the insect gut capable of depolymerizing and metabolizing PS, thereby establishing a biological pathway for plastic degradation. One of the key findings was the identification of *Exiguobacterium* sp. as a PS-degrading bacterium, highlighting the ecological and biotechnological significance of insect gut microbiota in addressing plastic waste.  

The research also examined the enzymatic mechanisms underlying this process, noting the secretion of extracellular depolymerases by gut bacteria as a critical factor in breaking down the polymer structure of PS. To validate the chemical and structural changes occurring during biodegradation, Yang et al. employed advanced analytical techniques, including X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). This method allowed the researchers to detect alterations in surface chemical components and functional groups of PS, providing direct evidence of microbial-driven transformations at the molecular level.  

Additionally, the study incorporated experimental controls involving the suppression of gut microbiota using gentamicin sulfate, which demonstrated the essential role of bacterial communities in facilitating PS degradation. The absence of effective degradation under antibiotic treatment reinforced the conclusion that microbial activity, rather than insect physiology alone, was responsible for the observed breakdown of plastic material.  

Overall, **Yang et al., 2015b** is a foundational reference in the field of plastic biodegradation research. It documents the isolation of PS-degrading bacterial strains from insect guts, the enzymatic processes involved, and the use of XPS to confirm chemical modifications during degradation. The study not only advanced scientific understanding of microbial plastic degradation but also opened new avenues for exploring sustainable biotechnological solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al., 2015b is a cited research group/publication that previously studied inhibition halo tests on larval gut microbes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386515</data>
</node>
