<node id="Beeswax">
  <data key="d0">Beeswax</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Beeswax is a natural substance produced by honeybees, primarily composed of esters of long-chain alcohols and fatty acids, along with other long-chain hydrocarbons. It plays a central role in honeybee colonies, where it is secreted and used to construct the wax combs that form the structural framework of the hive. Beyond its ecological role in honeybee nests, beeswax has also been studied in scientific contexts for its chemical composition and biological interactions.  

In research involving the greater wax moth, *Galleria mellonella*, beeswax has been used as a dietary component and experimental control. The larvae of *G. mellonella* naturally feed on beeswax, and studies have shown that it supports their growth, survival, and metabolism. When provided as a food source, beeswax led to positive weight changes and higher survival rates in larvae compared to alternative diets such as polystyrene (PS). It also influenced gene expression in the larvae, particularly in pathways related to fatty acid degradation and metabolism, highlighting its role in shaping physiological and metabolic responses.  

Beeswax is metabolized by *G. mellonella* and its associated holobiont (the host and its microbiome), with degradation pathways showing similarities to those observed in the breakdown of polyethylene (PE) plastic. This has made beeswax an important reference substance in studies investigating the potential of *G. mellonella* to degrade synthetic polymers. In these experiments, beeswax often serves as the natural control diet, providing a baseline for comparison with synthetic materials.  

Overall, beeswax is both a biologically essential material produced by honeybees for nest construction and a valuable experimental substrate for studying insect metabolism, microbial interactions, and biodegradation processes. Its dual significance in natural ecosystems and scientific research underscores its importance as a model substance for understanding both ecological and applied biological phenomena.&lt;SEP&gt;Long-chain hydrocarbon metabolized by Galleria mellonella hologenome independent of microbiota.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
