<node id="NCBI">
  <data key="d0">NCBI</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) is a U.S.-based institution that serves as a central repository and resource for biological and genomic data, computational tools, and bioinformatics services. Established as part of the National Institutes of Health (NIH), NCBI plays a pivotal role in supporting global research in molecular biology, genomics, and related fields by integrating data storage, analysis tools, and reference resources.  

NCBI maintains a wide range of databases, with GenBank being one of its most prominent, housing nucleotide sequences from diverse organisms. It also provides access to large-scale genomic datasets, such as the reference genome of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm), released in May 2021, which supports alignment, annotation, and comparative studies. Researchers frequently deposit raw sequencing data, transcriptomes, and microbial diversity datasets into NCBI repositories, ensuring long-term accessibility and standardized curation. Examples include the submission of metatranscriptomic sequences of *T. molitor*, microbial diversity data, DNA sequences of plastic-degrading strains, and transcriptome data under accession number SRR23339333. Other notable submissions include nucleotide sequencing data of *Achroia grisella* (accession number MH192348) and study-specific datasets available under BioProject number SRP186213.  

Beyond data storage, NCBI provides widely used computational tools that facilitate sequence analysis and interpretation. Among these, the Basic Local Alignment Search Tool (BLAST) is one of the most utilized, enabling researchers to compare nucleotide or protein sequences against curated databases to identify similarities, infer gene or protein functions, and establish phylogenetic relationships. BLAST is frequently applied in tasks such as COI sequence analysis, amino acid sequence alignment, and the identification of phylogenetic neighbors. Additional tools support primer design, comparative genomics, and evolutionary studies, making NCBI indispensable for both small-scale molecular investigations and large-scale genome projects.  

Overall, NCBI functions as a cornerstone of modern bioinformatics by combining comprehensive data repositories with powerful analytical tools. It ensures that genomic and molecular data are accessible, standardized, and usable for the global scientific community. Through its databases, tools, and curation services, NCBI enables researchers to validate experimental results, conduct sequence-based investigations, and explore evolutionary and functional relationships across a wide spectrum of organisms, thereby advancing biological discovery and innovation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90</data>
