<node id="Yellow mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Yellow mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Yellow mealworms (the larval stage of the beetle *Tenebrio molitor*) are widely studied insects recognized for their unique ability to biodegrade synthetic polymers. Research has demonstrated that these larvae can digest and break down various plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Their capacity for plastic degradation is linked to the activity of their gut microbiome, which hosts diverse microbial communities capable of metabolizing otherwise persistent materials. Studies have shown that bacterial strains such as M3, *Pseudomonas* sp. M7, *Pseudomonas aeruginosa* M8, and *Achromobacter xylosoxidans* M9 have been isolated from yellow mealworms, highlighting the role of symbiotic microorganisms in facilitating plastic biodegradation.  

Beyond their environmental significance, yellow mealworms are also valued in agricultural and food systems. They are commonly used as animal feed due to their high protein content and are increasingly considered a sustainable protein source for human consumption. Their ability to thrive on different diets has made them a subject of gut microbiome research, where scientists examine how dietary variations influence microbial composition and metabolic functions.  

Overall, yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) are important both as a biological model for studying plastic biodegradation and as a practical resource in food and feed applications. Their dual role in environmental biotechnology and sustainable nutrition underscores their growing relevance in scientific research and applied industries.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385022</data>
</node>
<node id="Tenebrio molitor larvae">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms or yellow mealworms, are the larval stage of a darkling beetle species that has gained considerable scientific attention for their unique ability to degrade both natural and synthetic polymers. These larvae have been extensively studied as a model macroinvertebrate for plastic biodegradation, demonstrating the capacity to ingest, depolymerize, and metabolize a wide range of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyethylene (PE), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), polyurethane (PUR), and polylactic acid (PLA). Among these, research indicates that Tenebrio molitor larvae are particularly efficient at degrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET), with higher performance compared to their degradation of PE, PS, PP, PVC, and PUR.  

Experimental studies have shown that the larvae not only consume plastics but also facilitate measurable reductions in polymer mass and structural integrity, including foamed plastics such as EPS and polyethylene foams, which are otherwise highly resistant to natural decomposition. In some applications, they have even been employed to process construction debris by consuming EPS residues, producing frass (insect excrement) as a byproduct. Their ability to degrade natural polymers, such as cellulose and corn straw, further broadens their ecological and biotechnological relevance.  

A critical factor in their plastic-degrading capacity lies in their gut microbiota. The microbial communities within the digestive system of Tenebrio molitor larvae play a central role in breaking down recalcitrant polymers. Isolated bacterial strains from their gut have been shown to degrade polyethylene and other plastics, and experiments using gut suspensions confirm that these microbes are essential to the biodegradation process. For example, larvae fed with polyethylene mulching films provided a source of PE-degrading bacteria, highlighting the symbiotic relationship between the insect host and its gut microbiota in facilitating polymer breakdown and detoxification.  

While Tenebrio molitor larvae are effective in degrading plastics, ingestion of certain polymers can negatively affect their physiology. Studies have reported outcomes such as weight loss, decreased survival rates, biomass depletion, and oxidative damage, with the severity of these effects varying depending on the type of plastic consumed. These findings underscore both the potential and the biological cost of plastic degradation in this species.  

Overall, Tenebrio molitor larvae represent a valuable biological model for studying the mechanisms of polymer biodegradation and the role of gut microbiota in these processes. Their demonstrated ability to process diverse synthetic and natural polymers positions them as a promising organism for biotechnological applications aimed at mitigating plastic pollution and advancing sustainable waste management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are insects used in the study to biodegrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) microplastics, producing frass and degradation byproducts.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are insects used in the study to investigate the biodegradation of aged and pristine microplastics (MPs).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are mealworms specifically studied for their ability to biodegrade PE, PS, and PVC microplastics and their physiological stress responses.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were studied under grouped, isolated, and starved conditions to understand the onset of metamorphosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399</data>
