<node id="Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor)">
  <data key="d0">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor)</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are the larval stage of the darkling beetle, belonging to the family Tenebrionidae, and are widely recognized for their ecological, biological, and applied significance. Traditionally, they have been used in research, agriculture, and as a protein-rich food and feed source for both humans and animals. In recent years, however, they have gained particular scientific attention for their unique ability to interact with and degrade synthetic polymers, positioning them as a promising biological model in the context of environmental sustainability.  

Studies have demonstrated that mealworms can ingest and partially biodegrade a variety of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS or Styrofoam), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Among these, polystyrene has been the most extensively studied, with mealworms serving as the primary organisms in experimental research on its biodegradation. The process is not solely mechanical; it is closely linked to the mealworm gut microbiome, which plays a critical role in breaking down polymer structures. While mealworms are capable of chewing and ingesting plastics such as LDPE, not all ingested material is fully digested, and the efficiency of degradation varies depending on the type of polymer.  

A notable outcome of this process is the production of frass, or excrement, which retains value as a potential fertilizer. This highlights a dual capacity: mealworms not only contribute to plastic degradation but also support nutrient recycling, offering a waste-to-resource pathway with agricultural applications. Their ability to convert otherwise persistent synthetic materials into biologically useful outputs underscores their potential role in sustainable waste management and environmental remediation strategies.  

Overall, mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are increasingly studied as macro-organisms with significant potential to address global plastic pollution challenges. Their combined roles as degraders of synthetic polymers, contributors to nutrient cycling, and sources of sustainable protein make them a versatile and valuable species in both scientific research and applied environmental solutions.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387667</data>
</node>
