<node id="Klebsiella oxytoca">
  <data key="d0">Klebsiella oxytoca</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Klebsiella oxytoca is a Gram-negative, rod-shaped, indole-positive bacterium closely related to *Klebsiella pneumoniae*. It has been identified and studied for its role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene. This species has been isolated from the gut of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm), where it has been linked to the breakdown of polystyrene, a plastic that is otherwise resistant to natural degradation. Molecular identification of *Klebsiella oxytoca* has been confirmed through polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and 16S ribosomal RNA gene amplification, ensuring accurate taxonomic classification.  

Research has specifically focused on the presence and function of the *alkB* gene in *Klebsiella oxytoca*, which is associated with hydrocarbon degradation pathways and is believed to play a role in the bacterium’s ability to metabolize polystyrene. Amplification and sequencing of this gene have been used to investigate the genetic basis of its plastic-degrading capabilities.  

Overall, *Klebsiella oxytoca* is recognized as a significant microbial species in the context of plastic waste biodegradation. Its isolation from the mealworm gut highlights the potential of insect-associated microbiota in addressing environmental challenges posed by persistent plastics. This bacterium’s genetic and metabolic features make it a subject of interest for biotechnological applications aimed at sustainable plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca is a microorganism isolated from Tenebrio molitor, tested on PS film for 60 days, showing 12.97% biodegradation/weight loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterial species studied in relation to polystyrene degradation in Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella oxytoca was found in the intestine of mealworms that digested polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386823</data>
</node>
<node id="alkB gene">
  <data key="d0">alkB gene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The alkB gene encodes alkane-1-monooxygenase, an enzyme involved in the oxidation of alkanes and is used as a marker for bacterial biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The alkB gene is the genetic target studied in this text, amplified and sequenced in Klebsiella oxytoca ATCC 13182.&lt;SEP&gt;The alkB gene is responsible for plastic degradation and was identified in bacterial isolates from Tenebrio molitor guts.&lt;SEP&gt;The alkB gene is identified in Pseudomonas as the key enzyme in alkane biodegradation during polyethylene breakdown.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0</data>
