</node>
<node id="Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665">
  <data key="d0">Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Klebsiella oxytoca JCM 1665 is a specific bacterial strain of the species *Klebsiella oxytoca* that has been extensively characterized through molecular and microbiological methods. Its identification has been confirmed using polymerase chain reaction (PCR) and 16S ribosomal RNA (rRNA) gene sequencing, with amplification of the 16S rRNA gene producing a product size of 262 base pairs when JCM-specific primers are applied. Comparative sequence analysis has shown a high degree of similarity, with 99.77% identity to isolates obtained from the gut microbiota of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm beetle).  

This strain, designated as JCM 1665, was specifically isolated from the gut of *Tenebrio molitor*, where it was identified as isolate number 4 among the microbial community. Its presence in this environment has drawn attention due to its potential role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, a synthetic polymer that is otherwise resistant to natural decomposition. The association of *Klebsiella oxytoca* JCM 1665 with polystyrene degradation highlights its ecological and biotechnological significance, particularly in the context of plastic waste management and microbial ecology.  

In addition to its environmental relevance, *Klebsiella oxytoca* JCM 1665 has been used in laboratory research for the design of primers targeting the 16S rRNA gene, underscoring its utility as a reference strain in molecular biology studies. Its reliable identification through both PCR-based methods and sequencing confirms its taxonomic placement and provides a foundation for further investigations into its metabolic capabilities and potential applications.  

Overall, *Klebsiella oxytoca* JCM 1665 is a well-characterized bacterial strain distinguished by its genetic confirmation via 16S rRNA analysis, its origin from the gut microbiota of *Tenebrio molitor*, and its potential role in the biodegradation of polystyrene, making it a subject of interest in both microbial taxonomy and applied environmental research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de</data>
