<node id="Expanded polystyrene (EPS)">
  <data key="d0">Expanded polystyrene (EPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic mentioned as part of the diet of Tenebrio molitor.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS), also known as Styrofoam, was studied as a feedstock for biodegradation by Tribolium castaneum.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is the plastic material tested for biodegradation by mealworms in this study.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene mentioned as part of plastic materials contributing to pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene waste mentioned as a significant environmental issue.&lt;SEP&gt;A form of polystyrene plastic tested for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383755</data>
</node>
<node id="Serratia marcescens">
  <data key="d0">Serratia marcescens</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Serratia marcescens is a bacterial species that has been identified as part of the gut microbiome of the mealworm *Tenebrio molitor*, where it plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene (PS). Research has shown that this microorganism can be isolated from the intestines of *T. molitor* and tested for its ability to degrade polystyrene, including in emulsion and expanded polystyrene (EPS) forms. It has been reported as one of the bacterial strains capable of contributing to the breakdown of synthetic polymers, making it of interest in studies on plastic biodegradation and microbial ecology.  

In addition to its role in plastic degradation, *Serratia marcescens* has also been associated with hydrocarbon degradation, highlighting its potential utility in broader bioremediation applications. Its identification in the mealworm gut microbiome, including work by Brandon et al., underscores its relevance in the context of insect-associated microbial communities that facilitate the digestion and transformation of otherwise recalcitrant materials such as polystyrene.  

Overall, *Serratia marcescens* is recognized as a versatile microorganism with demonstrated capacity for polystyrene and hydrocarbon degradation, positioning it as a promising candidate for research into sustainable waste management and environmental remediation strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
