<node id="Figure 1">
  <data key="d0">Figure 1</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 1 is a composite figure that integrates multiple experimental results related to the biodegradation of plastics and associated biological responses in *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. It encompasses several dimensions of the study, ranging from microbiological observations to biochemical assays and material analyses. Specifically, Figure 1 includes images of overnight bacterial cultures isolated from the midgut of *T. molitor* worms, highlighting both control and pure colonies, as well as data on microbial secreted factors in the &lt;30 kDa fraction. It also presents experimental outcomes on the physiological performance of mealworms, including survival rates, pupation rates, cannibalism rates, and cumulative plastic consumption under different dietary conditions.  

In addition, Figure 1 depicts the effects of mealworm extracts, derived from worms fed either control or polystyrene diets, on cell viability and cytotoxicity, as measured by MTT, NR, SRB, and LDH assays. The figure further illustrates the mass loss of microplastics consumed by mealworms collected from different geographic regions, such as Guangzhou, Tai’an, and Shenzhen, thereby providing comparative insights into regional variations in biodegradation capacity. Complementary to these biological data, Figure 1 also serves as a visual reference for the types of plastics used in the experiments, including those featured in time-lapse video analyses.  

From a materials science perspective, Figure 1 incorporates results of polymer degradation studies. These include Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analyses comparing polyurethane (PU) foam before and after ingestion by mealworms, as well as detailed assessments of polypropylene (PP) biodegradation. The latter encompasses molecular weight (MW) changes, isotopic signature shifts, and alterations in molecular weight distribution, all of which provide evidence of chemical modifications induced by larval feeding.  

Overall, Figure 1 functions as a central, multi-panel representation that synthesizes microbiological, physiological, biochemical, and materials-based evidence. It demonstrates the capacity of *T. molitor* larvae to consume and degrade various plastics, the microbial and biochemical factors potentially involved in this process, and the measurable impacts on both the insects and the polymers. This figure thus provides a comprehensive overview of the experimental findings, linking biological activity with material transformation in the context of plastic biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 shows daily EPS consumption trends by mealworms, beetles, and co-cultures in bead bedding.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 presents experimental results on the contribution of gut bacteria to Styrofoam degradation, including optical photographs, bacterial counts, molecular weight analysis, and CO₂ isotope values.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 presents data on plastic mass loss in mealworms fed PE, PS, or mixed diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 presents data on survival rate, pupation rate, and plastic consumption by mealworms fed plastic and bran, including visual evidence of chewing and ingestion of polyethylene and polystyrene foams.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 shows data on consumption, weight change, and survival of T. molitor larvae fed with MPs, bran, or unfed.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 1 illustrates the characterization of ingestion of PS and LDPE foams by mealworms, including images of foam-eating mealworms and extracted frass fractions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387093</data>
</node>
