<node id="Tenebrio molitor L.">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor L.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor L., commonly known as the yellow mealworm, is an insect species of significant interest in both agricultural and environmental research. It is widely recognized as an edible insect and a sustainable protein source, with applications in livestock and poultry nutrition. Studies have demonstrated that incorporating Tenebrio molitor L. into broiler feed does not negatively affect growth performance or carcass characteristics, and its larvae can be processed into mealworm powder (TMlp) for use in feed formulations. This positions the species as a promising alternative protein resource in animal husbandry.  

Beyond its nutritional value, Tenebrio molitor L. has attracted considerable attention for its unique ecological and biodegradation capabilities. The species has been shown to consume and degrade synthetic polymers such as expanded polystyrene (EPS) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Its ability to reduce the mass and alter the chemical and physical properties of these plastics is linked to the activity of its intestinal microbial community, which plays a central role in breaking down otherwise persistent materials. Research has also highlighted that Tenebrio molitor L. hosts nitrogen-fixing microbial communities, further underscoring its ecological significance.  

Laboratory experiments have investigated the feeding and metabolic responses of Tenebrio molitor L. when exposed to microplastics, bran, and PVC, providing insights into both its digestive adaptability and its potential role in mitigating plastic pollution. These findings suggest that the species could contribute to sustainable waste management strategies by facilitating the biodegradation of plastics that are otherwise resistant to natural decomposition.  

In summary, Tenebrio molitor L. (yellow mealworm) is a versatile insect species valued for its dual role as a sustainable protein source in animal feed and as a biological agent capable of degrading synthetic polymers. Its nutritional applications, combined with its environmental potential in plastic biodegradation and microbial symbiosis, make it an important subject of study in the fields of agriculture, food security, and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms studied in 2022 for nitrogen fixation and diazotrophic community activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor L. is the species of mealworm studied for nitrogen fixation and diazotrophic community in relation to plastic consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor L., also known as the mealworm, is capable of eating Styrofoam as its sole diet and degrading plastic with the help of gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor L., commonly known as the mealworm, is a coleopteran insect larva capable of eating and degrading vinyl plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385959</data>
</node>
