<node id="Soil">
  <data key="d0">Soil</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Soil is a fundamental terrestrial environment that plays a dual role in the context of plastic pollution and biodegradation research. On one hand, soil functions as a major sink for plastic-derived pollutants, particularly microplastics originating from materials such as expanded polystyrene (EPS) and polyethylene (PE). These pollutants accumulate in soil systems, where they can negatively affect soil quality, alter ecological balance, and impact soil-dwelling fauna. As such, soil is a critical site for observing and assessing the environmental impacts of plastic contamination in terrestrial ecosystems.  

On the other hand, soil is also a vital ecological resource and experimental medium for advancing knowledge of plastic biodegradation. It harbors a rich microbial diversity, including bacteria and fungi, that has been extensively studied for their ability to degrade synthetic polymers. Microorganisms isolated from soil have demonstrated the capacity to break down plastics such as polyethylene, polystyrene, and polyurethane. For example, soil has yielded bacterial strains used in studies of polystyrene biodegradation, though the degradation rates observed in soil environments are often low. Similarly, soil has provided microbial cultures capable of degrading polyester polyurethane, and it has been the source of bacterial consortia with demonstrated plastic-degrading activity. The fungus- and bacteria-rich environment of soil has also been employed in research on the breakdown of polystyrene-graft-starch copolymers, allowing scientists to test polymer degradation under realistic terrestrial conditions.  

In addition to its role as a microbial reservoir, soil itself has been used directly as the medium for experimental biodegradation studies. Investigations into the degradation of various plastics, including polyethylene and polyurethane, have been conducted in soil environments to better understand how these materials behave under natural aerobic conditions. Soil has also been studied as an environmental medium for the remediation of polymeric aromatic hydrocarbons, further highlighting its importance in pollution management and ecological restoration.  

Moreover, soil provides a habitat for diverse microorganisms such as *Achromobacter xylosoxidans*, which can thrive in aerobic soil environments and contribute to biodegradation processes. The consistent isolation of plastic-degrading bacteria and fungi from soil underscores its significance as a natural laboratory for exploring microbial and biochemical pathways of polymer decomposition.  

Overall, soil is both a recipient of plastic pollution and a source of potential solutions to the problem of plastic persistence. It serves as a contaminated environment where the impacts of microplastic accumulation are evident, while simultaneously offering the microbial diversity and ecological conditions necessary for studying and developing biodegradation strategies. This dual role makes soil central to understanding the environmental challenges posed by plastics and to advancing sustainable approaches for their remediation in terrestrial ecosystems.&lt;SEP&gt;Soil is mentioned as an environment where abandoned MPs pose threats to living organisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385654</data>
</node>
<node id="Rivers">
  <data key="d0">Rivers</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Rivers are mentioned as one of the natural systems where EPS disperses and contributes to microplastic accumulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Rivers are natural environments where EPS waste disperses, creating microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Rivers are natural water bodies mentioned as being contaminated by microplastic particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Rivers are identified as major recipients of dumped plastics, leading to pollution and microplastic formation.</data>
