<node id="Control Group">
  <data key="d0">Control Group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The control group is a fundamental component of experimental design, serving as a baseline against which the effects of treatments or interventions are measured. In the studies described, the control group was consistently defined as the set of organisms or conditions maintained without exposure to the experimental variable, thereby providing a reference point for comparison.  

In animal studies, the control group sometimes consisted of rats that were given only distilled water (DW), ensuring that no additional dietary or chemical factors influenced the outcomes. In insect-based experiments, particularly those involving *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae, the control group was maintained under acclimation conditions without exposure to polystyrene or polyethylene terephthalate (PET). These larvae were typically fed bran without antibiotics or specialized feedstocks, allowing researchers to assess baseline microbial colony counts, consumption rates, and survival rates. Notably, the control group of mealworms demonstrated the highest survival rate compared to groups fed with polystyrene, highlighting the physiological stress imposed by synthetic polymer diets.  

In microbial and materials-related experiments, multiple control groups were sometimes employed to isolate specific variables. For example, one control group was inoculated only with bacterial culture, while another was covered solely with high-density polyethylene (HDPE) film, ensuring that observed effects could be attributed to the intended experimental conditions rather than confounding factors. Similarly, in PET-fed experiments, the control group provided a baseline for gene expression analysis, showing lower levels of PET-degrading genes and pathways compared to treatment groups exposed to the polymer.  

Overall, the control group in these studies functioned as a critical benchmark, enabling accurate evaluation of survival, microbial activity, consumption behavior, and gene expression in response to experimental treatments. By maintaining organisms under standard or untreated conditions, the control group ensured that observed differences could be attributed to the experimental variables under investigation, thereby strengthening the validity and interpretability of the research findings.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group of mealworms was fed oats to serve as a baseline for comparison in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group of yellow mealworms was fed with bran to compare against mask-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group refers to larvae not treated with antibiotics, used as a comparison for depolymerization efficiency.&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental group of T. molitor larvae fed with oatmeal ad libitum.&lt;SEP&gt;The Control group refers to larvae fed under standard conditions, used as a baseline for comparison in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;The control group refers to larvae fed under normal feeding conditions, used as a baseline for comparison in the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388203</data>
</node>
