<node id="Ning D">
  <data key="d0">Ning D</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ning D is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics, particularly polystyrene and polyethylene, through biological processes involving mealworms. Ning D has co-authored multiple studies examining the capacity of yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*) to digest and degrade polystyrene wastes, highlighting the potential of insect-based systems for addressing plastic pollution. Their research extends beyond polystyrene to include investigations into the biodegradation of polyethylene and mixtures of different plastic polymers, further demonstrating the versatility of mealworms in processing diverse plastic materials. Collectively, Ning D’s contributions provide important insights into the mechanisms and feasibility of using mealworms as a biological solution for mitigating plastic waste, with implications for sustainable waste management and environmental remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Ning D is a co-author of a 2018 study on biodegradation of plastics in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Ning D is an author of research on microbial degradation and valorization of plastic wastes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ning D is an author of a study on polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
