<node id="Zhao J">
  <data key="d0">Zhao J</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhao J is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation and mineralization of plastics, particularly polystyrene, through the activity of insects and their associated microbial communities. Zhao J has co-authored multiple studies investigating the capacity of mealworms (the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*) to degrade and mineralize polystyrene, including a notable 2015 study that provided evidence of plastic consumption and transformation by these organisms. This research contributed to advancing scientific understanding of biological pathways for plastic degradation and the potential role of insect larvae in mitigating plastic pollution.  

In addition to work on mealworms, Zhao J has also contributed to studies examining the biodegradation of plastics by waxworms (*Galleria mellonella* larvae), which are similarly capable of consuming and breaking down synthetic polymers. A 2021 study co-authored by Zhao J specifically focused on isolating and characterizing polystyrene-degrading bacteria from *Galleria mellonella* larvae, highlighting the microbial mechanisms that may underlie insect-mediated plastic degradation.  

Overall, Zhao J’s research emphasizes the ecological and biotechnological significance of insect larvae and their gut microbiota in addressing the global challenge of plastic waste. By co-authoring studies across different insect models, Zhao J has helped to establish a foundation for future exploration of biological and microbial solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhao J is one of the authors of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhao J is an author of studies on polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386042</data>
</node>
<node id="Song Y">
  <data key="d0">Song Y</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Song Y is a researcher recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on polystyrene. Song Y is a co-author of multiple scientific studies investigating the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, organisms known for their ability to consume and process plastic materials. Among these works is a 2015 study that specifically examined the role of mealworms in breaking down and mineralizing polystyrene, providing important insights into potential biological solutions for plastic waste management. In addition to research on mealworms, Song Y has also contributed to studies exploring the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene by land snails, further broadening the scope of inquiry into how different organisms interact with and degrade synthetic polymers. Collectively, Song Y’s research has advanced understanding of the biological pathways and ecological potential for mitigating plastic pollution through natural degradation processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386046</data>
</node>
