<node id="Zophobas morio">
  <data key="d0">Zophobas morio</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zophobas morio, commonly known as the superworm, is the larval stage of a darkling beetle in the family Tenebrionidae and is closely related to the mealworm (Tenebrio molitor). This species has gained significant attention in both environmental biotechnology and food science due to its unique biological characteristics and applied potential.  

One of the most notable features of Zophobas morio is its ability to consume and partially degrade synthetic polymers, particularly polystyrene (Styrofoam) and, to a lesser extent, polyurethane. Research has shown that the larvae can chew and fragment plastics, with their gut microbiota playing a crucial role in facilitating biodegradation. While polystyrene degradation has been clearly demonstrated, the efficiency of polyurethane breakdown is lower, with studies reporting approximately 6% degradation. This plastic-degrading capacity positions Zophobas morio as a promising model organism for exploring sustainable waste management strategies and biological solutions to plastic pollution.  

In addition to its environmental relevance, Zophobas morio is also studied as an edible insect. Investigations have examined its nutritional value, protein content, and potential as a sustainable food source for humans and animals. Research has further explored how plastic consumption by the larvae may influence their nutritional quality, oxidative stress levels, and safety for consumption, including assessments of cytotoxicity. Such studies highlight the importance of evaluating both the benefits and risks of incorporating Zophobas morio into food systems, particularly in contexts where insects are promoted as alternative protein sources.  

Overall, Zophobas morio represents a species of dual significance: as a biological agent capable of contributing to plastic biodegradation and as a protein-rich edible insect with potential applications in sustainable nutrition. Its study provides valuable insights into the interactions between insect physiology, microbial symbiosis, environmental remediation, and food safety, making it an important organism at the intersection of ecological and nutritional research.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect studied for feeding and degradation effects on plastic.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio is the species of beetle whose larvae are superworms, which can also feed on expanded polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
