  <data key="d5">1756372476</data>
</node>
<node id="Yang et al. (2015a)">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al. (2015a)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang et al. (2015a) is a frequently cited research study that investigated the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene (EPS, commonly known as Styrofoam) by mealworms, the larval form of *Tenebrio molitor*. The study demonstrated that mealworms are capable of ingesting and metabolizing Styrofoam, converting it into carbon dioxide (CO₂), fecula (frass), and lipids, which contribute to their energy requirements and biomass formation. Through controlled experiments, the researchers provided evidence of mineralization of ingested polystyrene into mealworm biomass, while also detecting residual plastic particles in the frass, indicating partial degradation and incomplete assimilation.  

The work of Yang et al. (2015a) is notable for providing quantitative data on EPS ingestion efficiency and establishing carbon mass balance measurements that were used to estimate the rate of EPS degradation. Their methodology included the use of a CO₂ trapping system to monitor and confirm the metabolic conversion of polystyrene into gaseous byproducts, thereby supporting the conclusion that microbial and enzymatic processes within the mealworm gut facilitate plastic breakdown.  

In addition to focusing on *Tenebrio molitor*, the study also extended its relevance to related species such as *Zophobas atratus*, highlighting the broader potential of insect larvae in microbial plastic degradation. The findings of Yang et al. (2015a) have since been widely referenced as foundational evidence that Styrofoam, a material traditionally considered non-biodegradable, can undergo biological degradation through insect-mediated processes. This research has provided critical baseline data for subsequent studies on biodegradation rates, microbial mechanisms, and the potential application of insect-based systems for plastic waste management.  

In summary, Yang et al. (2015a) represents a pivotal contribution to the field of plastic biodegradation research, offering experimental proof that mealworms can ingest and metabolize EPS, quantifying ingestion and degradation efficiencies, and establishing methodological approaches for tracking carbon flux during the process. The study has become a cornerstone reference for understanding insect-driven plastic degradation and its implications for sustainable waste management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2015a) refers to a study that reported 31% PS consumption after 30 days for 500 larvae and survival rates of approximately 86%.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2015a) are cited researchers who reported biomass increase in control larvae and biomass decrease in unfed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2015a) refers to a research study cited in the text that investigated the biomass and carbon mass balance of larvae fed with PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388218</data>
</node>
