<node id="Fecula">
  <data key="d0">Fecula</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fecula is the term used to describe the excreted residue produced by mealworms after consuming Styrofoam. It represents the egested waste material that passes through the mealworm digestive system and is of particular scientific interest because it contains the byproducts of polystyrene degradation. Studies have shown that fecula consists of residual Styrofoam fragments, depolymerized metabolites of polystyrene, and intermediates formed during the digestive process. In addition, it may contain microbial components originating from the mealworm gut, which are thought to play a role in the breakdown of the polymer.  

Researchers have collected and analyzed fecula to better understand the chemical, physical, and thermal changes that occur when Styrofoam is processed through the mealworm digestive tract. These analyses have revealed alterations in the chemical composition and carbon content of the material, providing evidence of partial decomposition and transformation of polystyrene. The study of fecula is therefore significant for evaluating the potential of mealworms as biological agents for plastic degradation and for assessing the environmental implications of this process.  

In summary, fecula refers specifically to the egested residues of Styrofoam-fed mealworms, comprising a mixture of degraded polystyrene products, residual polymer fragments, and microbial matter, and it serves as a key material for investigating the mechanisms and outcomes of biological plastic decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula is a byproduct of EPS biodegradation in mealworms, consisting of degraded polymer fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the excrement of mealworms, which was analyzed and compared to polystyrene feedstock to evaluate biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula refers to the excreted waste of mealworms, analyzed to study polystyrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fecula is another term for excrement from macroinvertebrates, which may contain plastic fragments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387078</data>
</node>
<node id="Lipids">
  <data key="d0">Lipids</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipids are a class of biological macromolecules that play a central role in the metabolism and physiology of mealworms, particularly in the context of studies examining their ability to digest and metabolize polystyrene (PS). Research has shown that carbon derived from polystyrene, including isotopically labeled 13C-polystyrene, can be incorporated into lipids during mealworm digestion, demonstrating that these insects are capable of assimilating synthetic polymer carbon into natural biomolecules. Quantitatively, approximately 0.5% of ingested polystyrene carbon was found to be incorporated into lipid biomass, highlighting lipids as one of the metabolic sinks for plastic-derived carbon.  

In experimental studies, lipids were extracted from mealworm bodies using chloroform in a Soxhlet extractor to enable biochemical analysis. They were among the key macromolecules assessed to evaluate the metabolic impact of polystyrene ingestion. Results indicated that lipid levels decreased significantly in mealworms fed with polystyrene and polystyrene residues (PSr), suggesting that the insects experienced starvation-like metabolic stress when relying on plastic as a food source. This reduction in lipid content reflects the energetic and nutritional challenges posed by a polystyrene-based diet.  

Beyond their metabolic role, lipids have also been implicated in analytical interpretations of spectroscopic data. Weber et al. (2017) proposed that lipids could account for certain Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) absorbance signals previously attributed to ethylene glycol, suggesting that lipid residues may influence the interpretation of polymer degradation studies. Similarly, lipids have been mentioned as potential contributors to FTIR absorbance changes observed when organic molecules diffuse into low-density polyethylene (LDPE), further underscoring their relevance in polymer–biomolecule interaction research.  

Overall, lipids in mealworms represent both a metabolic endpoint for assimilated polystyrene carbon and a measurable indicator of physiological stress under plastic-based diets. Their extraction, analysis, and spectroscopic signatures provide critical insights into how synthetic polymers are processed biologically and how such processes may be misinterpreted in analytical studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids, particularly triglycerides, are energy storage compounds in mealworms, measured to assess stress and starvation effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Lipids are stored energy reserves in mealworms, used during starvation and while processing plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387870</data>
</node>
<node id="Carbohydrates">
  <data key="d0">Carbohydrates</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Carbohydrates were measured as part of the nutrient composition of insects fed Styrofoam or conventional diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates, present in high proportion (73%) in bran, significantly impact the growth and survival of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are used as an alternative energy source by insects when lipid levels are depleted.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are compounds that undergo thermal degradation over a broad range of temperatures, as noted in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are nutrients present in bran that contribute to a complex gut microbiome in bran-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are biochemical compounds providing energy to mealworms, measured in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Carbohydrates are stored energy reserves in mealworms, used to maintain metabolic rates and activities such as chewing and eating polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
