<node id="Kundungal, H.">
  <data key="d0">Kundungal, H.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kundungal, H. is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic plastics, particularly polyethylene and polystyrene, through the activity of insect larvae. He has authored and co-authored multiple studies investigating the potential of different insect species to degrade plastic waste, contributing to the growing field of biological solutions for plastic pollution.  

His research includes several studies on the biodegradation of polyethylene (including high-density polyethylene, HDPE, and low-density polyethylene, LDPE) by the lesser waxworm, *Achroia grisella* (also referred to as *Achroia griseola* in some reports). These studies explore the capacity of the larvae to break down polyethylene waste, highlighting their potential role in mitigating plastic accumulation in the environment. In addition, Kundungal, H. co-authored a 2019 study that specifically examined the biodegradation of polyethylene by the lesser waxworm, further reinforcing the significance of this insect species in plastic degradation research.  

Beyond polyethylene, Kundungal, H. has also contributed to research on the biodegradation of polystyrene, another persistent plastic pollutant, through the use of *Uloma* sp. larvae. This work expands the scope of his investigations to include multiple types of plastics and different insect species, thereby broadening the understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation.  

Furthermore, he has been involved in studies on the pretreatment and biodegradation of LDPE by the greater waxworm, demonstrating an interest not only in the natural degradation capabilities of insects but also in enhancing these processes through pretreatment methods.  

Overall, Kundungal, H. is recognized for his contributions to the study of insect-based biodegradation of plastics, with a particular emphasis on polyethylene and polystyrene. His work provides valuable insights into the potential use of insect larvae as biological agents for addressing plastic waste management challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713</data>
