<node id="Sarangapani, S.">
  <data key="d0">Sarangapani, S.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Sarangapani, S. is a researcher and author whose work has primarily focused on the biodegradation of polyethylene waste, particularly through the use of insect larvae such as waxworms. He has co-authored and authored multiple studies investigating the potential of biological agents, including the lesser waxworm (*Achroia grisella*), also referred to in some sources as the lesser waxmoth (*Achroia griseola*), in breaking down polyethylene materials such as high-density polyethylene (HDPE). His research contributions include a 2019 study that examined the biodegradation of polyethylene by the lesser waxworm, highlighting the organism’s capacity to degrade synthetic polymers that are otherwise resistant to natural decomposition.  

In addition to his work on the lesser waxworm, Sarangapani, S. has also contributed to studies exploring the role of the greater waxworm in polyethylene degradation. This includes research on pretreatment methods and their influence on the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE), aiming to enhance the efficiency of polymer breakdown by biological means.  

Overall, Sarangapani, S.’s body of work emphasizes the potential of insect larvae, particularly waxworms, as biological agents for mitigating plastic pollution. His studies contribute to the growing field of bioremediation research, which seeks sustainable solutions to the environmental challenges posed by persistent plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of the 2019 study on biodegradation of polyethylene by lesser waxworm.</data>
