<node id="Fig. 3">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fig. 3 is a figure referenced across multiple contexts in the provided descriptions, each highlighting different aspects of experimental results related to mealworms, superworms, and associated microbial strains. In some instances, Fig. 3 depicts Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectra comparing MPS feedstock with frass from mealworms at both mid-life stage (MLS) and old-life stage (OLS), providing evidence of polystyrene (PS) depolymerization occurring within the digestive systems of mealworms sourced from twelve different origins. Related to this, the figure is also described as presenting cumulative consumption data of polystyrene (PS) and polyurethane (PU) by superworms and yellow mealworms over a 35-day period, thereby illustrating their capacity to ingest and metabolize synthetic polymers.  

In another context, Fig. 3 is reported to show pathway impact analysis results from central carbon metabolism (CCM) in mealworms, offering insights into metabolic shifts associated with plastic degradation. Complementary to this, the figure is also described as presenting carbohydrate content measurements in mealworms subjected to different diets, including plastic-based feed and starvation, as well as data on larval volumetric length and survival under these varying nutritional conditions. These results collectively highlight the physiological and metabolic responses of mealworms to unconventional diets.  

Additionally, Fig. 3 is cited as displaying phylogenetic analyses. In one description, it shows the phylogenetic tree of a selected bacterial strain, while in another, it refers to the phylogenetic analysis of twelve bacterial strains evaluated for their ability to degrade polystyrene. These analyses provide a microbial perspective on plastic degradation, linking bacterial taxonomy to functional degradative potential.  

Taken together, Fig. 3 serves as a multifaceted figure that integrates biochemical, physiological, ecological, and microbiological data. It encompasses evidence of plastic depolymerization in mealworms, quantification of polymer consumption, metabolic pathway impacts, nutritional and survival outcomes, and phylogenetic relationships of bacterial strains associated with polystyrene degradation. This diversity of content underscores the central role of Fig. 3 in illustrating the interconnected biological and microbial processes underlying plastic biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388234</data>
</node>
