<node id="B Vitamins">
  <data key="d0">B Vitamins</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">B vitamins such as thiamin, riboflavin, and pantothenic acid are present in bran and play a significant role in mealworm growth and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372641</data>
</node>
<node id="Thiamin">
  <data key="d0">Thiamin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Thiamin is a B vitamin found in bran that contributes to mealworm growth and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372642</data>
</node>
<node id="Riboflavin">
  <data key="d0">Riboflavin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Riboflavin is a B vitamin found in bran that contributes to mealworm growth and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372642</data>
</node>
<node id="Pantothenic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Pantothenic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pantothenic acid is a B vitamin found in bran that contributes to mealworm growth and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372642</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic-Degrading Larvae">
  <data key="d0">Plastic-Degrading Larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic-degrading larvae are insects capable of ingesting and breaking down plastics, including mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae capable of degrading plastics, including T. molitor, are studied for their role in biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378181</data>
</node>
<node id="Sediment">
  <data key="d0">Sediment</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Sediment is an environmental medium where microplastic concentration correlates with uptake and weight loss in aquatic lugworms.&lt;SEP&gt;An environmental medium where microplastics have been detected.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756376345</data>
</node>
<node id="Food Chain">
  <data key="d0">Food Chain</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The food chain is the ecological system where trophic transfer of microplastics occurs, raising concerns when mealworms are consumed by poultry and fish.&lt;SEP&gt;The food chain is a pathway through which microplastics can enter the human body, threatening health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386208</data>
</node>
<node id="POPs">
  <data key="d0">POPs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">POPs (Persistent Organic Pollutants) are substances that can accumulate along with microplastics in the environment, increasing hazards in trophic transfer.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372643</data>
</node>
<node id="Chicken">
  <data key="d0">Chicken</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chicken is mentioned as an animal in which microplastics were detected in feces after ingestion of microplastics-fed earthworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chicken is mentioned as a less sustainable protein source compared to mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383750</data>
</node>
<node id="Earthworm">
  <data key="d0">Earthworm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Earthworm is mentioned as an organism that can ingest microplastics, which are then transferred to chickens when consumed.&lt;SEP&gt;Earthworms are studied for bacteria in their gut that can degrade low-density polyethylene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380882</data>
</node>
<node id="Predator-Prey Relationship">
  <data key="d0">Predator-Prey Relationship</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The predator-prey relationship is described as a model that enhances the transport of microplastics in soil.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372644</data>
</node>
