  <data key="d2">Zhu et al. (2018b) is a cited research group or author team that confirmed predator-prey co-occurrence enhances the transport of microplastics in soil.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372645</data>
</node>
<node id="Old Larval Stage">
  <data key="d0">Old Larval Stage</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Old larval stage refers to the developmental stage of mealworms where they ingest more plastics, leading to higher mortality, weight loss, and pupation changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372645</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Management">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Management</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic management refers to the consideration of safety, hazards, and post-treatment strategies for microplastics and POPs in the environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372645</data>
</node>
<node id="Lei He">
  <data key="d0">Lei He</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lei He is one of the authors of the study on gut microbiome responses to PET biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Lei He contributed to conceptualization, experimental design, formal analysis, sample collection, methodology, supervision, validation, and writing (original draft, review, and editing).&lt;SEP&gt;Lei He is one of the authors of the study on molecular-weight-dependent degradation of plastics by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lei He is an author affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Urban Water Resource and Environment, Harbin Institute of Technology, and the CAS Key Laboratory of Urban Pollutant Conversion, University of Science and Technology of China.&lt;SEP&gt;Lei He is one of the authors of the study on PET biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Lei He contributed to methodology, formal analysis, and writing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385291</data>
</node>
<node id="Shan-Shan Yang">
  <data key="d0">Shan-Shan Yang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Shan-Shan Yang is a researcher and corresponding author affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Urban Water Resource and Environment, School of Environment, Harbin Institute of Technology, China. She has played a significant role in studies focused on the biodegradation of plastics, particularly investigating the digestion and molecular-weight-dependent degradation of polystyrene by yellow mealworms. In her scholarly contributions, Shan-Shan Yang has been actively involved in multiple aspects of the research process, including methodology development, formal analysis, experimental investigation, and manuscript preparation, specifically contributing to writing, reviewing, and editing. Additionally, she has been responsible for securing funding to support the research. Through her work, Shan-Shan Yang has advanced scientific understanding of plastic degradation mechanisms in biological systems and has contributed to the broader field of environmental science and sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Shan-Shan Yang is one of the authors of the study on yellow mealworm gut microbiome and plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Shan-Shan Yang contributed to data curation and formal analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of the study on impacts of polyethylene properties on biodegradation in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Shan-Shan Yang is listed as an author in the CRediT authorship contribution statement.&lt;SEP&gt;Shan-Shan Yang contributed to conceptualization, methodology, supervision, validation, writing, review, editing, and funding acquisition.&lt;SEP&gt;Shan-Shan Yang is listed as one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of low-density polyethylene in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386459</data>
</node>
<node id="Jie Ding">
  <data key="d0">Jie Ding</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jie Ding is a researcher affiliated with the State Key Laboratory of Urban Water Resource and Environment at Harbin Institute of Technology. He is recognized as one of the authors and a corresponding author of studies investigating plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and the molecular-weight-dependent degradation of plastics by *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. In these research efforts, Jie Ding played a central role, contributing to conceptualization, supervision, validation, writing, and funding acquisition. His work emphasizes advancing scientific understanding of plastic biodegradation mechanisms and exploring sustainable solutions to plastic waste management. As a corresponding author, he can be contacted via the email address dingjie123@hit.edu.cn.&lt;SEP&gt;Jie Ding contributed to conceptualization and experimental design.&lt;SEP&gt;Jie Ding is listed as one of the authors of the study on biodegradation of low-density polyethylene in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386459</data>
</node>
<node id="Zhi-Li He">
  <data key="d0">Zhi-Li He</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhi-Li He is one of the authors of the study on PET biodegradation in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Zhi-Li He contributed to conceptualization, experimental design, and writing (review and editing).&lt;SEP&gt;Zhi-Li He is one of the authors of the study on PET biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
