<node id="Nan-Qi Ren">
  <data key="d0">Nan-Qi Ren</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Nan-Qi Ren is a researcher affiliated with the Harbin Institute of Technology who has made significant contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly in relation to mealworm species such as *Tenebrio molitor*. Ren is recognized as one of the authors of multiple research articles investigating the capacity of mealworms to digest and biodegrade various synthetic polymers, including polystyrene, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). These studies explore the mechanisms by which mealworms process plastics, the environmental implications of such biodegradation, and the influence of polymer properties on degradation efficiency.  

In these works, Nan-Qi Ren played a key role in conceptualization and experimental design, helping to shape the research framework and methodologies used to assess plastic degradation in insect systems. The studies co-authored by Ren have provided important insights into the potential of biological organisms to contribute to sustainable solutions for plastic waste management. Through this body of research, Ren has contributed to advancing the understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and its possible applications in addressing global environmental challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386465</data>
</node>
