<node id="Polyethylene terephthalate (PET)">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a synthetic, petroleum-based plastic polymer that is one of the most widely produced and utilized plastics globally. Classified as an aromatic polyester, PET is composed of polymerized units of monohydroxyethyl terephthalate (MHET). It is the most widely used polycondensate worldwide and is commonly referred to as polyester in the textile industry. PET has extensive applications in packaging, textiles, and household products, making it a ubiquitous material in modern life. Its global production is estimated at around 70 million tons annually, and in 2021 it accounted for approximately 8.4% of total plastic production, ranking among the six major petroleum-derived polymers.  

Despite its widespread utility, PET is a major contributor to plastic waste and environmental pollution. Its durability and chemical structure make it highly resistant to natural biodegradation, resulting in significant accumulation in ecosystems. Recycling rates for PET remain relatively low, with less than 20% of produced material being effectively recycled, which exacerbates its environmental impact.  

Scientific research has increasingly focused on the biodegradation of PET, particularly through enzymatic and microbial pathways. Certain microorganisms and enzymes have shown potential in breaking down PET, including degradation activity associated with Hafnia-Obsumbacter species in the gut of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm). These findings have spurred interest in developing biotechnological solutions to address PET waste.  

In summary, Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a mass-produced, fossil-based polymer that plays a central role in textiles, packaging, and consumer goods. While it is indispensable in many industries due to its strength, versatility, and durability, its resistance to biodegradation and low recycling rates make it a significant environmental challenge. Ongoing research into microbial and enzymatic degradation pathways highlights both the urgency and potential of developing sustainable strategies for managing PET waste.&lt;SEP&gt;PET is a type of plastic substrate tested in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;PET is one of the plastics tested in the insect metabolism study.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate is a commonly used plastic with widespread applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A plastic polymer degraded and assimilated by a bacterium discovered in a 2016 study.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a target for future follow-up research on plastic biodegradation and oxidative stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387426</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut microbiota">
  <data key="d0">Gut microbiota</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gut microbiota of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm larvae) refers to the diverse community of microorganisms inhabiting the larval digestive tract, which play a central role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers. These microbial communities have been shown to adapt to different diets, including polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), bran, and polystyrene, with their composition and activity shifting in response to the substrate consumed. In particular, the gut microbiota of *T. molitor* larvae contributes significantly to PET degradation through enzymatic activity and specialized metabolic pathways that enable the breakdown of complex plastic polymers into simpler compounds.  

Research highlights that the gut microbiota is not only essential for the larvae’s ability to survive on plastic-based diets but also provides insights into the mechanisms of biodepolymerization. The microorganisms within the gut employ enzymatic processes and metabolic interactions to depolymerize and metabolize plastics, making them a focal point for studies on sustainable plastic waste management. Furthermore, investigations into the behavior and functional dynamics of these microbial communities reveal their adaptability and potential utility in biotechnological applications aimed at mitigating plastic pollution.  

In summary, the gut microbiota of *Tenebrio molitor* represents a specialized microbial ecosystem that facilitates the degradation of plastics such as PET and polystyrene, with its enzymatic and metabolic capabilities offering promising avenues for advancing biodegradation research and developing eco-friendly strategies for plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in insect guts that plays a key role in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in the larvae's digestive system that contributes to PP biodegradation by secreting enzymes and bioemulsifying factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in the larvae's digestive system, which was analyzed for its role in digestion and affected by antibiotic treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in insect larvae, which contributes to nitrogen fixation and metabolism during plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in insect larvae, studied for its role in PP depolymerization and biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community present in the gut of mealworms, including nitrogen-fixing bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
