<node id="Polyurethane (PUR)">
  <data key="d0">Polyurethane (PUR)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyurethane (PUR) is a synthetic, petroleum-based plastic polymer that ranks among the six most widely used plastics worldwide. Representing approximately 7.4–7.9% of global plastic production, including about 7.8% in 2021, it is considered one of the most demanded and versatile polymers. Classified as a thermoplastic, polyurethane is valued for its durability, adaptability, and broad range of industrial and consumer applications, which has contributed to its significant role in global plastic use.  

From an environmental standpoint, polyurethane presents substantial challenges. It is highly resistant to natural biodegradation processes, leading to its persistence in ecosystems and accumulation as a major category of plastic waste. This resistance makes polyurethane a notable contributor to long-term environmental pollution and a concern for both ecological and human health.  

Despite its durability, scientific research has identified potential pathways for partial biodegradation of polyurethane. In particular, studies have shown that larvae of the insect *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms) are capable of breaking down polyurethane to some extent. This discovery has positioned polyurethane as a material of interest in biodegradation and bioremediation research, with ongoing investigations exploring biological methods to mitigate its environmental impact.  

In summary, polyurethane (PUR) is a widely produced and utilized plastic polymer that combines industrial importance with environmental persistence. While it remains a critical material in modern manufacturing and consumer products, its resistance to degradation underscores the urgency of developing sustainable waste management strategies and advancing research into biological degradation solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is one of the six widely used plastic polymers, accounting for 7.7% of polymer demand in Europe in 2018.&lt;SEP&gt;A plastic polymer mentioned as one of the major global plastic products.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a petroleum-based plastic that yellow mealworms can ingest and biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a subject for future biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyurethane (PUR) is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a target for future follow-up research on plastic biodegradation and oxidative stress.&lt;SEP&gt;PUR is one of the six major petroleum-derived polymers with high demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
