<node id="Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET)">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET) is a widely used thermoplastic polymer belonging to the polyester family, characterized by ester linkages in its molecular backbone. These ester bonds make PET theoretically more susceptible to microbial and enzymatic degradation than non-hydrolyzable plastics, though in practice it remains relatively resistant to natural biodegradation. Research has explored its potential breakdown by biological agents, including insect larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), as well as enzymatic remediation approaches for plastic waste management. However, experimental studies have shown that PET’s density and structural properties often limit its accessibility to organisms, making large-scale biodegradation challenging.  

From an industrial perspective, PET is one of the six most commonly used plastic polymers worldwide, representing approximately 6.9% to 7.7% of global plastic production and about 7.4% of polymer demand in Europe in 2018. Its popularity stems from its durability, transparency, lightweight nature, and recyclability, which make it especially valuable in the production of bottles, food containers, and other packaging materials. These properties have established PET as one of the most in-demand plastics globally, with a central role in consumer goods and packaging industries.  

Environmentally, PET is a major contributor to plastic waste and is frequently identified among the dominant categories of plastic pollution. Its persistence in natural ecosystems contributes to the growing problem of microplastic contamination, raising concerns about long-term ecological and health impacts. While its chemical structure offers some potential for biodegradation under controlled or engineered conditions, PET remains a significant environmental challenge due to its widespread use, durability, and resistance to natural breakdown.  

In summary, Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET) is a high-demand thermoplastic polyester with extensive applications in packaging and consumer products. Although its ester-based structure provides a theoretical pathway for microbial and enzymatic degradation, it remains largely resistant to natural biodegradation, making it both an industrially valuable material and a major contributor to global plastic waste and microplastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;A plastic polymer mentioned as one of the major global plastic products.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate microplastics were found in animal-based traditional medicinal materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a petroleum-based plastic that yellow mealworms can ingest and biodegrade.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene terephthalate is a petro-based polymer mentioned as a subject for future biodegradation research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
