<node id="PET-1">
  <data key="d0">PET-1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET-1 is a commercial PET resin microplastic powder with defined molecular weight and crystallinity, used in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a type of PET polymer tested in the study. It showed gradual mass reduction of 71.03% over 36 days when consumed by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a type of PET polymer studied for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae. It has a slightly higher molecular weight and shows lower depolymerization extent compared to PET-2.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-1 is a type of PET polymer with higher crystallinity and molecular weight, leading to lower degradation and depolymerization rates when consumed by larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372787</data>
</node>
<node id="PET-2">
  <data key="d0">PET-2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET-2 is a commercial PET resin microplastic powder with defined molecular weight and crystallinity, used in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is another type of PET polymer tested in the study. It showed gradual mass reduction of 73.28% over 36 days when consumed by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is another type of PET polymer studied for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae. It has a lower molecular weight than PET-1 and undergoes higher depolymerization.&lt;SEP&gt;PET-2 is a type of PET polymer with lower crystallinity and molecular weight compared to PET-1, used in experiments to study microbial communities in larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372787</data>
</node>
<node id="Wheat Bran (WB)">
  <data key="d0">Wheat Bran (WB)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wheat Bran (WB) is a commonly used feed material for insect rearing, particularly for mealworm larvae such as *Tenebrio molitor* and *Tenebrio obscurus*. In the referenced study, Wheat Bran served as the primary and traditional diet for *T. molitor* larvae prior to experimental testing. It was employed both as a standard feedstock and as a control diet, providing a baseline for comparison against alternative diets, including polypropylene (PP) and agar-based substrates. By functioning as the control diet, Wheat Bran allowed researchers to evaluate the effects of feeding larvae with synthetic polymers, such as polyethylene, relative to their normal nutritional intake. Its established role as a conventional and nutritionally adequate diet for mealworms made Wheat Bran an essential reference point in the biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat Bran (WB) is a material mentioned in the text, used as a feeding substrate in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
