<node id="PET">
  <data key="d0">PET</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a synthetic plastic polymer widely studied for its potential biodegradation, particularly in relation to the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms). Research has demonstrated that PET can be ingested and partially degraded by these larvae, with evidence of significant mass reduction and isotopic changes indicating metabolic processing. The degradation process is facilitated not only by the larvae themselves but also by their gut microbiome, including microbial communities and enzymes capable of breaking down the polymer. PET is classified as a hydrolysable polymer, which makes it susceptible to enzymatic and microbial degradation under certain conditions.  

Experimental studies have tested PET as a dietary substrate for *T. molitor* larvae, showing that while it can sustain survival and activity, it does not provide sufficient nutrition or essential minerals to support long-term growth and development. Nitrogen supplementation has been found necessary to maintain larval survival when PET is the primary food source, highlighting its inadequacy as a sole nutrient supply. Despite these limitations, PET-fed larvae exhibit measurable degradation activity, with nitrogen fixation processes also being observed in some cases.  

Comparative studies have shown that PET exhibits higher removal efficiency in *T. molitor* larvae than other common plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Investigations have also explored the influence of PET’s molecular weight on its biodegradation, with different forms such as PET1100, PET27100, and PET63500 being tested to assess variations in degradation efficiency. These findings suggest that polymer characteristics, including molecular weight, play a role in the extent and rate of biodegradation.  

In addition to larval studies, PET has been examined for microbial biodegradation more broadly, with microorganisms and enzymes identified as key agents in breaking down the polymer. The combined action of larvae and their gut microbiota provides a model system for studying PET biodegradation pathways and potential applications in plastic waste management. PET has also been suggested as a candidate material for further testing to evaluate physiological impacts on organisms exposed to plastic diets.  

Overall, PET (polyethylene terephthalate) is a widely used plastic polymer that has become a focus of biodegradation research due to its environmental persistence. Studies with *T. molitor* larvae and associated microbes demonstrate its partial degradability, highlight the biological and nutritional constraints of using it as a diet, and underscore its potential role in developing biological strategies for plastic waste reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET refers to polyethylene terephthalate plastic, one of the plastic treatments tested on insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET (Polyethylene terephthalate) is a type of plastic substrate tested in insect larva experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PET refers to a type of plastic substrate tested with BSF larvae, showing positive metabolic interaction.&lt;SEP&gt;PET (Polyethylene Terephthalate) is a hydrolyzable polymer whose biodegradation has been studied extensively. Its degradation is influenced by crystallinity, molecular weight, and surface hydrophobicity.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383217</data>
</node>
<node id="PUR">
  <data key="d0">PUR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyurethane (PUR) is a petroleum-based plastic polymer that has been the subject of multiple biodegradation studies due to its potential environmental impact and susceptibility to biological breakdown. Classified as a hydrolyzable polymer, PUR is considered more prone to biodegradation than non-hydrolyzable plastics. Research has demonstrated that PUR can be consumed and degraded by certain insects, particularly Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae, which have been reported to ingest it at a rate of approximately 18 mg per 100 larvae per day. These findings highlight the role of insect-mediated degradation as a possible pathway for reducing plastic waste.  

In addition to insect-based studies, PUR has also been shown to undergo biodegradation when exposed to landfill microbial cultures. Evidence of this process includes measurable changes in the molecular weight (Mw) of the polymer, indicating microbial activity that alters its chemical structure. Such results suggest that both insect digestion and microbial action contribute to the breakdown of PUR under specific conditions.  

Beyond its demonstrated biodegradability, PUR has been identified as a candidate material for further testing to assess potential physiological impacts on organisms that consume it. This reflects ongoing scientific interest in understanding not only the degradation pathways of PUR but also the broader ecological and biological consequences of its ingestion.  

Overall, Polyurethane (PUR) is recognized as a widely used plastic polymer with notable susceptibility to biodegradation through both insect and microbial processes. Its inclusion in experimental diets for mealworms and its observed breakdown in landfill microbial environments underscore its relevance in research on sustainable waste management and plastic pollution mitigation.&lt;SEP&gt;PUR (polyurethane) is a polymer reported to be biodegraded by a landfill microbial culture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387682</data>
</node>
<node id="Nanoplastics (NPs)">
  <data key="d0">Nanoplastics (NPs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles, typically defined as plastic fragments smaller than 1 μm, that arise from the degradation of larger plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). They represent the nanoscale products of plastic breakdown, often originating from the further fragmentation of microplastics. Due to their minute size, nanoplastics are of increasing concern in environmental and biological research, as they can interact with organisms at the cellular and physiological levels, potentially causing toxicological effects and physiological stress.  

Research has focused on the formation and biological impacts of nanoplastics, particularly in the context of biodegradation processes. For example, studies on Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae, which are capable of consuming and degrading certain plastics, have investigated whether nanoplastics are produced as a byproduct of this degradation. Findings indicate that no nanoplastics were detected in the frass (excrement) of larvae fed with PE and PS, and it is hypothesized that larvae fed with PET also do not generate nanoplastics. This suggests that the biodegradation of plastics by T. molitor may not contribute to nanoplastic pollution, thereby reducing the potential risk of nanoplastic-related toxicity in this context.  

Overall, nanoplastics are recognized as a critical environmental issue due to their persistence, their ability to result from the breakdown of common plastics, and their potential to exert harmful biological effects. However, evidence from biodegradation studies, such as those involving T. molitor larvae, indicates that not all plastic degradation pathways necessarily lead to nanoplastic formation. This dual perspective highlights both the risks associated with nanoplastics in ecosystems and the importance of understanding the conditions under which they are generated.&lt;SEP&gt;Extremely small plastic particles contributing to environmental contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are smaller plastic pollutants derived from PP and other plastics, found in various environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are plastic particles ranging from 1 to 1000 nm, considered emerging contaminants with potential health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics (NPs) are tiny particulate contaminants that pose environmental and biological risks, but were not detected in frass during the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles derived from polystyrene and other polymers, mentioned as a form of environmental plastic waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387666</data>
</node>
