<node id="Rhodococcus ruber">
  <data key="d0">Rhodococcus ruber</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterium belonging to the phylum *Actinobacteria* and the family *Nocardiaceae*. It is frequently described as an actinomycete and has been the subject of research due to its potential role in the biodegradation of synthetic polymers. Studies have reported that *Rhodococcus ruber* can colonize plastic surfaces, form biofilms, and contribute to the partial degradation of materials such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). Its ability to establish biofilms is considered a key factor in its interaction with these otherwise recalcitrant polymers, as biofilm formation facilitates adhesion to plastic surfaces and may enhance enzymatic or oxidative processes involved in polymer breakdown.  

Although *Rhodococcus ruber* has been identified as capable of degrading PE and PS, the extent of its biodegradation activity remains a subject of ongoing investigation. Some studies provide evidence of partial degradation, particularly of polystyrene, while others note that claims of its degradative capacity are limited or require further validation. Despite these uncertainties, the bacterium is widely recognized as a promising candidate for research into microbial plastic degradation, given its demonstrated colonization abilities and biofilm-mediated interactions with polymeric substrates.  

In summary, *Rhodococcus ruber* is an actinomycete bacterium of the family *Nocardiaceae* that has been studied for its ability to colonize plastics, form biofilms, and contribute to the partial biodegradation of polyethylene, polystyrene, and expanded polystyrene. While its degradative potential is not yet fully established, it remains an important microorganism of interest in the field of plastic biodegradation research.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is an actinomycete studied for its ability to form biofilms and partially biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is a microbial species associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is described as a polyethylene degrading bacterium studied for its biofilm development.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinomycete microorganism studied for biofilm formation and partial biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodococcus ruber is a bacterial strain studied for colonization, biofilm formation, and biodegradation of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial strain studied for its ability to colonize, form biofilms, and biodegrade polyethylene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385666</data>
</node>
