<node id="Microplastics (MPs)">
  <data key="d0">Microplastics (MPs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that originate from the degradation, fragmentation, and weathering of larger plastic materials, including common polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polylactic acid (PLA). They are typically defined as particles smaller than 5 mm, with some classifications specifying sizes down to 10 μm or less than 300 μm. Due to their persistence, resistance to degradation, and widespread distribution, microplastics are recognized as emerging environmental pollutants of global concern.  

These particles are generated through multiple processes, including physical weathering, mechanical abrasion, biodegradation, and biofragmentation. Once formed, they are found across diverse environments, including marine and freshwater systems, soils, landfill leachates, and terrestrial habitats. Their durability allows them to accumulate in ecosystems and enter food webs, where they can be ingested by a wide range of organisms.  

Scientific research has demonstrated that microplastics can exert harmful physiological and toxicological effects on living organisms. Experimental studies, including those involving macroinvertebrates and Tenebrio molitor larvae, have shown that exposure to microplastics can induce oxidative stress, disrupt lipid homeostasis, and trigger biotoxic responses. These effects may result in tissue damage, impaired metabolic processes, and broader health consequences for both invertebrates and vertebrates. The ability of microplastics to accumulate in organisms further amplifies their ecological and potential human health risks.  

Overall, microplastics represent a critical environmental issue due to their persistence, ubiquity, and biological impacts. They compromise ecological balance by interacting with organisms at multiple trophic levels and pose potential risks to human health through environmental exposure and food chain transfer. As a form of plastic waste that resists natural degradation, microplastics highlight the urgent need for continued research into their sources, degradation pathways, ecological consequences, and long-term effects on environmental and human systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
