  <data key="d5">1756372823</data>
</node>
<node id="Formic acid">
  <data key="d0">Formic acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Formic acid is produced during facultative anaerobic and anaerobic decomposition of PET and can be used by Dysgonomonas sp. and Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp. as an electron donor for mineralization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372823</data>
</node>
<node id="PVC (Polyvinyl chloride)">
  <data key="d0">PVC (Polyvinyl chloride)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyvinyl chloride is a non-hydrolysable plastic mentioned as being degraded more slowly than PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC (Polyvinyl chloride) is a plastic material consumed and degraded by mealworms and other insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a type of plastic studied for biodegradation by mealworms and superworms.&lt;SEP&gt;PVC is one of the three tested microplastics used in the experiment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387406</data>
</node>
<node id="PUR (Polyurethane)">
  <data key="d0">PUR (Polyurethane)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyurethane is a hydrolysable polymer mentioned as being degraded more slowly than PET by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PUR (Polyurethane) is a plastic material consumed by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373344</data>
</node>
<node id="National Engineering Research Center for Bioenergy (Harbin Institute of Technology)">
  <data key="d0">National Engineering Research Center for Bioenergy (Harbin Institute of Technology)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The National Engineering Research Center for Bioenergy at Harbin Institute of Technology supported the research through Grant No. 2021A001.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372824</data>
</node>
<node id="PET biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">PET biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">PET biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down polyethylene terephthalate by microbial and enzymatic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;PET biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down commercial PET microplastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down polyethylene terephthalate (PET) by biological means, studied here in the context of T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379723</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic degradation">
  <data key="d0">Plastic degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic degradation refers to the breakdown of plastics, including PET, through biological and enzymatic processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic degradation refers to the biological breakdown of plastic materials, observed in PS-fed mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic degradation is the process under investigation, focusing on microbial and enzymatic mechanisms that break down polystyrene and other polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic degradation is a biological process carried out by mealworm gut microbiota, enabling breakdown of plastic waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385998</data>
</node>
<node id="Facultative bacteria">
  <data key="d0">Facultative bacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Facultative bacteria are microorganisms that contributed to PET biodegradation in the larvae gut microbiome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372824</data>
</node>
<node id="PET polymer degrading enzymes">
  <data key="d0">PET polymer degrading enzymes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET polymer degrading enzymes include hydrolases, oxidases, decarboxylase, depolymerase, oxidoreductase, and dehydrogenase, which were enriched or upregulated in the gut microbiome.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372824</data>
</node>
<node id="Mineralization">
  <data key="d0">Mineralization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mineralization refers to the conversion of PET degradation products into inorganic substances by microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is a process described in the studies, referring to the conversion of polystyrene into simpler mineral compounds by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is studied as part of the biodegradation process of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374298</data>
</node>
<node id="Assimilation">
  <data key="d0">Assimilation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Assimilation refers to the incorporation of PET degradation products into microbial or host metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372825</data>
</node>
<node id="Interspecific interactions">
  <data key="d0">Interspecific interactions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Interspecific interactions refer to potential interactions among gut microbial species during PET degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756372826</data>
</node>
