<node id="Zhang, Z.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, Z.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, Z. is a researcher whose work primarily focuses on microbial degradation of plastics and the environmental impacts of microplastics. Zhang, Z. has contributed to multiple studies investigating the biodegradation of synthetic polymers by microorganisms, including a 2019 study on polyethylene biodegradation by *Enterobacter* sp. D1. This research explored the potential of bacterial strains to break down polyethylene, one of the most widely used and persistent plastics. In addition, Zhang, Z. has co-authored studies on the biodegradation of polyvinyl chloride (PVC), specifically examining the role of bacteria isolated from insect larvae in facilitating PVC degradation. These works highlight the potential of insect-associated microbial communities as a novel resource for addressing plastic pollution.  

Beyond plastics, Zhang, Z. has also investigated the biodegradation of nitrogen-containing heterocyclic compounds. This includes authorship of a study on the enhanced biodegradation of quinoline and indole using a symbiotic microbial system, which provided insights into cooperative microbial interactions for breaking down environmentally persistent organic pollutants.  

More recently, Zhang, Z. has expanded research to the environmental distribution and impacts of microplastics. As a co-author of a 2022 study on microplastics in agricultural soils, Zhang, Z. contributed to examining how microplastic contamination affects soil ecosystems, agricultural productivity, and potential risks to human health. This work emphasized the growing concern over microplastic accumulation in terrestrial environments, complementing earlier research on microbial degradation as a potential mitigation strategy.  

Overall, Zhang, Z.’s body of work integrates microbiology, environmental science, and pollution mitigation, with a strong emphasis on microbial solutions to plastic degradation and the ecological consequences of microplastic contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a 2023 study on Enterobacter asburiae E7 as a probiotic enhancing resistance in common carp.</data>
