<node id="Xu, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Xu, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Xu, Y. is a researcher whose work focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers and plastics through biological and microbial processes. Xu, Y. has co-authored multiple studies investigating the degradation of polyethylene microplastics and polyethylene mulching films, with particular emphasis on the role of mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) and their associated microbial communities. This includes research on the residual plastics and potential toxicity that may arise during the biodegradation of microplastics by Tenebrio molitor, as well as studies demonstrating the ability of bacterial co-cultures, isolated from the larvae, to degrade polyethylene mulching films.  

In addition to polyethylene, Xu, Y. has contributed to research on the degradation of other polymers. This includes studies on the biodegradation of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) by bacterial consortia derived from the gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor larvae, highlighting the potential of insect-associated microbes in tackling persistent plastic waste. Xu, Y. has also co-authored work on the biodegradation of bisphenol-A polycarbonate plastic by Pseudoxanthomonas sp. strain NyZ600, further extending the scope of research to different classes of synthetic polymers.  

Beyond insect-associated systems, Xu, Y. has participated in research exploring the biodegradation of bioplastics, such as polylactic acid (PLA), through studies involving earthworms as a model for evaluating degradability in soil ecosystems.  

Overall, Xu, Y.’s research contributions center on advancing the understanding of biological and microbial pathways for plastic degradation, with a focus on polyethylene, PVC, polycarbonate, and bioplastics. This body of work highlights the potential of insect gut microbiota, bacterial co-cultures, and soil organisms as promising agents for mitigating plastic pollution and promoting sustainable waste management strategies.</data>
