</node>
<node id="Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae">
  <data key="d0">Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae is the taxonomic family commonly known as the darkling beetles. This family encompasses a wide diversity of beetle species, including several that are of scientific and applied interest. Notable members of Tenebrionidae include *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as the yellow mealworm), *Tenebrio obscurus* (the dark mealworm), and species of the genus *Zophobas*, such as *Zophobas atratus*. These beetles are frequently studied in entomology, ecology, and applied sciences due to their biological and ecological roles.  

A particularly significant aspect of Tenebrionidae is the capacity of some species, especially *Tenebrio molitor*, to biodegrade synthetic polymers such as plastics. Research has demonstrated that these beetles can consume and break down materials like polystyrene, making them a subject of interest in studies on sustainable waste management and biodegradation processes. This ability is linked to their gut microbiota, which contributes to the enzymatic breakdown of otherwise persistent plastic materials.  

In taxonomy, Tenebrionidae is situated within the order Coleoptera, one of the largest and most diverse insect orders. The family includes thousands of species distributed worldwide, many of which are adapted to arid or semi-arid environments. While some species are considered pests due to their association with stored products, others are valued in research and as feeder insects in animal husbandry.  

Overall, Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae represents a diverse beetle family that is taxonomically significant, ecologically widespread, and increasingly important in scientific research, particularly for its members’ unique role in plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae is the beetle family to which Tenebrio molitor, Zophobas atratus, and Tenebrio obscurus belong.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385249</data>
</node>
<node id="Lepidoptera: Pyralidae">
