<node id="Peng et al., 2019">
  <data key="d0">Peng et al., 2019</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Peng et al., 2019 is a widely cited research study that significantly advanced understanding of insect-mediated plastic biodegradation, particularly in relation to the gut microbiome of mealworm larvae. Conducted by Peng and collaborators, the study investigated the ability of Tenebrio species, including yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and dark mealworms (Tenebrio obscurus), to degrade synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). The research demonstrated that these larvae are capable of ingesting, oxidizing, and metabolizing PS, thereby contributing to the growing body of evidence that insects may play a role in mitigating plastic pollution.  

A key contribution of Peng et al., 2019 was the comparative analysis of degradation efficiencies between T. molitor and T. obscurus, which revealed species-specific differences in their capacity to process plastics. The study also highlighted the importance of gut microbial communities in facilitating plastic breakdown. By analyzing shifts in the microbiota of T. obscurus when fed polystyrene versus a natural diet of bran, the researchers underscored the role of microbial symbionts in supporting polymer degradation. Enterobacteriaceae were identified as potential microbial candidates involved in plastic consumption, and the study further demonstrated that PS depolymerization was inhibited when larvae were treated with the antibiotic gentamicin, providing direct evidence of microbial involvement.  

Methodologically, Peng et al., 2019 has been frequently referenced for its use of Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and statistical approaches to assess changes in polymer molecular weights, oxidation, and fragmentation. These analytical frameworks have since informed subsequent studies on polymer chemistry and biodegradation processes. The research also provided a methodological reference for antibiotic suppression tests in larvae, which have been adopted in later investigations of microbiome-plastic interactions.  

By integrating larval feeding behavior, microbial ecology, and polymer chemistry, Peng et al., 2019 established a multidisciplinary foundation for studying plastic biodegradation in biological systems. The findings not only demonstrated that Tenebrio larvae can fragment and metabolize plastics but also highlighted the synergistic role of gut microbes in enhancing degradation. As a result, the study has become a cornerstone reference in the field of plastic biodegradation and insect-based bioremediation strategies, influencing ongoing research into the ecological and environmental implications of larval feeding on synthetic polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
