<node id="Biofilm">
  <data key="d0">Biofilm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A biofilm is a structured microbial community that develops on the surface of various plastic materials, playing a critical role in microbial colonization and subsequent biodegradation processes. In the context of plastic degradation studies, biofilm refers specifically to the microbial biomass that adheres to polymer surfaces such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and high-density polyethylene (HDPE). This microbial layer must often be removed prior to biodegradation analysis to ensure accurate assessment of plastic breakdown.  

Biofilm formation has been widely observed during microbial enrichment experiments, where bacterial strains or microbial consortia attach to plastic surfaces and establish stable communities. For example, biofilms formed on PS films have been associated with enhanced biodegradation, serving as both an indicator of microbial growth and a facilitator of polymer breakdown. Similarly, biofilms produced by bacterial strains on PE mulching films have been visualized using scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM), revealing structural changes such as cracks and surface erosion linked to microbial activity.  

Specific microbial strains and consortia have been shown to contribute to biofilm development on plastics. A consortium of strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 formed biofilms on PE mulching films, resulting in visible surface alterations. Strain PELW2042 produced biofilms on HDPE surfaces, demonstrating microbial colonization and degradation potential. Likewise, strain LG3 was capable of forming biofilms on PS films under anaerobic conditions, highlighting the adaptability of microbial communities to different environmental contexts.  

Overall, biofilm in the context of plastic degradation represents a critical interface between microorganisms and polymer surfaces. It not only signifies microbial attachment and growth but also facilitates the biochemical processes that lead to plastic deterioration. The study of biofilm formation on plastics provides essential insights into microbial strategies for colonization, the mechanisms of polymer degradation, and the potential for developing biotechnological solutions to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Biofilm formation on microplastics influences metal adsorption behavior and microbial community composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386267</data>
</node>
<node id="Ultrasound pretreatment">
  <data key="d0">Ultrasound pretreatment</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">A pretreatment method using ultrasound combined with sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) to remove biofilm or biomass from plastic surfaces before analysis.</data>
