<node id="Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)">
  <data key="d0">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is an advanced imaging technique widely employed in biodegradation and microbial interaction studies to provide detailed visualization of surface structures at the micro- and nanoscale. In the context of polymer and plastic degradation research, SEM is used to directly observe microbial colonization, biofilm formation, and the physical alterations that occur on material surfaces during degradation processes. For example, SEM enables the detection of cracks, holes, and other structural changes in polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene (PE) mulching films following microbial or bacterial activity. These observations are critical for understanding how microorganisms adhere to, colonize, and break down synthetic materials.  

Beyond synthetic polymers, SEM has also been applied to study the degradation of polyurethane foams, particularly in experiments where such materials are consumed by organisms like mealworms. In these cases, SEM provides micro-morphological images that reveal both the structural modifications in the feedstocks and the characteristics of the resulting frass, offering insights into the mechanisms of biological processing of plastics.  

In gut microbial degradation studies, SEM serves as a valuable analytical tool to visualize how microbial communities interact with and alter substrates, thereby contributing to the understanding of biodegradation pathways. Its ability to capture high-resolution images of surface morphology makes it indispensable for characterizing microbial activity, whether in controlled laboratory experiments or in applied environmental contexts.  

Overall, Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) is a critical method for analyzing the microstructural changes associated with microbial degradation of plastics and polymers. By providing direct visual evidence of colonization, biofilm formation, and material breakdown, SEM supports the evaluation of biodegradation processes and enhances knowledge of microbial-material interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) was used to examine the surface structure and characterization of nanoplastics before and after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM) was used to analyze PS film and nanoplastics, revealing surface alterations and size reduction after microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;SEM was used to investigate physical changes in degraded PS films.&lt;SEP&gt;An analytical method used in the study to analyze frass samples from mealworms fed with mask layers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f102938f307d7404d3f31b7200d93768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382758</data>
</node>
<node id="Water Contact Angle (WCA)">
  <data key="d0">Water Contact Angle (WCA)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">WCA is a method used to measure changes in hydrophobicity of polymer surfaces, indicating biodegradation activity.&lt;SEP&gt;WCA is an analytical method used for gut microbial degradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;WCA was measured to determine changes in the hydrophobicity of PS film surfaces after microbial treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;WCA was used to measure surface property changes in degraded PS films.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Contact Angle (WCA) is a measurement used to determine surface hydrophobicity of polymers. The study measured WCA of LDPE, LLDPE, and HDPE after being processed by Tenebrio larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Water Contact Angle (WCA) is a measurement used to assess the hydrophobicity of polymer surfaces, with lower WCAs indicating reduced hydrophobicity and possible biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Water contact angle (WCA) was one of the methods used to analyze metabolic differences in PS degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385610</data>
</node>
