<node id="T. obscurus">
  <data key="d0">T. obscurus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">**Comprehensive Summary of T. obscurus**  

T. obscurus, commonly known as the dark mealworm, is the larval form of a darkling beetle in the family Tenebrionidae. It is closely related to the well-known yellow mealworm, T. molitor, but is distinguished by specific nutritional and functional characteristics that have made it increasingly relevant in both agricultural and environmental research. Traditionally, T. obscurus has been reared as a feed insect in animal husbandry and aquaculture, where its relatively high cysteine content enhances its nutritional value compared to other mealworm species.  

Beyond its role as a feed source, T. obscurus has emerged as an important model organism in the study of plastic biodegradation. Its larvae are capable of ingesting and partially degrading a range of synthetic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE) in its various forms (low-density, linear low-density, and high-density), and polypropylene (PP). Among these, PS degradation has been the most extensively studied, with T. obscurus showing higher survival rates, greater consumption capacity, and superior biodegradability compared to T. molitor. Research on low-density polyethylene (LDPE) has also demonstrated that T. obscurus larvae can depolymerize and oxidize the material, with some studies suggesting higher polymer digestibility than T. molitor, although consumption rates may vary depending on experimental conditions.  

The ability of T. obscurus to degrade plastics is strongly linked to its gut microbiome. Studies have shown that the microbial community within its digestive system undergoes significant shifts depending on diet, with distinct microbial assemblages emerging when larvae are fed plastics instead of natural substrates such as wheat bran or corn flour. These symbiotic microorganisms are believed to play a central role in enabling depolymerization and partial mineralization of otherwise recalcitrant polymers. Experimental analyses of larval frass using techniques such as Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) have confirmed biochemical modifications of polymer residues, providing evidence of microbial and enzymatic activity in the degradation process. Antibiotic suppression experiments further highlight the essential role of gut microbes in facilitating plastic breakdown.  

Despite their capacity to survive on plastic-based diets, T. obscurus larvae exhibit physiological stress responses, including oxidative stress, indicating that plastic consumption imposes metabolic challenges. Nonetheless, their demonstrated ability to chew, ingest, and metabolize plastics has made them a valuable organism for investigating insect-mediated plastic degradation pathways.  

Overall, T. obscurus represents a species of dual importance: as a commercially valuable insect for sustainable feed production and as a promising biological model for addressing global plastic pollution. Its unique combination of nutritional benefits, plastic-degrading capacity, and microbe-mediated metabolic processes positions it at the intersection of sustainable agriculture, microbial ecology, and waste management. Ongoing research into its gut microbiota, degradation efficiency, and ecological applications may contribute to innovative strategies for reducing plastic waste while supporting sustainable food systems.&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is another species of larvae referenced in relation to PS biodegradation tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
