<node id="Z. atratus">
  <data key="d0">Z. atratus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zophobas atratus, commonly known as the superworm, is the larval stage of a darkling beetle species that has attracted significant scientific interest for its unique biological and ecological traits, particularly its ability to consume and biodegrade synthetic polymers. Belonging to the genus *Zophobas* within the family Tenebrionidae, Z. atratus has been studied extensively as a model organism in environmental biotechnology and waste management research.  

One of the most notable features of Z. atratus is its capacity to degrade a variety of plastics, including polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Experimental studies have demonstrated that the larvae can survive on plastic substrates such as PP foam and Styrofoam, in addition to conventional diets like wheat bran. The biodegradation process involves both the mechanical feeding activity of the larvae and the metabolic contributions of their gut microbiota, which facilitate depolymerization, surface modification, and chemical transformation of otherwise persistent polymers. Distinct chemical intermediates and modifications have been identified during these processes, underscoring the role of gut-associated microbes in enabling plastic breakdown.  

Z. atratus has also been used in comparative studies alongside other insect species, such as *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), to evaluate differences in plastic degradation efficiency. While Z. atratus is capable of consuming plastics, the rates of degradation are often limited, highlighting the importance of further research into optimizing conditions and understanding microbial contributions. The species has additionally been employed in antibiotic suppression tests and diet consumption experiments to better characterize the role of gut microbes in plastic depolymerization.  

Beyond its environmental applications, Z. atratus exhibits biological and behavioral traits of ecological and practical relevance. The larvae display cannibalistic behavior, a characteristic shared with some other members of the Tenebrionidae family, which has implications for their survival, feeding behavior, and management in laboratory or industrial rearing systems. Studies on their survival rates and feeding patterns under different diet conditions, including plastics and natural food sources, provide insights into their adaptability and potential for large-scale applications.  

Overall, Zophobas atratus (superworm) represents a promising insect species for advancing sustainable waste management strategies through insect-mediated plastic degradation. Its dual role as both a biological degrader of synthetic polymers and a model for studying gut microbial interactions positions it as a valuable subject in environmental biotechnology research aimed at addressing global plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Z. atratus refers to larvae species mentioned as capable of LDPE biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Z. atratus larvae are mentioned in relation to biodegradation of plastics such as PS and PVC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
