<node id="Dead Larvae">
  <data key="d0">Dead Larvae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dead larvae are counted to calculate the cannibal rate (CR) and are part of the physiological assessment of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Dead larvae refer to the remains of larvae in the incubators, which were consumed by surviving larvae when PP was the sole diet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385270</data>
</node>
<node id="Pupae">
  <data key="d0">Pupae</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Pupae represent a critical developmental stage in the life cycle of holometabolous insects, occurring after the larval stage and before emergence as adult beetles or moths. In experimental contexts, pupae are frequently monitored as indicators of larval health and developmental progress, with pupation rates (PRs) serving as a key metric to evaluate growth under different feeding conditions. For example, in studies involving mealworms, superworms, and lesser waxworms, pupae formation has been used to assess the effects of various diets, including plastic substrates such as Styrofoam, polyethylene (PE), and PE mixed with wheat bran (PE-WC).  

The appearance of pupae during experiments has important implications for plastic degradation studies. When larvae transition into pupae, the number of actively feeding individuals decreases, thereby reducing the overall rate of plastic consumption. In some cases, pupae have also been associated with cannibalistic behavior, further influencing experimental outcomes and lowering the efficiency of plastic degradation. Despite this, pupation remains a necessary and natural stage of insect development, ultimately leading to the emergence of adult beetles or moths.  

Observations across different species highlight variations in pupation timing and rates depending on diet. For instance, lesser waxworms fed on a PE-WC diet developed into pupae in approximately 6.8 days, demonstrating how feedstock composition can accelerate or delay developmental transitions. Similarly, superworms consuming Styrofoam progressed into pupae before emerging as adult beetles, while mealworms exhibited early pupation that reduced the number of larvae available for sustained plastic consumption.  

Overall, pupae are both a biological endpoint of larval growth and a variable influencing experimental outcomes in insect-plastic interaction studies. Their occurrence provides valuable insights into insect health, developmental dynamics, and the trade-offs between growth progression and the efficiency of plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The developmental stage of mealworms before becoming beetles, separated and transferred to incubators during experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the intermediate stage between mealworms and beetles in the experiment, separated and transferred to incubators for development.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage mealworms reached after feeding on PS and bran, before becoming beetles.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae are the developmental stage of Tenebrio molitor that were separated, reared, and observed during the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pupae were separated, placed in mini-greenhouses, and reared to the adult stage as part of the experimental process.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384254</data>
</node>
<node id="Beetles">
  <data key="d0">Beetles</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Beetles are included in the count of live organisms when calculating survival rate (SR).&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are lignocellulose-feeding insects with gut microbial communities that act as lignin degraders.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles were used in habitats either alone or in co-culture with mealworms to test EPS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Adult insects developed from pupae of PS-fed mealworms, used in breeding experiments for the second generation.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult form of mealworms, developed from pupae in the experiment. They were used for reproduction to produce fertilized eggs for further testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Beetles are the adult stage mealworms developed into after pupation, enabling the continuation of the life cycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383763</data>
</node>
