<node id="Soy protein">
  <data key="d0">Soy protein</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Soy protein is a nutrition-rich diet supplement used in co-diets with PS to enhance consumption and survival of T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a natural and nutritious feed additive that enhanced the plastic consumption rate of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein was used as an additive in experiments with polystyrene to test its effect on consumption rates.&lt;SEP&gt;A nutritional supplement purchased from General Nutrition Corporation and used in experiments to enhance PS degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein is a nutritional supplement that enhanced PS degradation when added to mealworm diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Soy protein was used as a dietary supplement in Yang et al. (2018a), which improved PS consumption by larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388220</data>
</node>
<node id="LCFBM">
  <data key="d0">LCFBM</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">LCFBM is a specialized liquid culture medium designed for use in microbial growth and biodegradation experiments involving synthetic polymers. It is primarily employed in studies where polystyrene (PS) or polyethylene (PE) materials, such as micro-powders, film pieces, mulching films, or particles, serve as the sole carbon source for bacterial cultivation. In this context, LCFBM provides the necessary nutrients and conditions to support bacterial enrichment and incubation while allowing researchers to assess the ability of microorganisms to utilize and degrade plastic substrates.  

The medium has been applied in various experimental setups, including flask cultures containing PS pieces for bacterial enrichment, as well as biodegradation assays where PS samples were incubated with specific bacterial strains, such as strain YT2. Similarly, LCFBM has been used in growth experiments with PE particles to evaluate microbial activity and plastic degradation potential. Beyond its role in direct biodegradation studies, LCFBM has also been mentioned in relation to research on nanoplastics (NPS) and their extraction methods, although detailed descriptions of its function in that context are limited.  

Overall, LCFBM serves as a critical experimental medium in environmental microbiology and biodegradation research, enabling the controlled study of bacterial interactions with plastic materials and supporting investigations into microbial pathways for polymer breakdown.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381130</data>
</node>
