<node id="FTIR analysis">
  <data key="d0">FTIR analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR analysis, or Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, is an analytical technique widely applied to study chemical modifications and structural changes in polymeric materials, particularly in the context of biodegradation and biotransformation processes. In research involving polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET), FTIR analysis has been employed to detect alterations in functional groups and surface morphology that occur as a result of microbial or insect-mediated degradation. For example, it has been used to examine mealworm frass derived from polystyrene diets, revealing chemical modifications in the polymer residues. Similarly, the method has been applied to study changes in PU foam after mealworm feeding, as well as in PS/corn starch (PS/CS) composite samples treated with Lac640, where differences in surface morphology and functional groups were observed between treated and untreated materials.  

The technique provides insight into specific chemical transformations by identifying characteristic absorption peaks corresponding to functional groups. Previous studies using FTIR analysis have detected signals such as C=O stretching, C–O stretching, and R–OH stretching, which indicate oxidation processes and the introduction of oxygen-containing groups. In the case of PET samples subjected to biodegradation, FTIR analysis revealed increased oxidation and enhanced hydrophilicity, pointing to structural breakdown and chemical modification of the polymer chains.  

Overall, FTIR analysis serves as a powerful tool for monitoring chemical changes in synthetic polymers during degradation, enabling researchers to track modifications in functional groups, assess oxidation levels, and evaluate surface alterations. Its application across different polymer types and experimental conditions highlights its versatility and importance in understanding the mechanisms of polymer biodegradation and transformation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared) analysis is a technique used to characterize oxidation and chemical modification of polyethylene microplastics after digestion by Tenebrio species.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis is a method used to provide evidence of oxidation and depolymerization of LDPE foams.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis was conducted to confirm the identity of LDPE film materials and detect chemical changes after biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386508</data>
</node>
<node id="Biodegradation of polystyrene wastes in yellow mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Biodegradation of polystyrene wastes in yellow mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">This refers to the experimental study conducted over 32 days to analyze the biodegradation of PS in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific study investigating factors affecting biodegradation rates of polystyrene in yellow mealworms and their ability to complete their life cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;A scientific study published in Chemosphere in 2018 examining factors affecting biodegradation rates and the life cycle of polystyrene-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced study in Chemosphere focusing on factors affecting biodegradation rates and life cycle completion of mealworms fed with polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
