</node>
<node id="Mw">
  <data key="d0">Mw</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mw, or weight-average molecular weight, is a fundamental parameter used to characterize polymers and evaluate their structural integrity, stability, and degree of degradation. It represents the average molecular weight of a polymer sample, weighted by the mass of each molecular species, and is commonly determined through gel permeation chromatography (GPC). This analytical technique provides detailed information on polymer chain length distribution, enabling researchers to monitor changes in molecular structure under various experimental and environmental conditions.  

In polymer science, Mw is widely applied to study plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). For polystyrene, Mw is frequently measured to assess degradation processes, including those occurring in biological systems such as larval digestion or in extracts from natural sources. A reduction in Mw in PS residues or frass indicates depolymerization, reflecting the breakdown of long polymer chains into smaller molecular fragments. Similarly, in PVC studies, Mw serves as a marker of molecular weight reduction, highlighting structural changes that occur during experimental treatments.  

In the context of microplastics research, Mw is particularly important for evaluating the extent of polymer degradation in biological and environmental systems. For example, in studies involving PET microplastics exposed to Tenebrio molitor larvae, decreases in Mw provide quantitative evidence of polymer breakdown during biodegradation. Comparable applications are seen in LDPE and PE, where Mw measurements before and after larval digestion or biodegradation experiments reveal reductions in molecular weight, confirming depolymerization and transformation of the polymer.  

Overall, Mw (weight-average molecular weight) is a critical metric for monitoring polymer degradation, depolymerization, and transformation across diverse experimental systems. By quantifying molecular weight changes, Mw enables researchers to assess the extent of chemical, physical, and biological processes acting on plastics. This makes it an essential tool for understanding polymer stability, environmental persistence, and biodegradation pathways, thereby contributing to broader insights into plastic pollution and its mitigation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e69c793c3b67aed7a68bf864c2c7a309</data>
