<node id="Liu, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Liu, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Liu, Y. is a researcher and author whose work is centered on environmental science, with a particular emphasis on plastic pollution, biodegradation processes, and their ecological and health implications. A significant portion of Liu, Y.’s research focuses on the biological degradation of plastics, especially expanded polystyrene (EPS) and polyethylene foams, through the digestive activity of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. Between 2020 and 2021, Liu, Y. co-authored multiple studies published in journals such as *Chemosphere* that investigated the capacity of mealworms to degrade EPS, low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and related plastic foams. These studies examined not only the extent of plastic biodegradation but also the fragmentation of plastics into smaller particles, the metabolic pathways involved, and the potential role of the gut microbiome in facilitating degradation. This body of work highlights the feasibility of insect-mediated biodegradation as a potential strategy for mitigating plastic waste and contributes to the broader understanding of sustainable waste management solutions.  

In addition to research on insect-based biodegradation, Liu, Y. has contributed to studies addressing the environmental fate of microplastics. A 2019 co-authored study investigated the occurrence, behavior, and treatment of microplastics in wastewater treatment plants, providing insights into how engineered water systems manage these pollutants and the challenges they pose to ecosystems and human health. Liu, Y. has also co-authored research on the interactions between microplastics and biological systems, including a study on zebrafish embryonic chorions, which explored the potential developmental impacts of microplastic exposure.  

Beyond plastics-focused research, Liu, Y. has participated in studies examining broader environmental and toxicological issues. This includes co-authorship of a study on breast cancer in mice related to exposure to polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 (PBDE-47), a flame retardant known for its persistence and potential health risks.  

Overall, Liu, Y.’s research contributions span multiple dimensions of environmental science, from the biodegradation of macroplastics by insect larvae to the occurrence and impacts of microplastics in aquatic systems, as well as the toxicological effects of persistent organic pollutants. This diverse body of work positions Liu, Y. as an active contributor to advancing knowledge on plastic pollution, biodegradation mechanisms, and environmental health challenges.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
