  <data key="d5">1756386557</data>
</node>
<node id="Gao, L.C.">
  <data key="d0">Gao, L.C.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gao, L.C. is a researcher recognized for contributions to studies on the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene, particularly through the activity of plastic-eating organisms such as mealworms. In 2015, Gao, L.C. co-authored a study published in *Chemosphere* that investigated the ability of mealworms to consume and break down polystyrene, a widely used plastic known for its persistence in the environment. This research provided evidence that mealworms can metabolize polystyrene, leading to its biodegradation and partial mineralization, thereby offering potential insights into biological solutions for plastic waste management. Gao, L.C.’s work in this area has been cited in multiple contexts, emphasizing the significance of mealworms as a model organism for studying plastic biodegradation and highlighting the role of biological processes in addressing environmental challenges posed by synthetic polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;Gao, L.C. is a co-author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f</data>
