<node id="Brandon, A. M.">
  <data key="d0">Brandon, A. M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Brandon, A. M. is a researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly through the use of insects such as mealworms. His work has focused on the role of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, commonly known as yellow mealworms, in the degradation of synthetic polymers. Brandon has co-authored multiple studies that investigate the capacity of these organisms to consume and break down polystyrene, one of the most widely used plastics, as well as other plastic mixtures including polyethylene.  

A significant portion of his research has examined the microbial processes underlying this biodegradation, highlighting the symbiotic relationship between mealworms and their gut microbiota in facilitating the breakdown of otherwise persistent plastic materials. In particular, Brandon was an author of a 2018 study that provided evidence of polystyrene waste degradation in yellow mealworms, contributing to a growing body of literature on biological solutions to plastic pollution. His work has also addressed the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in mealworms, suggesting that this phenomenon is not limited to isolated cases but may represent a broader biological capacity with potential applications in waste management and environmental remediation.  

Through these studies, Brandon, A. M. has helped advance scientific understanding of insect-mediated and microbially assisted plastic degradation, positioning mealworms as a model system for exploring sustainable strategies to mitigate the environmental impacts of plastic waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48</data>
