<node id="Polyethylene Mulching Films">
  <data key="d0">Polyethylene Mulching Films</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyethylene mulching films are agricultural plastic films studied for biodegradation by bacterial co-cultures isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene mulching films are agricultural plastics studied for biodegradation by microbial strains isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386557</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Microorganisms">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microorganisms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gut microorganisms are microbial populations residing in the digestive system of mealworms, particularly within their larval stage, where they play a critical role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene. These microorganisms have been shown to aid mealworms in breaking down polystyrene, a synthetic polymer that is otherwise resistant to natural degradation. Research has demonstrated that the presence of these gut microbes is essential for the effective processing of polystyrene, as they contribute to both its initial breakdown and its conversion into simpler mineralized compounds. Scientific studies often employ cultivation and sequencing methods to analyze the composition and functional capabilities of these microbial communities, providing insights into the mechanisms by which they facilitate plastic degradation. Overall, gut microorganisms in mealworms represent a key biological factor enabling the biodegradation of polystyrene, making them a subject of significant interest in environmental and biotechnological research.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms are studied for their role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms are microbial communities in mealworms that play a role in polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microorganisms are studied for their role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387997</data>
</node>
<node id="Synthetic Polymers">
  <data key="d0">Synthetic Polymers</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
  <data key="d2">Europe reported global production of synthetic polymers in 2019, totaling over 359 million tons.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are studied for enzymatic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are man-made macromolecules, discussed in the context of enzymatic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthetic polymers are materials that fungi have been described as efficient in degrading.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384770</data>
</node>
<node id="Insect larvae">
  <data key="d0">Insect larvae</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f</data>
  <data key="d2">Cline reported in 1978 that insect larvae were implicated in the penetration and deterioration of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae, including moths and beetles, are capable of chewing and damaging plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae are organisms that can directly consume polyethylene and secrete emulsifiers to mediate plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Insect larvae are identified as macroinvertebrates capable of rapid biodegradation of plastics.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387078</data>
</node>
