</node>
<node id="Gentamycin">
  <data key="d0">Gentamycin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gentamycin is a bactericidal antibiotic that inhibited depolymerization of polystyrene in mealworms, implicating the gut microbiome in the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Gentamycin is an antibiotic that, when fed to mealworms, reduced their ability to depolymerize and mineralize PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373311</data>
</node>
<node id="United States">
  <data key="d0">United States</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States is a country that serves as both a geographic and institutional context for research on polystyrene (PS) degradation and waste management. It is the location where Stanford University and the Woods Institute for the Environment are based, institutions that have played a central role in advancing studies on plastic biodegradation. Within the United States, a commercially available mealworm strain obtained from a pet store, referred to as strain CA, was identified and studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene foam. This strain, along with other yellow mealworms sourced in the country, demonstrated the capacity to digest PS, providing a significant contribution to scientific understanding of biological pathways for plastic waste reduction.  

In addition to being the source of these experimental organisms, the United States is also the geographic focus of the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency’s (EPA) 2016 report on waste management trends, which provides a broader national context for evaluating the environmental implications of plastic use and disposal. Together, these elements highlight the United States as both a site of scientific innovation in biodegradation research and a key jurisdiction for policy and environmental monitoring related to plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where Stanford University is located.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is one of the locations where biodegradation of commercial PS foams by mealworms was observed.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where Stanford University and its affiliated research centers are located.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387681</data>
</node>
<node id="Ms. Chong-Guan Chen">
  <data key="d0">Ms. Chong-Guan Chen</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ms. Chong-Guan Chen, in 2003, raised mealworms fed on polystyrene foam and theorized that the polystyrene biodegraded.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373312</data>
</node>
<node id="Ms. I-Ching Tseng">
  <data key="d0">Ms. I-Ching Tseng</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ms. I-Ching Tseng, in 2009, isolated bacteria from mealworm guts using polystyrene as the sole carbon source.</data>
