<node id="Table 1">
  <data key="d0">Table 1</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Table 1 provides a consolidated and detailed overview of experimental results examining the feeding behavior, survival, physiological responses, and biodegradation capacities of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae, as well as related insect and plant studies, under a variety of dietary and environmental conditions. The table integrates multiple dimensions of data, including survival rates, weight changes, feed consumption, and substrate degradation, thereby serving as a central reference for evaluating insect-plastic interactions and related biological outcomes.  

Specifically, Table 1 reports the survival and consumption rates of *T. molitor* when exposed to a range of natural and synthetic diets such as polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyurethane (PU), polypropylene (PP), agar, wheat bran (WB), and unfed controls. It documents the initial and final weights of plastics, the remnants detected in frass, and molecular weight changes in polymers, providing evidence of partial digestion, mechanical breakdown, and biodegradation of plastics. The table also includes data on the biodegradation of different polyethylene (PE) types by both *T. molitor* and *T. obscurus* larvae over a 21-day feeding period, as well as average expanded polystyrene (EPS) consumption and estimated biodegradation rates across experimental setups.  

In addition to plastic degradation, Table 1 presents physiological outcomes such as mass changes in larvae and adult beetles (imagines), highlighting the developmental impacts of diet type. It also records cannibalism rates, specific PP consumption rates, and comparative feeding behaviors of *Zophobas atratus* and *T. molitor* larvae. Furthermore, the table includes data on the physical characteristics of plastics, such as hardness, which influenced larval feeding behavior and consumption efficiency.  

Beyond insect-focused experiments, Table 1 extends to plant-related outcomes by summarizing the effects of different media on the rooting success and proportions of cacti, thereby broadening its scope to plant propagation studies.  

Overall, Table 1 functions as a comprehensive dataset that integrates survival, growth, consumption, and degradation metrics across diverse dietary treatments. By combining information on insect physiology, plastic biodegradation, and plant growth responses, it provides a multifaceted resource for understanding both the ecological potential of mealworms in plastic waste management and the broader biological impacts of experimental diets and substrates.&lt;SEP&gt;Table 1 presents the weight change of mealworms due to an EPS diet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69</data>
