<node id="Exiguobacterium sp. YT2">
  <data key="d0">Exiguobacterium sp. YT2</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 is a bacterial strain recognized for its unique ability to degrade polystyrene (PS), a widely used plastic that is otherwise resistant to natural decomposition. This microorganism was first isolated from the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as yellow mealworms. Identified and studied by Yang et al., Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 represents the first reported expanded polystyrene (EPS)-degrading microorganism, highlighting its significance in the field of plastic biodegradation research.  

Experimental studies have demonstrated the strain’s capacity to break down PS under controlled conditions. When tested on PS film for 30 days, Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 achieved a biodegradation rate corresponding to a 28.6% weight loss. In longer-term experiments, it was shown to degrade PS by up to 7.4% after 60 days. These results indicate that while the rate of degradation varies depending on conditions and duration, the bacterium possesses a measurable and reproducible ability to metabolize polystyrene.  

The isolation of Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 from the intestinal tract of mealworms underscores the role of insect gut microbiota in facilitating plastic degradation, a discovery that has broadened scientific understanding of potential biological solutions to plastic pollution. Its identification as a PS-degrading bacterium has positioned it as a model organism for studying microbial plastic biodegradation and for exploring biotechnological applications aimed at mitigating the environmental impact of synthetic polymers.  

In summary, Exiguobacterium sp. YT2 is a polystyrene-degrading bacterial strain isolated from Tenebrio molitor larvae, notable for being the first identified EPS-degrading microorganism. Through experimental validation, it has been shown to significantly reduce PS mass over time, making it an important subject of research in the development of sustainable strategies for plastic waste management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384527</data>
</node>
