<node id="Tenebrio molitor (mealworms)">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor (mealworms)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms, are insects studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373522</data>
</node>
<node id="Biodegradation of Polystyrene">
  <data key="d0">Biodegradation of Polystyrene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The process studied in which polystyrene is broken down by mealworms and their gut microbiome under different feeding strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polystyrene is the process studied, focusing on microbial and symbiotic mechanisms in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation of polystyrene is the process initiated by bacterial attachment to the film surface, leading to its breakdown.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386818</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Packaging">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Packaging</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic packaging refers to the use of polymers such as polystyrene for food packaging, associated with environmental and health risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic packaging is a contaminant in organic waste streams that must be removed before insect processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic packaging is described as widely used but environmentally harmful.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382563</data>
</node>
<node id="Zophobas morio (superworms)">
  <data key="d0">Zophobas morio (superworms)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zophobas morio, also known as superworms, are insects reported to have potential for biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas morio larvae, also known as superworms, are capable of degrading EPS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374401</data>
</node>
