</node>
<node id="Metabolomics Studies">
  <data key="d0">Metabolomics Studies</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolomics studies revealed the presence of bioactive compounds such as long-chain fatty acids, esters, and amides in insect biomass and frass during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373524</data>
</node>
<node id="Long-chain Fatty Acids">
  <data key="d0">Long-chain Fatty Acids</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-chain fatty acids are bioactive compounds observed in insect biomass and frass during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373524</data>
</node>
<node id="Esters">
  <data key="d0">Esters</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Esters are bioactive compounds identified in insect biomass and frass during polystyrene biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373524</data>
</node>
<node id="Amides">
  <data key="d0">Amides</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Amides are bioactive compounds formed during polystyrene biodegradation, associated with enzymatic activity and bio-catalytic amide bond formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Bioactive molecules identified in the polar fraction of frass during the biodegradation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Amides are reported as products of increased enzymatic activity and bio-catalytic amide bond formation during plastic biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382569</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrocarbons">
  <data key="d0">Hydrocarbons</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrocarbons are compounds present in connection with polystyrene degradation in insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbons are chemical compounds identified in frass, represented by alkyl- and methyl carbons.&lt;SEP&gt;Hydrocarbons, mostly with longer carbon chains, were identified in the polar fraction of frass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377102</data>
</node>
<node id="Free Fatty Acids (FFAs)">
  <data key="d0">Free Fatty Acids (FFAs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Free fatty acids are compounds that accumulate in insect biomass during polystyrene biodegradation, indicating enzymatic degradation and microbial metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373525</data>
</node>
<node id="Enzymatic Activity">
  <data key="d0">Enzymatic Activity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enzymatic activity is associated with the biodegradation of plastics, including the formation of amides and fatty acids.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity is considered as a potential mechanism for targeted functionalization of plastics, though its adequacy is undemonstrated.&lt;SEP&gt;Enzymatic activity refers to the production of enzymes such as lipase, esterase, laccase, catalase, oxidase, protease, and cellulase by bacterial isolates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381135</data>
</node>
<node id="Leucine Arylamidase">
  <data key="d0">Leucine Arylamidase</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Leucine arylamidase is an enzyme identified as predominant during the biodegradation of plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
