<node id="Lactobacillus">
  <data key="d0">Lactobacillus</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Lactobacillus is a bacterial genus that plays a notable role in the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm). It has been consistently reported in studies examining the microbial communities of mealworms, including those fed on polystyrene (PS) diets. Within the larval gut, Lactobacillus is often identified as a dominant genus, indicating its prevalence and potential functional importance in digestion and microbial balance. Its abundance, however, appears to be influenced by dietary conditions. For instance, Lactobacillus has been found to be negatively correlated with diets based on oxidized polyethylene (PE-oxo) and regular polyethylene (PE-reg), suggesting that certain synthetic polymer diets may reduce its presence. Conversely, the genus shows a strong positive association with diets combining polyethylene (PE) and bran. In fact, Lactobacillus has been reported as significantly enriched in mealworms fed with the PE + bran diet compared to those fed only bran or PE alone, highlighting a diet-dependent modulation of its abundance. Overall, Lactobacillus in *T. molitor* is a key gut microbial genus whose prevalence and associations vary with dietary inputs, particularly in relation to plastic- and bran-based feeding regimes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillus is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactobacillus is listed as a genus in the microbial community analysis, associated with relative abundance data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385288</data>
</node>
<node id="Clostridium">
  <data key="d0">Clostridium</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Clostridium is a bacterial genus that has been identified as part of the gut microbiome of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. It has been reported in multiple studies examining the digestive systems of these insects, particularly in relation to experimental diets involving polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE). Clostridium is associated with Diet 3 in such feeding trials and has been observed in both PS-fed and PE-fed groups, indicating its role in shaping gut microbial composition under different dietary conditions.  

Research highlights that Clostridium is an anaerobic bacterium that can develop in insects subjected to diets with a high bran-to-PS ratio. Its presence has been linked to nutrient digestion challenges, as well as to the production of short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which are important metabolic byproducts in microbial fermentation processes. In principal component analysis (PCA) of gut microbiome data, Clostridium has shown a negative correlation with protein levels and with PS-based diets, suggesting that its abundance may decrease under certain nutritional constraints.  

Beyond its role in insect gut ecology, Clostridium has also been mentioned in the scientific literature for its potential styrene-degrading abilities, which may be relevant to the biodegradation of plastics such as polystyrene. This functional capacity positions Clostridium as a genus of interest in studies exploring microbial contributions to plastic degradation and nutrient cycling in insect-based systems.  

Overall, Clostridium is a bacterial genus consistently reported in the digestive system of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae, with its occurrence influenced by diet composition, its metabolic activities linked to SCFA production, and its potential involvement in styrene degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a bacterial genus found in the posterior gut of mealworms, part of the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a bacterial genus with significantly different relative abundances between diet groups in the mealworm gut microbiome study.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a genus of bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a genus of anaerobic bacteria found in the hindgut of mealworms, potentially involved in PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria isolated from the guts of nitrogen-fixing insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Clostridium is a genus of bacteria isolated from the guts of nitrogen-fixing insects and also detected in mealworm gut microbial communities.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385965</data>
</node>
<node id="Diet 1 (Bran:PS 4:1)">
  <data key="d0">Diet 1 (Bran:PS 4:1)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Diet 1 is a feeding protocol with a bran-to-PS ratio of 4:1, leading to high PS consumption.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 1 is an experimental feeding condition where larvae were fed with a bran to PS ratio of 4:1.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756373529</data>
</node>
<node id="Diet 2 (Bran:PS 20:1 + H2O)">
  <data key="d0">Diet 2 (Bran:PS 20:1 + H2O)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Diet 2 is a feeding protocol with a bran-to-PS ratio of 20:1 plus water, leading to good growth and survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Diet 2 is an experimental feeding condition where larvae were fed with a bran to PS ratio of 20:1 with added water.</data>
