<node id="Control Diet">
  <data key="d0">Control Diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The control diet is a baseline feeding condition used in experimental studies, particularly those investigating insect feeding, biomass production, and biodegradation processes. It serves as the reference point against which experimental diets, such as polystyrene (PS)-based diets, are compared. In these studies, the control diet is defined as a non-polystyrene diet composed entirely of bran (100% bran). This simple, bran-only formulation provides a standardized nutritional baseline that allows researchers to assess the effects of alternative or experimental feeding strategies.  

In the context of insect feeding and biodegradation experiments, the control diet has been associated with several notable outcomes. Larvae fed the control diet demonstrated high survival rates, indicating that it supports healthy growth and development. Microbiome analyses revealed that the control diet produced microbial community profiles similar to those observed in certain other diets, such as Diet 1, while also being linked to higher nitrogen levels in frass and elevated counts of bacterial taxa including *Klebsiella* and *Spiroplasma* species. These findings highlight the role of the control diet not only as a nutritional baseline but also as a determinant of gut microbial composition and metabolic byproducts.  

Overall, the control diet represents the standard bran-only feeding condition against which experimental diets are evaluated. Its consistent use as a baseline enables researchers to isolate the specific effects of alternative diets, such as PS-fed regimens, on insect survival, microbiome composition, and biodegradation potential.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5922f60fcd06ac6ffa27018026871346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382571</data>
</node>
<node id="Diet 1">
  <data key="d0">Diet 1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Diet 1 is an experimental feeding condition designed for studies on polystyrene (PS) biodegradation using *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. It is consistently defined as a mixture of bran and polystyrene in a 4:1 weight-to-weight ratio (bran:PS). In some descriptions, Diet 1 is specified as being prepared without the addition of water, while in others it is noted as including water, reflecting slight variations in experimental setups. The formulation of Diet 1 was based on previously published studies to ensure comparability of results across biodegradation research.  

As a treatment condition, Diet 1 serves as a representative low PS-to-bran ratio diet, providing larvae with a primary food source (bran) supplemented by polystyrene to evaluate their capacity for plastic consumption and degradation. Experimental outcomes associated with Diet 1 indicate that it supports higher enzymatic activity and facilitates the production of primary amides, suggesting enhanced biochemical processing of PS compared to other diets. Additionally, larvae fed under Diet 1 conditions produce frass with higher nitrogen content relative to those on Diet 2 or Diet 3, highlighting differences in nutrient cycling and metabolic byproducts.  

Overall, Diet 1 represents a standardized and widely applied feed strategy in insect-based biodegradation experiments. Its 4:1 bran-to-PS composition provides a controlled balance between nutritional support and plastic exposure, enabling researchers to study PS depolymerization, degradation compound formation, and the physiological responses of *T. molitor* larvae under conditions that are both experimentally reproducible and biologically informative.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382581</data>
</node>
<node id="Diet 2">
  <data key="d0">Diet 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Diet 2 is an experimental feeding condition designed for studies on polystyrene (PS) biodegradation and insect nutrition, particularly in *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. It consists of a bran-to-polystyrene (bran:PS) mass ratio of 20:1 (w/w), with water supplementation provided at regular intervals, typically 1.5 mL every fourth day. The inclusion of water distinguishes Diet 2 from other feeding strategies, as hydration was introduced to test its effect on PS degradation and overall insect performance.  

This diet has been consistently associated with improved outcomes in biodegradation experiments. Specifically, Diet 2 demonstrated the highest extent of PS consumption and degradation, suggesting that hydration plays a critical role in facilitating microbial and enzymatic depolymerization processes. The feeding condition also supported higher survival rates and better growth of mealworm larvae compared to other diets, indicating that the combination of bran, PS, and water creates a more favorable nutritional and physiological environment.  

Microbiological analyses linked Diet 2 to the growth and activity of several bacterial and protozoan species, including *Enterococcus faecium*, *Weisella*, *Lactococcus lactis*, and *Gregarina polymorpha*. These microorganisms are thought to contribute to PS degradation and nutrient processing within the insect gut. Additionally, Diet 2 was associated with increased cholesterol levels in the larvae and enhanced excretion of fatty acids, reflecting its influence on insect metabolism and lipid regulation.  

Overall, Diet 2 represents a bran/PS mixture with hydration that not only optimizes PS biodegradation but also promotes insect health, survival, and microbial symbiosis. It serves as a key experimental condition for understanding the role of water and diet composition in plastic degradation and insect physiology.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382581</data>
</node>
<node id="Diet 3">
  <data key="d0">Diet 3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Diet 3 refers to an experimental feeding condition designed for Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae in studies of polystyrene (PS) biodegradation and insect physiology. It is defined by a bran-to-polystyrene (PS) mass ratio of 20:1 (w/w) without the addition of water. This formulation was chosen both to test larval feeding behavior and to minimize handling requirements during experimental procedures, making it one of the least labor-intensive feed strategies applied in the biodegradation trials.  

In terms of experimental outcomes, Diet 3 has been associated with distinct biological and microbial effects. Larvae reared under this condition exhibited higher average body weight but reduced survival rates compared to other diets. The diet also influenced gut microbiota composition, showing clustering with bacterial taxa such as *Chryseobacterium*, *Bacteroides*, *Clostridium*, and *Spiroplasma*. Additionally, Diet 3 was linked to metabolic changes, including increased cholesterol levels and enhanced excretion of fatty acids.  

From a biodegradation perspective, Diet 3 demonstrated lower levels of PS degradation relative to Diet 2, suggesting that the absence of added water and the specific bran-to-PS ratio may limit the extent of polymer breakdown. Despite this, it served as a critical comparative condition for evaluating how different feed formulations affect both PS depolymerization and insect health.  

Overall, Diet 3 represents a controlled treatment condition characterized by a 20:1 bran-to-PS mixture without water supplementation. It provides insights into the interplay between insect growth, survival, gut microbial communities, and the efficiency of PS biodegradation under minimal handling requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382586</data>
</node>
