<node id="Fan, H.-Q.">
  <data key="d0">Fan, H.-Q.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Fan, H.-Q. is a researcher recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the ability of yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) to digest and degrade polystyrene. As a co-author of multiple scientific studies, Fan, H.-Q. has investigated the ubiquity and mechanisms of polystyrene digestion in mealworms, highlighting their potential role in mitigating plastic waste. This body of work includes research on the biodegradation of polystyrene wastes, with a notable 2018 study examining the widespread occurrence of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms. Through these studies, Fan, H.-Q. has contributed to advancing scientific understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and its implications for sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Fan, H.-Q. is listed as an author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene in yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384307</data>
</node>
<node id="Wang, Z.-Y.">
  <data key="d0">Wang, Z.-Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, Z.-Y. is a researcher recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insects in breaking down synthetic polymers. Wang has co-authored multiple scientific studies investigating the biodegradation of polystyrene, a widely used plastic, by yellow mealworms (the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*). These works include research on the ubiquity of polystyrene digestion in mealworms, highlighting the capacity of these larvae to ingest and metabolize polystyrene waste. Wang’s studies have provided evidence that yellow mealworms can serve as a biological agent for the degradation of polystyrene, offering potential insights into sustainable waste management strategies. Notably, Wang was a co-author of a 2018 study that examined both the digestion and biodegradation processes of polystyrene in mealworms, contributing to the growing body of literature on insect-mediated plastic degradation. Through this research, Wang, Z.-Y. has helped advance understanding of how mealworms may play a role in addressing global plastic pollution challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Wang, Z.-Y. is listed as an author of a study on biodegradation of polystyrene in yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384308</data>
</node>
<node id="Gao, L.">
  <data key="d0">Gao, L.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Gao, L. is a researcher recognized for significant contributions to the field of plastic biodegradation, with a particular focus on the role of insects and microorganisms in breaking down synthetic polymers. Gao, L. is best known as a co-author of influential studies that investigated the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms, the larval form of the darkling beetle. These studies, including a widely cited 2015 publication in *Environmental Science &amp; Technology*, provided groundbreaking evidence that mealworms are capable of consuming and metabolizing polystyrene, resulting in its partial mineralization. This discovery suggested a novel biological pathway for mitigating plastic waste, highlighting the potential of insect-mediated processes as a sustainable approach to addressing global plastic pollution.  

Beyond polystyrene, Gao, L. has also contributed to research on the biodegradation of polyethylene, another pervasive plastic, through bacterial strains isolated from the digestive systems of waxworms. This work further emphasized the role of insect–microbe interactions in facilitating the breakdown of otherwise persistent synthetic polymers. Collectively, Gao, L.’s research advances the understanding of biological mechanisms for plastic degradation and underscores the promise of leveraging natural systems—particularly insects and their associated microbiota—as innovative strategies for reducing the environmental impact of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388241</data>
</node>
<node id="Yang, R.">
  <data key="d0">Yang, R.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang, R. is a researcher recognized for contributions to scientific studies on the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms. As a co-author of research published in *Environmental Science &amp; Technology* in 2015, Yang, R. played a role in advancing evidence that mealworms, the larval form of the darkling beetle, are capable of ingesting and breaking down polystyrene, one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics. The studies in which Yang, R. participated demonstrated not only the consumption of polystyrene by mealworms but also its subsequent biodegradation and mineralization, indicating that the plastic was biologically processed rather than simply fragmented. This work provided significant insights into a potential natural pathway for mitigating plastic pollution, highlighting the role of living organisms in addressing the environmental challenges posed by synthetic polymers. Through these contributions, Yang, R. is associated with pioneering research that broadened scientific understanding of biological solutions to plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;Author of studies on biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321</data>
