<node id="Urbanek, A.K.">
  <data key="d0">Urbanek, A.K.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Urbanek, A.K. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on microbial ecology, biodegradation, and sustainable waste management through biological systems. A significant part of their research has examined the microbiome diversity of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms) when fed with polystyrene waste, contributing to understanding how insect-associated microbial communities can facilitate plastic degradation. This line of research has been published in multiple studies, including a 2020 investigation into the microbial diversity of mealworms consuming polystyrene.  

In addition to work on insect-based biodegradation, Urbanek, A.K. has contributed to studies on the growth performance and feed conversion efficiency of edible mealworm species when reared on organic by-product diets, highlighting the potential of insects as sustainable protein sources and contributors to circular bioeconomy strategies.  

Urbanek, A.K. has also conducted research on microbial degradation of synthetic polymers in extreme environments. This includes a 2017 study on Arctic microorganisms capable of decomposing bioplastics, which provided insights into the potential of cold-adapted microbes for biotechnological applications in waste management. Furthermore, Urbanek, A.K. was one of the authors of a 2021 study investigating polyethylene terephthalate (PET) degradation by genetically modified microorganisms, advancing the field of synthetic biology and microbial engineering for plastic recycling.  

Through these diverse contributions, Urbanek, A.K. has established a research profile at the intersection of microbiology, biotechnology, and environmental sustainability, with particular emphasis on microbial and insect-based solutions for plastic and organic waste degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387461</data>
</node>
<node id="Rybak, J.">
  <data key="d0">Rybak, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Rybak, J. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation potential and microbiome diversity of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) in relation to plastic waste consumption. Their studies investigate how feeding *T. molitor* larvae with polystyrene waste influences various biological and ecological factors, including larval growth, development, survival, and gut microbial composition. Rybak, J. has co-authored multiple publications examining the capacity of *T. molitor* to degrade polystyrene, highlighting both the physiological responses of the larvae and the role of their gut microbiota in facilitating plastic breakdown. Through this research, Rybak, J. contributes to the broader understanding of insect-mediated biodegradation processes and the potential application of mealworms in sustainable waste management and environmental remediation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Description not available in text.&lt;SEP&gt;Rybak, J. is an author of a 2020 study on microbiome diversity in Tenebrio molitor fed with polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on growth performance and feed conversion efficiency of edible mealworm species.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387456</data>
</node>
<node id="Wróbel, M.">
  <data key="d0">Wróbel, M.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wróbel, M. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biological and ecological implications of plastic degradation by insects, particularly the species *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as the mealworm). Wróbel, M. has contributed to studies examining the microbiome diversity of *Tenebrio molitor* when the larvae are fed with polystyrene waste, a form of plastic pollution. These studies investigate how the gut microbial community adapts to and potentially facilitates the breakdown of synthetic polymers, providing insights into the role of insect-associated microbiota in biodegradation processes.  

In addition to microbiome-focused research, Wróbel, M. has also authored studies analyzing the effects of a polystyrene-based diet on the growth, development, and survival of *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. This line of research evaluates the physiological consequences of consuming plastic waste, including potential impacts on larval health, life cycle progression, and overall viability. By combining microbiological and developmental perspectives, Wróbel, M.’s work contributes to a broader understanding of the interactions between insect hosts, their gut microbiota, and synthetic materials in the environment.  

Overall, Wróbel, M. is recognized for co-authoring and authoring multiple studies, including a 2020 publication, that explore both the microbial ecology and biological outcomes of feeding *Tenebrio molitor* larvae with polystyrene waste. This research is significant in the context of environmental science and waste management, as it highlights the potential role of insect-microbiome systems in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on growth performance and feed conversion efficiency of edible mealworm species.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713</data>
