<node id="Plastic Waste Pollution">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Waste Pollution</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic waste pollution refers to the global environmental issue arising from the accumulation and improper disposal of synthetic plastic materials in natural ecosystems. It occurs when plastics escape waste collection and management systems, ultimately contaminating oceans, land, and other habitats. This form of pollution is particularly concerning because most plastics, including polyethylene, polystyrene, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polypropylene, are highly resistant to biodegradation. As a result, they persist in the environment for extended periods, leading to long-term ecological impacts.  

The problem is exacerbated by the widespread use of plastics with short service lives, such as packaging materials, which quickly become waste despite their durable chemical structures. Among these, polystyrene and related materials are often referred to as “white pollutants” due to their visible accumulation in the environment. Once released, plastic waste can fragment into smaller particles, further spreading contamination across ecosystems and posing risks to wildlife and human health.  

Plastic waste pollution is recognized as a significant global concern because it disrupts ecological balance, threatens marine and terrestrial species, and contributes to broader environmental degradation. Its persistence and scale highlight the urgent need for sustainable waste management practices, reduction in single-use plastics, and the development of biodegradable alternatives. In summary, plastic waste pollution represents a critical environmental challenge caused by the accumulation of non-degradable plastics in natural systems, with far-reaching consequences for ecosystems worldwide.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste pollution refers to the accumulation of plastics in the environment, causing ecological and socioeconomic problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic waste accumulation in the environment is described as a major risk to human and environmental health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387665</data>
</node>
<node id="Bioplastics">
  <data key="d0">Bioplastics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bioplastics are plastics capable of degradation under natural conditions, including polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA) and polylactide (PLA).&lt;SEP&gt;Bioplastics were studied in the 2017 research on Arctic microorganisms decomposing them.</data>
