<node id="Tai'an">
  <data key="d0">Tai'an</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Tai’an is a city and region located in Shandong Province, China, that has served as an important site for scientific research on the biodegradation of plastics by mealworms (larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*). In multiple studies, mealworms collected from Tai’an were used in controlled experiments to investigate their capacity to ingest, degrade, and metabolize various types of plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE and LDPE), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Research conducted in this region demonstrated that mealworm larvae are capable of consuming and breaking down these synthetic polymers, with one study reporting a 22.0% loss of polyethylene after experimental feeding trials.  

The work carried out in Tai’an has contributed to a broader understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation, highlighting the potential role of mealworms in addressing plastic pollution. Investigations in this region have examined not only the consumption rates of plastics but also the metabolic pathways involved in processing microplastics, providing insights into how these organisms transform or mineralize synthetic materials. By serving as a collection site and experimental location, Tai’an has become a notable reference point in the growing body of research on biological approaches to plastic waste management.  

In summary, Tai’an, Shandong Province, is recognized as a key geographic source and research site for studies on mealworm-mediated degradation of plastics such as PS, PE/LDPE, and PVC, contributing valuable data on the potential of biological systems to mitigate environmental microplastic pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374249</data>
</node>
<node id="Corn flour">
  <data key="d0">Corn flour</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Corn flour was used as a food additive in experiments to increase PS utilization by T. obscurus.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour was used as an additive in experiments with T. obscurus consuming polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour was used as part of the diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae in one experimental group.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is mentioned as part of a mixed diet with polystyrene for degradation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is mentioned as the preferred food of T. obscurus.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour is a codiet used in feeding experiments with T. obscurus larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Corn flour was used as a codiet supplement to PS, providing additional nutrition to larvae and enhancing PS consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384001</data>
</node>
