<node id="J. Hazard. Mater.">
  <data key="d0">J. Hazard. Mater.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The *Journal of Hazardous Materials* (abbreviated as *J. Hazard. Mater.*) is a peer-reviewed scientific journal dedicated to research on hazardous substances, their environmental impacts, and strategies for risk mitigation. It serves as a leading platform for interdisciplinary studies spanning environmental science, toxicology, waste management, and innovative remediation technologies.  

A significant focus of the journal has been on plastic pollution, recycling, and biodegradation. Since at least 2007, it has published influential work on chemical recycling of plastic wastes, reflecting early scientific efforts to address plastic pollution through chemical processes. More recently, *J. Hazard. Mater.* has become a prominent venue for research on biological and ecological approaches to plastic degradation. This includes numerous studies on the role of insect larvae, particularly the yellow mealworm (*Tenebrio molitor*), in degrading synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polylactic acid. Research published in the journal has examined the gut microbiome of mealworms, their metabolic responses to plastic-based diets, and their potential to contribute to sustainable waste management.  

The journal has also featured studies on microbial and fungal degradation of petroleum-based plastics, high-impact polystyrene, and other polymers, as well as investigations into hyperthermophilic composting for enhanced biodegradation in sewage sludge. Beyond degradation processes, *J. Hazard. Mater.* has published research on the fragmentation of mariculture Styrofoam floats, the digestion of microplastics in soils, and the ecological consequences of microplastic exposure in aquatic organisms such as zebrafish. These studies highlight the journal’s role in advancing understanding of both the environmental hazards posed by plastics and the biological or technological pathways for their mitigation.  

In addition to plastics research, the journal covers a wide range of hazardous materials and pollutants. It has published studies on isotopic responses to environmental contaminants, the toxicological effects of pollutants such as polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 in breast cancer mouse models, and broader investigations into the risks posed by hazardous chemicals in ecosystems and human health contexts.  

Overall, *J. Hazard. Mater.* is recognized as a leading international journal for advancing knowledge on hazardous substances. Its scope encompasses fundamental and applied research on pollutants, waste management strategies, biodegradation processes, and the environmental and health impacts of hazardous materials, with particular strength in the emerging field of plastic pollution and microplastic research.</data>
