<node id="Zhang, J.">
  <data key="d0">Zhang, J.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhang, J. is a researcher and author whose work spans multiple areas of environmental microbiology, biodegradation, soil science, and bioinformatics. A significant portion of Zhang’s research focuses on the microbial and biological degradation of synthetic plastics and polymers. This includes studies on the biodegradation of polyethylene microplastics by *Aspergillus flavus* isolated from wax moth guts, as well as investigations into the degradation of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) by bacteria derived from insect larvae gut communities. Zhang has also contributed to research on the biodegradation of bisphenol-A polycarbonate plastic by *Pseudoxanthomonas* sp. strain NyZ600, and co-authored work examining the ability of earthworms to process polylactic acid, a biodegradable bioplastic, as part of their diet.  

In addition to plastic degradation studies, Zhang, J. has explored sustainable agricultural practices, including research on the co-application of milk tea waste and NPK fertilizers to improve the biochemical properties of sandy soils and enhance wheat growth. This work reflects an interest in integrating organic waste recycling with soil fertility management to support crop productivity and environmental sustainability.  

Beyond applied environmental and agricultural research, Zhang, J. has also contributed to the field of bioinformatics. Zhang is a co-author of PEAR (Paired-End reAd mergeR), a widely used computational tool designed for merging Illumina paired-end sequencing reads. This tool has become an important resource in genomic and metagenomic studies, facilitating accurate and efficient sequence assembly.  

Overall, Zhang, J.’s body of work demonstrates a multidisciplinary approach that bridges microbiology, environmental biotechnology, soil science, and computational biology. The research contributions highlight innovative strategies for addressing plastic pollution through microbial and biological degradation, improving soil health through organic waste utilization, and advancing genomic data analysis through software development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90</data>
