  <data key="d0">Superworm Frass</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Superworm frass was used as a medium in grafting experiments with Hylocereus undatus.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm frass is the waste product of superworms fed on PS, which supported cacti growth and rooting.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm frass is a by-product of superworms that supported superior rooting and growth compared to other media, with less pungency and better moisture retention.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374057</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworm Frass">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm Frass</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm frass is the excrement and waste by-product generated by mealworms, often studied in the context of both agricultural applications and plastic degradation research. As a biological residue, it has been analyzed for its chemical composition and thermal properties using techniques such as Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), thermogravimetric analysis (TG), and gel permeation chromatography (GPC). These analyses have been particularly important in confirming the degradation of polystyrene (PS) by mealworms, since frass serves as a medium for detecting and characterizing the breakdown products of plastic digestion.  

In agricultural research, mealworm frass has been investigated as a potential fertilizer and biofertilizer due to its nutrient content and possible effects on soil properties, crop growth, and plant stress tolerance. Studies have examined its role in improving soil fertility and supporting plant development, though results vary depending on the context. For example, when used in grafting experiments with *Hylocereus undatus* (dragon fruit cactus), mealworm frass derived from PS-fed mealworms was found to impair cactus growth, leading to reduced rooting and plant height, and was associated with a strong ammonia odor. Despite these limitations, it did provide some support for root initiation, indicating partial potential for plant use.  

Overall, mealworm frass represents a dual-interest material: in environmental science, it is a key indicator of insect-mediated plastic degradation, while in agriculture, it is being explored as a sustainable fertilizer alternative. Its effectiveness and suitability, however, depend on the mealworms’ diet and the resulting chemical composition of the frass, which can influence its impact on plant growth and soil health.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382589</data>
</node>
