<node id="Cai, S.-Y.">
  <data key="d0">Cai, S.-Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Cai, S.-Y. is a researcher recognized for contributions to studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly focusing on its digestion and breakdown by yellow mealworms (the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*). As an author and co-author, Cai, S.-Y. has participated in multiple investigations that examine the capacity of mealworms to consume and metabolize polystyrene waste, a persistent plastic pollutant. These studies have explored both the biodegradation process itself and the broader ubiquity of this phenomenon, demonstrating that yellow mealworms can serve as a biological agent for reducing polystyrene pollution. Notably, Cai, S.-Y. contributed to a 2018 study that highlighted the widespread ability of mealworms to digest and biodegrade polystyrene, reinforcing the potential of insect-based biodegradation as a sustainable approach to plastic waste management. Through this body of work, Cai, S.-Y. has helped advance scientific understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and its implications for environmental remediation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384311</data>
</node>
