<node id="Mealworms (T. molitor larvae)">
  <data key="d0">Mealworms (T. molitor larvae)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworms (T. molitor larvae) were the experimental organisms used to study the effects of bran and EPS diets on intestinal microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms, specifically Tenebrio molitor larvae, are insects capable of biodegrading PS and PE plastics through digestive biofragmentation and microbial interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (T. molitor larvae) are organisms studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene (PS) polymers of varying molecular weights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387695</data>
</node>
<node id="EPS (Expanded Polystyrene)">
  <data key="d0">EPS (Expanded Polystyrene)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, foamed form of polystyrene produced from styrene monomer and characterized by its spherical bead structure containing effervescent gas. It is a synthetic polymer widely used in construction, particularly as an insulating material. Two main types are commonly distinguished: ordinary white EPS, which is primarily used for thermal insulation, and grey EPS, which incorporates graphite to enhance thermal performance and is often applied in energy-efficient building designs.  

Beyond its industrial applications, EPS has been the subject of experimental studies investigating its biodegradability. In these studies, EPS was provided as a dietary substance for mealworms and beetles. The experiments demonstrated that mealworms were capable of chewing, burrowing into, and consuming EPS, although it was found to be a poor nutrient source and did not support significant larval growth. Despite its limited nutritional value, EPS was identified as the most preferable and most biodegradable form of polystyrene for mealworms compared to other variants.  

The degradation of EPS in these biological experiments was associated with measurable material loss, indicating that mealworms and their gut bacteria can contribute to its breakdown. Results showed lower larval mass loss when EPS was consumed, alongside significant degradation of the material itself. These findings suggest that EPS, while not nutritionally beneficial for insects, can undergo partial biodegradation through biological processes, offering insights into potential waste management and recycling strategies.  

In summary, Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is both a widely used construction material and a subject of biodegradation research. Its dual role highlights its importance in industry for insulation and energy efficiency, as well as in environmental studies exploring biological pathways for managing plastic waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386814</data>
</node>
