<node id="Enterobacteriales">
  <data key="d0">Enterobacteriales</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterobacteriales is a bacterial order within the class **Gammaproteobacteria**, historically represented by the family **Enterobacteriaceae** and later reclassified into several distinct families, including **Erwiniaeae**. Members of this order are widely distributed and play diverse ecological and metabolic roles. They are commonly found in the gut microbiomes of insects, where they persist regardless of diet but show notable variation in abundance depending on dietary conditions. For example, Enterobacteriales are enriched in mealworms fed on polyvinyl chloride (PVC) and polyethylene (PE)-based diets, with relative abundances reaching around 8% in PE-fed groups, compared to lower levels (approximately 4.5% on average) in other conditions such as polystyrene (PS) diets. They are also partly associated with PE-oxo diets.  

Functionally, Enterobacteriales contribute to important biochemical processes, including the **degradation of pesticides**, **breakdown of lignocellulose**, and the **metabolism of aromatic compounds and heavy metals**, highlighting their role in both natural ecosystems and potential biotechnological applications. Their metabolic versatility makes them significant in the context of plastic degradation and environmental detoxification.  

Taxonomically, Enterobacteriales has undergone revisions, with proposals for reclassification into multiple families to better reflect phylogenetic relationships. Despite these changes, the order remains a central group within Gammaproteobacteria due to its ecological importance, metabolic diversity, and prevalence across different host-associated and environmental niches.  

In summary, Enterobacteriales is an order of Gammaproteobacteria comprising several families, notable for its presence in insect gut microbiomes, its enrichment under plastic-based diets, and its capacity to degrade complex organic and inorganic compounds, making it ecologically and biotechnologically significant.&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriales is the bacterial order to which Klebsiella belongs.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385993</data>
</node>
