<node id="Zhou J">
  <data key="d0">Zhou J</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zhou J is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biodegradation of synthetic polymers by mealworms, particularly yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*). Zhou J has contributed to multiple studies investigating the capacity of mealworms to degrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, polyether-polyurethane foam, and polymer mixtures. These studies explore not only the extent to which mealworms can consume and break down these persistent plastic materials but also the biological and ecological mechanisms underlying the process.  

A significant portion of Zhou J’s research centers on polystyrene, one of the most widely used and environmentally problematic plastics. Zhou J has co-authored several studies, including a 2018 publication, that demonstrate the ability of yellow mealworms to ingest and biodegrade polystyrene waste. These works examine both the physical degradation of the material and the biochemical pathways involved in its digestion. In addition, Zhou J has contributed to research on polyethylene degradation in mealworms, highlighting the broader potential of insect-based systems for addressing plastic waste.  

Another area of Zhou J’s scholarship involves the biodegradation of polyether-polyurethane foam, a material commonly used in consumer products. This research not only documents the capacity of yellow mealworms to process such foam but also investigates the effects of plastic consumption on the gut microbiome of the insects. By analyzing microbial community shifts, Zhou J’s work provides insights into the symbiotic role of gut microorganisms in facilitating plastic degradation.  

Collectively, Zhou J’s contributions form part of a growing body of scientific literature that positions mealworms as a potential biological solution for mitigating plastic pollution. Through studies on polystyrene, polyethylene, polyurethane, and mixed polymer waste, Zhou J has helped advance understanding of insect-mediated biodegradation processes and their implications for sustainable waste management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991</data>
