<node id="Polystyrene Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Polystyrene Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down polystyrene plastic waste, studied in the context of environmental science.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene biodegradation is the process studied across multiple insect larvae species, including mealworms, superworms, waxworms, and others.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene biodegradation is the process of breaking down polystyrene by biological organisms such as mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene biodegradation refers to the process studied in 2014, involving bacterial strains from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382792</data>
</node>
<node id="XRD">
  <data key="d0">XRD</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">XRD (X-ray Diffraction) is used to analyze the crystalline structure of polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD (X-ray Diffraction) analysis is used to characterize crystallinity changes in HDPE films before and after treatment with strain PELW2042.&lt;SEP&gt;XRD (X-ray diffraction) was one of the techniques used to characterize nanopolystyrene powder.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381109</data>
</node>
<node id="Raman Spectra">
  <data key="d0">Raman Spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Raman spectroscopy is used to study the vibrational modes of polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374550</data>
</node>
<node id="UV-Vis">
  <data key="d0">UV-Vis</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">UV-Vis spectroscopy is used to analyze the optical properties of polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374550</data>
</node>
<node id="NMR">
  <data key="d0">NMR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">NMR (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) spectroscopy is used to study the molecular structure of polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is an analytical technique used in the study to detect chemical modifications of degraded polymers.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR is an analytical technique used in the study, requiring specific solvents such as chloroform-d and tetramethylsilane.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance spectroscopy) is an analytical technique used to identify chemical shifts and metabolites during polymer biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385278</data>
</node>
<node id="Mass Spectra">
  <data key="d0">Mass Spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mass spectrometry is used to analyze the mass composition of polystyrene before and after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374551</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus)">
  <data key="d0">Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworms are larvae that can chew, ingest, and biodegrade polystyrene foam, mineralizing it into CO2 through microbe-dependent gut activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are the larvae of Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, shown to consume and degrade polystyrene in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380674</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbial Cultures">
  <data key="d0">Microbial Cultures</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microbial cultures from sources such as activated sludge, trash, soil, and manure have been evaluated for polystyrene biodegradation since the 1970s, though the process remains slow.&lt;SEP&gt;Microbial cultures are biological systems that may be capable of degrading plastics and could be applied in waste management technologies.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375892</data>
</node>
<node id="USEPA">
  <data key="d0">USEPA</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The United States Environmental Protection Agency (USEPA) provides data on plastic waste generation and its contribution to municipal solid waste.&lt;SEP&gt;USEPA (United States Environmental Protection Agency) is cited as a reference source in the document.&lt;SEP&gt;USEPA (United States Environmental Protection Agency) is referenced in a 2011 source related to municipal solid waste.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374552</data>
</node>
<node id="Municipal Solid Waste (MSW)">
  <data key="d0">Municipal Solid Waste (MSW)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Municipal solid waste includes various categories of waste, with plastics being a rapidly growing segment.</data>
