<node id="Extruded Polystyrene (XPS)">
  <data key="d0">Extruded Polystyrene (XPS)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is a commercial form of polystyrene that is widely used in both industrial and consumer applications. It is a type of polystyrene foam characterized by its higher density and greater compressive strength compared to expanded polystyrene (EPS). These properties make XPS particularly suitable for use in construction, where it serves as an effective insulation material, as well as in packaging and protective applications. In everyday consumer products, XPS is commonly found in disposable food containers, such as coffee cups and trays, due to its lightweight structure and insulating capabilities.  

Beyond its conventional uses, extruded polystyrene has also been studied in scientific research, particularly in relation to its biodegradation potential. For example, XPS has been tested as a feed material for mealworms to evaluate its effects on their growth, reproduction, and larval weight. These studies have shown that mealworms are capable of consuming XPS, with some experiments reporting significant increases in larval weight when fed this material. Such findings highlight the relevance of XPS not only as a commercial product but also as a subject of environmental and biological research, especially in the context of plastic waste management.  

Overall, Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is a versatile material that combines structural strength and insulating properties, making it valuable in construction, packaging, and food service industries, while also serving as an important focus of research into sustainable solutions for plastic degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387668</data>
</node>
<node id="Microplastic Pollution">
  <data key="d0">Microplastic Pollution</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microplastic pollution is caused by the breakdown of plastic materials such as PE, PP, PVC, PUR, PS, PET, and polycarbonate, and is widespread in oceans and the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;Environmental issue studied for its adverse impacts on immune and reproductive systems in marine research.&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastic pollution is described as one of the major environmental issues, alongside air, water, and soil pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
