<node id="PS biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">PS biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
  <data key="d2">Sielicki et al. reported microbial cultures degrading PS with 1.5%-3.0% removal over 4 months.&lt;SEP&gt;PS biodegradation refers to the process by which polystyrene is broken down by mealworms and other insect larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PS biodegradation refers to the process of breaking down polystyrene materials by bacterial strains such as LG3.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385644</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-1">
  <data key="d0">PE-1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-1 is a commercial low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam that has been widely studied as a substrate for biodegradation and depolymerization experiments involving insect larvae. Characterized by a number-average molecular weight of approximately 28.9 kDa, a weight-average molecular weight of 342.0 kDa, and a reported molecular weight of 130.6 kDa in some analyses, PE-1 represents a typical LDPE foam feedstock used in controlled laboratory studies.  

The material has been tested extensively with Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae, and in some cases with Tenebrio obscurus larvae, to evaluate its potential for biological breakdown. In these experiments, PE-1 served as both a dietary substrate and a polymer feedstock, allowing researchers to assess larval consumption, depolymerization patterns, and biodegradation processes. Results indicated that PE-1 undergoes broad depolymerization in the larval gut, showing degradation patterns comparable to those observed with polystyrene (PS).  

Beyond physical degradation, PE-1 has also been used to study microbial community responses within the larval gut. By feeding larvae with PE-1 under both control and antibiotic-treated conditions, researchers investigated the role of gut microbiota in facilitating or enhancing the breakdown of polyethylene. These studies highlighted the interaction between the polymer substrate and the gut flora, providing insights into the biological and microbial mechanisms underlying plastic degradation.  

Overall, PE-1 is a representative LDPE foam sample employed in biodegradation research to explore the capacity of insect larvae, particularly Tenebrio species, to consume, depolymerize, and potentially biodegrade synthetic polymers. Its use has contributed to advancing understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and the ecological role of gut microbiota in these processes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386474</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-2">
  <data key="d0">PE-2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-2 is a commercial low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam that was used as a feedstock in biodegradation and depolymerization experiments involving insect larvae. Specifically, it was tested as a dietary substrate for Tenebrio molitor (mealworm) larvae and also evaluated in comparative studies with Tenebrio obscurus larvae. The material was characterized by a number-average molecular weight of 27.3 kDa, a weight-average molecular weight of 264.1 kDa, and a reported molecular weight of approximately 288.7 kDa in some analyses.  

In the experimental context, PE-2 served as one of the polyethylene foam samples provided to larvae to assess their ability to consume, depolymerize, and biodegrade synthetic polymers. It was compared to another LDPE foam sample, PE-1, and was found to exhibit a distinct depolymerization pattern, highlighting differences in how various LDPE substrates are processed by insect digestive systems. The experiments were conducted under both control and antibiotic-treated conditions to evaluate the role of gut microbiota in the degradation process.  

Overall, PE-2 represents a commercial LDPE foam substrate that was systematically studied for its susceptibility to biological depolymerization and biodegradation when ingested by Tenebrio larvae, contributing to research on plastic waste bioremediation and the potential role of insect-based systems in managing synthetic polymer pollution.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386472</data>
</node>
