<node id="Plastic Biodegradation Studies">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Biodegradation Studies</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A series of studies conducted since the 1970s on the biodegradation of plastics such as PE, LDPE, and PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374776</data>
</node>
<node id="EPS foam">
  <data key="d0">EPS foam</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Expanded polystyrene (EPS) foam used as test material in biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;EPS foam with a PS purity of over 98% was used as a material in the biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;EPS foam is a type of polystyrene foam feed given to mealworms in the study, which affected their growth and reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;EPS foam is a type of polystyrene foam with higher permeability and recycled content, and it supports better productivity in mealworms compared to XPS foam.&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded polystyrene foam, used as a test material for biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383755</data>
</node>
<node id="LDPE foam (PE-1)">
  <data key="d0">LDPE foam (PE-1)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low-density polyethylene foam with pink color additives, used as test material in biodegradation experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374777</data>
</node>
<node id="LDPE film (PE-2)">
  <data key="d0">LDPE film (PE-2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Colorless low-density polyethylene film without additives, used as test material in biodegradation experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374777</data>
</node>
<node id="Biodegradation Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Biodegradation Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Experiments conducted to test the biodegradation of EPS and LDPE by Tenebrio molitor larvae under controlled conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The biodegradation experiment refers to the 36-day study in which T. molitor larvae consumed polypropylene microplastics, leading to measurable degradation and isotopic changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The experiment involved feeding T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae with different types of polyethylene (LDPE, LLDPE, HDPE) and wheat bran for 21 days to measure survival, consumption, and polymer degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380161</data>
</node>
<node id="Intestinal Flora">
  <data key="d0">Intestinal Flora</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Intestinal flora refers to the microbial community in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, which shifted under different dietary conditions and was associated with biodegradation of EPS and LDPE.&lt;SEP&gt;Intestinal flora of yellow mealworms contains bacterial strains capable of degrading plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;The intestinal flora of yellow mealworms is influenced by ingestion of different types of plastics, leading to significant differences in microbial composition.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378664</data>
</node>
<node id="Seawater">
  <data key="d0">Seawater</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Seawater was used as an environmental microbial source in biodegradation tests of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is mentioned as an environment where PE-degrading microorganisms are commonly found.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is an environment from which PE-degrading microorganisms were isolated.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is mentioned as an environmental source containing microorganisms used in biodegradation studies of polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is one of the natural environmental conditions where polyethylene biodegradation has been studied.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is an environmental source containing microbial communities used in biodegradation studies of PE.&lt;SEP&gt;Seawater is the environmental medium where polycarbonate degradation was studied.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385296</data>
</node>
<node id="Sediments">
  <data key="d0">Sediments</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Sediments were used as environmental microbial sources in biodegradation tests of plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374778</data>
</node>
<node id="Sludge">
  <data key="d0">Sludge</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Sludge was used as an environmental microbial source in biodegradation tests of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Sludge is mentioned as one of the environmental sources from which microorganisms have been used to investigate biodegradation of polyethylene (PE).&lt;SEP&gt;Sludge is an environmental medium where polyethylene biodegradation has been studied.&lt;SEP&gt;Sludge is an environmental source from which microorganisms for PE biodegradation studies have been obtained.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383223</data>
</node>
<node id="Compost">
  <data key="d0">Compost</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Compost was used as an environmental microbial source in biodegradation tests of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Compost is mentioned as an environmental source where microorganisms have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Compost is one of the intended end products of organic waste processing, which can be negatively impacted by plastic contamination.&lt;SEP&gt;Compost is an environmental medium where polyethylene biodegradation has been studied.&lt;SEP&gt;Compost is an environmental source where microorganisms capable of degrading PE have been enriched or isolated.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756383225</data>
</node>
<node id="EPS Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">EPS Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">EPS depolymerization refers to the breakdown of expanded polystyrene, which was significantly inhibited by gentamicin treatment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374779</data>
</node>
<node id="LDPE Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">LDPE Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">LDPE depolymerization refers to the breakdown of low-density polyethylene, which was less dependent or independent of gut microbes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756374779</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic Mass Loss">
  <data key="d0">Plastic Mass Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic mass loss was measured in the biodegradation experiments to assess the extent of degradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
