<node id="Tenebrio genus">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio genus</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio genus insects were reported to depolymerize PS and LDPE in multiple studies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Tenebrio genus includes species such as T. molitor, T. obscurus, and Z. atratus, which are studied for their feeding behaviors and biodegradation potential.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio genus refers to a group of insects studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics, particularly polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio genus is a group of insects that includes T. molitor and T. obscurus, studied for their potential plastic-degrading abilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio genus refers to a group of beetles, including mealworms, studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386476</data>
</node>
