<node id="Morganella">
  <data key="d0">Morganella</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Morganella is a gut microbe whose abundance decreased in larvae fed PE and PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a bacterial genus found in the gut of yellow mealworms, significantly increased in the inner-fed group and associated with mask diet and PP diet in previous studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a bacterial genus that increased in abundance in the gut microbial community of yellow mealworms after mask ingestion.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is a bacterial genus mentioned in the genus-level abundance analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Morganella is listed as a genus in the microbial community analysis, associated with relative abundance data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385286</data>
</node>
<node id="Bacillus sp.">
  <data key="d0">Bacillus sp.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacillus sp. refers to bacterial strains belonging to the genus *Bacillus* that have been identified in multiple ecological and experimental contexts, particularly in relation to plastic degradation and insect gut microbiota. These bacteria have been isolated from diverse environments, including mangrove sediments and the digestive tracts of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworm) larvae. Within the larvae, Bacillus sp. was detected at low levels across all diets but showed notable enrichment in larvae fed on polystyrene (PS) and low molecular weight polypropylene (PP-L), suggesting a diet-dependent role in microbial community dynamics.  

Experimental studies have demonstrated that Bacillus sp. is capable of interacting with synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polypropylene. On PS sheets, Bacillus strains were observed to form tunnels, cracks, and aggregates, indicating active physical and biochemical modification of the material. These bacteria have also been reported to metabolize styrene, a monomer of polystyrene, into phenylacetate, highlighting their potential role in the biochemical transformation of plastic-derived compounds. Similarly, Bacillus sp. has been associated with the degradation of low molecular weight polypropylene, further supporting its relevance in the breakdown of synthetic polymers.  

Beyond plastics, Bacillus sp. has been identified as a key microorganism involved in lignocellulose degradation, underscoring its metabolic versatility and ecological importance. Its presence in both natural environments, such as mangrove sediments, and in insect digestive systems suggests that Bacillus sp. contributes to biodegradation processes across different ecosystems. In particular, its involvement in the biodeterioration of polypropylene microplastics in sediments and its enrichment in plastic-fed insect larvae position Bacillus sp. as a significant microbial genus for studies on plastic biodegradation and bioremediation.  

In summary, Bacillus sp. is a versatile bacterial genus with demonstrated roles in the degradation of polystyrene, polypropylene, and lignocellulose. Its ability to colonize diverse habitats and participate in the transformation of recalcitrant materials highlights its ecological significance and potential applications in environmental biotechnology, particularly in addressing plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial strain studied in relation to polystyrene degradation in Galleria mellonella.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial group reported to contribute to iPP biodegradation in enrichment culture.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial species isolated from mangrove sediment and studied for its ability to degrade polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial species studied for its role in biodeterioration of polypropylene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Bacillus sp. is a bacterial species reported in previous studies as being capable of degrading polystyrene from the intestines of mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
