<node id="Sivan, A.">
  <data key="d0">Sivan, A.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Sivan, A. is a researcher and author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of the bacterium *Rhodococcus ruber*. Their work has examined both polyethylene and polystyrene, two of the most widely used synthetic polymers, and the potential for microbial processes to contribute to their breakdown.  

In 2004, Sivan, A. co-authored a study that investigated the colonization, biofilm formation, and biodegradation of polyethylene by a strain of *Rhodococcus ruber*. This research provided early evidence that the bacterium could adhere to polyethylene surfaces, form biofilms, and initiate partial degradation of the polymer, highlighting a possible biological pathway for addressing plastic persistence in the environment.  

Building on this foundation, Sivan, A. continued to explore the biodegradation potential of *Rhodococcus ruber*. In 2008, they co-authored a study on biofilm formation and the partial biodegradation of polystyrene by the same bacterium. This work demonstrated that *Rhodococcus ruber* could also colonize polystyrene, form biofilms, and contribute to its partial breakdown, extending the scope of microbial degradation research beyond polyethylene.  

Across these studies, Sivan, A. has consistently focused on the interactions between *Rhodococcus ruber* and synthetic polymers, with particular emphasis on biofilm development as a critical step in the biodegradation process. Their research has been widely referenced in the field of environmental microbiology and plastic biodegradation, contributing to the understanding of how microbial activity might be harnessed to mitigate plastic pollution.  

In summary, Sivan, A. is an author of multiple influential studies on the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene by *Rhodococcus ruber*, with key contributions including the 2004 study on polyethylene colonization and degradation and the 2008 study on polystyrene biofilm formation and partial biodegradation. These works collectively underscore the potential of microbial processes in addressing the environmental challenges posed by persistent plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Sivan, A. is an author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene by Brevibacillus borstelensis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c</data>
