<node id="Ahmed, S.">
  <data key="d0">Ahmed, S.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ahmed, S. is a researcher and author whose work focuses on the biological degradation of synthetic plastics, particularly polystyrene and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). In 2008, Ahmed, S. co-authored a review that examined the biological degradation of plastics, providing an overview of microbial and enzymatic processes involved in breaking down these persistent materials. Building on this foundation, Ahmed, S. contributed to multiple studies investigating the isolation and identification of microorganisms capable of degrading plastics. This includes research on soil-derived bacteria with the ability to biodegrade polystyrene, with publications such as a 2010 study that detailed the isolation and characterization of such bacterial strains.  

In addition to bacterial studies, Ahmed, S. has also co-authored work on the isolation and molecular characterization of fungal isolates capable of degrading PVC, highlighting the potential role of fungi in addressing plastic pollution. Collectively, Ahmed, S.’s research contributions span both bacterial and fungal systems, emphasizing the exploration of microbial diversity and biodegradation mechanisms as potential solutions to the environmental challenges posed by plastic waste. Through this body of work, Ahmed, S. has helped advance scientific understanding of plastic biodegradation and its applications in sustainable waste management.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387716</data>
</node>
