<node id="Tenebrio molitor (Mealworm Larvae)">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor (Mealworm Larvae)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor, also known as yellow mealworms, are insect larvae whose gut microbiome is studied for its ability to biodegrade plastics such as polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375004</data>
</node>
<node id="Citrobacter freundii">
  <data key="d0">Citrobacter freundii</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Citrobacter freundii is a microorganism identified within the mealworm gut microbiome that is associated with polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial species identified as the most prevalent in mixed enrichment, capable of degrading long-chain polymers, hydrocarbons, and potentially PS.&lt;SEP&gt;A gut microorganism associated with PS biodegradation identified by Brandon et al.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384528</data>
</node>
<node id="Klebsiella aerogenes">
  <data key="d0">Klebsiella aerogenes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Klebsiella aerogenes is a microorganism identified within the mealworm gut microbiome that is associated with polystyrene biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial species identified among isolates, associated with hydrocarbon degradation and bioremediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Klebsiella aerogenes is a bacterial strain identified as playing a role in polystyrene decomposition.&lt;SEP&gt;A gut microorganism associated with PS biodegradation identified by Brandon et al.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
