<node id="High Doses of Supernatant (0.5 and 1.0 mL)">
  <data key="d0">High Doses of Supernatant (0.5 and 1.0 mL)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High doses of supernatant led to rapid CO2 production in the first 24 hours, followed by stabilization, suggesting growth of fast-growing bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375026</data>
</node>
<node id="Low Doses of Supernatant (0.05 and 0.1 mL)">
  <data key="d0">Low Doses of Supernatant (0.05 and 0.1 mL)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Low doses of supernatant increased CO2 production compared to negative controls, highlighting beneficial effects on PS biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375026</data>
</node>
<node id="Fast-Growing Bacteria">
  <data key="d0">Fast-Growing Bacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Fast-growing bacteria thrived on readily available soluble carbon from high doses of supernatant or glucose, competing against PS-degrading bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375026</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-Degrading Bacteria">
  <data key="d0">PS-Degrading Bacteria</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-degrading bacteria are microbes capable of breaking down PS, but their activity was suppressed under competitive pressure from fast-growing bacteria.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375026</data>
</node>
