  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">In situ conditions refer to the natural environment inside the mealworm gut, where PS biodegradation is enhanced by emulsifying agents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375029</data>
</node>
<node id="Emulsifying Agent">
  <data key="d0">Emulsifying Agent</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">An emulsifying agent is a substance produced endogenously by the mealworm that coats hydrophobic plastic particles, increasing bioavailability and accelerating biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375029</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrophobic Pollutants">
  <data key="d0">Hydrophobic Pollutants</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrophobic pollutants, including plastics, are substances whose degradation rates can be increased by emulsifying surfactants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375029</data>
</node>
<node id="Liquid Hydrocarbons">
  <data key="d0">Liquid Hydrocarbons</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Liquid hydrocarbons are hydrophobic compounds whose remediation is enhanced by emulsifying agents produced by microorganisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375029</data>
</node>
<node id="Solid Hydrocarbons">
  <data key="d0">Solid Hydrocarbons</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Solid hydrocarbons, such as plastics, may be biodegraded more effectively by the mealworm microbiome with the aid of emulsifying agents.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375030</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-fed Mealworm Gut Microbiome">
  <data key="d0">PS-fed Mealworm Gut Microbiome</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The PS-fed mealworm gut microbiome is a microbial community extracted from mealworms that have been fed polystyrene, used in experiments to study plastic biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375030</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbial Enrichment">
  <data key="d0">Microbial Enrichment</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The microbial enrichment is a cultured microbial community derived from PS-fed mealworm gut microbiome, cultivated over several months with PS film to select for more efficient plastic-degrading bacteria and biofilm-forming microbes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375030</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Film">
  <data key="d0">PS Film</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS film is a form of polystyrene plastic used as a carbon source in microbial biodegradation experiments, particularly for biofilm formation studies.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film refers to polystyrene plastic used as the substrate for biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film was prepared from Styrofoam dissolved in xylene and used for microbial degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PS film refers to polystyrene film used as a carbon source in experiments to test bacterial degradation under anaerobic conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385641</data>
</node>
<node id="Supernatant Fractions (&lt;30 kDa, 30-100 kDa, &gt;100 kDa)">
  <data key="d0">Supernatant Fractions (&lt;30 kDa, 30-100 kDa, &gt;100 kDa)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Supernatant fractions are molecular weight-separated components of the mealworm gut microbiome supernatant, tested for their ability to enhance microbial respiration on polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375030</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbial Secreted Factors (&lt;30 kDa)">
  <data key="d0">Microbial Secreted Factors (&lt;30 kDa)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microbial secreted factors in the &lt;30 kDa fraction are substances produced by microbes that stimulate increased CO2 production during PS degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375031</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworm-produced Emulsifying Agent (30-100 kDa)">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm-produced Emulsifying Agent (30-100 kDa)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The mealworm-produced emulsifying agent in the 30-100 kDa fraction enhances microbial respiration on polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375031</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-degrading Bacterial Species">
  <data key="d0">PS-degrading Bacterial Species</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-degrading bacterial species are bacteria identified through 16S rRNA sequencing from microbial enrichment, capable of breaking down polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375031</data>
</node>
<node id="Antibiotic-treated Supernatant">
  <data key="d0">Antibiotic-treated Supernatant</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The antibiotic-treated supernatant is a version of the gut microbiome supernatant treated with antibiotics, used to test the role of microbial activity in plastic biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375031</data>
</node>
<node id="Unfractionated Supernatant">
  <data key="d0">Unfractionated Supernatant</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The unfractionated supernatant is the untreated, whole supernatant from the gut microbiome, used as a control in experiments to compare with fractionated samples.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375031</data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR Spectra">
  <data key="d0">FTIR Spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR spectra are infrared spectroscopy measurements used to analyze chemical changes in PS films after microbial degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra were used to identify absorbance peaks corresponding to decomposition products such as carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) spectra were used to identify functional groups in frass from PS- and CS-fed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;NMR spectra were used to analyze functional groups and chemical changes in residual polymers from frass.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR spectra were used to study oxidation and depolymerization of Styrofoam to frass in larvae guts.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384515</data>
</node>
<node id="Polymer Surface Chemistry">
  <data key="d0">Polymer Surface Chemistry</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymer surface chemistry refers to the chemical properties of PS films that change during microbial degradation, analyzed using FTIR and contact angle analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375032</data>
</node>
<node id="Mass Loss">
  <data key="d0">Mass Loss</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mass loss refers to the reduction in PS film weight after microbial degradation, used as an indicator of biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mass loss is a measurement parameter used in the experiment to evaluate the degradation of polystyrene materials over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379493</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 3a">
  <data key="d0">Figure 3a</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3a shows experimental results comparing CO2 production from different supernatant fractions.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3a presents data comparing molecular weights (Mₙ and Mₓ) of PS feedstock and frass samples from larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3a shows the relative abundance of bacterial phyla in the gut microbiome of mealworms under different diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387687</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 3b">
  <data key="d0">Figure 3b</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 3b shows experimental results comparing CO2 production between microbial enrichment and gut microbiome on PS film and microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure 3b shows alterations in the gut microbiome at the family level after feeding PS polymers of different molecular weights.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387687</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure S3">
  <data key="d0">Figure S3</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure S3 shows the formation of a stable biofilm on PS film during microbial enrichment.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure S3 presents data on PGF values and plastic mineralization in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure S3 presents rarefaction curves used to confirm amplicon quality in the gut microbial analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387686</data>
</node>
