<node id="S.-S. Yang">
  <data key="d0">S.-S. Yang</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">S.-S. Yang is a researcher and academic author recognized for contributions to the study of plastic biodegradation, particularly focusing on the role of insects in this process. Yang has authored and co-authored multiple scientific publications examining the biodegradation of polystyrene wastes in yellow mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae). This body of work includes investigations into the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms and its role in facilitating the breakdown of synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyester. Through these studies, Yang has contributed to advancing understanding of insect-mediated plastic degradation and the microbial mechanisms underlying this process.  

In addition to experimental research, S.-S. Yang has also published bibliometric analyses of scientific literature, reflecting an interest in mapping and evaluating research trends in biodegradation and related fields. Yang’s work has appeared in peer-reviewed journals, including *Science of the Total Environment*, where Yang is listed as one of the contributing authors.  

S.-S. Yang frequently serves as a corresponding author for these studies, with contact information provided through an institutional email address at Harbin Institute of Technology (hit.edu.cn), specifically shanshanyang@hit.edu.cn. This role underscores Yang’s leadership and coordination in collaborative research projects.  

Overall, S.-S. Yang is an active scholar whose research integrates microbiology, environmental science, and materials degradation, with a particular emphasis on the ecological and biotechnological potential of insect gut microbiomes in addressing plastic waste challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;S.-S. Yang is one of the authors of the study published in Science of the Total Environment.&lt;SEP&gt;S.-S. Yang is an author of multiple studies on biodegradation of polystyrene in mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Co-author of a study on biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures in mealworms and effects on the gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;S.-S. Yang is an author of a study on biochar generation using frass of yellow mealworms fed with wheat straw.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c</data>
