<node id="Fig. 2">
  <data key="d0">Fig. 2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">**Summary of Fig. 2**  

Fig. 2 is a composite figure that presents multiple experimental results related to the feeding, physiology, and biodegradation capabilities of mealworms and superworms under different dietary conditions. It encompasses several aspects of the research, including microbial, biochemical, and material degradation analyses. Specifically, Fig. 2 illustrates the division of mealworms into four experimental diet groups and compares bacterial counts across various breeding groups of larvae and imagines subjected to different feeding regimes. It also presents protein content measurements in mealworms fed with diverse food sources or subjected to starvation, highlighting nutritional impacts.  

In addition to nutritional and microbial data, Fig. 2 provides visual and analytical evidence of plastic degradation processes. It shows the morphology of foam plastics after five days of incubation with superworm or yellow mealworm larvae, offering insight into physical changes in the material. The figure further depicts depolymerization and biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) within larvae, including detailed molecular weight and fraction distribution profiles, which demonstrate the extent of polymer breakdown. Complementing this, it presents δ15N values of Styrofoam-eating and antibiotic-treated mealworms from six different sources, providing isotopic evidence of dietary assimilation and microbial involvement in plastic degradation.  

Moreover, Fig. 2 includes microscopic images of twelve bacterial cells selected for evaluating their potential in polystyrene degradation, thereby linking larval gut microbiota to observed biodegradation phenomena. Collectively, Fig. 2 integrates nutritional, microbial, isotopic, and material science data to illustrate how mealworms and superworms interact with conventional and synthetic diets, particularly plastics, and how these interactions influence both the organisms and the degradation of polymeric materials.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387876</data>
</node>
