<node id="Strain NyZ450">
  <data key="d0">Strain NyZ450</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain NyZ450 is identified as Acinetobacter sp., a bacterial strain tested for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) mulching films. It was cultured in LCFBM medium and showed partial degradation effects on PE when tested individually.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain NyZ450 is a bacterial strain identified as the major player in the co-culture grown on polyethylene (PE) mulching films. It is responsible for breaking down PE and utilizing it predominantly as a carbon source.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375683</data>
</node>
<node id="Strain NyZ451">
  <data key="d0">Strain NyZ451</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Strain NyZ451 is identified as Bacillus sp., a bacterial strain tested for its ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) mulching films. It was cultured in LCFBM medium and showed different effects on PE molecular weight when tested individually.&lt;SEP&gt;Strain NyZ451 is a bacterial strain that plays an auxiliary role in the degradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching films. It has a relatively limited ability to break chemical bonds of PE but contributes to the consortium's overall degradation ability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375683</data>
</node>
<node id="Consortium of Strains NyZ450 and NyZ451">
  <data key="d0">Consortium of Strains NyZ450 and NyZ451</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The consortium is a combination of Acinetobacter sp. strain NyZ450 and Bacillus sp. strain NyZ451. Together, they were able to grow on PE particles, form biofilms, and significantly degrade PE mulching films, unlike when tested individually.&lt;SEP&gt;The consortium is a co-culture of strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 that together degrade polyethylene (PE) mulching films more effectively than either strain alone. Their cooperation suggests complementary catabolic pathways or enhanced capability of activating recalcitrant substrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375683</data>
</node>
