  <data key="d0">Kontich, Belgium</data>
  <data key="d1">geo</data>
  <data key="d2">Kontich, Belgium, is the location where the Amphibia hobby shop provided larvae for the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375867</data>
</node>
<node id="Carrefour">
  <data key="d0">Carrefour</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Carrefour is a supermarket chain that provided commercial LDPE fruit bags used as substrates in the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375867</data>
</node>
<node id="Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR)">
  <data key="d0">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical method used in Bombelli et al. (2017) to detect ethylene glycol, though its results were questioned by Weber et al. (2017).&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR analysis was used to detect new functional groups and carboxyl peaks during plastic biodegradation experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756376349</data>
</node>
<node id="High-temperature gel permeation chromatography">
  <data key="d0">High-temperature gel permeation chromatography</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High-temperature gel permeation chromatography is an analytical method used by Brandon et al. (2018) to determine molecular weight shifts in degraded polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756375868</data>
</node>
<node id="Proteins">
  <data key="d0">Proteins</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Proteins were suggested by Weber et al. (2017) as possible residues that could explain FTIR results attributed to ethylene glycol.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrients absent in Styrofoam but present in bran, necessary for mealworm growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are biochemical compounds measured in larvae, positively correlated with the PS diet in PCA analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins were one of the macromolecules biochemically assessed in mealworms fed with polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are biochemical compounds in mealworms that were measured in the study, with levels not significantly affected by food type.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are compounds that undergo thermal degradation over a broad range of temperatures, as noted in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Proteins are part of the body biomass digested by starved mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387689</data>
</node>
