<node id="Curr. Biol.">
  <data key="d0">Curr. Biol.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Current Biology (abbreviated as *Curr. Biol.*) is a scientific journal that has served as the publication venue for significant research on plastic biodegradation. Notably, it published the study conducted by Bombelli, Howe, and Bertocchini, which demonstrated the ability of wax moth caterpillars (*Galleria mellonella*) to biodegrade polyethylene, one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics. This research highlighted the potential of biological organisms, specifically the larvae of the wax moth, to contribute to solutions for plastic waste management. Through this publication, *Curr. Biol.* played a key role in disseminating findings that advanced scientific understanding of polyethylene degradation and its possible applications in addressing global plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;An academic journal where research on polyethylene biodegradation by caterpillars was published.&lt;SEP&gt;Current Biology is the journal where the study on polyethylene biodegradation by Galleria mellonella caterpillars was published in 2017.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387179</data>
</node>
