<node id="Gut Microbiota">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microbiota</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gut microbiota refers to the diverse community of microorganisms, primarily bacteria, inhabiting the digestive systems of insects such as mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor*), superworms, and wax moth larvae (*Galleria mellonella*). This microbial ecosystem plays a critical role in both conventional digestion and the biodegradation of otherwise recalcitrant materials, including synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS), Styrofoam, polypropylene (PP), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyethylene microplastics, as well as natural feedstocks like corn straw.  

Within the larval gut, the gut microbiota contributes to the breakdown of plastics through the secretion of extracellular enzymes that catalyze depolymerization, converting complex polymer fragments into simpler, more metabolizable compounds. This enzymatic activity enables insect larvae to survive on plastic-based diets, a capacity that is strongly linked to the presence and composition of their gut microbial communities. In addition to facilitating plastic degradation, gut microbiota also supports the digestion of organic matter, thereby influencing host nutrition, survival, and phenotypic traits.  

The composition and activity of gut microbiota are dynamic and influenced by external factors such as diet, environmental conditions, and exposure to antibiotics. For example, antibiotic treatment can suppress microbial populations, significantly reducing or eliminating the larvae’s ability to degrade plastics and other substrates. Conversely, dietary adaptation, such as exposure to LDPE, can alter the microbial community structure, enhancing the larvae’s survival and metabolic capacity on plastic diets.  

Research on gut microbiota in insects like *Tenebrio molitor* and superworms highlights its unique ecological and environmental significance. By enabling the biodegradation of persistent synthetic polymers, these microbial communities provide a natural mechanism for reducing plastic waste. This function positions insect gut microbiota as a promising biological system with potential applications in waste management, plastic bioremediation, and sustainable recycling strategies.  

In summary, the gut microbiota in insect larvae represents a specialized microbial consortium that not only supports host digestion but also drives the biodegradation of plastics such as PS, PP, LDPE, and polyethylene microplastics. Its activity is essential for larval survival on unconventional diets, is modulated by environmental and dietary factors, and holds significant promise for addressing global challenges related to plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms in the digestive tract of mealworms, which play an essential role in the biodegradation of plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to the microbial community in the digestive system of mealworms and other organisms, including PS-degrading bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiota refers to microbial communities in organisms, studied for changes during plastic biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Refers to microbial communities in insect and termite digestive systems studied in multiple works.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173</data>
