<node id="Mealworm (Tenebrio molitor L.)">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm (Tenebrio molitor L.)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm larvae are insects studied for their ability to consume and metabolize various waste materials such as polystyrene, polyethylene, and lignocellulose. Their growth, metabolism, and gut microbiome are influenced by these diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377803</data>
</node>
<node id="Oatmeal">
  <data key="d0">Oatmeal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oatmeal was used as the control diet for mealworm larvae, providing balanced nutrition and serving as the baseline for comparison with waste-based diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as the control food for mealworms in the experiment.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as the control food source for the larvae in the biodegradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was the food provided to the control group of mealworms, serving as a standard diet for comparison.&lt;SEP&gt;Oatmeal was used as a control food for mealworms, resulting in the highest lipid content compared to other feeding conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387867</data>
</node>
<node id="Digestive Microbiome">
  <data key="d0">Digestive Microbiome</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The digestive microbiome of mealworm larvae changes depending on the diet, with specific microbial cultures and protozoa forming to support digestion and survival.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377803</data>
</node>
<node id="FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy)">
  <data key="d0">FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">FTIR is a method used to analyze frass and detect chemical modifications in polystyrene consumed by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;FTIR is an analytical technique used to characterize the chemical structure of PS-fed and PE-fed frass compared to original foams.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387098</data>
</node>
<node id="Gas-chromatography–mass-spectrometry Untargeted Screening Method">
  <data key="d0">Gas-chromatography–mass-spectrometry Untargeted Screening Method</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">This method is used to detect plastics such as polystyrene in insect products and assess their degree of degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377803</data>
</node>
