  <data key="d0">Biometrical Parameters</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Biometrical parameters include measurements such as survival rate, larval mass, pupal counts, and adult emergence of Tenebrio molitor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="Metagenomes">
  <data key="d0">Metagenomes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Metagenomes were compared statistically in the study using Fisher's exact test and the Newcombe-Wilson method.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The experiment lasted 45 days and measured the growth, survival, and development of Tenebrio molitor under different feed types.&lt;SEP&gt;The experiment involved feeding mealworms different types of polystyrene materials and measuring their biochemical responses, as well as material degradation parameters.&lt;SEP&gt;The experiment involved feeding superworms, greater wax moths, and yellow mealworms with PS and natural feeds (bran, beeswax) to study survival, weight change, and evidence of biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384513</data>
</node>
<node id="OAT (Oatmeal Diet)">
  <data key="d0">OAT (Oatmeal Diet)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">OAT refers to the oatmeal diet group used in the experiment on Tenebrio molitor larvae. It was characterized by the lowest protein content (22.9%), highest fatty acid content (14%), fastest metamorphosis, moderate mortality, and fewer dead insects compared to other diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="CEL (Cardboard Diet)">
  <data key="d0">CEL (Cardboard Diet)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">CEL refers to the cardboard diet group used in the experiment. It showed similarities in fatty acid composition with PE groups and produced a high number of adults after 45 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-oxo (Oxo-degradable Polyethylene Diet)">
  <data key="d0">PE-oxo (Oxo-degradable Polyethylene Diet)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-oxo refers to the oxo-degradable polyethylene diet group. It was characterized by high insect mortality, low metamorphosis, and fatty acid composition similar to CEL.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-reg (Polyethylene Regranulate Diet)">
  <data key="d0">PE-reg (Polyethylene Regranulate Diet)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-reg refers to the polyethylene regranulate diet group. It showed development patterns similar to OAT, had high mortality, relatively high adult production, and high pupae proportion (36% on day 45). It also suggested possible cannibalism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377825</data>
</node>
<node id="PS (Polystyrene Diet)">
  <data key="d0">PS (Polystyrene Diet)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS refers to the polystyrene diet group. It was distinct from all other groups, characterized by the highest protein content (27.2%), lowest fatty acid content (10%), high mortality, and lowest metamorphosis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Lignocellulose Diet">
  <data key="d0">Lignocellulose Diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The lignocellulose diet group showed the lowest number of dead insects, the highest number of larvae, but severe body mass loss at the last measurement. Insects on this diet had low consumption and activity, suggesting rapid metamorphosis as a stress response.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Biometry Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Biometry Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Biometry analysis compared dissimilarities in insect body measurements across different diets (OAT, PE-reg, PS, CEL, PE-oxo).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbiome Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Microbiome Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Microbiome analysis compared dissimilarities in gut microbiota of insects fed different diets (OAT, CEL, PS, PE-oxo, PE-reg).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Gene Counts Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Gene Counts Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Gene counts analysis compared dissimilarities in gene expression across insects fed different diets (PS, PE-oxo, PE-reg, OAT, CEL).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Fatty Acids Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Fatty Acids Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Fatty acids analysis compared dissimilarities in fatty acid composition across insects fed different diets (OAT, PS, CEL, PE-oxo, PE-reg).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Metabolism Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Metabolism Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Metabolism analysis compared dissimilarities in metabolic activity across insects fed different diets (PE-oxo, PE-reg, CEL, OAT, PS).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377826</data>
</node>
<node id="Oleic Acid (C18:1 c9)">
  <data key="d0">Oleic Acid (C18:1 c9)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oleic acid accounted for about 50% of fatty acid content in the PE and CEL diet groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377827</data>
</node>
<node id="Linoleic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Linoleic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Linoleic acid content was about 20% in OAT larvae and about 27% in larvae fed other diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377827</data>
</node>
<node id="Palmitic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Palmitic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Palmitic acid content was 16.8% in OAT larvae and about 12% in larvae fed other diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377827</data>
</node>
<node id="Stearic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Stearic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Stearic acid content was 3.7% in OAT larvae and 2.8% in larvae fed other diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377827</data>
</node>
<node id="Palmitoleic Acid">
  <data key="d0">Palmitoleic Acid</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Palmitoleic acid content was 2% in OAT larvae and 1.6% in larvae fed other diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377827</data>
</node>
<node id="Margaric Acid">
  <data key="d0">Margaric Acid</data>
