</node>
<node id="Rhodobacterales">
  <data key="d0">Rhodobacterales</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Rhodobacterales is a bacterial order with a mean abundance of 3.9%, highest in OAT (7.2%) and PE-oxo (5.2%) groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Rhodobacterales is a bacterial order negatively correlated with the PS diet in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;An order of bacteria associated with PE-oxo and OAT diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377834</data>
</node>
<node id="Nitrosiriplas">
  <data key="d0">Nitrosiriplas</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nitrosiriplas is a bacterial order with an average abundance of 1.5%, about fourfold higher in the PS variant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377834</data>
</node>
<node id="Planctomycetales">
  <data key="d0">Planctomycetales</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Planctomycetales is a bacterial order with an average abundance of 9.9%, about fourfold higher in the PS variant.&lt;SEP&gt;Planctomycetales is a bacterial order positively correlated with the PS diet in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;An order of bacteria associated with PS diet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377834</data>
</node>
<node id="Coriobacteriales">
  <data key="d0">Coriobacteriales</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Coriobacteriales is a bacterial order with an average abundance of 1.8%, lower in the PS group.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377834</data>
</node>
<node id="OAT diet">
  <data key="d0">OAT diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">OAT is a dietary variant associated with higher levels of Bacteroidales, Actinomycetales, Clostridiales, and Rhodobacterales.&lt;SEP&gt;The OAT diet refers to an oatmeal-based diet, considered the optimal diet for Tenebrio molitor larvae, supporting normal growth and development.&lt;SEP&gt;The OAT diet is associated with broad sugar metabolism and high fractions of Flavobacteriales, Clostridiales, Bacteroidales, and Rhodobacterales.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377836</data>
</node>
<node id="CEL diet">
  <data key="d0">CEL diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">CEL is a dietary variant associated with higher levels of Selenomonadales and Bacteroidales.&lt;SEP&gt;The CEL diet refers to a cardboard-based diet, which caused starvation and lower developmental transition rates in Tenebrio molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;The CEL diet is a dietary variant where microbiota switched from glucose metabolism to adipic acid consumption, combined with acetoin and urease production.&lt;SEP&gt;The CEL diet is one of the diets tested in the study, producing unique features and correlations in T. molitor development.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377836</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-oxo diet">
  <data key="d0">PE-oxo diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-oxo is a dietary variant associated with higher levels of Lactobacillales, Enterobacteriales, Rhodobacterales, and Entomoplasmatales, but lower Clostridiales.&lt;SEP&gt;The PE-oxo diet refers to an oxo-degradable polyethylene-based diet given to Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with starvation and disrupted development.&lt;SEP&gt;The PE-oxo diet is characterized by urease activity and the uptake of trisde citrate, D-sorbitol, and decanoic acid. It is associated with Rhodobacterales, Lactobacillales, Entomoplasmatales, and Enterobacteriales.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-oxo is a polyethylene-based diet variant characterized by high nifH and nirS gene abundance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377836</data>
</node>
<node id="PE-reg diet">
  <data key="d0">PE-reg diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE-reg is a dietary variant associated with higher levels of Rhizobiales, Lactobacillales, Enterobacteriales, and Pseudomonadales.&lt;SEP&gt;The PE-reg diet refers to a polyethylene regranulate-based diet, which provided favorable development conditions for Tenebrio molitor larvae, though with increased cannibalism.&lt;SEP&gt;The PE-reg diet is associated with a wide uptake of acids and sugars, and microbiota such as Rhizobiales and Clostridiales, indicating complex aerobic and anaerobic processes.&lt;SEP&gt;PE-reg is a polyethylene-based diet variant characterized by high nirS gene abundance.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377836</data>
</node>
<node id="PS diet">
  <data key="d0">PS diet</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS is a dietary variant associated with higher levels of Nitrosiriplas, Nitrosomonadales, Planctomycetales, and Lactobacillales, but lower Coriobacteriales, Burkholderiales, and Enterobacteriales.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS diet refers to a polystyrene-based diet fed to Tenebrio molitor larvae, influencing protein levels, nitrogen metabolism, and microbial composition.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS diet is a protein-deficient substrate that influences microbiota composition and metabolism, including nitrogen conversion and amino acid synthesis.&lt;SEP&gt;PS diet refers to feeding conditions where polystyrene (PS) is included in the insect diet.&lt;SEP&gt;PS diet refers to a polystyrene-based diet fed to T. molitor larvae, which may cause insufficient food supply and toxic effects.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388216</data>
</node>
<node id="amoA gene">
  <data key="d0">amoA gene</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The amoA gene is responsible for nitrogen metabolism and showed high load in PS diet conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The amoA gene encodes ammonium monooxygenase and was abundant in CEL and PS groups, indicating nitrification potential.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377837</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 1B">
  <data key="d0">Figure 1B</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 1B is a referenced figure showing the structure of the bacterial microbiome at the row level, with dissimilarity at 0.34.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377837</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5a">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5a</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5a is a principal component analysis (PCA) plot testing correlations between diets and parameters such as insect development, protein and fatty acid concentrations, and microbial presence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377837</data>
</node>
<node id="Imagines">
  <data key="d0">Imagines</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Imagines refer to the adult stage of Tenebrio molitor, negatively correlated with the PS diet in PCA analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imagines are adult insects that incur higher physiological and genetic costs in contaminated environments but tolerate pollutants.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377839</data>
</node>
<node id="Mancini et al. (2019)">
  <data key="d0">Mancini et al. (2019)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mancini et al. (2019) reported that a high-protein diet resulted in low final larval mass in Tenebrio molitor.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756377840</data>
</node>
<node id="Yang et al. (2018a)">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al. (2018a)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang et al. (2018a) reported that Tenebrio molitor can consume polystyrene, with the second generation degrading it more efficiently.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2018a) is cited as a research group or authors who showed that feeding mealworms with a mixture of cereal bran and polystyrene/polyethylene improved larval survival and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced study related to EPS consumption and degradation by mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2018a) is a cited research group or author reference for antibiotic suppression tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2018a) refers to a study that reported 46% PS consumption after 32 days for 120 larvae and survival rates of 86.7%. It also showed higher PS consumption with bran and soy protein supplementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2018a) are cited researchers who found that larvae fed with PS and bran supplementation could complete their life cycle.&lt;SEP&gt;Yang et al. (2018a) refers to a research study cited in the text that analyzed biomass changes in larvae fed with PS over 32 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f65bf081bdd3c6718fa0310993bfc0d</data>
