<node id="Wang, Y.">
  <data key="d0">Wang, Y.</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, Y. is a researcher whose work lies at the intersection of microbiology, environmental science, and bioinformatics, with a strong emphasis on insect–microbe interactions, plastic biodegradation, and the ecological impacts of microplastics. A central theme of Wang, Y.’s research is the investigation of insect larvae and their gut microbiota as potential agents for mitigating plastic pollution. This includes studies on *Tenebrio molitor* (yellow mealworm) and *Zophobas aratus* (superworm) larvae, focusing on their ability to ingest and degrade synthetic polymers, as well as their preferences for different types of plastics. These studies highlight the potential of insect-based systems as a biological solution to plastic waste management.  

Complementing this work, Wang, Y. has co-authored multiple studies on the gut-associated bacterial and fungal communities of insects, including *Bactrocera minax* and *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. A notable 2015 study provided early insights into the microbial symbioses that may facilitate plastic digestion and nutrient assimilation, underscoring the role of gut microbiota in enabling insects to process otherwise recalcitrant materials.  

Wang, Y. has also contributed significantly to research on the toxicological and ecological effects of plastics. This includes studies on the harmful impacts of polystyrene microplastics and phthalates on zebrafish larvae, as well as investigations into the effects of polystyrene nanoplastics on duckweed and the influence of microplastics on gut microbiome composition. Additional work has examined the adsorption of oxytetracycline by microplastics and the mixture effects of emerging organic contaminants on cytotoxicity, thereby broadening the understanding of how plastic pollution and associated chemicals affect aquatic organisms and ecosystems.  

Beyond experimental research, Wang, Y. has engaged in computational and bioinformatics tool development. As a co-author of a study on Majorbio Cloud, a platform for multi-omics data analysis, Wang, Y. has contributed to advancing data-driven approaches that support microbiome, environmental, and toxicological research.  

Overall, Wang, Y.’s scholarly contributions integrate laboratory experimentation with computational innovation. By exploring insect–microbe symbioses, plastic biodegradation pathways, and the toxicological consequences of microplastics, Wang, Y. has advanced both the scientific understanding of plastic pollution and the potential for biological and computational strategies to address pressing environmental challenges.</data>
