<node id="PP Foam">
  <data key="d0">PP Foam</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Commercial PP foam is a polypropylene-based material confirmed to be biodegraded by T. molitor and Z. atratus.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam with a molecular weight of 356.00 kDa was studied in a previous experiment with T. molitor, where Kluyvera sp. was associated with its biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam is a polypropylene-based material referenced for comparison with mask layer consumption by yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam ingestion by insect worms was associated with an increase of Enterobacteriaceae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam is a form of polypropylene tested as a diet for larvae, with survival rate results compared to unfed larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam was used as a control in the biodegradation experiments to compare against the ingested polypropylene processed by larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PP foam is a form of polypropylene compared with residual PP polymers extracted from frass to assess depolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385272</data>
</node>
<node id="Host Gene Expression">
  <data key="d0">Host Gene Expression</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Host gene expression refers to the genetic response of the larvae to different molecular weights of PP during biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378153</data>
