<node id="WB">
  <data key="d0">WB</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">WB, or Wheat Bran, is described in the study as a standard feed and control diet used in experiments involving *Tenebrio molitor* larvae. It was obtained from the same farm as the larvae, located in Dezhou, Shandong, China, ensuring consistency in experimental conditions. WB is characterized as a nutrient-rich food source that provides essential nitrogen and protein nutrition, supporting the normal growth, development, and life cycle of the larvae.  

In the experimental design, WB served as the baseline or control diet against which alternative feeding conditions, such as polypropylene (PP)-fed larvae and unfed larvae, were compared. This allowed researchers to evaluate the effects of unconventional diets, such as plastic-based feeding, relative to a natural and nutritionally adequate food source. WB was consumed by the larvae over an 11-day period, during which it functioned as the reference condition for assessing physiological and microbial responses.  

Additionally, WB was included as one of the feeding conditions in the gut microbial analysis, although the study did not provide detailed elaboration on its specific microbial outcomes. Its role was primarily to represent a normal, protein-rich diet in contrast to experimental diets that lacked conventional nutritional value.  

Overall, WB (Wheat Bran) is presented as the standard, nutrient-dense control diet in the study, serving as a critical reference point for evaluating the impact of alternative feeding regimes on *T. molitor* larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;WB refers to wheat bran, used as a dietary component in larval feeding experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;WB is a normal feedstock for yellow mealworms and superworms, providing all necessary nutrients for their life cycle.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385257</data>
</node>
<node id="SPCR (Specific PP Consumption Rate)">
  <data key="d0">SPCR (Specific PP Consumption Rate)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">SPCR stands for specific PP consumption rate, calculated based on the weight of digested polymer over the test period (32 days), divided by consumption days and the initial number of larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378162</data>
