<node id="PDI (Polydispersity Index)">
  <data key="d0">PDI (Polydispersity Index)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PDI is the polydispersity index of polypropylene, used to describe the distribution of molecular weights in the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;PDI is a measure of heterogeneity in chain-length distributions of polymers, which increased during PS biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PDI is a measure of molecular weight distribution in polymers, which increased in PS and PVC residues after mealworm digestion, indicating random internal scission.&lt;SEP&gt;PDI is a parameter determined by GPC analysis to characterize the distribution of molecular weights in PS samples.&lt;SEP&gt;PDI is a measure of heterogeneity in polymer chain length distribution, which increased in all tested PS polymers after biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387678</data>
</node>
<node id="δ13C Stable Isotope Signature">
  <data key="d0">δ13C Stable Isotope Signature</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">δ13C stable isotope signature was used to analyze isotopic fractionation in pristine PP versus PP residues in frass after larval digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;δ13C stable isotope signature was used to confirm biodegradation of PET by comparing residual PET with virgin PET MPs.&lt;SEP&gt;δ13C stable isotope signature was used to compare residual PS and LDPE with original foams, providing chemical characterization of biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
