<node id="PP polymers">
  <data key="d0">PP polymers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polypropylene (PP) polymers of varying molecular weights (MW 0.83 to 575 kDa) are the plastics fed to T. molitor larvae to study biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378172</data>
</node>
<node id="Monoxygenase">
  <data key="d0">Monoxygenase</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Monoxygenase is an enzyme enriched in PP-fed larvae, involved in PP oxidation and degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378173</data>
</node>
<node id="Peroxidase">
  <data key="d0">Peroxidase</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Peroxidase is an enzyme enriched in PP-fed larvae, contributing to PP oxidation and degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;An oxidase enzyme identified in transcriptomic analysis, related to lignocellulose and PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxidase is an enzyme that contributes to aromatic benzene ring cleavage and lignin degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Peroxidase is a degradative enzyme upregulated during PS biodegradation, active against oxidized PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385650</data>
</node>
<node id="MRPs">
  <data key="d0">MRPs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">MRPs are transporters activated in larvae to efflux plastics within cells, reducing metabolic pressure and aiding in adaptation to PP diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378173</data>
</node>
<node id="Plastic biodegradation-related pathways">
  <data key="d0">Plastic biodegradation-related pathways</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Plastic biodegradation-related pathways are metabolic processes activated in larvae to accelerate PP degradation and metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378174</data>
</node>
<node id="Polyolefin degradation oxidases">
  <data key="d0">Polyolefin degradation oxidases</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polyolefin degradation oxidases include enzymes such as monoxygenase, laccase, and peroxidase, which are enriched in PP-fed larvae and play a role in PP oxidation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378174</data>
</node>
<node id="Commercial PP products">
  <data key="d0">Commercial PP products</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Commercial PP products are polypropylene materials with molecular weights ranging from 120.00 to 550.00 kDa, which the study suggests can be biodegraded by T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378174</data>
</node>
<node id="Volcano plots">
  <data key="d0">Volcano plots</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Volcano plots are graphical representations used in the study to show differentially expressed genes (DEGs) between PP-fed and WB-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Volcano plots were used to visualize differential expression of genes (DEGs) in response to PS and CS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379159</data>
</node>
<node id="Differentially expressed genes (DEGs)">
  <data key="d0">Differentially expressed genes (DEGs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) are genes that were either up-regulated or down-regulated in T. molitor larvae depending on the PP diet.&lt;SEP&gt;DEGs are genes showing differences in expression levels in response to PS, CS, and CB diets.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379159</data>
</node>
<node id="Innate digestive enzymes">
  <data key="d0">Innate digestive enzymes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Innate digestive enzymes are genes up-regulated in larvae fed with PP, associated with digestion and adaptation to nutrient-deficient conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378175</data>
</node>
<node id="Signal transduction">
  <data key="d0">Signal transduction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Signal transduction refers to gene functions up-regulated in larvae as an adaptive response to nutrient-deficient conditions induced by PP diets.&lt;SEP&gt;Signal transduction is described as a process linked to the two-component system in gut flora during PE degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380188</data>
</node>
<node id="Bioemulsifying factors">
  <data key="d0">Bioemulsifying factors</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bioemulsifying factors are secreted by both host larvae and gut microbes, helping enzymes attach to PP polymers and accelerate biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378176</data>
</node>
<node id="Transmembrane proteins">
  <data key="d0">Transmembrane proteins</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmembrane proteins transport degraded PP molecules into microbial cells for metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756378176</data>
</node>
<node id="Nitrogen fixation">
  <data key="d0">Nitrogen fixation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nitrogen fixation is a process carried out by gut microbes, fixing ingested nitrogen gas for protein synthesis needed by larvae and microbiota.&lt;SEP&gt;A biological process studied in 2022 in plastic-eating mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Nitrogen fixation is the biological process demonstrated in mealworms, where atmospheric nitrogen is converted into usable forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385998</data>
</node>
<node id="Δδ13C">
  <data key="d0">Δδ13C</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Δδ13C is a measurement used to assess PP biodegradation by comparing carbon isotope values before and after degradation.</data>
