<node id="Oxidative Stress">
  <data key="d0">Oxidative Stress</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Oxidative stress is a physiological and biochemical condition observed in insect larvae, particularly in species such as mealworms (Tenebrio larvae) and black soldier fly (BSF) larvae, when exposed to plastics and microplastics including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). It arises as a consequence of plastic ingestion and biodegradation, during which reactive oxygen species (ROS) are generated. The accumulation of ROS leads to cellular imbalance, triggering oxidative damage and activating stress response pathways.  

In experimental studies, oxidative stress in larvae has been assessed through multiple biomarkers, including ROS levels, antioxidant enzyme activities such as superoxide dismutase (SOD), and lipid peroxidation indicators like malondialdehyde (MDA). Elevated homocysteine levels and disruptions in nucleotide metabolism have also been reported as indicators of oxidative stress in BSF larvae exposed to plastics. These findings suggest that plastic ingestion not only induces free radical formation but also alters metabolic processes and upregulates cellular defense mechanisms.  

Although oxidative stress has been consistently identified as a physiological response to plastic exposure, some studies note that its full extent and long-term effects have not been comprehensively assessed. Nonetheless, the evidence indicates that oxidative stress represents a key biological response to microplastic ingestion in larvae, reflecting both immediate cellular damage and broader metabolic alterations.  

In summary, oxidative stress in the context of plastic exposure refers to the imbalance between ROS production and antioxidant defenses in insect larvae, leading to physiological stress, enzyme activity changes, and metabolic disruptions. It serves as a critical indicator of the biological impacts of microplastic ingestion and biodegradation in these organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;A condition induced in mealworms by PS ingestion, leading to metabolic dysbiosis, antioxidant system damage, and immune responses.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387694</data>
</node>
