<node id="E. coli">
  <data key="d0">E. coli</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">E. coli is a widely studied bacterial strain that serves multiple roles in experimental research, particularly in studies involving biodegradation, biofilm formation, and microbial growth dynamics. In biodegradation experiments, E. coli has been genetically engineered to overexpress the gene lac640, which has been associated with the ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) and lignin. This modification was tested to evaluate its potential in breaking down synthetic and natural polymers, including PS and corn stover (CS). However, in its unmodified form, E. coli is frequently employed as a negative control strain, particularly in experiments assessing biofilm production, adhesion, and degradation of polystyrene films. In these contexts, E. coli does not exhibit degradation activity, making it a reliable baseline for comparison with other microorganisms or engineered strains.  

Beyond biodegradation studies, E. coli is also used in comparative analyses of biofilm behavior, especially in relation to nanoplastics (NPS). Its role as a reference organism allows researchers to evaluate how different bacterial strains interact with plastic surfaces and form biofilms under controlled conditions. Additionally, E. coli is a standard reference in microbial growth studies, with a documented doubling time of approximately 68.3 minutes in LB medium during the exponential growth phase. This consistent growth rate provides a benchmark for comparing the proliferation of other bacterial strains under similar conditions.  

Overall, E. coli functions both as a model organism and as a control strain in diverse experimental frameworks. Its applications range from serving as a baseline in biofilm and adhesion studies to acting as a host for genetic modifications aimed at enhancing polymer degradation. This dual role underscores its importance in microbiological research, where it provides both a reliable reference point and a versatile platform for testing novel genetic and metabolic functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa1d3c9710e10c5eb533d8d791c93e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386820</data>
</node>
