<node id="Vancomycin">
  <data key="d0">Vancomycin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Vancomycin is an antibiotic used in experiments to eliminate gut microbes of larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is an antibiotic tested for its inhibitory effect on larvae gut flora.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is one of the antibiotics used in the suppression treatment assay.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is one of the six antibiotics tested for its ability to inhibit mealworm gut bacteria.&lt;SEP&gt;Vancomycin is an antibiotic tested in inhibition halo tests on larval gut microbes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386515</data>
</node>
<node id="Ampicillin">
  <data key="d0">Ampicillin</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Ampicillin is an antibiotic that has been widely employed in experimental studies to investigate its effects on the gut microbiota of insect larvae, particularly mealworms. It has been tested for its ability to inhibit or suppress gut bacteria, making it a useful tool in experiments designed to eliminate or reduce microbial populations within the larval digestive tract. Ampicillin has been included in suppression tests and treatment assays aimed at evaluating the role of gut microbes in larval physiology and development. In these contexts, it is often one of several antibiotics—specifically one of six—that are systematically tested for their inhibitory effects on gut flora. Experimental approaches have included inhibition halo tests, where ampicillin demonstrated the capacity to suppress or inhibit bacterial growth associated with larval gut communities. Overall, ampicillin is consistently described as an effective antibiotic for targeting and reducing gut microbial populations in larvae, thereby serving as a key agent in studies focused on host–microbe interactions and microbial suppression in insect models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756386518</data>
</node>
<node id="Tryptic Soy Agar (TSA)">
  <data key="d0">Tryptic Soy Agar (TSA)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">TSA medium was used to culture bacterial suspensions for 24 hours to count gut bacteria colonies.&lt;SEP&gt;TSA is a medium used for cultivating gut microbes during the antibiotic suppression test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385257</data>
</node>
