<node id="Gut microbiomes">
  <data key="d0">Gut microbiomes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial communities in mealworms that synergistically biodegrade PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources.&lt;SEP&gt;Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial communities in the digestive systems of larvae, which showed significant differences between superworms and yellow mealworms during PS and PU biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381577</data>
</node>
<node id="Host genes">
  <data key="d0">Host genes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Host genes in mealworms are upregulated for PET depolymerization and metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379693</data>
</node>
<node id="Biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor">
  <data key="d0">Biodegradation of PET by Tenebrio molitor</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The study investigated the biodegradation of high-crystallinity PET by mealworms, focusing on polymer chain size, gut metabolome, and host genes.</data>
