</node>
<node id="Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus 1758">
  <data key="d0">Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus 1758</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Tenebrio molitor, also known as yellow mealworm, is the larval form of a beetle species. It is found worldwide, naturally inhabiting forests with rotting wood, and has been commercialized as animal feed. It can biodegrade multiple plastics including PS, PE, PVC, PP, PUR, PLA, and PET.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus 1758 is the scientific name of the yellow mealworm, a larva capable of biodegrading plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387076</data>
</node>
<node id="Wheat bran (WB)">
  <data key="d0">Wheat bran (WB)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Wheat bran is mentioned in the text, but no further description is provided.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is a common feed for insect larvae, used as a control diet in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural feedstock without pesticides or antibiotics, used as a diet for larvae in the experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran (WB) was used as a co-diet feedstock for larvae, enhancing PP consumption.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
