  <data key="d0">Water-soluble fraction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The water-soluble fraction of larval frass contained hydrophilic digested organic substances and salts, which increased over the 36-day test.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379729</data>
</node>
<node id="Ethanol-extracted fraction">
  <data key="d0">Ethanol-extracted fraction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The ethanol-extracted fraction of larval frass contained lipophilic substances, accounting for 2.88% to 3.99% of the frass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379729</data>
</node>
<node id="Residual solid content">
  <data key="d0">Residual solid content</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Residual solid content in larval frass increased from 33.26% to 60.12% over 36 days and included enzymes, microorganisms, insoluble metabolized components, and inorganic residues.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379730</data>
</node>
<node id="Inorganic residues">
  <data key="d0">Inorganic residues</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Inorganic residues were part of the residual solid fraction in larval frass after PET degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379730</data>
</node>
<node id="Bacterial culture">
  <data key="d0">Bacterial culture</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Bacterial culture refers to in vitro microbial PET degradation systems, used as a comparison to larval degradation efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379730</data>
</node>
<node id="High-crystallinity PET">
  <data key="d0">High-crystallinity PET</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">High-crystallinity PET (18.00%) was used in larval degradation tests, showing much faster degradation compared to bacterial culture.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379731</data>
