  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379732</data>
</node>
<node id="PET1100">
  <data key="d0">PET1100</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET1100 represents low molecular weight PET polymers (1.10 kDa) tested for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET1100 is a polyethylene terephthalate (PET) sample with low molecular weight that was almost completely degraded by T. molitor larvae, showing high biodegradability.&lt;SEP&gt;PET1100 is a PET-fed group where low abundance of transportation and chemotaxis-related genes was observed, possibly due to higher digestive ability of larvae toward low molecular weight polymers.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379732</data>
</node>
<node id="PET27100">
  <data key="d0">PET27100</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET27100 represents medium molecular weight PET polymers (27.10 kDa) tested for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET27100 is a polyethylene terephthalate (PET) sample with medium molecular weight, representative of most commercial PET and polyester products, which underwent partial biodegradation by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET27100 is a PET-fed group where genes involved in lipid metabolism and nitrogen metabolism were abundant.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379732</data>
</node>
<node id="PET63500">
  <data key="d0">PET63500</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PET63500 represents high molecular weight PET polymers (63.50 kDa) tested for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is a polyethylene terephthalate (PET) sample with high molecular weight that showed lower biodegradability compared to PET1100 and PET27100 when fed to T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;PET63500 is one of the PET-fed groups used in the metagenome sequencing analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379733</data>
</node>
