  <data key="d0">Antibiotic treatment group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The antibiotic treatment group refers to larvae fed PET under antibiotic suppression to test the role of gut microbes in PET depolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379733</data>
</node>
<node id="Non-antibiotic treatment group">
  <data key="d0">Non-antibiotic treatment group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The non-antibiotic treatment group refers to larvae fed PET without antibiotic suppression, serving as a comparison to the antibiotic treatment group.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379733</data>
</node>
<node id="Virgin PET MPs">
  <data key="d0">Virgin PET MPs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Virgin PET microplastics (MPs) were used as a control reference to compare with residual PET after larval biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379733</data>
</node>
<node id="FT-IR spectra">
  <data key="d0">FT-IR spectra</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">FT-IR spectra were used to characterize chemical changes in PET polymers before and after biodegradation by T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379734</data>
</node>
<node id="MW">
  <data key="d0">MW</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">MW (Molecular Weight) of PET polymers was a key factor studied in relation to biodegradation efficiency by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;MW refers to Mealworm larvae, which were studied for survival, larval weight, and metabolic effects when exposed to plastics and supplements.&lt;SEP&gt;MW refers to a model insect group used in the study to test interactions with plastic substrates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756380861</data>
</node>
<node id="SR">
  <data key="d0">SR</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">SR (Survival Rate) was measured in larvae groups fed different PET polymers, influencing weight gain outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379734</data>
</node>
<node id="BD pattern">
  <data key="d0">BD pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">BD (bulk depolymerization) pattern is a degradation pathway where all molecular weight parameters of PET decrease, indicating relatively rapid biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379735</data>
</node>
<node id="LD pattern">
  <data key="d0">LD pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">LD (limited depolymerization) pattern is a degradation pathway where only low molecular weight polymers are broken down, with limited ability to degrade long molecular chains.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379735</data>
</node>
<node id="MWD">
  <data key="d0">MWD</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">MWD (molecular weight distribution) refers to the distribution of polymer chain lengths, which shifted towards lower molecular weight after PET degradation by T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;MWD (Molecular Weight Distribution) is a measure of polymer size distribution, analyzed by GPC to evaluate PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Molecular weight distribution (MWD) measured in PS degradation experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;MWD refers to molecular weight distribution, which becomes heterogeneous after depolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387686</data>
</node>
<node id="Crystallinity degree">
  <data key="d0">Crystallinity degree</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystallinity degree refers to the proportion of crystalline versus amorphous regions in PET polymers, which affects susceptibility to enzymatic and microbial degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379736</data>
</node>
<node id="Amorphous PET">
  <data key="d0">Amorphous PET</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Amorphous PET refers to the less ordered regions of PET polymers, which are more susceptible to microbial attack compared to crystalline regions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756379736</data>
