<node id="PS film">
  <data key="d0">PS film</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS film refers to polystyrene film that is widely used as a substrate in biodegradation studies, particularly for evaluating microbial and bacterial activity on synthetic polymers. Derived from expanded polystyrene (EPS), PS film serves as a controlled and uniform material to investigate the potential of microorganisms, including insect gut microbiota and specific bacterial strains such as YT2, WJ2020, and WJ2025, to degrade polystyrene. In these experiments, PS film functions as a carbon source, allowing researchers to assess microbial degradation capacity through measurable indicators such as weight loss, surface deterioration, and chemical oxidation.  

Studies involving PS film have demonstrated that microbial exposure can lead to significant physical and chemical alterations, including surface roughening, pitting, and oxidative modifications of the polymer structure. These changes provide evidence of microbial activity and potential pathways for polystyrene biodegradation. By monitoring both structural and compositional transformations, researchers use PS film to better understand the mechanisms of plastic degradation and the role of specific microorganisms in breaking down persistent synthetic polymers.  

Overall, PS film is an essential experimental material in biodegradation research, enabling systematic evaluation of microbial and bacterial interactions with polystyrene, and contributing to the broader study of plastic waste management and environmental sustainability.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
