<node id="Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9">
  <data key="d0">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, capable of degrading polystyrene nanoplastics efficiently.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial isolate that exhibited the highest growth rate and strongest ability to degrade polystyrene. It achieved 56% turbidity removal, 7% PS film weight loss, and showed enzymatic activity including esterase, laccase, catalase, oxidase, and cellulase.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworms and studied for its biofilm behavior on nanoplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of yellow mealworms. It has been observed degrading petroleum hydrocarbons, plastics, and nanoplastics (NPS). It produces enzymes such as laccases, cellulases, and esterases, and can thrive on NPS as a sole carbon source, producing acids as by-products.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a microorganism isolated from Tenebrio molitor, tested on NPS and PS film for 30 days, showing 7% biodegradation/weight loss.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381098</data>
</node>
<node id="Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071">
  <data key="d0">Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain identified as capable of decomposing polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a microorganism isolated from Zophobas atratus, tested on PS film, studied in microbiome context.&lt;SEP&gt;A bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of the superworm (Zophobas atratus), capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestine of the superworm (Zophobas atratus), capable of degrading PS and showing increased serine hydrolase expression during degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381961</data>
</node>
