  <data key="d5">1756381118</data>
</node>
<node id="Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8">
  <data key="d0">Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A bacterial strain identified and deposited in NCBI with accession number PP126567.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial isolate with soft, creamy colonies. It showed enzymatic activity including lipase, esterase, laccase, catalase, and oxidase.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 (PP12657) is a microbial strain identified as a potential NPS degrader, showing 1.2% reduction in PS film weight.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworms and identified as capable of degrading polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas aeruginosa M8 is a bacterial strain isolated from the intestines of yellow mealworms. It is known to break down polystyrene (PS).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381118</data>
</node>
<node id="Achromobacter xylosidans M9">
  <data key="d0">Achromobacter xylosidans M9</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A bacterial strain identified and deposited in NCBI with accession number OR859752.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter xylosidans M9 is a microbial strain identified as a strong degrader of nanoplastics (NPS) and polystyrene (PS) films, showing significant biodegradation activity including weight loss of PS films and reduction in nanoplastic particle size and count.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de</data>
