<node id="Achromobacter">
  <data key="d0">Achromobacter</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Achromobacter is a bacterial genus studied for its potential role in polystyrene biodegradation due to its enzymatic activity and history in xenobiotic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a bacterial genus identified in Tenebrio molitor larval gut isolates.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a bacterial species identified among the T. molitor larval gut isolates.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a bacterial genus studied for its potential in xenobiotics biodegradation, enzymatic activity, and pollutant remediation.&lt;SEP&gt;Achromobacter is a bacterial genus with significantly different relative abundances between diet groups in the mealworm gut microbiome study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756382183</data>
</node>
<node id="A. xylosidans M9">
  <data key="d0">A. xylosidans M9</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">A. xylosidans M9 is a strain of Achromobacter studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene and its biodegradation efficiency in relation to polystyrene size.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756381148</data>
