<node id="30 d Test">
  <data key="d0">30 d Test</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A 30-day experimental period during which larvae consumed polystyrene and their survival rates and consumption were measured.&lt;SEP&gt;The 30-day test refers to the experimental period during which larvae were fed PS or natural feed to study survival, weight change, and degradation effects.&lt;SEP&gt;The 30-day test was conducted to study the thermal modification of larvae from pristine Styrofoam to frass.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384512</data>
</node>
<node id="Feed Group">
  <data key="d0">Feed Group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Control group of larvae fed with regular feed instead of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;The feed group refers to larvae that were fed with natural feed sources such as bran or beeswax instead of PS. They showed positive weight gain and higher survival rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384512</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Group">
  <data key="d0">PS Group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental group of larvae fed with polystyrene as their sole diet.&lt;SEP&gt;The PS group refers to larvae that were fed with polystyrene (PS). They showed significant weight loss and lower survival rates compared to the feed group.&lt;SEP&gt;Experimental group of T. molitor larvae fed with PS (Styrofoam chips).&lt;SEP&gt;The PS group refers to larvae fed with PS (unspecified diet type) that did not meet the criterion for sufficient reserves.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388203</data>
</node>
<node id="Superworm">
  <data key="d0">Superworm</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Superworm is a type of larva studied for its ability to consume polystyrene (PS). It showed the highest survival rate and PS consumption among the tested larvae, with significant weight loss when fed PS.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm is one of the insect larvae species studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) through gut microbial activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Superworm is one of the insect larvae species studied for its gut microbiome response to polystyrene (PS) feeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384512</data>
</node>
<node id="Mn (Number-averaged Molecular Weight)">
  <data key="d0">Mn (Number-averaged Molecular Weight)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mn refers to the number-averaged molecular weight of polymers, used in the study to measure changes in Styrofoam and frass after larval digestion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Mw (Weight-averaged Molecular Weight)">
  <data key="d0">Mw (Weight-averaged Molecular Weight)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mw refers to the weight-averaged molecular weight of polymers, used in the study to measure changes in Styrofoam and frass after larval digestion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Polymer Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">Polymer Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymer depolymerization refers to the breakdown of long polymer chains into smaller molecules, observed in the degradation of PS by larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Chain Scission">
  <data key="d0">Chain Scission</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chain scission refers to the breaking of polymer chains, identified as evidence of enzymatic depolymerization or microbial attack in the larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbial Attack">
  <data key="d0">Microbial Attack</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Microbial attack refers to the role of microorganisms in breaking down PS inside the larvae’s gut.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Enzymatic Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">Enzymatic Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Enzymatic depolymerization refers to the process by which enzymes in the larvae’s gut break down PS into lower molecular weight substances.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756384514</data>
</node>
<node id="Intestinal Microorganisms">
  <data key="d0">Intestinal Microorganisms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Intestinal microorganisms are microbes present in the larvae’s gut, suggested to play a role in PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Intestinal microorganisms in Tenebrio molitor larvae contribute to the depolymerization of plastics.</data>
