<node id="Copolymer">
  <data key="d0">Copolymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Copolymer is a type of plastic purchased from specialty stores and used as an experimental feedstock in the study.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385000</data>
</node>
<node id="EVA (Ethylene-vinyl acetate)">
  <data key="d0">EVA (Ethylene-vinyl acetate)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">EVA is a type of plastic foam used as a sole feedstock for yellow mealworms in one of the experimental groups. It showed toxic or inhibitory effects on survival.&lt;SEP&gt;EVA is a type of plastic polymer tested in the study. It was consumed by yellow mealworms and showed significant depolymerization in their gut.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385001</data>
</node>
<node id="Starvation Group">
  <data key="d0">Starvation Group</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The starvation group consisted of yellow mealworms deprived of food, used as a control to compare survival and weight changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The starvation group served as another control, showing lower weight gain compared to plastic-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;The starvation group refers to mealworms that were not fed, leading to gut microbiome diversification through ingestion of frass, skeletons, and body biomass.&lt;SEP&gt;The Starvation group refers to larvae subjected to a diet without sufficient reserves, leading to decreased reserve density and poor growth outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388221</data>
</node>
<node id="Supporting Information (Method S1-S4)">
  <data key="d0">Supporting Information (Method S1-S4)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Supporting Information (Method S1-S4) provided methodological details for frass collection and polymer analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385004</data>
</node>
<node id="Supporting Information (Method S5)">
  <data key="d0">Supporting Information (Method S5)</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Supporting Information (Method S5) outlined the operations for gut microbial analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385004</data>
</node>
<node id="Foam Plastics Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Foam Plastics Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Foam plastics biodegradation refers to the process of plastics being broken down after passing through the larval gut.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385005</data>
</node>
<node id="Yang et al. (2018a, 2018b)">
  <data key="d0">Yang et al. (2018a, 2018b)</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Yang and colleagues conducted previous studies reporting that plastic consumption provided energy and carbon for yellow mealworms but did not significantly affect weight changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385008</data>
</node>
<node id="Chao">
  <data key="d0">Chao</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chao is a diversity index used to estimate bacterial richness in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Chao is a diversity estimator used to measure fungal species richness in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385010</data>
</node>
<node id="Simpson">
  <data key="d0">Simpson</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Simpson is a diversity index used to measure bacterial diversity in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;Simpson is a diversity index used to measure fungal community diversity in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385010</data>
</node>
<node id="ACE">
  <data key="d0">ACE</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">ACE is a diversity index used to estimate bacterial richness in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.&lt;SEP&gt;ACE is a diversity estimator used to measure fungal species richness in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385011</data>
</node>
<node id="PU-fed group">
  <data key="d0">PU-fed group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The PU-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with PU foam, which showed dominance of Proteobacteria and Enterobacteriaceae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385011</data>
</node>
<node id="EVA-fed group">
  <data key="d0">EVA-fed group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The EVA-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with EVA foam, which showed dominance of Proteobacteria and increased Enterobacteriaceae and Porphyromonadaceae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385011</data>
</node>
<node id="PP-fed group">
  <data key="d0">PP-fed group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The PP-fed group refers to yellow mealworms fed with PP plastic, which showed dominance of Tenericutes and increases in Spiroplasmataceae, Moraxellaceae, and Pseudomonadaceae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756385011</data>
</node>
<node id="Moraxellaceae">
  <data key="d0">Moraxellaceae</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Moraxellaceae is a bacterial family that increased to 13.29% in PP-fed groups, associated with PP degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Moraxellaceae is one of the bacterial families classified under Gammaproteobacteria, identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Moraxellaceae is one of the bacterial families within Gammaproteobacteria identified in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;Moraxellaceae is a bacterial family that accounted for 80% of the strains identified in this study.</data>
