  <data key="d3">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387415</data>
</node>
<node id="SOD enzyme assay kit">
  <data key="d0">SOD enzyme assay kit</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The SOD enzyme assay kit was used to measure superoxide dismutase activity in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387415</data>
</node>
<node id="24-day test period">
  <data key="d0">24-day test period</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The 24-day test period refers to the duration of the experiment in which mealworms were fed with different diets including microplastics and bran.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387415</data>
</node>
<node id="Chlorinated Intermediates">
  <data key="d0">Chlorinated Intermediates</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chlorinated intermediates are toxic substances produced during PVC depolymerization, impairing the intestinal epithelium of mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387415</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrogen Chloride">
  <data key="d0">Hydrogen Chloride</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrogen chloride is a toxic byproduct of PVC depolymerization that can damage the intestinal epithelium of mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387416</data>
</node>
<node id="Invertebrate-Mediated Plastic Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Invertebrate-Mediated Plastic Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The process studied in which invertebrates, specifically mealworms, degrade microplastics such as PE, PS, and PVC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387417</data>
</node>
<node id="Transporters">
  <data key="d0">Transporters</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Transporters are biological components required for nutrient uptake in mealworms, and their production is impaired by a plastic-only diet.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387417</data>
</node>
<node id="Nutrient Uptake">
  <data key="d0">Nutrient Uptake</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Nutrient uptake refers to the process by which mealworms absorb nutrients, which is decreased when they are fed a plastic-only diet.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrient uptake is negatively affected in organisms that ingest microplastics without the ability to degrade them.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387417</data>
</node>
<node id="Intestinal Epithelium">
  <data key="d0">Intestinal Epithelium</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The intestinal epithelium is the lining of the mealworm gut, which can be impaired by toxic byproducts of PVC depolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387417</data>
</node>
<node id="Random Internal Scission">
  <data key="d0">Random Internal Scission</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Random internal scission, also called endo-type depolymerization, is a process where polymer macromolecules are broken into mid- and short-chain polymers in mealworm intestines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387418</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Bacterial Consortiums">
  <data key="d0">Gut Bacterial Consortiums</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Gut bacterial consortiums are microbial communities in mealworms that contribute to the depolymerization of plastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387418</data>
</node>
<node id="Amorphous Fractions">
  <data key="d0">Amorphous Fractions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Amorphous fractions are non-crystalline regions of polymers that are more susceptible to depolymerization in mealworm digestion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387419</data>
</node>
<node id="Crystalline Fractions">
  <data key="d0">Crystalline Fractions</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Crystalline fractions are ordered regions of polymers that are less susceptible to depolymerization in mealworm digestion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387419</data>
</node>
<node id="Macromolecular Structure">
  <data key="d0">Macromolecular Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Macromolecular structure refers to the molecular arrangement of polymers, which influences their degradability in mealworm digestion.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387419</data>
</node>
<node id="Material Crystallinity">
  <data key="d0">Material Crystallinity</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Material crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in polymers, which affects their susceptibility to depolymerization.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387419</data>
</node>
<node id="Polymer Degradability">
  <data key="d0">Polymer Degradability</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymer degradability refers to the ability of polymers to break down, which varies among PE, PS, and PVC.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387420</data>
</node>
<node id="PE Polymer">
  <data key="d0">PE Polymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PE polymer is a crystalline plastic with side chains that is less susceptible to depolymerization compared to PS and PVC in the gut microenvironment of mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387420</data>
</node>
<node id="PVC Polymer">
  <data key="d0">PVC Polymer</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PVC polymer is more likely to be attacked and depolymerized than PE in the gut microenvironment of mealworms, but residual PVC microplastics persist longer in mealworm intestines.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387420</data>
</node>
<node id="LDIR Technique">
  <data key="d0">LDIR Technique</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The LDIR (Laser Direct Infrared Spectrometry, Agilent 8700 LDIR) technique was used to identify and count residual microplastics in mealworm intestines after depuration.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387420</data>
</node>
<node id="Great Wax Moth (Galleria mellonella larvae)">
  <data key="d0">Great Wax Moth (Galleria mellonella larvae)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The great wax moth larvae are invertebrates that can digest PS microplastics, with complete disappearance observed after 48 hours of feeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387421</data>
</node>
<node id="Copepods">
  <data key="d0">Copepods</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Copepods are organisms without plastic-degrading capacities, and ingestion of microplastics causes intestinal blockage and physical damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387421</data>
</node>
<node id="Crabs">
  <data key="d0">Crabs</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Crabs are organisms without plastic-degrading capacities, and ingestion of microplastics causes intestinal blockage and physical damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387421</data>
</node>
<node id="Zebra Fish">
  <data key="d0">Zebra Fish</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Zebra fish are organisms without plastic-degrading capacities, and ingestion of microplastics causes intestinal blockage and physical damage.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387421</data>
</node>
<node id="Straw Powder">
  <data key="d0">Straw Powder</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Straw powder is a normal feedstock that mealworms can rapidly digest.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387421</data>
</node>
<node id="Endo-type Depolymerization">
  <data key="d0">Endo-type Depolymerization</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Endo-type depolymerization is a specific type of depolymerization that occurs more readily in amorphous fractions of polymers than crystalline fractions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Microenvironment">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microenvironment</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gut microenvironment refers to the digestive environment inside mealworms where depolymerization and degradation of plastics occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Mealworm Frass Residue">
  <data key="d0">Mealworm Frass Residue</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mealworm frass residue is the excretion produced by mealworms after digesting feedstocks such as wheat bran, straw powder, and vegetables.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Depuration">
  <data key="d0">Depuration</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Depuration refers to the process of clearing undigested microplastics from the intestines of mealworms over time, measured at intervals such as 12, 24, 36, 48, and 60 hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Wang, S. et al., 2022">
  <data key="d0">Wang, S. et al., 2022</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Wang, S. et al., 2022 is a cited study reporting that PS particles disappeared from the gut of the great wax moth larvae after 48 hours of force feeding.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Petro-based Polymers">
  <data key="d0">Petro-based Polymers</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Petro-based polymers are plastics derived from petroleum sources, such as PP, PET, and PUR, mentioned as subjects for future biodegradation research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387424</data>
</node>
<node id="Degradable Plastics">
  <data key="d0">Degradable Plastics</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Degradable plastics such as PLA and PBAT are mentioned as subjects for future biodegradation research.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387425</data>
</node>
<node id="Bran-fed control">
  <data key="d0">Bran-fed control</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The bran-fed control group of mealworms served as a baseline comparison, showing lower ROS, SOD, and MDA levels compared to plastic-fed groups.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387425</data>
</node>
<node id="Eisenia fetida (earthworms)">
  <data key="d0">Eisenia fetida (earthworms)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Eisenia fetida are earthworms that also showed oxidative damage when exposed to PE microplastics, similar to mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387425</data>
</node>
<node id="Polybutylene adipate terephthalate (PBAT)">
  <data key="d0">Polybutylene adipate terephthalate (PBAT)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polybutylene adipate terephthalate (PBAT) is a degradable plastic mentioned as a target for future follow-up research on plastic biodegradation and oxidative stress.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387426</data>
</node>
<node id="Chlorinated intermediates">
  <data key="d0">Chlorinated intermediates</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Chlorinated intermediates are toxic degradation products formed during PVC biodegradation, contributing to oxidative stress in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387426</data>
</node>
<node id="Hydrogen chloride">
  <data key="d0">Hydrogen chloride</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Hydrogen chloride is a toxic degradation product formed during PVC biodegradation, contributing to oxidative stress in mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387427</data>
</node>
<node id="Mitochondrial dysfunction">
  <data key="d0">Mitochondrial dysfunction</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Mitochondrial dysfunction is a biological effect triggered by microplastic-induced oxidative damage in organisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387427</data>
</node>
<node id="Survival rate of mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Survival rate of mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The survival rate of mealworms is a physiological index affected by oxidative damage from microplastic ingestion.</data>
