<node id="Polystyrene Microplastics (PS MPs)">
  <data key="d0">Polystyrene Microplastics (PS MPs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Polystyrene microplastics (PS MPs) are synthetic polymer particles whose molecular weight changes were studied after passing through the larval intestinal tract of mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387668</data>
</node>
<node id="Gut Microbiome of Mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Gut Microbiome of Mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The gut microbiome of mealworms contributes significantly to the degradation of polystyrene microplastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387668</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-1">
  <data key="d0">PS-1</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-1 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-1 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics with a molecular weight of 6.70 kDa (PS-L).&lt;SEP&gt;PS-1 is a group of PS MPs with low molecular weight, consumed by mealworms, showing relatively high SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-1 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups whose molecular weight and distribution were measured after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387668</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-2">
  <data key="d0">PS-2</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-2 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-2 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics. Specific molecular weight not provided in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-2 is a group of PS MPs with low molecular weight, consumed by mealworms, showing relatively high SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-2 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups whose molecular weight and distribution were measured after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387669</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-3">
  <data key="d0">PS-3</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-3 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-3 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics. Specific molecular weight not provided in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-3 is a group of PS MPs with medium molecular weight, consumed by mealworms, showing relatively high SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-3 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups whose molecular weight and distribution were measured after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387669</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-4">
  <data key="d0">PS-4</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-4 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-4 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics with a molecular weight of 192.9 kDa (PS-M).&lt;SEP&gt;PS-4 is a group of PS MPs with medium molecular weight (192.9 kDa), consumed by mealworms, showing the highest SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-4 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups whose molecular weight and distribution were measured after biodegradation by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387669</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-5">
  <data key="d0">PS-5</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-5 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-5 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics. Specific molecular weight not provided in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-5 is a group of PS MPs with high molecular weight, consumed by mealworms, showing relatively high SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-5 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups, noted as a high-molecular-weight polymer that was more difficult for mealworms to degrade.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387669</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-6">
  <data key="d0">PS-6</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-6 is one of the experimental groups of polystyrene microplastics tested for molecular weight changes after passing through mealworm intestines, with distinct results compared to other groups.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-6 is one of the experimental feeding conditions where mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics with a molecular weight of 1346 kDa (PS-H).&lt;SEP&gt;PS-6 is a group of PS MPs with ultrahigh molecular weight (1346 kDa), consumed by mealworms, showing the lowest SPCR.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-6 is one of the tested polystyrene microplastic groups whose polydispersity index increased after biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387669</data>
</node>
<node id="Experimental Design of PS Consumption and Biodegradation">
  <data key="d0">Experimental Design of PS Consumption and Biodegradation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The experimental design tested eight groups of mealworms under different feeding conditions to evaluate consumption and biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387670</data>
</node>
<node id="Size-Average Molecular Weight (Mc)">
  <data key="d0">Size-Average Molecular Weight (Mc)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Size-average molecular weight (Mc) is a parameter used to characterize polystyrene microplastics, measured before and after digestion by mealworms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387670</data>
</node>
<node id="Physiological Response of Mealworms">
  <data key="d0">Physiological Response of Mealworms</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The physiological response of mealworms to polystyrene microplastics was studied, including weight change, survival rates, consumption rates, and frass yield.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387672</data>
</node>
<node id="Specific PS Consumption Rates (SPCRs)">
  <data key="d0">Specific PS Consumption Rates (SPCRs)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific PS consumption rates (SPCRs) measure how quickly mealworms consumed polystyrene microplastics under different feeding conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387672</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Removal">
  <data key="d0">PS Removal</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS removal refers to the percentage of polystyrene microplastics eliminated by mealworms during the feeding experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PS removal is the process of estimating how much PS was digested or degraded by mealworms, based on mass balance calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387672</data>
</node>
<node id="Frass Yield">
  <data key="d0">Frass Yield</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Frass yield is the ratio of frass produced to polystyrene microplastics consumed by mealworms, measured under different dietary conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387672</data>
</node>
<node id="Starvation (St)">
  <data key="d0">Starvation (St)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Starvation (St) was used as a control feeding condition in the experimental design of mealworm consumption and biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation was used as a control condition where mealworms were not provided with food.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387673</data>
</node>
<node id="Laboratory Thermostatic Incubator">
  <data key="d0">Laboratory Thermostatic Incubator</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The laboratory thermostatic incubator maintained controlled conditions of 25 ± 0.5 °C and 70 ± 5% humidity for the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387673</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Consumption and Biodegradation Experiment">
  <data key="d0">PS Consumption and Biodegradation Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental study conducted to evaluate the consumption and biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms under eight feeding conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387673</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Removal and Depolymerization Pattern Analysis">
  <data key="d0">PS Removal and Depolymerization Pattern Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental procedure to analyze the removal and depolymerization of polystyrene using frass samples and GPC analysis.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387673</data>
</node>
<node id="Microbial Community and Metabolomic Analysis">
  <data key="d0">Microbial Community and Metabolomic Analysis</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An experimental procedure to investigate the gut microbiome and metabolome of mealworms fed with different PS conditions, bran, or under starvation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756387674</data>
</node>
<node id="PS-L">
  <data key="d0">PS-L</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS-L refers to polystyrene microplastics with a molecular weight of 6.70 kDa, corresponding to PS-1 in the feeding experiments.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-L is a feeding condition representing low molecular weight PS MPs in the gut microbiome analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-L is a low molecular weight polystyrene (6.70 kDa) fed to mealworms in the study.&lt;SEP&gt;PS-L refers to low molecular weight (6.70 kDa) polystyrene polymers fed to mealworms, leading to specific metabolic pathway perturbations.</data>
