  <data key="d0">Dovecotes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Dovecotes are one of the habitats where mealworms live and feed.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) Model">
  <data key="d0">Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model is a mathematical framework used to describe energy and mass fluxes in organisms, predicting responses to environmental stress and changes in physiological processes such as maintenance, growth, development, and reproduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Standard DEB Model">
  <data key="d0">Standard DEB Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The standard DEB model is the foundational version of the Dynamic Energy Budget model, often used as a starting point for deriving new equations and model versions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Simplified DEB Model">
  <data key="d0">Simplified DEB Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The simplified DEB model is a version of the DEB model that reduces complexity in equations and is used to assess the impact of toxic substances on the energy budgets of organisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Holometabolic Insects DEB Model">
  <data key="d0">Holometabolic Insects DEB Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The DEB model adapted for holometabolic insects, which includes specific assumptions and equations to account for their life cycle characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Extended Simplified DEB Model">
  <data key="d0">Extended Simplified DEB Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The extended simplified DEB model is derived from the standard and simplified DEB models, with additional assumptions for holometabolic insects, and is used to analyze the effects of food type on the growth and metabolism of T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388198</data>
</node>
<node id="Jager">
  <data key="d0">Jager</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jager is an author cited in multiple years (2016, 2014, 2013) for contributions to DEB model research.&lt;SEP&gt;Jager is cited in relation to the definition and use of equation (3) in the DEB model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388199</data>
</node>
<node id="Kooijman">
  <data key="d0">Kooijman</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Kooijman is an author cited in 2010 for contributions to the development and assumptions of the DEB model.&lt;SEP&gt;Kooijman is cited in the context of the DEB model, specifically regarding the survival of organisms when somatic maintenance costs can no longer be paid.&lt;SEP&gt;Kooijman (2010) is cited in relation to DEB model parameters and larval responses to starvation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388199</data>
</node>
<node id="Zimmer">
  <data key="d0">Zimmer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Zimmer is an author cited along with Jager in 2012 for work on the simplified DEB model.&lt;SEP&gt;Zimmer is cited in relation to the definition and use of equation (3) in the DEB model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388199</data>
</node>
<node id="Llandres">
  <data key="d0">Llandres</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Llandres is an author cited in 2015 for work on DEB models for holometabolic insects.&lt;SEP&gt;Llandres is cited in relation to research on pupation of larvae and reproduction buffer density.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388200</data>
</node>
<node id="Holometabolic Organisms">
  <data key="d0">Holometabolic Organisms</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Holometabolic organisms are those undergoing complete metamorphosis, and their life cycles are considered in the context of DEB model applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388200</data>
</node>
<node id="Energy Fluxes">
  <data key="d0">Energy Fluxes</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Energy fluxes describe the flow of energy in the DEB model, including assimilation, reserve accumulation, mobilization, and allocation to maintenance, growth, maturation, or reproduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388200</data>
</node>
<node id="Maturity Maintenance">
  <data key="d0">Maturity Maintenance</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maturity maintenance refers to the constant energy cost required to maintain maturity during larval growth in the DEB context.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388201</data>
</node>
<node id="Isomorphism">
  <data key="d0">Isomorphism</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Isomorphism is an assumption in the DEB model that individuals do not change shape during growth, applied to T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388202</data>
</node>
<node id="V0 and V1 - Morphy">
  <data key="d0">V0 and V1 - Morphy</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">V0 and V1 - Morphy are terms mentioned as applicable to different species or life stages in the context of isomorphism assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388202</data>
</node>
<node id="DEB Model">
  <data key="d0">DEB Model</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">The DEB (Dynamic Energy Budget) model is a theoretical framework used to describe energy allocation in organisms, including reserves, growth, and reproduction. A simplified version is applied to T. molitor larval development.&lt;SEP&gt;The DEB (Dynamic Energy Budget) model is used to fit growth curve data and analyze feeding and growth patterns in larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388202</data>
</node>
<node id="Jager and Zimmer">
  <data key="d0">Jager and Zimmer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Jager and Zimmer are authors of a 2012 paper that provides a comprehensive description of the simplified DEB model and its application.&lt;SEP&gt;Jager and Zimmer are cited authors (2012) whose work is referenced in relation to the derivation of equation (19) and toxic action modes in DEB models.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388202</data>
</node>
<node id="Cricket Farm">
  <data key="d0">Cricket Farm</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Cricket Farm is the supplier of T. molitor larvae used in the experiments.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388202</data>
</node>
<node id="Starvation 1 Group">
  <data key="d0">Starvation 1 Group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental group of T. molitor larvae with no food source, kept in seclusion without access to droppings or moult residues.&lt;SEP&gt;The Starvation 1 group refers to larvae subjected to starvation conditions, showing no increase in volumetric length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388203</data>
</node>
<node id="Starvation 2 Group">
  <data key="d0">Starvation 2 Group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental group of T. molitor larvae with no food source, but kept together with access to droppings and moult residues.&lt;SEP&gt;The Starvation 2 group refers to larvae subjected to starvation conditions, also showing no increase in volumetric length.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388203</data>
</node>
<node id="PS Large Group">
  <data key="d0">PS Large Group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental group of older and larger T. molitor larvae (0.06–0.1 g each) fed with PS.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="PS + Oat Group">
  <data key="d0">PS + Oat Group</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental group of T. molitor larvae fed with PS and crushed oatmeal, with oatmeal provided every 10 days.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="Laboratory Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Laboratory Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The overall experiment conducted on T. molitor larvae under controlled conditions (25 °C, 80% humidity, 91 days).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (1)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (1)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (1) in the simplified DEB model describes reserves dynamics in organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (1) is referenced in the derivation of volumetric length changes related to reserves density.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (2)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (2)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (2) in the simplified DEB model describes changes in the structural length of organisms.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (3)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (3)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (3) in the simplified DEB model describes the reproduction rate of organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (3) is used to estimate the time of pupation and describes energy flux into the reproduction buffer.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (3) describes the relationship between scaled reserve density, reproduction rate, and the time required to reach the reproduction buffer threshold.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388204</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (4)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (4)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (4) is a simplified form of Equation (2), expressed with only two parameters: maximum body length (Lm) and Von Bertalanffy growth rate (rB).&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (4) describes growth, specifically the increase in structure volume, which does not occur during starvation.&lt;SEP&gt;Equation (4) describes growth as the increase in structure volume, but does not apply during starvation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388205</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (7)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (7)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (7) is the analytical solution of Equation (4) when f and e are constant, describing structural length over time.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388205</data>
</node>
<node id="Von Bertalanffy Growth Rate (rB)">
  <data key="d0">Von Bertalanffy Growth Rate (rB)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Von Bertalanffy growth rate (rB) is a parameter used in the simplified DEB model to describe organism growth.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388205</data>
</node>
<node id="Maximum Body Length (Lm)">
  <data key="d0">Maximum Body Length (Lm)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Maximum body length (Lm) is a parameter in the simplified DEB model representing the maximum structural length of an organism.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388205</data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Length (L0)">
  <data key="d0">Structural Length (L0)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural length (L0) is the initial body length of larvae at the beginning of the experiment.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388205</data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Length (Lm0)">
  <data key="d0">Structural Length (Lm0)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural length (Lm0) represents the maximum structural length above L0 in the simplified DEB model.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388206</data>
</node>
<node id="Materials and Methods Section">
  <data key="d0">Materials and Methods Section</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Materials and Methods section introduces additional assumptions and extensions of the simplified DEB model specific for T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388206</data>
</node>
<node id="PS large">
  <data key="d0">PS large</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS large refers to older and larger larvae compared to the PS group, with a mass in the range 0.06–0.1 g each, fed with PS.&lt;SEP&gt;PS large refers to a group of larvae fed with larger pieces of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;PS large refers to a diet group where larvae were fed larger amounts of polystyrene, which still proved insufficient to sustain life.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388206</data>
</node>
<node id="PS + oat.">
  <data key="d0">PS + oat.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS + oat. refers to larvae fed with PS and crushed oatmeal, with oatmeal provided every 10 days for 24 hours before removal.&lt;SEP&gt;PS + oat. refers to a diet group where polystyrene was supplemented with oats, resulting in survival rates similar to control but with inhibited growth and reproduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388206</data>
</node>
<node id="PS + oat. + vit.">
  <data key="d0">PS + oat. + vit.</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">PS + oat. + vit. refers to larvae fed with PS, crushed oatmeal, and vitamins and minerals, with supplements added every 10 days for 24 hours before removal.&lt;SEP&gt;This group refers to larvae fed with PS diet supplemented with oatmeal and vitamins, which resulted in one pupa being observed.&lt;SEP&gt;PS + oat. + vit. refers to a diet group where polystyrene was supplemented with oats and vitamins, leading to survival rates similar to control but with inhibited growth and reproduction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388206</data>
</node>
<node id="Equation (15)">
  <data key="d0">Equation (15)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Equation (15) was used to estimate parameters in the control sample.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388207</data>
</node>
<node id="Structural Body Length (L)">
  <data key="d0">Structural Body Length (L)</data>
  <data key="d1">category</data>
  <data key="d2">Structural body length (L) is a measured variable representing the physical length of the organism's structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1756388207</data>
