<edge source="Tenebrio molitor" target="Polystyrene">
  <data key="d6">315.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Polystyrene and *Tenebrio molitor*** centers on the capacity of the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*, commonly known as yellow mealworms, to ingest, digest, and biodegrade polystyrene, including expanded polystyrene (EPS). Scientific research has demonstrated that these larvae can survive and even grow when polystyrene is provided as their sole carbon source, indicating that they are capable of breaking down this highly persistent synthetic polymer through their digestive processes. This biodegradation is facilitated by the larvae’s gut microbiome, which has been studied for its diversity and potential role in enabling the metabolic breakdown of polystyrene waste.  

Polystyrene is a widely used plastic that poses significant environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural decomposition and its accumulation in landfills and ecosystems. The discovery that *Tenebrio molitor* larvae can consume and metabolize polystyrene has therefore attracted considerable scientific and environmental interest. This interaction positions *Tenebrio molitor* as a promising biological model for exploring sustainable strategies to mitigate plastic pollution.  

Overall, the relation between polystyrene and *Tenebrio molitor* is defined by the insect’s unique ability to biodegrade a material traditionally considered non-biodegradable, highlighting its potential role in innovative waste management and environmental remediation approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene biodegradation was studied in yellow mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor (mealworms) consume and biodegrade polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,degradation,diet,entomology,environmental research,experimental diet,experimental feeding,insect activity,insect biodegradation,insect biology,insect digestion,insect larvae,insect microbiota,insect research,insect-plastic interaction,microbiome diversity,plastic biodegradation,plastic degradation,plastic digestion,plastic pollution,plastic waste,polymer biodegradation,survival,waste management</data>
