<edge source="Tenebrio molitor" target="Polyethylene">
  <data key="d6">118.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Polyethylene and Tenebrio molitor** refers to the capacity of Tenebrio molitor, particularly its larval stage (commonly known as mealworms), to biodegrade polyethylene, a widely used synthetic plastic. Research has demonstrated that Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of ingesting and breaking down polyethylene materials, including polyethylene foams and plastic mixtures, even though such substances are not naturally present in their environment. This biodegradation process has been linked to the activity of the larvae’s gut microbiome, which plays a key role in facilitating the breakdown of the polymer. Studies have focused not only on the larvae’s ability to consume and degrade polyethylene but also on the microbial and physiological responses within their digestive system during this process. Overall, the relation highlights Tenebrio molitor as a biological agent of interest in addressing plastic waste, with particular emphasis on its potential to contribute to polyethylene degradation and the associated microbial mechanisms involved.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae were studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,gut microbiome,insect biodegradation,insect biology,insect digestion,insect larvae,insect-plastic interaction,microbiome,plastic biodegradation,plastic degradation,plastic digestion,plastic waste</data>
