<edge source="Tenebrio molitor" target="Polyethylene (PE)">
  <data key="d6">82.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Polyethylene (PE) and Tenebrio molitor** refers to the capacity of the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, to ingest and biodegrade polyethylene, one of the most widely used synthetic plastics. Research has demonstrated that Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume various forms of polyethylene, including microplastics, films, and foam packaging materials. During this process, the larvae physically chew the plastic, creating holes and altering its morphology, while also contributing to its biochemical degradation.  

Studies indicate that the larvae are capable of breaking down polyethylene into smaller fragments and partially metabolizing it, suggesting a potential role in mitigating plastic pollution. However, the efficiency of this biodegradation process is influenced by several factors. For instance, while the larvae can ingest and degrade polyethylene, exposure to high doses of the material has been shown to be toxic, reducing their survival rates and lowering the overall conversion efficiency.  

Overall, the relation highlights Tenebrio molitor as a promising biological agent for polyethylene degradation, though its effectiveness is constrained by toxicity at higher intake levels and the need for further research to optimize conditions for safe and efficient biodegradation.&lt;SEP&gt;PE was used as part of the mealworm diet in combination with EPS.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biological interaction,diet,experimental setup,insect diet,insect digestion,plastic biodegradation,plastic degradation,plastic waste,species study,toxicity</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756381213</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Tenebrio molitor" target="Polystyrene (PS)">
  <data key="d6">154.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Polystyrene (PS)** and **Tenebrio molitor** refers to the demonstrated ability of the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*, commonly known as mealworms, to ingest and partially biodegrade polystyrene materials. Scientific studies have consistently shown that these larvae can consume different forms of polystyrene, including foam and microplastics, and initiate its breakdown through biological processes. The degradation is facilitated by the larvae’s gut microbiome, which plays a central role in metabolizing the polymer. Physical evidence of this activity is observed in the form of holes and tunnels created in polystyrene substrates, indicating active consumption and mechanical alteration of the material.  

Although *Tenebrio molitor* larvae are capable of degrading polystyrene, the process has limitations. Polystyrene does not provide sufficient nutritional value to support the larvae’s growth and development, and in some experimental conditions, feeding on polystyrene has been shown to inhibit growth. This suggests that while the larvae can survive on polystyrene for a period of time, the material cannot serve as a sustainable or nutritionally adequate food source.  

The relation between polystyrene and *Tenebrio molitor* is therefore significant in the context of research on plastic biodegradation. It highlights the potential role of biological organisms in addressing plastic waste, particularly the environmental persistence of polystyrene. However, the effectiveness of *Tenebrio molitor* as a large-scale or practical solution for polystyrene pollution remains limited by the nutritional inadequacy of the material for the larvae and the relatively slow rate of degradation. This relation underscores both the promise and the constraints of using mealworms as a biological tool for mitigating synthetic polymer waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and degrade polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,consumption,ingestion,insect diet,insect digestion,insect feeding,insect-plastic interaction,microbiome,plastic biodegradation,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,plastic waste,species study</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999</data>
