  <data key="d6">17.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The study examines how biodegradation of plastics by mealworms affects their gut microbiome.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms was studied in relation to plastic degradation.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,microbiome impact,microbiome interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756386331</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Gut Microbiome" target="Mealworms">
  <data key="d6">104.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **“Gut Microbiome – Mealworms”** refers to the interaction between mealworms and the microbial community residing in their digestive system, with particular emphasis on its role in plastic biodegradation. Research has shown that the gut microbiome of mealworms is directly involved in the depolymerization and degradation of polystyrene (PS) and other plastics. The microbial community not only resides within the digestive tract but also secretes biochemical factors that enhance the breakdown of these otherwise persistent synthetic polymers.  

Studies analyzing the gut microbiome of mealworms have identified bacterial species associated with this process, highlighting the importance of microbial composition in enabling plastic degradation. The structure and function of the gut microbiome are influenced by the mealworms’ diet, with notable changes observed when mealworms consume different types of polystyrene or other substrates. These dietary shifts alter the microbial community, which in turn affects the efficiency of plastic biodegradation.  

Overall, the relation underscores that the gut microbiome of mealworms is a critical biological system enabling the biodegradation of plastics, particularly polystyrene. It demonstrates a dynamic interaction where microbial composition and activity are shaped by diet, while simultaneously contributing to the environmentally significant process of plastic breakdown.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms was studied to understand bacterial populations and their role in plastic degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms was analyzed under different feeding conditions to study microbial changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The gut microbiome of mealworms was analyzed to study its role in PS biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biological function,diet impact,digestion,host-microbe interaction,host-microbiome,insect digestion,microbial adaptation,microbial analysis,microbial ecology,microbial interaction,microbial symbiosis,microbiome analysis,microbiome interaction</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756387791</data>
</edge>
