<edge source="Polystyrene (PS)" target="Mealworms">
  <data key="d6">115.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Mealworms, Polystyrene (PS))** refers to the ability of mealworms, the larval form of *Tenebrio molitor*, to consume and biodegrade polystyrene, a widely used synthetic plastic. Research has demonstrated that mealworms are capable of chewing and ingesting polystyrene foam, after which the material undergoes microbial-assisted digestion within their gut. This process leads to the depolymerization and partial mineralization of the plastic, indicating that mealworms play an active role in breaking down polystyrene rather than simply fragmenting it.  

Experimental studies have shown that mealworms can survive on diets containing raw polystyrene, with some investigations noting that their pupation and development remain successful under such conditions. The presence of sucrose in their diet has been observed to significantly increase the rate of polystyrene consumption, suggesting that co-feeding with additional nutrients may enhance biodegradation efficiency.  

Overall, the relation highlights the ecological and biotechnological significance of mealworms in addressing plastic waste. Their ability to metabolize polystyrene through gut microbe-dependent processes provides a natural model for plastic biodegradation and offers potential pathways for developing sustainable waste management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms fed with PS showed depolymerization, chemical modifications, and mineralization of the polymer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms chewed through PS foam, leaving visible holes.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed polystyrene, which showed relatively high compatibility as a feeding source with less mass loss compared to other plastics.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were tested with polystyrene as a feed source to evaluate its contribution to their energy needs.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biodegradation experiment,compatibility,consumption,depolymerization,digestion,experimental feed,feeding experiment,feeding test,insect nutrition,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,research,waste management</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756384339</data>
</edge>
