<edge source="Mealworms" target="Frass">
  <data key="d6">219.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Frass, Mealworms)** refers to the connection between mealworms and the excreted material, known as frass, that results from their digestion of various feedstocks, including both natural substrates such as bran and synthetic polymers such as plastics. Frass represents the solid waste produced after mealworms metabolize ingested material, and it has become a central focus of research in the study of insect-mediated plastic degradation.  

When mealworms consume plastics such as polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polyurethane (PU) foam, the frass they produce often contains residual polymer fragments, indicating that digestion is incomplete. However, detailed analyses of frass using techniques such as Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy and other chemical characterization methods have revealed evidence of oxidative transformations, crystalline structural changes, and other chemical modifications to the polymers. These findings suggest that while mealworms do not fully mineralize plastics, partial degradation and transformation occur during their passage through the digestive system.  

The composition and properties of frass vary depending on the type of plastic consumed and the feeding conditions. For example, frass derived from mealworms fed on PS alone or PS supplemented with bran has been shown to contain altered polymer signatures, oxidative residues, and partially degraded fragments. Similarly, frass collected after feeding on EPS or other synthetic polymers has been analyzed to detect chemical modifications that provide insight into the mechanisms of plastic breakdown. In addition to chemical changes, microbial and structural alterations in frass have also been studied to better understand the biological and biochemical processes involved.  

In this context, frass is not merely a waste product but a valuable analytical resource. By examining frass, researchers can assess the extent of plastic degradation, identify transformation products, and evaluate the efficiency of mealworms as potential agents of bioremediation for persistent plastic waste. Thus, the relation highlights that mealworm frass serves both as an inevitable byproduct of digestion and as a critical indicator of the pathways and effectiveness of plastic degradation facilitated by these insects.</data>
  <data key="d8">analysis,biodegradation,biodegradation analysis,biodegradation byproduct,biodegradation evidence,biological byproduct,biological digestion,biological output,byproduct,byproduct analysis,degradation analysis,degradation byproduct,degradation evidence,digestion,digestion byproduct,digestion outcome,excrement analysis,experimental analysis,feeding outcome,measurement,metabolism,residue analysis,waste analysis,waste byproduct,waste product,waste production</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
