</edge>
<edge source="Mealworms" target="Bran">
  <data key="d6">234.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Bran, Mealworms)** refers to the role of bran as a fundamental dietary component in experimental research involving mealworms, particularly in studies of feeding behavior, growth, survival, and plastic degradation. Bran is widely employed as a **control diet**, providing a nutritionally adequate baseline against which alternative or experimental substrates, such as polystyrene (PS), microplastics (MPS), or polyether polyurethane (PU) foam, are compared. When mealworms are reared exclusively on bran, they typically exhibit healthy growth, weight gain, and biomass accumulation, outcomes that are not observed when they are fed solely on plastics. This makes bran an essential reference point for evaluating the physiological and metabolic impacts of plastic-based diets.  

Beyond its role as a control, bran is also used as a **co-feeding supplement** in plastic degradation experiments. When bran is provided alongside plastics, mealworm survival rates, biomass gain, and overall health are significantly improved compared to those fed plastics alone. The nutritional support from bran compensates for the deficiencies of a plastic-only diet, enabling mealworms to sustain growth and maintain higher survival levels. Importantly, bran supplementation has been shown to enhance the rate of PS consumption and degradation, in some cases nearly doubling the degradation rate relative to PS-only diets. This effect, however, can vary across studies, with some reporting modest or statistically insignificant increases.  

Researchers have also tested methods of physically incorporating bran into plastic substrates, such as coating or embedding bran into PS, to stimulate greater ingestion. These approaches suggest that bran can encourage plastic consumption, though results remain mixed. Additionally, bran has been observed to influence the gut microbiome of mealworms, further shaping their physiological responses and biodegradation capacity.  

In summary, the relation **(Bran, Mealworms)** highlights bran’s dual function in experimental contexts: it serves as a **standard control diet** for baseline comparisons and as a **nutritional supplement** that enhances survival, growth, and plastic biodegradation when combined with plastics. This dual role makes bran a critical factor in understanding mealworm dietary responses and in assessing their potential applications in the bioremediation of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed bran as a control diet to compare with PS-fed groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed with bran as a control diet.</data>
