<edge source="Mealworms" target="Styrofoam">
  <data key="d6">140.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Mealworms and Styrofoam** refers to the ability of mealworms (the larval form of *Tenebrio molitor*) to ingest and biodegrade Styrofoam, a form of polystyrene (PS) plastic foam. Scientific studies and controlled experiments have demonstrated that mealworms can consume Styrofoam as a food source, even when it is provided as their sole diet. Within their gut, mealworms are capable of breaking down the polymer fraction of Styrofoam, with research showing degradation rates of up to 65%.  

The biodegradation process involves the conversion of Styrofoam into multiple byproducts, including carbon dioxide (CO₂), biomass, and fecula (worm excrement). This indicates that mealworms not only ingest the material but also metabolize and mineralize it, contributing to its transformation into simpler compounds. The ability of mealworms to degrade Styrofoam has been studied both as a natural biodegradation mechanism and as a potential model for plastic waste management.  

Comparative studies have also highlighted that while mealworms are effective in consuming and degrading Styrofoam, their degradation rate is lower than that of superworms (*Zophobas morio* larvae), which are often used as a benchmark in related research. Nonetheless, mealworms remain a significant reference organism in the study of biological plastic degradation due to their demonstrated capacity to process Styrofoam and convert it into environmentally less harmful outputs.  

In summary, the relation (Mealworms, Styrofoam) captures the experimentally verified phenomenon that mealworms can ingest, degrade, and metabolize Styrofoam, transforming it into CO₂, biomass, and waste products, thereby offering insights into potential biological pathways for mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were studied for growth, development, and fecundity when fed styrofoam waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were shown to chew and digest styrofoam, a polystyrene product.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed Styrofoam as a sole diet to study PS degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms ingested Styrofoam, and their gut bacteria contributed to its degradation.</data>
