<edge source="Mealworms" target="Polystyrene">
  <data key="d6">116.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) and polystyrene refers to the demonstrated ability of mealworms to consume and biodegrade this synthetic polymer. Research has shown that mealworms can ingest polystyrene, a material traditionally considered resistant to natural degradation, and convert it into biomass. This process is facilitated by microbial activity within the mealworm gut, where symbiotic bacteria play a critical role in breaking down the polymer into simpler compounds. Multiple studies have investigated this phenomenon, highlighting the potential of mealworms as a biological agent for polystyrene degradation. The capacity of mealworms to process and metabolize polystyrene has attracted scientific interest due to its implications for sustainable waste management and plastic pollution mitigation. Overall, the relation underscores the unique biological interaction in which mealworms serve as a natural contributor to the biodegradation of polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed polystyrene in the experiment, resulting in high mortality due to low nutritional value.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are able to degrade polystyrene at a rate of up to 0.12 mg/d.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor larvae) are studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are studied for their ability to biodegrade and mineralize polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,diet impact,environmental research,feeding experiment,ingestion,insect digestion,insect feeding,insect-plastic degradation,insect-plastic interaction,nutrition deficiency,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,plastic waste</data>
