<edge source="Mealworms" target="PS (Polystyrene)">
  <data key="d6">170.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Mealworms, PS (Polystyrene))** describes the interaction between mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and polystyrene (PS), a synthetic plastic widely used in packaging and consumer products. Scientific studies have demonstrated that mealworms are capable of ingesting PS in various forms, including raw polystyrene, foam, and microplastics. Among different plastics tested, mealworms consumed PS at the highest rates, with measurable daily and yearly intake values, and their consumption increased steadily over time.  

When used as a feed source, PS enables mealworms to survive under nutrient-limited conditions, although ingestion is associated with physiological effects such as weight loss and structural changes within their bodies. Importantly, the digestive activity of mealworms, supported by their gut microbiota, contributes to the partial biodegradation of PS. This process involves depolymerization and chemical modification of the plastic, with evidence of digestion, mineralization, and the presence of residual particles in the frass (excreta). Analyses of frass from PS-fed mealworms have confirmed that the material undergoes chemical transformation, indicating that the larvae not only ingest but also metabolize and alter the polymer.  

The relation highlights mealworms as a biological model for studying plastic degradation, microbial interactions, and metabolic responses to synthetic polymers. Their ability to consume and partially degrade PS has attracted attention in the context of plastic waste management, as it suggests a potential biological pathway for mitigating environmental plastic pollution. Overall, the relation **(Mealworms, PS (Polystyrene))** underscores that mealworms ingest, metabolize, and partially degrade polystyrene, making them a significant focus of research into sustainable biodegradation strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms ingested PS microplastics, which underwent significant depolymerization and molecular weight reduction.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms consume and decompose PS with the help of gut microorganisms, though it is not an efficient energy source.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were tested for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene, but their effectiveness is doubtful.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,consumption,consumption preference,diet,diet experiment,digestion,experimental diet,feeding experiment,insect feeding,metabolism,plastic degradation,plastic digestion,plastic ingestion,survival,waste management</data>
