<edge source="Mealworms" target="PS">
  <data key="d6">99.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Mealworms, PS)** refers to the interaction between mealworms (*Tenebrio molitor* larvae) and polystyrene (PS), a common plastic material. Research has demonstrated that mealworms are capable of chewing and consuming PS, leading to notable physical and chemical changes in the material. Specifically, ingestion by mealworms results in significant surface alterations, degradation of the polymer, and modifications in the crystalline structure as well as the thermal decomposition behavior of PS. These findings indicate that mealworms can metabolize PS across different regions of their digestive system, contributing to its breakdown.  

Experimental studies have shown that mealworms steadily increase their consumption of PS over time, with moderate changes in body mass compared to when they are fed other plastics such as polypropylene (PP). In fact, mealworms have been observed to consume PS at higher rates than certain other plastic types, highlighting their relative efficiency in processing this material.  

However, the consumption of PS imposes physiological costs on the mealworms. Individuals fed with PS exhibit significant declines in lipid and carbohydrate reserves, reflecting metabolic stress and reduced nutritional balance. Among tested groups, mealworms fed exclusively on PS displayed the lowest lipid content and reduced sugar levels compared to control groups maintained on more nutritious diets. These metabolic impacts suggest that while mealworms can degrade and metabolize PS, the process is energetically demanding and nutritionally insufficient for sustaining optimal health.  

Overall, the relation (Mealworms, PS) captures the dual aspects of this biological interaction: on one hand, mealworms demonstrate a unique capacity to ingest and degrade polystyrene, contributing to its physical and chemical breakdown; on the other hand, reliance on PS as a feedstock negatively affects their metabolic health, indicating that while mealworms play a potential role in plastic biodegradation, PS is not a nutritionally viable food source for them.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms biodegraded PS, with residual microplastics measured in frass.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms were fed polystyrene (PS) to study its effects on their mass and biochemical composition.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are fed with PS to study its effects on survival, growth, and reproduction.</data>
  <data key="d8">biochemical response,biodegradation,consumption trend,diet,diet effect,dietary experiment,experimental condition,feeding behavior,feeding experiment,insect digestion,insect feeding,metabolism,organism response,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,plastic digestion,structural change</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756388257</data>
</edge>
