<edge source="PS" target="T. molitor larvae">
  <data key="d6">79.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(PS, *T. molitor* larvae)** refers to the interaction between polystyrene (PS) and the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms), particularly in the context of plastic biodegradation studies. Research has shown that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of consuming PS when it is provided as their sole diet, although the efficiency of this process is relatively low compared to their consumption of other plastics such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET).  

Feeding experiments indicate that while the larvae can ingest and metabolize PS, the process is associated with significant physiological stress. Larvae fed exclusively on PS exhibit weight loss, reduced survival rates, dehydration, malnutrition, and impaired growth. At the cellular level, ingestion of PS microplastics has been linked to oxidative stress and the generation of reactive oxygen species (ROS), suggesting that the degradation process imposes metabolic strain.  

Despite these challenges, *T. molitor* larvae demonstrate some capacity to metabolize PS, producing energy and biochemical intermediates through partial plastic degradation pathways. However, the biodegradation process is incomplete, as depolymerization of PS is often inhibited or halted, limiting the extent of breakdown. Experimental studies have reported that PS mass reduction by *T. molitor* larvae remains below 70%, underscoring the restricted efficiency of this biological treatment.  

Overall, the relation highlights that while *T. molitor* larvae can consume and partially degrade PS, the process is inefficient and detrimental to larval health, making PS a less favorable substrate compared to other plastics. This underscores both the potential and the limitations of using *T. molitor* larvae in bioremediation strategies targeting polystyrene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;PS diet group involved feeding T. molitor larvae polystyrene, which proved insufficient for survival due to lack of nutrients.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biodegradation efficiency,biodegradation limitation,comparison,diet,diet insufficiency,limited efficiency,metabolism,negative physiological response,nutritional deficiency,oxidative stress,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,plastic digestion,plastic ingestion,polymer degradation,survival impact</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ed790759cee2111ae15e840cf2c48e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
