<edge source="PS" target="T. molitor">
  <data key="d6">107.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(PS, T. molitor)** refers to the interaction between polystyrene (PS) and the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor*, commonly known as mealworms, in the context of biodegradation research. *T. molitor* larvae have been repeatedly studied for their ability to consume and degrade PS plastic, with experiments demonstrating measurable reductions in PS weight over time when fed to the insects. These studies have highlighted the larvae’s capacity to utilize PS as a carbon source, thereby serving as a biological model for testing PS utilization efficiency.  

Research has also shown that *T. molitor* hosts PS-degrading bacterial strains within its gut microbiota. Feeding the larvae with PS induces shifts in their microbial community composition, suggesting that gut symbionts play a significant role in the degradation process. In addition to breaking down PS, the larvae exhibit nitrogen fixation activity, which may contribute to their ability to survive on a PS-based diet despite its limited nutritional value.  

Overall, the relation underscores that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, making them a focal species in studies investigating biological solutions to plastic waste management. Their role as both direct degraders and hosts of PS-degrading microbes positions them as a promising model organism for advancing sustainable approaches to mitigating plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae were studied for their ability to biodegrade PS.&lt;SEP&gt;PS is used as a food component to study its effects on the survival, growth, and reproduction of T. molitor larvae.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae were fed with PS to test their ability to consume and survive on polystyrene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biological consumption,degradation,diet,dietary impact,experimental organism,experimental study,feeding experiment,insect activity,insect biodegradation,nitrogen fixation,plastic biodegradation,plastic breakdown,plastic degradation</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
