<edge source="PVC" target="T. molitor larvae">
  <data key="d6">60.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(PVC, T. molitor larvae)** refers to the interaction between polyvinyl chloride (PVC), a widely used synthetic plastic, and the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms), which have been studied for their ability to ingest and partially degrade various plastics. Research has shown that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of consuming PVC when it is provided as their sole diet, demonstrating their general capacity to ingest synthetic polymers. However, unlike with some other plastics such as polystyrene or polyethylene terephthalate (PET), the nutritional value of PVC for the larvae is extremely limited, and ingestion does not support healthy growth or survival.  

Comparative studies highlight that PVC is significantly less biodegradable than PET, and *T. molitor* larvae exhibit reduced efficiency in breaking it down. When fed PVC microplastics, the larvae display severe negative physiological effects, including elevated reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels, increased mortality, and oxidative damage. These outcomes indicate that PVC ingestion imposes a high level of physiological stress. Furthermore, the biodegradation process is incomplete, with depolymerization either inhibited or halted, leading to the accumulation of chlorinated organic byproducts rather than full mineralization.  

Experimental results also show that PVC mass reduction by *T. molitor* larvae remains below 70%, underscoring the limited extent of degradation compared to other plastics. Overall, the relation between PVC and *T. molitor* larvae demonstrates that while the larvae can ingest PVC, the process is inefficient, nutritionally unsustainable, and physiologically harmful, with incomplete biodegradation yielding potentially toxic intermediates. This highlights the challenges of using biological systems for the remediation of PVC waste.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation limitation,diet,larval stress,limited efficiency,nutritional deficiency,oxidative stress,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,plastic degradation comparison,plastic digestion,plastic ingestion,plastic toxicity,toxicity</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756379876</data>
</edge>
