<edge source="Frass" target="T. molitor">
  <data key="d6">83.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Frass, T. molitor)** refers to the connection between the frass, or excrement, produced by *Tenebrio molitor* larvae (commonly known as mealworms), and the materials these larvae consume, particularly synthetic polymers such as polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). Frass in this context is not only a byproduct of digestion but also a key material of interest in studies investigating the biodegradation of plastics.  

Research has shown that when *T. molitor* larvae are fed on plastic feedstocks, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foams, polyethylene microplastics, and polystyrene, the resulting frass contains residual polymers that were not fully degraded during digestion. These residuals are analyzed to assess the extent of polymer breakdown, depolymerization, and the efficiency of the larvae in processing synthetic materials. Analytical approaches often focus on identifying remaining polymer fragments, evaluating surface hydrophobicity, and characterizing the organic composition of the frass.  

In addition to chemical analyses, frass samples are studied for morphological features and microbial communities, as these may provide insights into the mechanisms of plastic degradation and the role of gut microbiota in facilitating polymer breakdown. The presence of residual polymers in frass highlights both the potential and the limitations of *T. molitor* larvae as biological agents for plastic waste management.  

Overall, the relation emphasizes that frass from *T. molitor* serves as a critical indicator of the larvae’s digestive interactions with synthetic polymers, offering valuable data on the degradation pathways, efficiency of biotransformation, and possible environmental implications of using insects for plastic bioremediation.</data>
  <data key="d8">analysis,biodegradation,biological byproduct,digestion byproduct,digestion outcome,experimental analysis,experimental waste,larval digestion,residue analysis,waste analysis,waste production</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756386639</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Frass" target="T. obscurus">
  <data key="d6">75.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Frass, T. obscurus)** refers to the connection between the waste material, or frass, produced by *Tenebrio obscurus* larvae and its role in studies of plastic degradation. Frass from *T. obscurus* has been extensively analyzed in the context of larval feeding on synthetic polymers such as polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polystyrene (PS). Researchers have collected frass samples after larvae consumed these plastic feedstocks to investigate multiple aspects of the degradation process.  

Analyses of *T. obscurus* frass have focused on detecting residual polymers, assessing the extent of polymer depolymerization, and characterizing changes in surface hydrophobicity of the remaining material. These studies provide evidence of partial digestion and transformation of plastics within the larval gut, as indicated by the presence of residual polymer fragments in the frass. In particular, frass derived from larvae fed on LDPE foams and polyethylene microplastics has been examined to determine the efficiency of plastic breakdown and the persistence of polymeric residues.  

Beyond chemical composition, frass from PS-fed *T. obscurus* larvae has also been studied for its morphology, microbial community content, and the broader degradation effects associated with the digestion process. Such investigations highlight the role of gut-associated microbes and enzymatic activity in facilitating polymer degradation, as well as the potential environmental implications of frass as a byproduct of plastic biotransformation.  

In summary, the relation (Frass, *T. obscurus*) captures the scientific focus on frass as a key output of larval plastic consumption, serving as a measurable indicator of polymer degradation, microbial interactions, and the fate of synthetic materials after passage through the insect digestive system.</data>
  <data key="d8">analysis,biodegradation,biological byproduct,digestion byproduct,digestion outcome,experimental analysis,experimental waste,larval digestion,residue analysis,waste analysis,waste production</data>
