<edge source="Mealworm" target="Bran">
  <data key="d6">85.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Bran, Mealworm)** refers to the role of bran as a dietary component in experimental studies involving mealworms. Bran is consistently used as a natural and nutrient-rich control diet in feeding experiments, providing a baseline for comparison against alternative or experimental diets such as plastics. When mealworms are fed bran alone, they exhibit improved survival, growth, pupation, and enzyme activity, highlighting its nutritional value and positive physiological effects.  

In experimental contexts, bran is often supplied either as the sole diet or in combination with plastics to assess its influence on mealworm health and plastic degradation. Co-feeding with bran has been shown to mitigate the negative impacts of microplastic or plastic-only diets, reducing mortality and supporting larval development. Specifically, bran supplementation enhances mealworm resistance to microplastic toxicity, improves growth rates, and promotes higher survival compared to groups fed exclusively on plastics.  

Additionally, bran plays a role in shaping the gut microbiome of mealworms, with co-diets of bran and plastics influencing microbial composition in ways that may support digestion and detoxification processes. Overall, the relation emphasizes that bran serves both as a control diet and as a beneficial co-diet, providing essential nutrients that enhance mealworm health, growth, and resilience in experimental feeding studies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bran was used as a comparative diet to measure mealworm viability against plastic diets.</data>
  <data key="d8">control diet,control experiment,control feed,diet,diet comparison,dietary control,dietary influence,dietary supplement,experiment,experimental control,experimental design,experimental factor,growth support,mitigation,nutrition,nutrition comparison,resistance,survival improvement</data>
