<edge source="Bran" target="T. molitor">
  <data key="d6">72.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(Bran, T. molitor)** refers to the role of bran as a dietary substrate for the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms). Bran is commonly used as a primary feedstock in both experimental and control diets for these larvae. It serves as a natural and nutritionally supportive substrate, often forming the baseline diet against which alternative feeding conditions are compared. Studies have shown that *T. molitor* larvae exhibit a preference for bran over other substrates such as polystyrene (PS), resulting in higher weight gain and improved growth performance when bran is included in their diet.  

In experimental contexts, bran is frequently combined with plastics, including PS, to investigate the larvae’s ability to consume and metabolize synthetic polymers. When provided as a codiet with PS, bran not only supports larval survival but also facilitates comparative analyses of feeding behavior, nutritional intake, and microbial community dynamics. In microbiome studies, bran-fed larvae have been used to examine gut colonization patterns, with associations observed between bran diets and the presence of bacterial taxa such as *Clostridium* spp. and *Bacillus* spp.  

As a control diet, bran enables researchers to assess baseline survival rates, weight gain, and gut microbial composition in *T. molitor* larvae, providing a reference point for evaluating the effects of alternative or experimental feeding substrates. Overall, the relation highlights bran’s dual role as both a standard nutritional substrate and a comparative dietary component in studies of *T. molitor* feeding ecology, physiology, and microbiome interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Bran diets influenced the microbiota composition in T. molitor larvae.</data>
  <data key="d8">codiet,control diet,control group,diet,diet composition,diet influence,diet preference,dietary experiment,dietary supplement,experimental feeding,feeding experiment,insect feeding,microbiome,microbiome influence,microbiota composition,survival comparison</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756388079</data>
</edge>
