<edge source="PS (Polystyrene)" target="T. molitor">
  <data key="d6">97.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between *Polystyrene (PS)* and *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms) refers to the ability of the insect’s larvae to consume and biodegrade this synthetic polymer. Research has demonstrated that *T. molitor* is among the first insect larvae reported to degrade and mineralize polystyrene, highlighting its potential role in plastic waste bioremediation. Experimental studies show that mealworms are capable of ingesting PS and breaking it down within their digestive tract, where enzymatic activity and gut microbiota play a critical role in initiating depolymerization and subsequent degradation pathways. This gut-microbe dependent process enables the conversion of polystyrene into simpler compounds, indicating a biological mechanism for plastic decomposition.  

Although *T. molitor* can successfully degrade PS, its efficiency is comparatively lower than that of *Tenebrio obscurus*, another closely related beetle species. Nonetheless, among various plastics tested, polystyrene was found to be the most susceptible to biodegradation by *T. molitor*, underscoring the insect’s unique capacity to process this otherwise persistent material. Overall, the relation (PS, *T. molitor*) captures the significant finding that mealworm larvae can consume, depolymerize, and partially mineralize polystyrene, albeit at moderate rates, through a gut-microbe mediated mechanism. This discovery positions *T. molitor* as a model organism for studying insect-driven plastic biodegradation and its potential applications in sustainable waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae were referenced in relation to PS biodegradation tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworm larvae digestive tracts were studied under the PS diet variant, correlated with alkaline phosphatase, acid phosphatase, and esterase-lipase C8.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae were fed with PS to study its effects on growth, survival, and development.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biological interaction,consumption,degradation potential,diet-enzyme correlation,efficiency,enzyme activity,experimental reference,feeding experiment,growth impact,microbial dependence,plastic degradation,scientific discovery,susceptibility</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756388301</data>
</edge>
