<edge source="PS (Polystyrene)" target="T. molitor larvae">
  <data key="d6">54.0</data>
  <data key="d7">T. molitor larvae ingest and degrade PS foam, with survival and consumption rates measured under different diet conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Larvae were fed PS, and their consumption, survival, pupation, and digestion rates were measured.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae are involved in PS biodegradation, which is inhibited under antibiotic suppression.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene, showing broad depolymerization patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae biodegrade PS microplastics, producing compounds such as 1,3-di-tert-butyl-2,5-dimethylbenzene.&lt;SEP&gt;T. molitor larvae were studied to determine if they could use polystyrene as a source of mass and energy, but results showed it was insufficient.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,digestion,experimental study,feeding behavior,feeding experiment,microbial dependence,nutrition,plastic biodegradation,plastic breakdown,plastic degradation</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
