<edge source="T. molitor" target="LDPE">
  <data key="d6">207.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(LDPE, *Tenebrio molitor*)** refers to the interaction between low-density polyethylene (LDPE), a common plastic material, and the larvae of *T. molitor*, also known as mealworms. Research has demonstrated that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of ingesting LDPE, including its foam and microplastic forms, and facilitating its biodegradation. This process involves not only the physical consumption of the polymer but also its chemical depolymerization, as evidenced by reductions in molecular weight and the transformation of LDPE into carbon dioxide, biomass, and frass containing chemically modified residues. These findings establish *T. molitor* as a potential biological agent for mitigating plastic waste.  

The biodegradation of LDPE by *T. molitor* is mediated through a combination of larval activity and the metabolic functions of their gut microbiota. Symbiotic microorganisms within the larval gut play a critical role in polymer breakdown, contributing to processes such as nitrogen fixation and other metabolic pathways that enhance degradation. Comparative studies of gut microbial communities have shown that LDPE-fed larvae share more microbial taxa with those fed linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) than with those fed high-density polyethylene (HDPE), suggesting that the gut microbiome adapts differently depending on the type of polyethylene ingested. Importantly, experiments have also demonstrated that LDPE depolymerization can occur even under antibiotic suppression, though at reduced efficiency, indicating that both the larvae and their microbiota contribute to the process.  

Physiological studies have examined the effects of LDPE ingestion on the larvae themselves. Feeding trials that monitored survival rates, weight, and health indicators revealed that while larvae can survive on LDPE, they experience biological stress. Observed effects include oxidative stress and ecotoxicological responses, which suggest that the process of metabolizing LDPE imposes a physiological cost and may reduce larval fitness.  

Overall, the relation (LDPE, *T. molitor*) highlights a dual perspective. On one hand, it demonstrates the capacity of *T. molitor* larvae to consume and biodegrade LDPE through a synergistic insect–microbe system, offering insights into potential biological strategies for addressing plastic pollution. On the other hand, it underscores the ecological and physiological complexities of this interaction, as the degradation process can negatively impact larval health. This makes *T. molitor* an important model organism for studying plastic biodegradation and for exploring sustainable, biologically based approaches to mitigating the environmental challenges posed by persistent plastics.</data>
