<edge source="T. molitor" target="LLDPE">
  <data key="d6">112.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(LLDPE, T. molitor)** refers to the interaction between linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), a commonly used plastic, and the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (mealworms), which have been studied for their potential role in plastic biodegradation. Research has demonstrated that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of ingesting and biodegrading LLDPE, including its microplastic forms. When fed LLDPE, the larvae consumed the material and exhibited measurable biodegradation effects, although the extent of depolymerization was limited. Specifically, residual polymers from LLDPE-fed larvae showed significant increases in molecular weight, suggesting that while chemical modifications and partial degradation occurred, complete breakdown of the polymer chains was not achieved.  

Experimental studies have also examined the physiological and ecological impacts of LLDPE ingestion on *T. molitor*. Feeding on LLDPE was associated with oxidative stress and ecotoxicity effects in the larvae, indicating potential biological costs of plastic consumption. Nutritional impacts were also observed, as the larvae’s diet of LLDPE influenced their health and metabolic responses. Comparative microbial analyses revealed that larvae fed with LLDPE shared more operational taxonomic units (OTUs) with those fed low-density polyethylene (LDPE) than with those fed high-density polyethylene (HDPE), suggesting similarities in gut microbial responses to different polyethylene substrates.  

Overall, the relation highlights that *T. molitor* larvae can ingest and partially biodegrade LLDPE, including microplastics, leading to chemical modifications of the polymer. However, the process results in limited depolymerization and is accompanied by physiological stress and ecological risks for the larvae. This interaction underscores both the potential and the limitations of using *T. molitor* as a biological agent for LLDPE biodegradation.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biodegradation experiment,depolymerization,dietary effect,experiment,experimental diet,experimental feeding,feeding behavior,ingestion,microbial overlap,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,polymer breakdown,stress response</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
