  <data key="d11">1756383348</data>
</edge>
<edge source="T. molitor" target="HDPE">
  <data key="d6">102.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(HDPE, T. molitor)** refers to the interaction between high-density polyethylene (HDPE), a widely used plastic, and the larvae of *Tenebrio molitor* (commonly known as mealworms) in the context of biodegradation studies. Research has demonstrated that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of ingesting HDPE and initiating its biodegradation, though the process is less efficient compared to their degradation of other plastics such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE) and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE). When fed HDPE, the larvae exhibited measurable but limited depolymerization effects, indicating that while some breakdown of the polymer occurs, the extent of degradation remains relatively constrained.  

Experimental studies have involved feeding *T. molitor* larvae HDPE in both bulk and microplastic forms to assess their biodegradation potential, as well as to evaluate the physiological and nutritional impacts of such a diet. The larvae were observed to consume HDPE and produce signs of biodegradation, but the efficiency of this process was modest, suggesting that HDPE’s higher crystallinity and structural stability make it more resistant to enzymatic or microbial breakdown within the larval gut.  

In addition to biodegradation outcomes, physiological responses of the larvae have been documented. Feeding on HDPE induced strong oxidative stress responses, highlighting the biological strain imposed by metabolizing this material. These findings suggest that while *T. molitor* larvae can serve as a biological model for studying HDPE degradation, the process is accompanied by stress-related impacts on the organism and is not as effective as with other polyethylene variants.  

Overall, the relation underscores that *T. molitor* larvae are capable of ingesting and partially biodegrading HDPE, including its microplastic forms, but the degradation is limited in extent and efficiency. This makes them a useful but constrained biological system for exploring potential pathways of HDPE biodegradation and the ecological implications of plastic ingestion by organisms.</data>
