<edge source="Waxworms" target="Polyethylene">
  <data key="d6">95.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between polyethylene and waxworms refers to the discovery that waxworms, particularly through the microbial communities in their digestive systems, have the ability to contribute to the biodegradation of polyethylene, one of the most widely used and environmentally persistent plastics. Scientific studies have shown that bacterial strains isolated from the guts of waxworms are capable of breaking down polyethylene, suggesting a biological pathway for mitigating plastic pollution. Research has focused both on the waxworms themselves and on the specific gut bacteria they host, with findings indicating that these microorganisms play a central role in the degradation process. This relation highlights the potential of waxworms and their gut microbiota as a natural model for developing biotechnological solutions to address the global challenge of polyethylene waste.&lt;SEP&gt;Waxworms contain gut bacterial strains capable of biodegrading polyethylene.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,gut microbes,insect biodegradation,insect gut microbiota,insect larvae,insect-plastic interaction,microbial activity,microbial symbiosis,plastic biodegradation,plastic breakdown,plastic degradation,plastic type</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b</data>
  <data key="d10">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d11">1756386685</data>
</edge>
