<edge source="LDPE" target="T. obscurus">
  <data key="d6">187.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation between **Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) and Tenebrio obscurus (T. obscurus)** refers to the demonstrated ability of T. obscurus larvae to ingest and partially biodegrade LDPE, including both bulk plastic and microplastic forms. Experimental studies have shown that when larvae are fed LDPE, they consume the material and initiate measurable biodegradation processes. These processes are characterized by reductions in molecular weight, indicating depolymerization of the polymer chains, as well as decreases in hydrophobicity, reflecting chemical and structural alterations of the plastic surface. In some studies, LDPE degradation by T. obscurus larvae has been linked to the conversion of plastic into carbon dioxide and biomass, suggesting metabolic assimilation of breakdown products.  

Feeding experiments have also been conducted to assess the physiological and ecological implications of LDPE ingestion. Results indicate that while larvae can metabolize LDPE, the process induces oxidative stress and ecotoxicological responses, highlighting biological costs associated with plastic consumption. Comparative microbial community analyses revealed that larvae fed LDPE shared more operational taxonomic units (OTUs) with those fed linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE) than with those fed high-density polyethylene (HDPE), suggesting that gut microbial responses are substrate-specific and more similar between LDPE and LLDPE. Furthermore, studies have shown that T. obscurus larvae can still depolymerize LDPE under antibiotic suppression, though with reduced efficiency, indicating that both the larvae and their gut microbiota contribute to the biodegradation process.  

In addition to bulk LDPE, T. obscurus larvae have been tested with LDPE foams and microplastics. In these cases, ingestion led to fragmentation and observable degradation effects, confirming that the larvae are capable of altering plastic particles and initiating depolymerization pathways. The oxidative and biodegradation patterns observed in T. obscurus are similar to those reported in Tenebrio molitor, another insect species studied for plastic degradation, suggesting a broader potential among mealworm species for polyethylene breakdown.  

Overall, the relation (LDPE, T. obscurus) highlights the capacity of T. obscurus larvae to act as biological agents in the partial biodegradation of LDPE. This includes the ability to ingest, fragment, and depolymerize the polymer, with contributions from both larval physiology and gut microbial activity. While these findings suggest potential applications in mitigating plastic pollution, they also underscore the need to consider the physiological stress on the larvae and the ecological implications of using insects as agents for synthetic polymer degradation.</data>
