<edge source="LLDPE" target="T. obscurus">
  <data key="d6">112.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The relation **(LLDPE, T. obscurus)** refers to the interaction between larvae of *Tenebrio obscurus* and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), particularly in the context of plastic biodegradation studies. Research has demonstrated that *T. obscurus* larvae are capable of ingesting and partially biodegrading LLDPE, including in its microplastic form. When fed LLDPE, the larvae consumed the material and exhibited measurable biodegradation effects, such as reduced hydrophobicity of the polymer and changes in its physical and chemical properties. However, the extent of degradation was limited, as indicated by increases in the molecular weights of residual polymers, suggesting restricted depolymerization.

Experimental studies have also examined the physiological and ecological impacts of LLDPE ingestion on *T. obscurus* larvae. Feeding trials revealed that consumption of LLDPE induced oxidative stress and ecotoxicity effects, highlighting potential biological costs associated with plastic ingestion. Nutritional impacts were also investigated, as the larvae’s ability to derive sustenance from LLDPE remains constrained compared to natural diets. Comparative microbial community analyses showed that larvae fed with LLDPE shared more operational taxonomic units (OTUs) with those fed low-density polyethylene (LDPE) than with those fed high-density polyethylene (HDPE), suggesting similarities in gut microbial responses to different polyethylene substrates.

Overall, the relation underscores that *T. obscurus* larvae can ingest and partially biodegrade LLDPE, including microplastics, but the process is characterized by limited depolymerization and accompanied by physiological stress. This makes the species a useful biological model for studying the biodegradation potential of synthetic polymers, while also highlighting the challenges and constraints of using insect larvae for effective plastic waste management.</data>
  <data key="d8">biodegradation,biodegradation experiment,depolymerization,dietary effect,experiment,experimental diet,experimental feeding,feeding behavior,ingestion,microbial overlap,plastic consumption,plastic degradation,polymer breakdown,stress response</data>
  <data key="d9">chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
